const questions = [{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes scarcity?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.","Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."],"answer":1,"why":"Scarcity: Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Scarcity","Point beyond a PPC","Labour","Firm opportunity cost"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates scarcity: Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of scarcity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field.","An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm."],"answer":0,"why":"A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. This illustrates scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to scarcity?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":3,"why":"Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to prioritise essential water infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.","Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","The family must give up the planned holiday."],"answer":2,"why":"It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to prioritise essential water infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.","Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":0,"why":"It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 54 tonnes of rice and 103 rolls of cloth to 69 tonnes of rice and 97 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["15 tonnes of rice","6 rolls of cloth","97 rolls of cloth","21 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 6 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to prioritise essential water infrastructure less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Extra capacity may create additional local jobs.","Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."],"answer":3,"why":"Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to prioritise essential water infrastructure. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."],"answer":1,"why":"It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."],"answer":0,"why":"Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes unlimited wants?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another.","Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Unlimited wants: Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Capital","Opportunity cost","Point beyond a PPC","Unlimited wants"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates unlimited wants: Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of unlimited wants?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours."],"answer":2,"why":"As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. This illustrates human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to unlimited wants?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other."],"answer":0,"why":"Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs."],"answer":2,"why":"Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some households may prefer other goods and services.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","New skills may improve later employment opportunities."],"answer":0,"why":"Some households may prefer other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.","Farm productivity may remain low without investment.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","Projects may take years before capacity rises."],"answer":0,"why":"The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","The economy could move closer to efficient production.","The designer could offer higher-value services later."],"answer":1,"why":"Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 58 tonnes of rice and 106 rolls of cloth to 78 tonnes of rice and 98 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["98 rolls of cloth","8 rolls of cloth","28 rolls of cloth","20 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 8 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":2,"why":"Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes what to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."],"answer":0,"why":"What to produce: This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["What to produce","Firm opportunity cost","Allocation choices","Scarcity"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates what to produce: This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of what to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday."],"answer":2,"why":"A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. This illustrates this allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to what to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","Machinery purchases require significant finance.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":3,"why":"It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.","Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase production of renewable-energy equipment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings.","Competition may limit the café's future profit.","It could create employment in a growing industry.","Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers."],"answer":2,"why":"It could create employment in a growing industry."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase production of renewable-energy equipment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology.","Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.","Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":1,"why":"It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."],"answer":1,"why":"Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0031","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes how to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","This allocation question concerns which goods and services an economy should make."],"answer":1,"why":"How to produce: This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0032","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 62 tonnes of rice and 109 rolls of cloth to 87 tonnes of rice and 99 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["25 tonnes of rice","35 rolls of cloth","10 rolls of cloth","99 rolls of cloth"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 10 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0033","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of how to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers."],"answer":3,"why":"A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. This illustrates this allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0034","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to how to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other."],"answer":0,"why":"Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0035","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce automated production at a bakery. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Training and investment consume resources today.","Restarting factories may require extra investment.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources."],"answer":0,"why":"Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0036","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce automated production at a bakery. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","High machinery costs may strain cash flow.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."],"answer":1,"why":"High machinery costs may strain cash flow."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0037","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce automated production at a bakery?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies."],"answer":2,"why":"Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0038","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce automated production at a bakery less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products.","The work may build experience useful in later jobs.","The economy could move closer to efficient production.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers."],"answer":3,"why":"Automation may reduce demand for some workers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0039","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce automated production at a bakery. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0040","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0041","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes for whom to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."],"answer":1,"why":"For whom to produce: This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0042","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["For whom to produce","Wages as a factor reward","Household opportunity cost","Scarcity"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates for whom to produce: This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0043","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of for whom to produce?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes."],"answer":3,"why":"A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. This illustrates this allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0044","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 66 tonnes of rice and 112 rolls of cloth to 96 tonnes of rice and 100 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["100 rolls of cloth","12 rolls of cloth","42 rolls of cloth","30 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 30 tonnes sacrifices 12 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0045","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The immediate fee from the client project is forgone.","Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators."],"answer":2,"why":"It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0046","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","The student earns income for future purchases.","The developer may receive less revenue per home."],"answer":3,"why":"The developer may receive less revenue per home."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0047","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reserve some new homes for lower-income households?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers.","Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.","It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs."],"answer":3,"why":"It could reduce overcrowding and associated social costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0048","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reserve some new homes for lower-income households less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","The worker may access a growing sector.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Farmers could expand agricultural output."],"answer":2,"why":"Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0049","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reserve some new homes for lower-income households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Long-run productive capacity may rise."],"answer":1,"why":"It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0050","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."],"answer":2,"why":"Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0051","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes economic good?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.","Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."],"answer":0,"why":"Economic good: An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0052","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Interest as a factor reward","Outward shift of a PPC","Free good","Economic good"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates economic good: An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0053","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of economic good?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week."],"answer":0,"why":"A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. This illustrates an economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0054","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to economic good?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0055","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current income will be lower during training.","Machinery purchases require significant finance.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may save time previously spent collecting water."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0056","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit."],"answer":1,"why":"Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0057","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 70 tonnes of rice and 115 rolls of cloth to 105 tonnes of rice and 101 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["49 rolls of cloth","35 tonnes of rice","101 rolls of cloth","14 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 35 tonnes sacrifices 14 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0058","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products.","Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Higher productive capacity could support economic growth."],"answer":1,"why":"Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0059","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":2,"why":"Households may save time previously spent collecting water."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0060","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0061","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes free good?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."],"answer":2,"why":"Free good: A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0062","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Free good","Point on a PPC","Land","Labour"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates free good: A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0063","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of free good?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it.","A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials.","A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators."],"answer":1,"why":"On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. This illustrates a free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0064","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to free good?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills."],"answer":0,"why":"Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0065","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Training and investment consume resources today.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Current income will be lower during training.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre."],"answer":1,"why":"Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0066","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food."],"answer":1,"why":"Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0067","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure.","A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.","Public access may support well-being and recreation.","Competition may limit the café's future profit."],"answer":2,"why":"Public access may support well-being and recreation."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0068","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","The designer could offer higher-value services later."],"answer":2,"why":"Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0069","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 74 tonnes of rice and 118 rolls of cloth to 114 tonnes of rice and 102 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["16 rolls of cloth","40 tonnes of rice","56 rolls of cloth","102 rolls of cloth"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 40 tonnes sacrifices 16 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0070","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."],"answer":2,"why":"Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0071","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes allocation choices?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."],"answer":3,"why":"Allocation choices: Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0072","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Allocation choices","Wages as a factor reward","Outward shift of a PPC","Scarcity in time allocation"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates allocation choices: Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0073","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of allocation choices?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons."],"answer":3,"why":"A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. This illustrates resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0074","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to allocation choices?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":2,"why":"Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0075","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to give priority to science classes in the spare room. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding.","Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching."],"answer":3,"why":"More pupils could access specialist science teaching."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0076","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to give priority to science classes in the spare room. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Language classes may become overcrowded.","Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Language classes may become overcrowded."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0077","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to give priority to science classes in the spare room?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","The choice may improve science-related skills.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."],"answer":2,"why":"The choice may improve science-related skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0078","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to give priority to science classes in the spare room less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output.","Reduced disruption may help economic activity.","Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject.","Extra capacity may create additional local jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0079","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to give priority to science classes in the spare room. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching."],"answer":3,"why":"More pupils could access specialist science teaching."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0080","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."],"answer":3,"why":"Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0081","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes land?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services."],"answer":3,"why":"Land: Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0082","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 78 tonnes of rice and 121 rolls of cloth to 88 tonnes of rice and 103 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["103 rolls of cloth","28 rolls of cloth","18 rolls of cloth","10 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 10 tonnes sacrifices 18 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0083","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of land?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables."],"answer":3,"why":"A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. This illustrates land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0084","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to land?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0085","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Investment diverts funds from current consumption.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Current income will be lower during training.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold."],"answer":1,"why":"It could preserve the capacity to grow food."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0086","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":1,"why":"It can restrict housing supply near growing cities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0087","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."],"answer":0,"why":"It may protect ecosystems that support future production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0088","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms could produce more with available inputs.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.","The choice may improve science-related skills.","Farm productivity may remain low without investment."],"answer":3,"why":"Farm productivity may remain low without investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0089","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."],"answer":1,"why":"It could preserve the capacity to grow food."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0090","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."],"answer":3,"why":"Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0091","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.","Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."],"answer":3,"why":"Labour: Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0092","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Economic good","Labour","Wages as a factor reward","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates labour: Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0093","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls.","A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre."],"answer":2,"why":"A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. This illustrates labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0094","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 82 tonnes of rice and 124 rolls of cloth to 97 tonnes of rice and 104 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["104 rolls of cloth","20 rolls of cloth","35 rolls of cloth","15 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 20 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0095","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund training for healthcare workers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0096","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund training for healthcare workers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Public transport supply could increase.","Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Training costs reduce funds available for current services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0097","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to fund training for healthcare workers?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity."],"answer":3,"why":"A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0098","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to fund training for healthcare workers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.","Higher productive capacity could support economic growth.","Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness."],"answer":2,"why":"Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0099","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to fund training for healthcare workers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher skills could improve the quality of treatment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0100","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers use their skills and time to deliver services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0101","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes capital?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.","Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.","For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."],"answer":2,"why":"Capital: Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0102","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["For whom to produce","Capital","Profit as a factor reward","Firm opportunity cost"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates capital: Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0103","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of capital?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment."],"answer":3,"why":"A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. This illustrates capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0104","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to capital?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers."],"answer":3,"why":"Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0105","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in more efficient factory machinery. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.","Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Training and investment consume resources today.","It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0106","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in more efficient factory machinery. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Machinery purchases require significant finance.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.","Output and employment could rise together."],"answer":0,"why":"Machinery purchases require significant finance."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0107","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 86 tonnes of rice and 127 rolls of cloth to 106 tonnes of rice and 123 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["20 tonnes of rice","123 rolls of cloth","4 rolls of cloth","24 rolls of cloth"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 4 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0108","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest in more efficient factory machinery less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered.","The choice may improve science-related skills.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0109","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in more efficient factory machinery. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0110","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other."],"answer":1,"why":"Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0111","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes enterprise?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.","Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.","For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."],"answer":1,"why":"Enterprise: Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0112","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Land","Movement along a PPC","Labour","Enterprise"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates enterprise: Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0113","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of enterprise?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm.","A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials."],"answer":2,"why":"An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. This illustrates enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0114","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to enterprise?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":1,"why":"The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0115","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The showroom refurbishment must be postponed.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.","Current income will be lower during training.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice."],"answer":3,"why":"New firms may introduce innovation and more choice."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0116","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support new entrepreneurs with business advice. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","Public transport supply could increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0117","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support new entrepreneurs with business advice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed.","Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output."],"answer":3,"why":"Successful start-ups can create jobs and raise output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0118","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support new entrepreneurs with business advice less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","The designer could offer higher-value services later.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness."],"answer":0,"why":"Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0119","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 90 tonnes of rice and 130 rolls of cloth to 115 tonnes of rice and 124 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["25 tonnes of rice","6 rolls of cloth","124 rolls of cloth","31 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 6 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0120","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An entrepreneur combines a premises, staff and equipment to open a food-processing firm. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":1,"why":"The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0121","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes rent as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."],"answer":1,"why":"Rent as a factor reward: Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0122","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Interest as a factor reward","Rent as a factor reward","Firm opportunity cost","Allocation choices"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates rent as a factor reward: Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0123","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of rent as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field."],"answer":3,"why":"A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. This illustrates rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0124","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to rent as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."],"answer":1,"why":"Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0125","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Language classes may become overcrowded.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":2,"why":"The owner could earn rent from previously idle land."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0126","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs."],"answer":2,"why":"Rent payments increase farmers' production costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0127","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice.","Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Farmers could expand agricultural output."],"answer":3,"why":"Farmers could expand agricultural output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0128","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Reduced disruption may help economic activity.","Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology.","The choice may improve science-related skills."],"answer":0,"why":"Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0129","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The student earns income for future purchases.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":1,"why":"The owner could earn rent from previously idle land."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0130","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."],"answer":0,"why":"Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0131","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes wages as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced."],"answer":1,"why":"Wages as a factor reward: Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0132","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 50 tonnes of rice and 133 rolls of cloth to 80 tonnes of rice and 125 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["125 rolls of cloth","30 tonnes of rice","38 rolls of cloth","8 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 30 tonnes sacrifices 8 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0133","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of wages as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours."],"answer":3,"why":"A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. This illustrates wages are payments received for supplying labour in production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0134","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to wages as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":2,"why":"Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0135","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Language classes may become overcrowded.","Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher wages may attract qualified drivers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0136","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0137","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.","Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0138","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The choice may improve science-related skills.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability.","Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0139","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher wages may attract qualified drivers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0140","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Using the room for one activity prevents its simultaneous use for the other."],"answer":0,"why":"Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0141","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes interest as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.","Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."],"answer":3,"why":"Interest as a factor reward: Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0142","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Interest as a factor reward","Land","Outward shift of a PPC","Opportunity cost of investment"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates interest as a factor reward: Interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0143","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of interest as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank.","An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls."],"answer":2,"why":"A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. This illustrates interest is the reward earned for supplying capital or lending funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0144","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 54 tonnes of rice and 136 rolls of cloth to 89 tonnes of rice and 126 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["45 rolls of cloth","35 tonnes of rice","126 rolls of cloth","10 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 35 tonnes sacrifices 10 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0145","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.","Training and investment consume resources today.","High machinery costs may strain cash flow.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may build larger financial reserves."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0146","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The student earns income for future purchases.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding."],"answer":3,"why":"Banks may face a higher cost of funding."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0147","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.","Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."],"answer":1,"why":"Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0148","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.","The economy could move closer to efficient production.","A safer home can improve living conditions.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0149","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","The student earns income for future purchases.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Households may build larger financial reserves."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may build larger financial reserves."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0150","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A saver receives interest after depositing money in a bank. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example."],"answer":1,"why":"Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0151","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes profit as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative.","Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.","An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."],"answer":1,"why":"Profit as a factor reward: Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0152","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Profit as a factor reward","Firm opportunity cost","For whom to produce","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates profit as a factor reward: Profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0153","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of profit as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert."],"answer":0,"why":"A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. This illustrates profit is the residual reward to enterprise after business costs are deducted from revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0154","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to profit as a factor reward?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."],"answer":0,"why":"Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0155","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Investment diverts funds from current consumption.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold."],"answer":2,"why":"The café may serve more customers and increase revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0156","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Expansion can raise fixed costs before demand grows."],"answer":3,"why":"Expansion can raise fixed costs before demand grows."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0157","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 58 tonnes of rice and 100 rolls of cloth to 98 tonnes of rice and 88 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["88 rolls of cloth","52 rolls of cloth","12 rolls of cloth","40 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 40 tonnes sacrifices 12 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0158","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms could produce more with available inputs.","Competition may limit the café's future profit.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Competition may limit the café's future profit."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0159","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","The student earns income for future purchases.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":0,"why":"The café may serve more customers and increase revenue."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0160","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."],"answer":2,"why":"Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0161","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment.","Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made.","A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."],"answer":2,"why":"Opportunity cost: Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0162","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Economic good","Point inside a PPC","Opportunity cost","Movement along a PPC"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates opportunity cost: Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0163","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert."],"answer":3,"why":"A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. This illustrates opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone when a choice is made."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0164","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":3,"why":"Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0165","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training costs reduce funds available for current services.","Language classes may become overcrowded.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","The student earns income for future purchases."],"answer":3,"why":"The student earns income for future purchases."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0166","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice."],"answer":2,"why":"The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0167","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The work may build experience useful in later jobs.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed.","Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.","A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required."],"answer":0,"why":"The work may build experience useful in later jobs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0168","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["It could create employment in a growing industry.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork.","A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity."],"answer":2,"why":"Fatigue may reduce performance in schoolwork."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0169","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 62 tonnes of rice and 103 rolls of cloth to 72 tonnes of rice and 89 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["89 rolls of cloth","24 rolls of cloth","14 rolls of cloth","10 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 10 tonnes sacrifices 14 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0170","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0171","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes household opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision."],"answer":2,"why":"Household opportunity cost: For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0172","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Scarcity in time allocation","Enterprise","Household opportunity cost","How to produce"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates household opportunity cost: For households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0173","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of household opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday."],"answer":3,"why":"A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. This illustrates for households, spending on one good prevents spending the same money on an alternative."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0174","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to household opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."],"answer":3,"why":"The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0175","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance."],"answer":0,"why":"Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0176","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","The family must give up the planned holiday."],"answer":3,"why":"The family must give up the planned holiday."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0177","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use household savings to repair a leaking roof?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A safer home can improve living conditions.","Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run.","Farm productivity may remain low without investment."],"answer":0,"why":"A safer home can improve living conditions."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0178","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use household savings to repair a leaking roof less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.","Public access may support well-being and recreation.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings."],"answer":3,"why":"Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0179","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Public transport supply could increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Repairs may prevent more expensive damage later."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0180","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A family uses its savings on home repairs rather than a holiday. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."],"answer":3,"why":"The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0181","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes worker opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.","Land is the factor of production comprising natural resources used to produce goods and services.","The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."],"answer":1,"why":"Worker opportunity cost: For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0182","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 66 tonnes of rice and 106 rolls of cloth to 81 tonnes of rice and 90 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["90 rolls of cloth","16 rolls of cloth","31 rolls of cloth","15 tonnes of rice"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 16 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0183","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of worker opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A farmer pays an annual fee to use a neighbouring field.","A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water.","A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs."],"answer":1,"why":"A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. This illustrates for a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0184","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to worker opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."],"answer":2,"why":"The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0185","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Restarting factories may require extra investment."],"answer":0,"why":"New skills may improve later employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0186","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching.","Current income will be lower during training."],"answer":3,"why":"Current income will be lower during training."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0187","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Projects may take years before capacity rises.","Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support.","The worker may access a growing sector.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable."],"answer":2,"why":"The worker may access a growing sector."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0188","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Farmers could expand agricultural output.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."],"answer":3,"why":"Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0189","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment.","New skills may improve later employment opportunities.","Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."],"answer":2,"why":"New skills may improve later employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0190","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."],"answer":2,"why":"The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0191","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes firm opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","Labour is the human physical and mental effort used in production."],"answer":0,"why":"Firm opportunity cost: For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0192","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Capital","Firm opportunity cost","Interest as a factor reward","Land"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates firm opportunity cost: For a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0193","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of firm opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables.","A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom."],"answer":3,"why":"A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. This illustrates for a firm, using funds in one project means forgoing the best alternative investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0194","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 70 tonnes of rice and 109 rolls of cloth to 90 tonnes of rice and 91 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["38 rolls of cloth","91 rolls of cloth","18 rolls of cloth","20 tonnes of rice"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 18 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0195","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in a new packaging line. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Restarting factories may require extra investment.","Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents."],"answer":2,"why":"Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0196","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in a new packaging line. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","The showroom refurbishment must be postponed.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":1,"why":"The showroom refurbishment must be postponed."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0197","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in a new packaging line?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.","Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land."],"answer":0,"why":"The business may reduce labour cost per item."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0198","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest in a new packaging line less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The economy could move closer to efficient production.","Machine performance might be worse than expected.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness."],"answer":1,"why":"Machine performance might be worse than expected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0199","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in a new packaging line. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":1,"why":"Packaging could become faster and less wasteful."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0200","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."],"answer":1,"why":"The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0201","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes government opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."],"answer":3,"why":"Government opportunity cost: Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0202","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Government opportunity cost","Opportunity cost","PPC opportunity cost","Household opportunity cost"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates government opportunity cost: Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0203","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of government opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons."],"answer":2,"why":"A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. This illustrates public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0204","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to government opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices."],"answer":0,"why":"The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0205","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","Training and investment consume resources today.","Restarting factories may require extra investment."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0206","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre."],"answer":3,"why":"Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0207","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 74 tonnes of rice and 112 rolls of cloth to 99 tonnes of rice and 92 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["92 rolls of cloth","25 tonnes of rice","20 rolls of cloth","45 rolls of cloth"],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 20 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0208","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen.","The spending may reduce hardship where essential services are scarce.","Extra capacity may create additional local jobs."],"answer":1,"why":"Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0209","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0210","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."],"answer":3,"why":"The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0211","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes opportunity cost of investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology.","The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","Capital consists of man-made resources used to produce other goods and services."],"answer":2,"why":"Opportunity cost of investment: The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0212","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Opportunity cost of investment","Inward shift of a PPC","Scarcity in time allocation","Profit as a factor reward"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates opportunity cost of investment: The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0213","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of opportunity cost of investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","A medical clinic employs nurses, technicians and reception staff.","As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips."],"answer":0,"why":"A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. This illustrates the opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0214","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to opportunity cost of investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."],"answer":3,"why":"Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0215","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumers may face fewer refrigerators.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Some households may prefer other goods and services.","The developer may receive less revenue per home."],"answer":1,"why":"The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0216","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Future production possibilities may increase.","Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries."],"answer":3,"why":"Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0217","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds.","Demand for solar equipment may be too weak to justify the switch.","Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed."],"answer":1,"why":"Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0218","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Extra capacity may create additional local jobs.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs.","Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate."],"answer":3,"why":"Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0219","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 78 tonnes of rice and 115 rolls of cloth to 108 tonnes of rice and 111 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["4 rolls of cloth","111 rolls of cloth","34 rolls of cloth","30 tonnes of rice"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 30 tonnes sacrifices 4 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0220","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A delivery firm can afford new vehicles or new warehouse software, but not both. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone.","Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."],"answer":2,"why":"Choosing the vehicles means the potential benefit of improved warehouse software is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0221","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes scarcity in time allocation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Human wants are numerous and expand as people seek more goods and services.","Public spending on one project prevents use of the same funds for another.","This allocation question concerns which methods and combinations of inputs should be used.","Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."],"answer":3,"why":"Scarcity in time allocation: Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0222","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["How to produce","Scarcity in time allocation","Scarcity","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates scarcity in time allocation: Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0223","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of scarcity in time allocation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls.","A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week.","A school can use a spare classroom for either science lessons or language lessons.","An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing."],"answer":1,"why":"A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. This illustrates time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0224","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to scarcity in time allocation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Scarce inputs used for supply could have produced alternative goods.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone."],"answer":2,"why":"Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0225","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take the training course rather than the new client project. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Some households may prefer other goods and services.","Machinery purchases require significant finance."],"answer":1,"why":"Training may increase the designer's future productivity."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0226","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to take the training course rather than the new client project. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The immediate fee from the client project is forgone.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households."],"answer":0,"why":"The immediate fee from the client project is forgone."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0227","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to take the training course rather than the new client project?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","Automation may reduce demand for some workers.","Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours.","The designer could offer higher-value services later."],"answer":3,"why":"The designer could offer higher-value services later."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0228","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to take the training course rather than the new client project less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Higher productive capacity could support economic growth.","The course may not improve marketable skills."],"answer":3,"why":"The course may not improve marketable skills."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0229","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to take the training course rather than the new client project. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may build larger financial reserves.","Meeting basic needs may raise welfare for lower-income households.","More pupils could access specialist science teaching.","Training may increase the designer's future productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Training may increase the designer's future productivity."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0230","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A self-employed designer can accept either a new client project or an advanced training course this week. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0231","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes point on a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Rent is the income earned by owners for supplying land or natural resources.","An economic good is scarce relative to demand and has an opportunity cost of provision.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time."],"answer":2,"why":"Point on a PPC: A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0232","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 82 tonnes of rice and 118 rolls of cloth to 117 tonnes of rice and 112 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["6 rolls of cloth","112 rolls of cloth","35 tonnes of rice","41 rolls of cloth"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 35 tonnes sacrifices 6 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0233","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of point on a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment.","A bakery considers using automated ovens instead of hiring additional bakers.","An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom."],"answer":2,"why":"An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. This illustrates a point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0234","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to point on a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The holiday is forgone when scarce savings are committed to repairs.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","Payment provides an incentive to make land available for production."],"answer":2,"why":"At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0235","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Current income will be lower during training.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle.","Language classes may become overcrowded.","Banks may face a higher cost of funding."],"answer":1,"why":"Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0236","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","The output mix may not match society's priorities.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":1,"why":"The output mix may not match society's priorities."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0237","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to keep existing resources fully employed in current production?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology."],"answer":3,"why":"Maximum feasible output can be obtained with current technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0238","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to keep existing resources fully employed in current production less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies.","Public access may support well-being and recreation.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0239","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher output per hour may reduce unit production costs.","Households may build larger financial reserves.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."],"answer":3,"why":"Resources are not left unnecessarily idle."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0240","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy operates on its boundary while producing medicines and clothing. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better equipment can increase the productive capacity of workers.","At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."],"answer":1,"why":"At full productive efficiency, more of one output requires less of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0241","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes point inside a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["This allocation question concerns who receives goods and services that are produced.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used.","Resource allocation is the decision about how scarce inputs are distributed among alternative uses.","An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."],"answer":1,"why":"Point inside a PPC: A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0242","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Enterprise","Economic good","Point inside a PPC","Scarcity in time allocation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates point inside a ppc: A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0243","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of point inside a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A town has funds for either a water plant or a new stadium, but not both.","A transport firm pays drivers for their working hours.","A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials."],"answer":3,"why":"A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. This illustrates a point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0244","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 86 tonnes of rice and 121 rolls of cloth to 126 tonnes of rice and 113 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["48 rolls of cloth","40 tonnes of rice","113 rolls of cloth","8 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 40 tonnes sacrifices 8 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0245","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reactivate unused productive capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.","Output and employment could rise together.","Some households may prefer other goods and services.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":1,"why":"Output and employment could rise together."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0246","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reactivate unused productive capacity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Restarting factories may require extra investment.","The owner could earn rent from previously idle land."],"answer":2,"why":"Restarting factories may require extra investment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0247","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reactivate unused productive capacity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The economy could move closer to efficient production.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met."],"answer":0,"why":"The economy could move closer to efficient production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0248","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reactivate unused productive capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","Improved reliability can lower the rate of faulty products."],"answer":0,"why":"Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0249","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reactivate unused productive capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Output and employment could rise together.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":2,"why":"Output and employment could rise together."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0250","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A region has idle workshops and unemployed workers despite adequate raw materials. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting.","Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other.","Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques.","Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative."],"answer":1,"why":"Better use of idle inputs could raise both products without sacrificing the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0251","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes point beyond a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The opportunity cost of an investment is the value of the next best alternative investment not selected.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."],"answer":3,"why":"Point beyond a PPC: A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0252","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Point beyond a PPC","Point inside a PPC","Opportunity cost of investment","Scarcity"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates point beyond a ppc: A point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0253","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of point beyond a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist.","A student chooses a paid weekend shift rather than attending a concert.","A manufacturer chooses a new packaging line instead of renovating its showroom.","A remote village pays workers and uses pipes to deliver treated drinking water."],"answer":0,"why":"A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. This illustrates a point outside the current production possibility curve is unattainable with existing resources and technology."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0254","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to point beyond a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","Time spent on one activity cannot be used for the next best option.","Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output."],"answer":0,"why":"Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0255","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand productive capacity through training and investment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.","Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Future production possibilities may increase.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":2,"why":"Future production possibilities may increase."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0256","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand productive capacity through training and investment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Packaging could become faster and less wasteful.","Training and investment consume resources today.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","It could expand access to low-emission energy equipment."],"answer":1,"why":"Training and investment consume resources today."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0257","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 90 tonnes of rice and 124 rolls of cloth to 100 tonnes of rice and 114 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["10 rolls of cloth","114 rolls of cloth","20 rolls of cloth","10 tonnes of rice"],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing rice output by 10 tonnes sacrifices 10 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0258","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand productive capacity through training and investment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A larger skilled workforce could expand healthcare capacity.","Projects may take years before capacity rises.","It could help workers remain productive by reducing water-related illness.","Considering alternatives can reduce waste of scarce investment funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Projects may take years before capacity rises."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0259","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand productive capacity through training and investment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future production possibilities may increase.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses."],"answer":0,"why":"Future production possibilities may increase."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0260","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A planner proposes output targets that require more skilled workers than currently exist. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Interest rewards the postponement of spending and can encourage saving."],"answer":1,"why":"Current capacity cannot reach that combination without growth or technological change."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0261","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes movement along a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","Wages are payments received for supplying labour in production.","The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product.","Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Movement along a PPC: Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0262","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["What to produce","Movement along a PPC","PPC opportunity cost","Interest as a factor reward"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates movement along a ppc: Moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0263","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of movement along a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A government builds a flood barrier instead of upgrading a public sports centre.","A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories.","A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it."],"answer":2,"why":"A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. This illustrates moving along a production possibility curve involves reallocating resources between two goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0264","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to movement along a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growing wants exceed available resources, creating continuing choices.","Positive profit rewards risk-taking and can finance expansion.","Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment."],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0265","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Public transport supply could increase.","Residents lose the benefits of the delayed sports centre.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","The showroom refurbishment must be postponed."],"answer":0,"why":"Public transport supply could increase."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0266","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","It could improve public health by providing safe water to more households.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Consumers may face fewer refrigerators."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers may face fewer refrigerators."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0267","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better transport could support workers' mobility.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable.","Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice.","Competition may limit the café's future profit."],"answer":0,"why":"Better transport could support workers' mobility."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0268","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production.","Reduced disruption may help economic activity.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Public access may support well-being and recreation."],"answer":0,"why":"Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0269","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 50 tonnes of rice and 127 rolls of cloth to 65 tonnes of rice and 115 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["115 rolls of cloth","15 tonnes of rice","27 rolls of cloth","12 rolls of cloth"],"answer":3,"why":"Increasing rice output by 15 tonnes sacrifices 12 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0270","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country uses more of its factories for buses and fewer for refrigerators. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other.","The showroom upgrade is the opportunity cost of choosing packaging equipment.","Pay encourages people to supply time and skills to employers.","Natural resources provide inputs whose availability constrains output."],"answer":0,"why":"Increasing one product at full efficiency requires sacrificing some output of the other."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0271","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes outward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision.","An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output.","A point on a production possibility curve represents an attainable combination using resources efficiently at a given time.","A point inside a production possibility curve represents attainable output with some resources underused or inefficiently used."],"answer":1,"why":"Outward shift of a PPC: An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0272","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Scarcity in time allocation","Firm opportunity cost","Point on a PPC","Outward shift of a PPC"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates outward shift of a ppc: An outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0273","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of outward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A housing developer must decide whether to build high-price apartments or low-cost homes.","A qualified mechanic chooses a lower-paid apprenticeship in renewable energy.","A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment.","On a breezy coast, residents can enjoy naturally occurring wind without using scarce resources to create it."],"answer":2,"why":"A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. This illustrates an outward shift of a production possibility curve shows growth in maximum potential output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0274","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to outward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Choosing the shift sacrifices the benefit of the concert, the next best alternative.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","Workers use their skills and time to deliver services.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."],"answer":1,"why":"More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0275","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Long-run productive capacity may rise.","Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources.","It can restrict housing supply near growing cities.","The student loses leisure time and enjoyment."],"answer":0,"why":"Long-run productive capacity may rise."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0276","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food.","The café may serve more customers and increase revenue.","Investment diverts funds from current consumption."],"answer":3,"why":"Investment diverts funds from current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0277","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to finance training and productivity-improving equipment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings.","Poor environmental management could reduce amenity quality.","Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance.","Firms could produce more with available inputs."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms could produce more with available inputs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0278","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to finance training and productivity-improving equipment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on the quality of training and equipment.","Farmers could expand agricultural output.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Banks may attract funds they can use to finance lending."],"answer":0,"why":"Benefits depend on the quality of training and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0279","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Training may increase the designer's future productivity.","Long-run productive capacity may rise.","New firms may introduce innovation and more choice.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price."],"answer":1,"why":"Long-run productive capacity may rise."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0280","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country trains technicians and adopts more productive manufacturing equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods.","The entrepreneur coordinates inputs and risks losing invested funds.","Abundant natural availability means using the good does not displace another production use in that setting."],"answer":1,"why":"More or better resources allow greater possible production of both goods."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0281","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["For a worker, choosing one occupation involves giving up the next best job or use of time.","Time is a scarce resource, and its allocation involves opportunity cost.","An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."],"answer":2,"why":"Inward shift of a PPC: An inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0282","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 54 tonnes of rice and 130 rolls of cloth to 74 tonnes of rice and 116 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["34 rolls of cloth","14 rolls of cloth","20 tonnes of rice","116 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 20 tonnes sacrifices 14 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0283","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of inward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A small café earns revenue that exceeds wages, rent and ingredient costs.","As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks.","A furniture factory installs new cutting machines and warehouse equipment."],"answer":2,"why":"Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. This illustrates an inward shift of a production possibility curve shows a fall in the maximum attainable output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0284","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inward shift of a ppc?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","The sports centre improvement is the next best alternative forgone.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output."],"answer":3,"why":"Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0285","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rent payments increase farmers' production costs.","Nearby construction may reduce access to the open space.","It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents.","Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."],"answer":3,"why":"Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0286","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher wages may attract qualified drivers.","Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs.","Lower flood damage could protect homes and businesses.","The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit."],"answer":1,"why":"Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0287","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions.","Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land.","Competition may limit the café's future profit.","Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs."],"answer":3,"why":"Repair work may reopen trade routes and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0288","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs.","Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure.","It may protect ecosystems that support future production.","Consistent production may reduce waste and improve reliability."],"answer":1,"why":"Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0289","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Restored infrastructure could recover lost production.","Households may save time previously spent collecting water.","Households may build larger financial reserves.","It could preserve the capacity to grow food."],"answer":0,"why":"Restored infrastructure could recover lost production."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0290","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Severe flooding destroys roads, factories and electricity networks. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand, resources and social priorities shape the mix of output.","Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output.","Relative factor costs, productivity and technology determine production techniques."],"answer":1,"why":"Lost productive resources reduce the economy's possible output."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0291","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes ppc opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Enterprise involves organising other factors of production and taking business risks.","The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product.","Scarcity occurs when limited resources cannot satisfy all human wants.","A free good is available without scarcity under the relevant conditions and has no opportunity cost of provision."],"answer":1,"why":"PPC opportunity cost: The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0292","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Rent as a factor reward","Profit as a factor reward","PPC opportunity cost","Scarcity"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates ppc opportunity cost: The slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0293","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of ppc opportunity cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["As incomes rise in a city, households demand larger homes and more leisure trips.","A farm uses fertile soil and a river to grow vegetables.","An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls.","A region considers producing more solar panels or more furniture with its existing factories."],"answer":2,"why":"An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. This illustrates the slope of a production possibility curve reflects the opportunity cost of producing more of one product."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0294","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economy moves along its PPC from 58 tonnes of rice and 133 rolls of cloth to 83 tonnes of rice and 117 rolls of cloth. What is the opportunity cost of the extra rice?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["25 tonnes of rice","16 rolls of cloth","117 rolls of cloth","41 rolls of cloth"],"answer":1,"why":"Increasing rice output by 25 tonnes sacrifices 16 rolls of cloth, which is the opportunity cost."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0295","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["High machinery costs may strain cash flow.","The family must give up the planned holiday.","Fewer resources may remain for furniture manufacturing.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."],"answer":3,"why":"Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0296","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The firm may choose the project with the highest expected net benefit.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price.","The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold.","It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes."],"answer":2,"why":"The country gives up cloth output that could have been sold."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0297","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reallocate productive resources towards wheat?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports.","Expected gains depend on the demand for each subject.","Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered.","Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher wheat output may reduce dependence on food imports."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0298","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reallocate productive resources towards wheat less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required.","The business may reduce labour cost per item.","It could create employment in a growing industry.","Improved staff retention could reduce recruitment costs."],"answer":0,"why":"A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0299","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reallocate productive resources towards wheat. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It could improve access to housing for people with low incomes.","Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong.","Machines may raise output per worker and reduce unit costs.","Residents may enjoy the amenity without a purchase price."],"answer":1,"why":"Food availability could rise if demand for wheat is strong."},{"id":"0455-C01-P1-MCQ-0300","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy increases wheat production from 50 to 60 tonnes by reducing cloth output from 90 to 82 rolls. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The mechanic forgoes immediate earnings in exchange for possible future skills.","Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example.","Limited funds force a choice, so another valued project is forgone.","Purchasing power and public priorities help determine who receives output."],"answer":1,"why":"Ten extra tonnes of wheat require sacrificing eight rolls of cloth in this example."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define scarcity. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Resources are finite / limited (1); Wants exceed those resources / not all wants can be satisfied (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define unlimited wants. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• People desire many goods and services (1); Satisfying some wants leads to further wants / wants are not all fully satisfiable (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what is meant by the economic question “what to produce”. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• An economy must choose the goods and services to make (1); Scarce resources prevent every desired product being produced (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• The choice concerns the mix of output (1); For example, more consumer goods may require fewer capital goods (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what is meant by the economic question “how to produce”. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Producers choose a method of production (1); They decide the combination of labour and capital / other factors to use (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Labour-intensive production uses relatively more labour (1); Capital-intensive production uses relatively more machinery (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what is meant by the economic question “for whom to produce”. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Output must be distributed between people or groups (1); The question concerns who receives or can consume the goods and services (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Income affects ability to buy market output (1); People with greater purchasing power can obtain more goods and services (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define free good. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A good that is not scarce in the relevant circumstances (1); Supplying or consuming it has no opportunity cost / no alternative output is sacrificed (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define resource allocation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Deciding how factors of production are distributed (1); Between competing uses / to produce different goods and services (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define land. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Natural resources / resources supplied by nature (1); Used in producing goods and services (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define labour. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Human effort / work (1); Physical or mental effort used in production (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define capital. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Human-made goods / equipment (1); Used to produce other goods and services (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define rent as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A payment / income received by an owner (1); For supplying land / natural resources to production (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define wages as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Payments / income received by workers (1); In return for supplying labour / work in production (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define interest as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Income received by the owner / lender of capital (1); In return for making capital / loanable funds available for production (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define profit as a factor reward. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Total revenue less total cost (1); The residual reward for enterprise / risk-taking (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define opportunity cost. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The next best alternative (1); Forgone / given up when making a choice (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how opportunity cost applies to a worker choosing an occupation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A worker can use time in different jobs or activities (1); Choosing one gives up the next best alternative (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A worker accepts one occupation (1); The best alternative occupation or leisure opportunity is forgone (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how opportunity cost applies to a firm choosing an investment. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Funds used on one project cannot finance another simultaneously (1); The next best investment return is forgone (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A firm uses scarce resources for one product (1); Output of the next best alternative product is sacrificed (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how opportunity cost applies to government spending. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Public funds are limited (1); Spending on one project gives up the next best alternative use (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A government finances one service rather than another (1); The benefit of the best rejected service is the opportunity cost (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define opportunity cost of investment. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The next best alternative use of investment funds (1); The return / benefit forgone by choosing the selected investment (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain why deciding how to use time involves opportunity cost. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Time is finite (1); Time spent on one activity gives up the next best alternative (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A person spends an hour working (1); The best alternative use of that hour, such as leisure, is forgone (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what a point inside a production possibility curve shows. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Output is attainable with current resources (1); Some resources are unemployed / used inefficiently (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• The combination is below maximum feasible output (1); More of at least one good could be produced without reducing the other (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what a point beyond a production possibility curve shows. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The output combination is currently unattainable (1); Existing resources and technology are insufficient (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• The combination exceeds current productive capacity (1); Growth in resources or better technology would be needed to reach it (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what a movement along a production possibility curve shows. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Resources are reallocated between two products (1); More of one requires less of the other / an opportunity cost arises (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• The economy changes the combination of output (1); It remains on the same PPC with unchanged productive capacity (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what an outward shift of a production possibility curve shows. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Maximum possible output increases (1); Productive capacity grows / more combinations become attainable (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• The PPC moves away from the origin (1); More of either or both goods can be produced with improved resources or technology (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M02-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what an inward shift of a production possibility curve shows. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Maximum possible output decreases (1); Productive capacity falls / fewer combinations are attainable (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• The PPC moves towards the origin (1); Resource loss or worsening productive capability reduces potential output (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to prioritise essential water infrastructure. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Safe water reduces waterborne illness (1) → Households spend less on treatment (1) → More income remains for other needs (1).\n\n2. Healthier workers are absent less often (1) → More working hours are available (1) → Output and labour productivity may rise (1).\n\n3. Construction requires workers and materials (1) → Demand for construction labour increases (1) → Previously unemployed workers gain earnings (1) → Their living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More essential services reach poorer households (1) → Basic needs are better satisfied (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Better nutrition and healthcare improve health (1) → Workers lose fewer days to illness (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).\n\n3. Essential services become more affordable (1) → Low-income households retain more disposable income (1) → They can purchase other necessities (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Renewable equipment replaces fossil-fuel equipment (1) → Less fuel combustion reduces emissions (1) → External environmental costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Expanding equipment production requires workers (1) → Demand for labour increases (1) → Employment and earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Producing more equipment can lower its price (1) → Renewable energy becomes more affordable (1) → Users may reduce spending on imported fuels (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers introduce automated production at a bakery. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Ovens automate repetitive baking tasks (1) → Existing workers can supervise more production (1) → Labour cost per loaf may fall (1).\n\n2. Automated controls reduce baking errors (1) → Fewer loaves and ingredients are wasted (1) → Average variable cost may fall (1).\n\n3. Ovens can operate for longer periods (1) → Daily production capacity increases (1) → The bakery can meet additional demand (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reserve some new homes for lower-income households could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More homes are available to low-income households (1) → Fewer households lack adequate shelter (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Better housing reduces overcrowding and damp (1) → Residents experience fewer health problems (1) → Their attendance at work or school may improve (1).\n\n3. Access to a home near jobs can improve (1) → Travel costs for residents may fall (1) → A wider range of jobs becomes accessible (1) → Geographical mobility of labour may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Reliable clean water reduces disease (1) → Households need less medical treatment (1) → Healthcare resources are freed for other uses (1).\n\n2. Firms face fewer water interruptions (1) → Production downtime falls (1) → Output per period may increase (1).\n\n3. Expanding the network improves access (1) → Households spend less time collecting water (1) → More time is available for paid work (1) → Household earnings may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Free public access avoids an admission charge (1) → Low-income households can enjoy outdoor recreation (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).\n\n2. Conserving open space protects natural habitats (1) → Environmental degradation is reduced (1) → External benefits accrue to local residents (1).\n\n3. Coastal wind remains available for renewable generation (1) → Producers can use a natural energy resource (1) → Dependence on fossil fuels may fall (1) → Air pollution may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to give priority to science classes in a spare classroom at a school. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More science lessons improve scientific knowledge (1) → Pupils gain skills needed in technical occupations (1) → Occupational mobility may increase (1).\n\n2. Access to science teaching broadens qualifications (1) → More pupils become eligible for skilled jobs (1) → Their future earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Using a spare classroom avoids building another room (1) → More teaching can occur with existing capital (1) → The school uses its resources more fully (1) → Cost per lesson may be lower (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Farmland remains available for cultivation (1) → Domestic food production can be maintained (1) → Dependence on imported food may fall (1).\n\n2. Farming continues to require labour (1) → Rural workers retain employment (1) → Rural household incomes are protected (1).\n\n3. Fertile soil produces relatively high yields (1) → More food can be grown per hectare (1) → Average production costs may be lower (1) → Consumers may benefit from lower food prices (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to fund training for healthcare workers could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training improves healthcare workers’ skills (1) → Treatment becomes more effective (1) → Patients recover and return to work sooner (1).\n\n2. More workers gain recognised qualifications (1) → The supply of skilled healthcare labour rises (1) → Staff shortages may decrease (1).\n\n3. Trained staff can treat more patients effectively (1) → Output per worker-hour increases (1) → Cost per successful treatment may fall (1) → Existing healthcare budgets can serve more people (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to invest in more efficient factory machinery. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. New machinery performs production tasks faster (1) → Workers can produce more per hour (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. More precise machinery reduces defective output (1) → Less raw material is wasted (1) → Average variable cost may decrease (1).\n\n3. Machines expand the factory’s maximum output (1) → More orders can be accepted (1) → Sales volume may increase (1) → Total revenue may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to support new entrepreneurs with business advice might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Advice improves business planning (1) → Entrepreneurs make better allocation decisions (1) → The risk of avoidable business failure falls (1).\n\n2. Advice on finance helps viable firms obtain funds (1) → Firms can buy productive equipment (1) → Their productive capacity may increase (1).\n\n3. More entrepreneurs can enter markets (1) → The number of competing firms increases (1) → Existing firms face pressure to reduce prices (1) → Consumers may gain purchasing power (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Previously unused land is cultivated (1) → More agricultural goods are produced (1) → Total output increases (1).\n\n2. Landowners receive rental income (1) → Their household income rises (1) → Their ability to buy goods and services increases (1).\n\n3. The cooperative needs farm workers (1) → Demand for rural labour increases (1) → Unemployed workers may find work (1) → Rural poverty may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher wages make driving more attractive (1) → More experienced drivers apply or remain (1) → Unfilled vacancies may decrease (1).\n\n2. Higher pay may improve motivation (1) → Absenteeism and staff turnover may fall (1) → Recruitment and training costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. Drivers’ disposable incomes increase (1) → They can afford more goods and services (1) → Material living standards may improve (1) → Poverty among low-paid drivers may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Saving earns a higher return (1) → Households have a stronger incentive to save (1) → Deposits may increase (1).\n\n2. Savers receive more interest income (1) → Their disposable incomes rise (1) → They can afford more consumption in the future (1).\n\n3. Additional deposits provide banks with funds (1) → Banks may lend more to viable firms (1) → Firms can finance capital investment (1) → Productive capacity may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More seats allow more customers to be served (1) → Sales volume can increase (1) → Total revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Expansion requires additional staff (1) → Demand for café labour rises (1) → Employment and wage income may increase (1).\n\n3. Using retained profit avoids a bank loan (1) → No interest is payable on that funding (1) → Financial costs are lower than with borrowing (1) → More future revenue can be retained as profit (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Working the shift provides additional wages (1) → Disposable income increases (1) → More goods and services become affordable (1).\n\n2. Earnings can be saved (1) → Households build a financial buffer (1) → Unexpected expenses become easier to meet (1).\n\n3. Extra work can develop experience (1) → The worker’s skills may improve (1) → Future job opportunities may expand (1) → Future earning potential may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to use household savings to repair a leaking roof. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Repair prevents further water damage (1) → Future repair costs may be avoided (1) → Household resources are conserved (1).\n\n2. A drier home reduces health risks (1) → Medical expenses may fall (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).\n\n3. Paying from savings avoids a new loan (1) → No loan interest needs to be paid (1) → Future disposable income is less committed (1) → Financial pressure on the household may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Specialist training develops scarce skills (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Future wages may be higher (1).\n\n2. New qualifications make more occupations accessible (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Risk of long-term unemployment may fall (1).\n\n3. Training can support entry into a growing industry (1) → The worker faces more job vacancies (1) → Employment prospects may become more secure (1) → Future income may be more stable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to invest in a new packaging line could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Machinery performs packaging tasks more quickly (1) → Existing workers can handle more output (1) → Unit labour cost may fall (1).\n\n2. Consistent packaging reduces damaged products (1) → Fewer products are wasted or returned (1) → Average cost may fall (1).\n\n3. More packaging capacity removes a production bottleneck (1) → The firm can fulfil larger orders (1) → Sales volume may rise (1) → Total revenue may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Flood barriers protect homes and business premises (1) → Damage and repair costs are reduced (1) → Resources remain available for other uses (1).\n\n2. Fewer floods interrupt production (1) → Firms lose fewer working days (1) → Output and incomes may be more stable (1).\n\n3. Safer infrastructure reduces the risk of investment (1) → Firms may be more willing to expand locally (1) → Investment and demand for labour may rise (1) → Local employment may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Comparing costs reveals cheaper suitable vehicles (1) → The same transport service costs less (1) → Profit may rise (1).\n\n2. Comparing fuel use identifies efficient vehicles (1) → Operating costs per kilometre fall (1) → The firm may offer more competitive prices (1).\n\n3. Comparing expected returns exposes opportunity costs (1) → Funds can go to the better-performing investment (1) → Output generated per unit of investment may rise (1) → Scarce capital is allocated more efficiently (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to undertake advanced training rather than accept a new client project as a self-employed designer. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Advanced training improves design skills (1) → The designer can produce higher-quality work (1) → Future fees may increase (1).\n\n2. New skills broaden the services offered (1) → The designer can appeal to more clients (1) → Future demand may increase (1).\n\n3. Improved techniques shorten time per project (1) → More projects can be completed each month (1) → Revenue per working hour may increase (1) → Future earnings may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers keep existing resources fully employed in current production. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Existing labour and capital are used fully (1) → Output is higher than with idle resources (1) → More consumer wants can be satisfied (1).\n\n2. More workers remain employed (1) → Household wage income is maintained (1) → Consumer spending may be supported (1).\n\n3. Fixed costs are spread over more output (1) → Average fixed cost falls (1) → Average total cost may decrease (1) → Firms may lower prices or increase profit (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reactivate unused productive capacity could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Idle equipment returns to production (1) → Output can rise with existing resources (1) → The economy moves towards its PPC (1).\n\n2. Firms need workers to operate equipment (1) → Demand for labour rises (1) → Unemployment may fall (1).\n\n3. Extra output spreads existing fixed costs (1) → Average fixed cost decreases (1) → Firms may become more price-competitive (1) → Sales may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to expand productive capacity through training and investment. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training improves worker skills (1) → Output per worker may rise (1) → The economy’s productive potential increases (1).\n\n2. New capital expands production capacity (1) → More goods can be produced with existing labour (1) → The PPC may shift outwards (1).\n\n3. Greater capacity makes sustained output growth possible (1) → Firms can meet higher demand without shortages (1) → Pressure on prices may be lower (1) → Real living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More buses can expand public transport services (1) → More commuters can reach workplaces (1) → Geographical mobility of labour may improve (1).\n\n2. Greater bus supply may lower bus prices (1) → Transport operators can expand more affordably (1) → Passenger fares may fall (1).\n\n3. More public transport can replace private car journeys (1) → Road congestion may decrease (1) → Time lost by workers and deliveries may fall (1) → Productivity may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to finance training and productivity-improving equipment. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training teaches workers more efficient methods (1) → Output per worker-hour rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Better equipment produces more per hour (1) → Unit production costs may fall (1) → Firms may become more competitive (1).\n\n3. Workers can use new machinery to make a wider range of goods (1) → The firm can enter previously inaccessible markets (1) → Additional demand can support higher output (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Restored roads reconnect firms with suppliers (1) → Deliveries become more reliable (1) → Production interruptions decrease (1).\n\n2. Rebuilding utilities restores electricity and water (1) → Previously closed businesses can reopen (1) → Employment may recover (1).\n\n3. Reconstruction requires construction workers (1) → Demand for labour increases (1) → Household earnings rise (1) → Spending in other local businesses may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M04-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reallocate productive resources towards wheat could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More resources are used to grow wheat (1) → Wheat supply can increase (1) → Domestic food availability improves (1).\n\n2. Greater wheat supply may lower its market price (1) → Bread production costs may fall (1) → Staple food becomes more affordable (1).\n\n3. Higher domestic output can replace wheat imports (1) → Spending on imported wheat may decrease (1) → Demand for foreign currency may fall (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to prioritise essential water infrastructure. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Safe water reduces waterborne illness (1) → Households spend less on treatment (1) → More income remains for other needs (1).\n\n2. Healthier workers are absent less often (1) → More working hours are available (1) → Output and labour productivity may rise (1).\n\n3. Construction requires workers and materials (1) → Demand for construction labour increases (1) → Previously unemployed workers gain earnings (1) → Their living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More essential services reach poorer households (1) → Basic needs are better satisfied (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Better nutrition and healthcare improve health (1) → Workers lose fewer days to illness (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).\n\n3. Essential services become more affordable (1) → Low-income households retain more disposable income (1) → They can purchase other necessities (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to increase production of renewable-energy equipment could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Renewable equipment replaces fossil-fuel equipment (1) → Less fuel combustion reduces emissions (1) → External environmental costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Expanding equipment production requires workers (1) → Demand for labour increases (1) → Employment and earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Producing more equipment can lower its price (1) → Renewable energy becomes more affordable (1) → Users may reduce spending on imported fuels (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to introduce automated production at a bakery. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Ovens automate repetitive baking tasks (1) → Existing workers can supervise more production (1) → Labour cost per loaf may fall (1).\n\n2. Automated controls reduce baking errors (1) → Fewer loaves and ingredients are wasted (1) → Average variable cost may fall (1).\n\n3. Ovens can operate for longer periods (1) → Daily production capacity increases (1) → The bakery can meet additional demand (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to reserve some new homes for lower-income households could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More homes are available to low-income households (1) → Fewer households lack adequate shelter (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Better housing reduces overcrowding and damp (1) → Residents experience fewer health problems (1) → Their attendance at work or school may improve (1).\n\n3. Access to a home near jobs can improve (1) → Travel costs for residents may fall (1) → A wider range of jobs becomes accessible (1) → Geographical mobility of labour may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Reliable clean water reduces disease (1) → Households need less medical treatment (1) → Healthcare resources are freed for other uses (1).\n\n2. Firms face fewer water interruptions (1) → Production downtime falls (1) → Output per period may increase (1).\n\n3. Expanding the network improves access (1) → Households spend less time collecting water (1) → More time is available for paid work (1) → Household earnings may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Free public access avoids an admission charge (1) → Low-income households can enjoy outdoor recreation (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).\n\n2. Conserving open space protects natural habitats (1) → Environmental degradation is reduced (1) → External benefits accrue to local residents (1).\n\n3. Coastal wind remains available for renewable generation (1) → Producers can use a natural energy resource (1) → Dependence on fossil fuels may fall (1) → Air pollution may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to give priority to science classes in a spare classroom at a school could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More science lessons improve scientific knowledge (1) → Pupils gain skills needed in technical occupations (1) → Occupational mobility may increase (1).\n\n2. Access to science teaching broadens qualifications (1) → More pupils become eligible for skilled jobs (1) → Their future earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Using a spare classroom avoids building another room (1) → More teaching can occur with existing capital (1) → The school uses its resources more fully (1) → Cost per lesson may be lower (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Farmland remains available for cultivation (1) → Domestic food production can be maintained (1) → Dependence on imported food may fall (1).\n\n2. Farming continues to require labour (1) → Rural workers retain employment (1) → Rural household incomes are protected (1).\n\n3. Fertile soil produces relatively high yields (1) → More food can be grown per hectare (1) → Average production costs may be lower (1) → Consumers may benefit from lower food prices (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to fund training for healthcare workers could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training improves healthcare workers’ skills (1) → Treatment becomes more effective (1) → Patients recover and return to work sooner (1).\n\n2. More workers gain recognised qualifications (1) → The supply of skilled healthcare labour rises (1) → Staff shortages may decrease (1).\n\n3. Trained staff can treat more patients effectively (1) → Output per worker-hour increases (1) → Cost per successful treatment may fall (1) → Existing healthcare budgets can serve more people (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to invest in more efficient factory machinery. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. New machinery performs production tasks faster (1) → Workers can produce more per hour (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. More precise machinery reduces defective output (1) → Less raw material is wasted (1) → Average variable cost may decrease (1).\n\n3. Machines expand the factory’s maximum output (1) → More orders can be accepted (1) → Sales volume may increase (1) → Total revenue may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to support new entrepreneurs with business advice may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Advice improves business planning (1) → Entrepreneurs make better allocation decisions (1) → The risk of avoidable business failure falls (1).\n\n2. Advice on finance helps viable firms obtain funds (1) → Firms can buy productive equipment (1) → Their productive capacity may increase (1).\n\n3. More entrepreneurs can enter markets (1) → The number of competing firms increases (1) → Existing firms face pressure to reduce prices (1) → Consumers may gain purchasing power (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Previously unused land is cultivated (1) → More agricultural goods are produced (1) → Total output increases (1).\n\n2. Landowners receive rental income (1) → Their household income rises (1) → Their ability to buy goods and services increases (1).\n\n3. The cooperative needs farm workers (1) → Demand for rural labour increases (1) → Unemployed workers may find work (1) → Rural poverty may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher wages make driving more attractive (1) → More experienced drivers apply or remain (1) → Unfilled vacancies may decrease (1).\n\n2. Higher pay may improve motivation (1) → Absenteeism and staff turnover may fall (1) → Recruitment and training costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. Drivers’ disposable incomes increase (1) → They can afford more goods and services (1) → Material living standards may improve (1) → Poverty among low-paid drivers may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Saving earns a higher return (1) → Households have a stronger incentive to save (1) → Deposits may increase (1).\n\n2. Savers receive more interest income (1) → Their disposable incomes rise (1) → They can afford more consumption in the future (1).\n\n3. Additional deposits provide banks with funds (1) → Banks may lend more to viable firms (1) → Firms can finance capital investment (1) → Productive capacity may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More seats allow more customers to be served (1) → Sales volume can increase (1) → Total revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Expansion requires additional staff (1) → Demand for café labour rises (1) → Employment and wage income may increase (1).\n\n3. Using retained profit avoids a bank loan (1) → No interest is payable on that funding (1) → Financial costs are lower than with borrowing (1) → More future revenue can be retained as profit (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Working the shift provides additional wages (1) → Disposable income increases (1) → More goods and services become affordable (1).\n\n2. Earnings can be saved (1) → Households build a financial buffer (1) → Unexpected expenses become easier to meet (1).\n\n3. Extra work can develop experience (1) → The worker’s skills may improve (1) → Future job opportunities may expand (1) → Future earning potential may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to use household savings to repair a leaking roof could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Repair prevents further water damage (1) → Future repair costs may be avoided (1) → Household resources are conserved (1).\n\n2. A drier home reduces health risks (1) → Medical expenses may fall (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).\n\n3. Paying from savings avoids a new loan (1) → No loan interest needs to be paid (1) → Future disposable income is less committed (1) → Financial pressure on the household may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Specialist training develops scarce skills (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Future wages may be higher (1).\n\n2. New qualifications make more occupations accessible (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Risk of long-term unemployment may fall (1).\n\n3. Training can support entry into a growing industry (1) → The worker faces more job vacancies (1) → Employment prospects may become more secure (1) → Future income may be more stable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to invest in a new packaging line could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Machinery performs packaging tasks more quickly (1) → Existing workers can handle more output (1) → Unit labour cost may fall (1).\n\n2. Consistent packaging reduces damaged products (1) → Fewer products are wasted or returned (1) → Average cost may fall (1).\n\n3. More packaging capacity removes a production bottleneck (1) → The firm can fulfil larger orders (1) → Sales volume may rise (1) → Total revenue may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Flood barriers protect homes and business premises (1) → Damage and repair costs are reduced (1) → Resources remain available for other uses (1).\n\n2. Fewer floods interrupt production (1) → Firms lose fewer working days (1) → Output and incomes may be more stable (1).\n\n3. Safer infrastructure reduces the risk of investment (1) → Firms may be more willing to expand locally (1) → Investment and demand for labour may rise (1) → Local employment may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Comparing costs reveals cheaper suitable vehicles (1) → The same transport service costs less (1) → Profit may rise (1).\n\n2. Comparing fuel use identifies efficient vehicles (1) → Operating costs per kilometre fall (1) → The firm may offer more competitive prices (1).\n\n3. Comparing expected returns exposes opportunity costs (1) → Funds can go to the better-performing investment (1) → Output generated per unit of investment may rise (1) → Scarce capital is allocated more efficiently (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to undertake advanced training rather than accept a new client project as a self-employed designer could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Advanced training improves design skills (1) → The designer can produce higher-quality work (1) → Future fees may increase (1).\n\n2. New skills broaden the services offered (1) → The designer can appeal to more clients (1) → Future demand may increase (1).\n\n3. Improved techniques shorten time per project (1) → More projects can be completed each month (1) → Revenue per working hour may increase (1) → Future earnings may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to keep existing resources fully employed in current production. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Existing labour and capital are used fully (1) → Output is higher than with idle resources (1) → More consumer wants can be satisfied (1).\n\n2. More workers remain employed (1) → Household wage income is maintained (1) → Consumer spending may be supported (1).\n\n3. Fixed costs are spread over more output (1) → Average fixed cost falls (1) → Average total cost may decrease (1) → Firms may lower prices or increase profit (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to reactivate unused productive capacity could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Idle equipment returns to production (1) → Output can rise with existing resources (1) → The economy moves towards its PPC (1).\n\n2. Firms need workers to operate equipment (1) → Demand for labour rises (1) → Unemployment may fall (1).\n\n3. Extra output spreads existing fixed costs (1) → Average fixed cost decreases (1) → Firms may become more price-competitive (1) → Sales may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to expand productive capacity through training and investment. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training improves worker skills (1) → Output per worker may rise (1) → The economy’s productive potential increases (1).\n\n2. New capital expands production capacity (1) → More goods can be produced with existing labour (1) → The PPC may shift outwards (1).\n\n3. Greater capacity makes sustained output growth possible (1) → Firms can meet higher demand without shortages (1) → Pressure on prices may be lower (1) → Real living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More buses can expand public transport services (1) → More commuters can reach workplaces (1) → Geographical mobility of labour may improve (1).\n\n2. Greater bus supply may lower bus prices (1) → Transport operators can expand more affordably (1) → Passenger fares may fall (1).\n\n3. More public transport can replace private car journeys (1) → Road congestion may decrease (1) → Time lost by workers and deliveries may fall (1) → Productivity may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to finance training and productivity-improving equipment could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training teaches workers more efficient methods (1) → Output per worker-hour rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Better equipment produces more per hour (1) → Unit production costs may fall (1) → Firms may become more competitive (1).\n\n3. Workers can use new machinery to make a wider range of goods (1) → The firm can enter previously inaccessible markets (1) → Additional demand can support higher output (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Restored roads reconnect firms with suppliers (1) → Deliveries become more reliable (1) → Production interruptions decrease (1).\n\n2. Rebuilding utilities restores electricity and water (1) → Previously closed businesses can reopen (1) → Employment may recover (1).\n\n3. Reconstruction requires construction workers (1) → Demand for labour increases (1) → Household earnings rise (1) → Spending in other local businesses may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M06-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to reallocate productive resources towards wheat could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More resources are used to grow wheat (1) → Wheat supply can increase (1) → Domestic food availability improves (1).\n\n2. Greater wheat supply may lower its market price (1) → Bread production costs may fall (1) → Staple food becomes more affordable (1).\n\n3. Higher domestic output can replace wheat imports (1) → Spending on imported wheat may decrease (1) → Demand for foreign currency may fall (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0001","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to prioritise essential water infrastructure would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Safe water reduces waterborne illness → Households spend less on treatment → More income remains for other needs.\n\n2. Healthier workers are absent less often → More working hours are available → Output and labour productivity may rise.\n\n3. Construction requires workers and materials → Demand for construction labour increases → Previously unemployed workers gain earnings → Their living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. It could postpone leisure facilities valued by local residents → Fewer recreation opportunities may limit non-material living standards.\n\n2. Its benefits may be delayed if construction is costly or poorly managed → Delays postpone improvements in health and productivity.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0002","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to allocate a larger budget to basic needs rather than luxury projects. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More essential services reach poorer households → Basic needs are better satisfied → Absolute poverty may fall.\n\n2. Better nutrition and healthcare improve health → Workers lose fewer days to illness → Productive capacity may increase.\n\n3. Essential services become more affordable → Low-income households retain more disposable income → They can purchase other necessities → Material living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some households may prefer other goods and services → Consumer preferences may be satisfied less well.\n\n2. Limited tax revenue may prevent all basic needs from being met → A limited budget restricts the number of households helped.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0003","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to increase production of renewable-energy equipment. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Renewable equipment replaces fossil-fuel equipment → Less fuel combustion reduces emissions → External environmental costs may fall.\n\n2. Expanding equipment production requires workers → Demand for labour increases → Employment and earnings may rise.\n\n3. Producing more equipment can lower its price → Renewable energy becomes more affordable → Users may reduce spending on imported fuels → The trade in goods balance may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Resources diverted from other manufacturing have an opportunity cost → Alternative goods become less available.\n\n2. Demand for renewable-energy equipment may be too weak to justify the switch → Unsold output may waste scarce resources.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0004","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to introduce automated production at a bakery is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Ovens automate repetitive baking tasks → Existing workers can supervise more production → Labour cost per loaf may fall.\n\n2. Automated controls reduce baking errors → Fewer loaves and ingredients are wasted → Average variable cost may fall.\n\n3. Ovens can operate for longer periods → Daily production capacity increases → The bakery can meet additional demand → Sales revenue may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. High machinery costs may strain cash flow → Less finance remains for other business needs.\n\n2. Automation may reduce demand for some workers → Displaced workers may lose their wage incomes.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0005","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reserve some new homes for lower-income households would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More homes are available to low-income households → Fewer households lack adequate shelter → Absolute poverty may fall.\n\n2. Better housing reduces overcrowding and damp → Residents experience fewer health problems → Their attendance at work or school may improve.\n\n3. Access to a home near jobs can improve → Travel costs for residents may fall → A wider range of jobs becomes accessible → Geographical mobility of labour may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The developer may receive less revenue per home → Lower returns may discourage future house building.\n\n2. Subsidies may be needed to make the policy financially viable → Public funds are diverted from other services.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0006","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to invest public funds in a reliable water-supply network would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Reliable clean water reduces disease → Households need less medical treatment → Healthcare resources are freed for other uses.\n\n2. Firms face fewer water interruptions → Production downtime falls → Output per period may increase.\n\n3. Expanding the network improves access → Households spend less time collecting water → More time is available for paid work → Household earnings may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Infrastructure requires scarce funds and maintenance → Other public projects may have to be delayed.\n\n2. Water pricing may exclude low-income households without support → Some households may remain unable to afford safe water.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0007","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to protect public access to naturally available coastal wind and open space would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Free public access avoids an admission charge → Low-income households can enjoy outdoor recreation → Non-material living standards may improve.\n\n2. Conserving open space protects natural habitats → Environmental degradation is reduced → External benefits accrue to local residents.\n\n3. Coastal wind remains available for renewable generation → Producers can use a natural energy resource → Dependence on fossil fuels may fall → Air pollution may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Protecting access may require enforcement and public funding → Enforcement uses funds that could finance other services.\n\n2. Poor environmental management could reduce the quality of the open space → Environmental benefits may fail to materialise.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0008","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.1","topicName":"The nature of the basic economic problem","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to give priority to science classes in a spare classroom at a school. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More science lessons improve scientific knowledge → Pupils gain skills needed in technical occupations → Occupational mobility may increase.\n\n2. Access to science teaching broadens qualifications → More pupils become eligible for skilled jobs → Their future earnings may rise.\n\n3. Using a spare classroom avoids building another room → More teaching can occur with existing capital → The school uses its resources more fully → Cost per lesson may be lower.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Giving science priority may displace other valuable lessons → A narrower subject mix may reduce pupils’ range of future job options.\n\n2. Expected gains depend on the demand for scientific skills compared with alternative subjects → Investment may not match pupils’ future skills needs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0009","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to protect fertile farmland from conversion to buildings. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Farmland remains available for cultivation → Domestic food production can be maintained → Dependence on imported food may fall.\n\n2. Farming continues to require labour → Rural workers retain employment → Rural household incomes are protected.\n\n3. Fertile soil produces relatively high yields → More food can be grown per hectare → Average production costs may be lower → Consumers may benefit from lower food prices.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. It can restrict housing supply near growing cities → Fewer homes may increase urban housing prices.\n\n2. Farm productivity may remain low without investment → Farming incomes may remain low.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0010","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to fund training for healthcare workers is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training improves healthcare workers’ skills → Treatment becomes more effective → Patients recover and return to work sooner.\n\n2. More workers gain recognised qualifications → The supply of skilled healthcare labour rises → Staff shortages may decrease.\n\n3. Trained staff can treat more patients effectively → Output per worker-hour increases → Cost per successful treatment may fall → Existing healthcare budgets can serve more people.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training costs reduce funds available for current services → Patients may receive less immediate treatment.\n\n2. Trained workers may move to higher-paying regions → The domestic economy loses the skills it funded.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0011","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to invest in more efficient factory machinery would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. New machinery performs production tasks faster → Workers can produce more per hour → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. More precise machinery reduces defective output → Less raw material is wasted → Average variable cost may decrease.\n\n3. Machines expand the factory’s maximum output → More orders can be accepted → Sales volume may increase → Total revenue may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Machinery purchases require significant finance → Funds cannot be spent on other productive inputs.\n\n2. Equipment may become obsolete before costs are recovered → The firm may fail to recover its investment.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0012","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to support new entrepreneurs with business advice would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Advice improves business planning → Entrepreneurs make better allocation decisions → The risk of avoidable business failure falls.\n\n2. Advice on finance helps viable firms obtain funds → Firms can buy productive equipment → Their productive capacity may increase.\n\n3. More entrepreneurs can enter markets → The number of competing firms increases → Existing firms face pressure to reduce prices → Consumers may gain purchasing power.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some new enterprises will fail and waste invested resources → Public or private funds may be lost.\n\n2. Advice alone may not solve a shortage of finance → Viable firms may remain unable to buy equipment.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0013","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to lease unused land to a small farming cooperative would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Previously unused land is cultivated → More agricultural goods are produced → Total output increases.\n\n2. Landowners receive rental income → Their household income rises → Their ability to buy goods and services increases.\n\n3. The cooperative needs farm workers → Demand for rural labour increases → Unemployed workers may find work → Rural poverty may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Rent payments increase farmers' production costs → Higher costs may raise food prices.\n\n2. Poor soil quality may limit the value of leasing the land → Output gains may be too small to cover rent.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0014","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to raise hourly pay for experienced bus drivers. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher wages make driving more attractive → More experienced drivers apply or remain → Unfilled vacancies may decrease.\n\n2. Higher pay may improve motivation → Absenteeism and staff turnover may fall → Recruitment and training costs may decrease.\n\n3. Drivers’ disposable incomes increase → They can afford more goods and services → Material living standards may improve → Poverty among low-paid drivers may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher wage bills may lead to higher fares → Passengers’ purchasing power may fall.\n\n2. Wages alone may not solve inconvenient working hours → Driver vacancies may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0015","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to offer a higher interest rate on savings accounts. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Saving earns a higher return → Households have a stronger incentive to save → Deposits may increase.\n\n2. Savers receive more interest income → Their disposable incomes rise → They can afford more consumption in the future.\n\n3. Additional deposits provide banks with funds → Banks may lend more to viable firms → Firms can finance capital investment → Productive capacity may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Banks may face a higher cost of funding → Higher costs may reduce lending or bank profit.\n\n2. Higher saving may reduce household consumption in the short run → Firms may receive fewer current sales.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0016","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.2","topicName":"Factors of production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to reinvest a café's profit in expanding seating capacity is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More seats allow more customers to be served → Sales volume can increase → Total revenue may rise.\n\n2. Expansion requires additional staff → Demand for café labour rises → Employment and wage income may increase.\n\n3. Using retained profit avoids a bank loan → No interest is payable on that funding → Financial costs are lower than with borrowing → More future revenue can be retained as profit.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Expansion can raise fixed costs before demand grows → Average costs may rise if seats remain empty.\n\n2. Competition may limit the café's future profit → Extra sales may not cover expansion costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0017","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to choose a paid shift instead of a leisure activity would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Working the shift provides additional wages → Disposable income increases → More goods and services become affordable.\n\n2. Earnings can be saved → Households build a financial buffer → Unexpected expenses become easier to meet.\n\n3. Extra work can develop experience → The worker’s skills may improve → Future job opportunities may expand → Future earning potential may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The worker gives up leisure and enjoyment → Non-material living standards may deteriorate.\n\n2. Working longer hours may cause fatigue and lower performance → Lower productivity may offset some extra earnings.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0018","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to use household savings to repair a leaking roof would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Repair prevents further water damage → Future repair costs may be avoided → Household resources are conserved.\n\n2. A drier home reduces health risks → Medical expenses may fall → Non-material living standards may improve.\n\n3. Paying from savings avoids a new loan → No loan interest needs to be paid → Future disposable income is less committed → Financial pressure on the household may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Money used on repairs cannot finance the next best alternative purchase → Other household wants remain unsatisfied.\n\n2. Unexpected repair costs may exhaust emergency savings → Later emergencies may require costly borrowing.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0019","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to take specialist training instead of a higher-paid current job would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Specialist training develops scarce skills → Labour productivity may increase → Future wages may be higher.\n\n2. New qualifications make more occupations accessible → Occupational mobility increases → Risk of long-term unemployment may fall.\n\n3. Training can support entry into a growing industry → The worker faces more job vacancies → Employment prospects may become more secure → Future income may be more stable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Current income will be lower during training → Fewer goods and services are affordable now.\n\n2. Future wages are uncertain and may not justify the sacrifice → The worker may not recover lost earnings.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0020","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to invest in a new packaging line. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Machinery performs packaging tasks more quickly → Existing workers can handle more output → Unit labour cost may fall.\n\n2. Consistent packaging reduces damaged products → Fewer products are wasted or returned → Average cost may fall.\n\n3. More packaging capacity removes a production bottleneck → The firm can fulfil larger orders → Sales volume may rise → Total revenue may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Buying machinery postpones the next best alternative investment → Other improvements to the business may be delayed.\n\n2. Machine performance might be worse than expected → Output and cost savings may disappoint.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0021","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to fund flood protection ahead of leisure facilities. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Flood barriers protect homes and business premises → Damage and repair costs are reduced → Resources remain available for other uses.\n\n2. Fewer floods interrupt production → Firms lose fewer working days → Output and incomes may be more stable.\n\n3. Safer infrastructure reduces the risk of investment → Firms may be more willing to expand locally → Investment and demand for labour may rise → Local employment may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Other public services or leisure facilities may have to be delayed → Funds are unavailable for other useful services.\n\n2. Protection may be inadequate if flood conditions worsen → Flood damage and disruption may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0022","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to compare alternative investments carefully before buying vehicles is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Comparing costs reveals cheaper suitable vehicles → The same transport service costs less → Profit may rise.\n\n2. Comparing fuel use identifies efficient vehicles → Operating costs per kilometre fall → The firm may offer more competitive prices.\n\n3. Comparing expected returns exposes opportunity costs → Funds can go to the better-performing investment → Output generated per unit of investment may rise → Scarce capital is allocated more efficiently.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Delaying vehicle purchases may cause missed deliveries → Customers may switch to other suppliers.\n\n2. Forecast benefits of each alternative may turn out to be inaccurate → The chosen project may have a low actual return.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0023","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.3","topicName":"Opportunity cost","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to undertake advanced training rather than accept a new client project as a self-employed designer would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Advanced training improves design skills → The designer can produce higher-quality work → Future fees may increase.\n\n2. New skills broaden the services offered → The designer can appeal to more clients → Future demand may increase.\n\n3. Improved techniques shorten time per project → More projects can be completed each month → Revenue per working hour may increase → Future earnings may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The immediate fee from the client project is forgone → Current disposable income is lower.\n\n2. The course may not improve marketable skills → Future earnings may not rise enough to compensate.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0024","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to keep existing resources fully employed in current production would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Existing labour and capital are used fully → Output is higher than with idle resources → More consumer wants can be satisfied.\n\n2. More workers remain employed → Household wage income is maintained → Consumer spending may be supported.\n\n3. Fixed costs are spread over more output → Average fixed cost falls → Average total cost may decrease → Firms may lower prices or increase profit.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The output mix may not match society's priorities → Unwanted goods may coexist with unsatisfied needs.\n\n2. Operating at full capacity can leave little spare capacity for emergencies → New demand may cause shortages or price rises.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0025","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to reactivate unused productive capacity would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Idle equipment returns to production → Output can rise with existing resources → The economy moves towards its PPC.\n\n2. Firms need workers to operate equipment → Demand for labour rises → Unemployment may fall.\n\n3. Extra output spreads existing fixed costs → Average fixed cost decreases → Firms may become more price-competitive → Sales may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Restarting factories may require extra investment → Other capital projects may receive less finance.\n\n2. Unused capacity may lack the skills demanded by buyers → Output may remain difficult to sell.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0026","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to expand productive capacity through training and investment. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training improves worker skills → Output per worker may rise → The economy’s productive potential increases.\n\n2. New capital expands production capacity → More goods can be produced with existing labour → The PPC may shift outwards.\n\n3. Greater capacity makes sustained output growth possible → Firms can meet higher demand without shortages → Pressure on prices may be lower → Real living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training and investment consume resources today → Current consumption may have to fall.\n\n2. Projects may take years before capacity rises → Short-run living standards may not improve.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0027","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to shift some factories from refrigerators to buses. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More buses can expand public transport services → More commuters can reach workplaces → Geographical mobility of labour may improve.\n\n2. Greater bus supply may lower bus prices → Transport operators can expand more affordably → Passenger fares may fall.\n\n3. More public transport can replace private car journeys → Road congestion may decrease → Time lost by workers and deliveries may fall → Productivity may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Consumers may face fewer refrigerators → Refrigerator prices may rise as their supply falls.\n\n2. Factories may not be easily adapted to bus production → Conversion costs may exceed extra bus revenue.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0028","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to finance training and productivity-improving equipment is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training teaches workers more efficient methods → Output per worker-hour rises → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. Better equipment produces more per hour → Unit production costs may fall → Firms may become more competitive.\n\n3. Workers can use new machinery to make a wider range of goods → The firm can enter previously inaccessible markets → Additional demand can support higher output → Sales revenue may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Investment diverts funds from current consumption → Households may have fewer goods available today.\n\n2. Benefits depend on the quality of training and equipment → Poor equipment or unsuitable courses may waste funds.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0029","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to rebuild essential infrastructure after destructive flooding would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Restored roads reconnect firms with suppliers → Deliveries become more reliable → Production interruptions decrease.\n\n2. Rebuilding utilities restores electricity and water → Previously closed businesses can reopen → Employment may recover.\n\n3. Reconstruction requires construction workers → Demand for labour increases → Household earnings rise → Spending in other local businesses may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Reconstruction uses scarce funds that could serve other needs → Spending on other services may be postponed.\n\n2. Further floods could damage repaired infrastructure → Repeated repairs may absorb scarce public funds.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C01-P2-M08-0030","c":0,"chapter":"The Basic Economic Problem","topic":"1.4","topicName":"Production possibility curve (PPC) diagrams","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to reallocate productive resources towards wheat would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More resources are used to grow wheat → Wheat supply can increase → Domestic food availability improves.\n\n2. Greater wheat supply may lower its market price → Bread production costs may fall → Staple food becomes more affordable.\n\n3. Higher domestic output can replace wheat imports → Spending on imported wheat may decrease → Demand for foreign currency may fall → The trade in goods balance may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Producing more wheat sacrifices output of the next best alternative product → Consumption or sale of alternative goods is sacrificed.\n\n2. A shortage of suitable farmland may raise the sacrifice required → Increasing wheat output may require higher unit costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price.","Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Market: A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sellers in a market","Inelastic demand","Market","Market equilibrium"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates market: A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of market?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market."],"answer":3,"why":"Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. This illustrates a market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 210 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 30% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["0.333","20","3","4"],"answer":2,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to create a digital trading platform for local growers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More people may purchase bicycles.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","River pollution may fall.","Some growers may not have reliable internet access."],"answer":3,"why":"Some growers may not have reliable internet access."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to create a digital trading platform for local growers?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Buyers could compare products and prices more easily.","Operating and maintenance costs may be high.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."],"answer":0,"why":"Buyers could compare products and prices more easily."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to create a digital trading platform for local growers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Tax revenue could fund waste management.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread."],"answer":1,"why":"Platform fees might increase sellers' costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to create a digital trading platform for local growers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":2,"why":"Growers could reach more potential customers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."],"answer":3,"why":"The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes buyers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products.","A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."],"answer":2,"why":"Buyers in a market: Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shortage","Negative external costs","Price elasticity of supply","Buyers in a market"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates buyers in a market: Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 110 units at $5 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$110","$115","$555","$550"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $5 × 110 = $550."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to buyers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."],"answer":2,"why":"Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use customer demand information to plan bread output. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.","Cycle routes use public funds and road space.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock."],"answer":3,"why":"The bakery may reduce unsold stock."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use customer demand information to plan bread output. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand could change before new production is ready.","More customers may buy tickets.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise.","River pollution may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand could change before new production is ready."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use customer demand information to plan bread output?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Output may better match what customers want.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data."],"answer":0,"why":"Output may better match what customers want."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use customer demand information to plan bread output less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Tax revenue could fund waste management.","Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity."],"answer":2,"why":"Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use customer demand information to plan bread output. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock."],"answer":3,"why":"The bakery may reduce unsold stock."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales."],"answer":1,"why":"Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($6, 99, 59); ($8, 89, 69); ($10, 79, 79); ($12, 69, 89). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$8","$10","$6","$12"],"answer":1,"why":"At $10, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 79 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand","Mixed economic system","Price elasticity of demand","Sellers in a market"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates sellers in a market: Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of sellers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided.","A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning.","The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months."],"answer":2,"why":"Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning. This illustrates sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to sellers in a market?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."],"answer":3,"why":"Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Revenue per pair of shoes will decline."],"answer":2,"why":"Competition may increase the quantity offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change.","Farmers may face lower prices and profits.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use."],"answer":2,"why":"Farmers may face lower prices and profits."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up.","Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period."],"answer":1,"why":"Buyers may benefit from greater choice."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Entry may remain difficult if transport costs are high.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Less unsold food may be wasted."],"answer":0,"why":"Entry may remain difficult if transport costs are high."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","More people may purchase bicycles."],"answer":2,"why":"Competition may increase the quantity offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 160 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 30%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["7","6","25","0.167"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0031","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services.","Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand: Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0032","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand","Mixed economic system","Increase in demand and price","Price elasticity of supply"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates demand: Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0033","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price."],"answer":2,"why":"A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. This illustrates demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0034","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0035","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services."],"answer":0,"why":"Ordering decisions may match expected sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0036","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Survey answers may differ from actual purchases.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Lower prices could attract more buyers.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."],"answer":0,"why":"Survey answers may differ from actual purchases."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0037","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to survey local demand before ordering bicycles?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Some buyers lack information about alternative products."],"answer":0,"why":"The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0038","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 250 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 20% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["3","10","0.5","2"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 2."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0039","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Orders may be met without a long delay.","Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."],"answer":1,"why":"Ordering decisions may match expected sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0040","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0041","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes movement along demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."],"answer":3,"why":"Movement along demand: A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0042","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Movement along demand","Negative external costs","Determinants of PES","Public goods"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates movement along demand: A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0043","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of movement along demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110.","Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price.","A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers."],"answer":1,"why":"Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. This illustrates a change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0044","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to movement along demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."],"answer":1,"why":"A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0045","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs.","More customers may buy tickets.","The subsidy has a cost to taxpayers.","Cold storage has high energy and capital costs."],"answer":1,"why":"More customers may buy tickets."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0046","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 170 units at $9 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$179","$1,539","$170","$1,530"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $9 × 170 = $1,530."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0047","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.","Savings depend on electricity use and machine reliability."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher attendance could increase sales of refreshments."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0048","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic.","Higher productive efficiency may increase output.","Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages."],"answer":1,"why":"The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0049","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More customers may buy tickets.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Growers could reach more potential customers."],"answer":0,"why":"More customers may buy tickets."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0050","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."],"answer":0,"why":"A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0051","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes shift in demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good.","Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale."],"answer":0,"why":"Shift in demand: A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0052","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Surplus","Negative external costs","Shift in demand","Inelastic supply"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates shift in demand: A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0053","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of shift in demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. This illustrates a non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0054","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to shift in demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0055","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($10, 111, 71); ($12, 101, 81); ($14, 91, 91); ($16, 81, 101). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$10","$14","$12","$16"],"answer":1,"why":"At $14, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 91 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0056","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cycle routes use public funds and road space.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","River pollution may fall.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":0,"why":"Cycle routes use public funds and road space."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0057","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."],"answer":3,"why":"Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0058","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages.","Greater output may make fruit more accessible.","Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling."],"answer":3,"why":"Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0059","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More people may purchase bicycles.","River pollution may fall.","More people may receive protection from disease.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices."],"answer":0,"why":"More people may purchase bicycles."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0060","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."],"answer":3,"why":"Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0061","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.","Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."],"answer":3,"why":"Supply: Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0062","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Supply","Increase in demand and price","Demand","Inelastic supply"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates supply: Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0063","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 200 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 25%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["6","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (25%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0064","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0065","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Some growers may not have reliable internet access.","The subsidy has a cost to taxpayers.","Survey answers may differ from actual purchases."],"answer":0,"why":"The farm may identify profitable production levels."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0066","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage additional supply.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","Weather may make planned supply impossible.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":2,"why":"Weather may make planned supply impossible."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0067","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to plan output using a detailed supply schedule?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Unexpected income changes could alter demand.","Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices.","Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up."],"answer":2,"why":"Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0068","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to plan output using a detailed supply schedule less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fishing livelihoods and health may improve.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","Input prices may change after plans are made.","Less unsold food may be wasted."],"answer":2,"why":"Input prices may change after plans are made."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0069","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to plan output using a detailed supply schedule. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More people may purchase bicycles.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."],"answer":2,"why":"The farm may identify profitable production levels."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0070","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0071","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 290 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 60% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["6","7","50","0.167"],"answer":0,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (60%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0072","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shift in supply","Market economy limitations","Movement along supply","Subsidy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates movement along supply: A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0073","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of movement along supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built."],"answer":0,"why":"A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises. This illustrates a change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0074","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to movement along supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available."],"answer":1,"why":"A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0075","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","State services require sufficient tax revenue.","Spare capacity is costly to maintain."],"answer":0,"why":"Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0076","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0077","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More seasonal jobs may be offered.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."],"answer":0,"why":"More seasonal jobs may be offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0078","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.","Buyers could compare products and prices more easily."],"answer":0,"why":"Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0079","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."],"answer":0,"why":"Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0080","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 125 units at $4 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$125","$129","$504","$500"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $4 × 125 = $500."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0081","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes shift in supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.","Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale.","An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."],"answer":1,"why":"Shift in supply: Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0082","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Increase in demand and price","Market economic system","Mixed economic system","Shift in supply"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates shift in supply: Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0083","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of shift in supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%."],"answer":1,"why":"A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. This illustrates non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0084","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to shift in supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0085","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower costs may support lower bread prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0086","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","New ovens require investment.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":1,"why":"New ovens require investment."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0087","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher productive efficiency may increase output.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher productive efficiency may increase output."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0088","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($14, 123, 83); ($16, 113, 93); ($18, 103, 103); ($20, 93, 113). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$18","$14","$20","$16"],"answer":0,"why":"At $18, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 103 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0089","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower costs may support lower bread prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0090","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0091","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market equilibrium?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.","Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit.","Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."],"answer":3,"why":"Market equilibrium: Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0092","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Inelastic demand","Increase in supply and price","Market equilibrium","Price elasticity of supply"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates market equilibrium: Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0093","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of market equilibrium?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding."],"answer":0,"why":"At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. This illustrates equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0094","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market equilibrium?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."],"answer":1,"why":"When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0095","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unsold stock could be reduced.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","Idle machines still require maintenance and space.","Demand could change before new production is ready."],"answer":0,"why":"Unsold stock could be reduced."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0096","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 240 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 20%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["15","4","0.25","5"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 4."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0097","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."],"answer":2,"why":"Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0098","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.","The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock."],"answer":0,"why":"The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0099","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Unsold stock could be reduced.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":2,"why":"Unsold stock could be reduced."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0100","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."],"answer":2,"why":"When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0101","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged.","A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."],"answer":3,"why":"Shortage: A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0102","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Mixed economic system","Sellers in a market","Inelastic demand","Shortage"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates shortage: A shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0103","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A tomato producer lists quantities offered for sale at a series of prices.","Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises."],"answer":2,"why":"At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. This illustrates a shortage occurs when quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0104","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":1,"why":"Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0105","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 330 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 50% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["40","0.2","6","5"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (50%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0106","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Low-income households may find bread less affordable.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Orders may be met without a long delay."],"answer":0,"why":"Low-income households may find bread less affordable."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0107","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage.","Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators."],"answer":1,"why":"Some consumers may reduce demand, easing the shortage."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0108","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","More seasonal jobs may be offered.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage."],"answer":3,"why":"Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0109","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Growers could reach more potential customers.","Higher prices may encourage additional supply.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher prices may encourage additional supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0110","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."],"answer":3,"why":"Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0111","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price.","Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products."],"answer":2,"why":"Surplus: A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0112","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Inelastic supply","Surplus","Price elasticity of demand","Sellers in a market"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates surplus: A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0113","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 80 units at $8 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$648","$640","$80","$88"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $8 × 80 = $640."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0114","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":2,"why":"Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0115","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Spare capacity is costly to maintain.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","Lower prices could attract more buyers.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower prices could attract more buyers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0116","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The bakery may reduce unsold stock.","More people may receive protection from disease.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Revenue per pair of shoes will decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Revenue per pair of shoes will decline."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0117","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to discount shoes when inventories are excessive?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic."],"answer":0,"why":"Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0118","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to discount shoes when inventories are excessive less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Fishing livelihoods and health may improve.","More seasonal jobs may be offered.","Extra revenue could fund service improvements."],"answer":0,"why":"The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0119","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to discount shoes when inventories are excessive. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower prices could attract more buyers.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower prices could attract more buyers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0120","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0121","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($18, 135, 95); ($20, 125, 105); ($22, 115, 115); ($24, 105, 125). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$22","$18","$20","$24"],"answer":0,"why":"At $22, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 115 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0122","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Negative external costs","Increase in demand and price","Market","Shift in supply"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates increase in demand and price: An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0123","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of increase in demand and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding."],"answer":0,"why":"A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms. This illustrates an increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0124","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to increase in demand and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price."],"answer":3,"why":"The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0125","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Revenue per pair of shoes will decline.","Demand could change before new production is ready.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.","Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0126","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More customers may buy tickets.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.","Lower prices could attract more buyers."],"answer":2,"why":"Visitors may face higher accommodation costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0127","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some hotels can fund future capacity investment.","Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."],"answer":0,"why":"Some hotels can fund future capacity investment."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0128","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","Output may better match what customers want."],"answer":0,"why":"Supply may be fixed in the very short run."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0129","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0130","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 280 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 15%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["10","0.333","4","3"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (15%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0131","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes increase in supply and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Increase in supply and price: An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0132","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods","Increase in supply and price","Inelastic demand","Mixed economic system"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates increase in supply and price: An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0133","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of increase in supply and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market.","Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals."],"answer":2,"why":"A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. This illustrates an increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0134","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to increase in supply and price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":0,"why":"The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0135","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support more efficient apple cultivation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","Survey answers may differ from actual purchases.","Idle machines still require maintenance and space."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may pay less for apples."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0136","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support more efficient apple cultivation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Farmers may receive a lower price per kilogram.","Orders may be met without a long delay."],"answer":2,"why":"Farmers may receive a lower price per kilogram."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0137","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support more efficient apple cultivation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Greater output may make fruit more accessible.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data."],"answer":2,"why":"Greater output may make fruit more accessible."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0138","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 370 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 40% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["30","4","5","0.25"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (40%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 4."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0139","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support more efficient apple cultivation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumers may pay less for apples.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","More customers may buy tickets.","The farm may identify profitable production levels."],"answer":0,"why":"Consumers may pay less for apples."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0140","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods.","The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."],"answer":1,"why":"The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0141","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes simultaneous shifts?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.","When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."],"answer":1,"why":"Simultaneous shifts: When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0142","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Movement along supply","Sellers in a market","Merit goods","Simultaneous shifts"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates simultaneous shifts: When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0143","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of simultaneous shifts?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. This illustrates when demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0144","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to simultaneous shifts?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","Sellers' responses to prices help allocate resources across products."],"answer":0,"why":"Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0145","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand could change before new production is ready.","New ovens require investment.","Cold storage has high energy and capital costs.","Higher sales volumes may be served."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher sales volumes may be served."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0146","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 140 units at $3 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$420","$143","$423","$140"],"answer":0,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $3 × 140 = $420."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0147","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","Pollution and other external costs may be ignored."],"answer":2,"why":"Extra competition may limit price increases."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0148","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","Extra revenue could fund service improvements.","Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households.","The direction of price change is uncertain without shift magnitudes."],"answer":3,"why":"The direction of price change is uncertain without shift magnitudes."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0149","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher sales volumes may be served.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher sales volumes may be served."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0150","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods."],"answer":1,"why":"Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0151","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes price elasticity of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.","Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit."],"answer":1,"why":"Price elasticity of demand: Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0152","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Price elasticity of demand","Increase in supply and price","Determinants of PES","Inelastic demand"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates price elasticity of demand: Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0153","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of price elasticity of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A bakery notices customers buying more wholegrain bread than white bread.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%."],"answer":3,"why":"A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. This illustrates price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0154","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to price elasticity of demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices."],"answer":2,"why":"The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0155","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($22, 147, 107); ($24, 137, 117); ($26, 127, 127); ($28, 117, 137). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$22","$26","$24","$28"],"answer":1,"why":"At $26, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 127 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0156","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0157","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.","Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators."],"answer":2,"why":"Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0158","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.","Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households."],"answer":2,"why":"PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0159","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growers could reach more potential customers.","Unsold stock could be reduced.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":3,"why":"Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0160","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand."],"answer":3,"why":"The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0161","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inelastic demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change.","An increase in supply tends to reduce equilibrium price and raise quantity sold if demand is unchanged.","A change in a good's own price causes movement along its demand curve, other factors unchanged."],"answer":1,"why":"Inelastic demand: Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0162","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Market","Inelastic demand","Merit goods","Simultaneous shifts"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates inelastic demand: Demand is price inelastic when the percentage change in quantity demanded is smaller than the percentage price change."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0163","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 320 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 10%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["2","5","3","0.5"],"answer":0,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (10%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 2."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0164","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inelastic demand?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."],"answer":3,"why":"The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0165","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Revenue from each ticket will be lower.","Demand could change before new production is ready.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0166","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0167","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.","Extra revenue could fund service improvements.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."],"answer":2,"why":"Extra revenue could fund service improvements."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0168","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Buyers may benefit from greater choice.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Output may better match what customers want.","Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0169","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","More customers may buy tickets.","The farm may identify profitable production levels."],"answer":0,"why":"Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0170","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."],"answer":3,"why":"The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0171","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 410 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 30% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["20","3","0.333","4"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0172","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shift in supply","Movement along supply","Mixed economic system","Determinants of PED"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates determinants of ped: Availability of substitutes, necessity and time influence the price elasticity of demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0173","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of determinants of ped?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280."],"answer":2,"why":"A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service. This illustrates availability of substitutes, necessity and time influence the price elasticity of demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0174","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to determinants of ped?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Changes in purchases signal which goods consumers value at prevailing prices.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price."],"answer":0,"why":"Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0175","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider substitute availability when pricing products. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Visitors may face higher accommodation costs.","Demand may change with the weather.","Survey answers may differ from actual purchases."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0176","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider substitute availability when pricing products. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage additional supply.","The farm may identify profitable production levels.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Competitors can introduce new substitutes unexpectedly."],"answer":3,"why":"Competitors can introduce new substitutes unexpectedly."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0177","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to consider substitute availability when pricing products?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Demand may be too inelastic for usage to fall significantly.","Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."],"answer":2,"why":"Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0178","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to consider substitute availability when pricing products less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some buyers lack information about alternative products.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.","Higher productive efficiency may increase output."],"answer":0,"why":"Some buyers lack information about alternative products."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0179","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to consider substitute availability when pricing products. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0180","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 95 units at $7 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$102","$672","$665","$95"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $7 × 95 = $665."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0181","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes price elasticity of supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale.","Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply.","Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."],"answer":3,"why":"Price elasticity of supply: Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0182","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Simultaneous shifts","Subsidy","Price elasticity of supply","Determinants of PED"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates price elasticity of supply: Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0183","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of price elasticity of supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand.","Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price."],"answer":2,"why":"A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. This illustrates price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0184","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to price elasticity of supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance."],"answer":2,"why":"The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0185","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Revenue per pair of shoes will decline.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","New ovens require investment.","Demand may change with the weather."],"answer":1,"why":"The firm may expand output when prices rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0186","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Spare capacity is costly to maintain.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":0,"why":"Spare capacity is costly to maintain."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0187","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs.","Demand may be too inelastic for usage to fall significantly.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand."],"answer":3,"why":"Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0188","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($26, 159, 119); ($28, 149, 129); ($30, 139, 139); ($32, 129, 149). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$30","$26","$28","$32"],"answer":0,"why":"At $30, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 139 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0189","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":3,"why":"The firm may expand output when prices rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0190","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Excess supply can put downward pressure on price and encourage stock clearance.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information."],"answer":2,"why":"The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0191","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inelastic supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["When demand and supply both change, the effect on price depends on the relative sizes of the shifts.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.","Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","A change in the product's own price causes movement along its supply curve."],"answer":2,"why":"Inelastic supply: Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0192","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Negative external costs","Inelastic supply","Price elasticity of supply","Movement along demand"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates inelastic supply: Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0193","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of inelastic supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Independent fruit growers meet customers at a weekly neighbourhood market.","The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months.","At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110.","A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers."],"answer":1,"why":"The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. This illustrates supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0194","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inelastic supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged."],"answer":2,"why":"Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0195","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Weather may make planned supply impossible.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","New ovens require investment.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0196","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 360 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 30%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["25","6","7","0.167"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (30%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0197","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Operating and maintenance costs may be high.","Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need."],"answer":2,"why":"Less spoilage may increase the quantity available for purchase."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0198","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","Buyers could compare products and prices more easily."],"answer":0,"why":"It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0199","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growers could reach more potential customers.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."],"answer":3,"why":"Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0200","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: The price of fresh strawberries rises rapidly but the harvest cannot increase for several months. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."],"answer":3,"why":"Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0201","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes determinants of pes?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good.","Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."],"answer":3,"why":"Determinants of PES: Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0202","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Market economic system","Shift in supply","Indirect tax","Determinants of PES"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates determinants of pes: Spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0203","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of determinants of pes?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","At a price of $8, customers demand 200 umbrellas and sellers offer 200.","A good harvest increases the supply of apples at a regional market."],"answer":0,"why":"A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. This illustrates spare capacity, stock levels, production time and factor mobility influence elasticity of supply."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0204","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to determinants of pes?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":3,"why":"Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0205","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 450 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 20% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["10","2","3","0.5"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 2."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0206","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumers may pay less for apples.","Idle machines still require maintenance and space.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Idle machines still require maintenance and space."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0207","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.","Harvest output depends on weather and storage capacity."],"answer":2,"why":"The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0208","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.","The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Customers may be less likely to encounter shortages."],"answer":2,"why":"Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0209","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."],"answer":2,"why":"Orders may be met without a long delay."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0210","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","The magnitude of PED is 2, indicating elastic demand.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."],"answer":0,"why":"Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0211","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","Price elasticity of supply measures how strongly quantity supplied responds to a percentage change in price.","An increase in demand tends to raise equilibrium price and quantity if supply is unchanged.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price."],"answer":0,"why":"Market economic system: A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0212","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Market economic system","Consumer sovereignty","Buyers in a market","Market equilibrium"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates market economic system: A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0213","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 155 units at $11 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$166","$155","$1,716","$1,705"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $11 × 155 = $1,705."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0214","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce."],"answer":3,"why":"Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0215","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services.","Demand could change before new production is ready.","Cold storage has high energy and capital costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0216","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","More customers may buy tickets.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0217","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods.","Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.","Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Platform fees might increase sellers' costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumer preferences can affect business decisions."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0218","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Pollution and other external costs may be ignored.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","Extra competition may limit price increases.","The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock."],"answer":0,"why":"Pollution and other external costs may be ignored."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0219","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","Lower prices could attract more buyers.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use."],"answer":2,"why":"Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0220","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce.","The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price."],"answer":0,"why":"Profit and consumer choices influence the goods firms produce."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0221","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($30, 171, 131); ($32, 161, 141); ($34, 151, 151); ($36, 141, 161). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$36","$32","$30","$34"],"answer":3,"why":"At $34, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 151 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0222","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Merit goods","Consumer sovereignty","Determinants of PES","Supply"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer sovereignty: Consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0223","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer sovereignty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","At $3 per loaf, buyers want 400 loaves while bakeries offer only 280.","A medicine with few substitutes has less price-sensitive buyers than an optional entertainment service."],"answer":0,"why":"Several cafés add plant-based meals after sustained customer demand. This illustrates consumer sovereignty means buying decisions influence what firms offer for sale."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0224","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer sovereignty?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic."],"answer":1,"why":"Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0225","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Idle machines still require maintenance and space.","Subsidies require public funding.","Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0226","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Lower costs may support lower bread prices.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers."],"answer":1,"why":"Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0227","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to let customer demand guide café menu decisions?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Less unsold food may be wasted.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.","The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data."],"answer":0,"why":"Less unsold food may be wasted."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0228","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to let customer demand guide café menu decisions less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.","Price decisions may better reflect consumer sensitivity.","Output may better match what customers want."],"answer":1,"why":"Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0229","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise.","More people may purchase bicycles."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0230","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 400 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 25%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["6","20","0.2","5"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (25%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0231","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes market economy limitations?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period.","A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision.","Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Market economy limitations: Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0232","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Determinants of PED","Simultaneous shifts","Market economy limitations","Inelastic demand"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates market economy limitations: Markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0233","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of market economy limitations?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A lower electricity bill enables bakeries to offer more bread at every price.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","A producer sees price rise by 8% and quantity supplied rise by 16%.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%."],"answer":1,"why":"Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. This illustrates markets may allocate goods according to income rather than need and fail to account for external costs."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0234","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to market economy limitations?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market.","A higher selling price can justify output from more expensive production methods.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power.","Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."],"answer":3,"why":"Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0235","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Spare capacity is costly to maintain.","New ovens require investment.","Cycle routes use public funds and road space.","More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."],"answer":3,"why":"More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0236","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fares may fall and ridership may rise.","River pollution may fall.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","Public housing programmes require tax funding."],"answer":3,"why":"Public housing programmes require tax funding."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0237","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to supplement market housing with targeted public housing?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data.","Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion."],"answer":2,"why":"Poor housing conditions may become less widespread."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0238","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 490 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 60% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["7","0.167","6","50"],"answer":2,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (60%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 6."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0239","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation.","Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","The firm may expand output when prices rise.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":0,"why":"More low-income households may gain suitable accommodation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0240","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","The PES value is 2, indicating supply is price elastic.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price."],"answer":0,"why":"Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0241","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes public goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Non-price changes in production conditions shift the supply curve.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services.","Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption.","Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at different prices over a period."],"answer":2,"why":"Public goods: Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0242","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply","Surplus","Public goods","Negative external costs"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates public goods: Public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0243","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of public goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Independent firms compete to supply restaurants while customers choose where to eat.","A shop records how many bicycles customers would buy at each possible selling price.","A coffee farmer supplies more beans after the market price rises.","A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal."],"answer":3,"why":"A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. This illustrates public goods are non-excludable and non-rival in consumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0244","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to public goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus.","Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0245","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices.","Revenue from each ticket will be lower."],"answer":0,"why":"All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0246","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 110 units at $6 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$666","$110","$116","$660"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $6 × 110 = $660."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0247","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to finance a public lighthouse through taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged.","Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers."],"answer":0,"why":"The service can be provided even when individual users cannot be charged."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0248","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to finance a public lighthouse through taxation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Better forecasts may guide pricing and revenue decisions.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand.","Poor housing conditions may become less widespread.","Operating and maintenance costs may be high."],"answer":3,"why":"Operating and maintenance costs may be high."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0249","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to finance a public lighthouse through taxation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","Storage may spread sales more evenly across the year.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","More customers may buy tickets."],"answer":0,"why":"All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0250","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A coastal lighthouse guides all nearby ships without preventing others from using its signal. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic.","Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain."],"answer":2,"why":"Private firms may struggle to charge users, leading to underprovision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0251","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes merit goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply is price inelastic when quantity supplied changes by a smaller percentage than price.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","Buyers signal demand through their willingness and ability to purchase products.","Equilibrium is the price and quantity at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."],"answer":1,"why":"Merit goods: Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0252","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Simultaneous shifts","Price elasticity of supply","Movement along demand","Merit goods"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates merit goods: Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0253","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of merit goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Several farmers offer tomatoes at a wholesale market each morning.","A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others.","A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","A popular sports event increases demand for nearby hotel rooms."],"answer":1,"why":"A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. This illustrates merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0254","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to merit goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower input costs shift market supply right, other factors unchanged.","The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption."],"answer":3,"why":"External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0255","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($34, 183, 143); ($36, 173, 153); ($38, 163, 163); ($40, 153, 173). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$36","$40","$38","$34"],"answer":2,"why":"At $38, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 163 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0256","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise childhood vaccination services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Managers may estimate how sales volume will change.","Subsidies require public funding.","Ordering decisions may match expected sales.","Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells."],"answer":1,"why":"Subsidies require public funding."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0257","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to subsidise childhood vaccination services?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Lower transmission may benefit the wider community.","Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower transmission may benefit the wider community."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0258","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to subsidise childhood vaccination services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Consumer preferences can affect business decisions.","The firm may gain sales during sudden demand increases.","Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0259","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to subsidise childhood vaccination services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Farm revenue may increase if extra output sells.","Orders may be met without a long delay.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","More people may receive protection from disease."],"answer":3,"why":"More people may receive protection from disease."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0260","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A vaccination programme protects recipients and reduces disease spread to others. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","The supply curve shifts right and produces excess supply at the old price.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","A lower price causes an extension in quantity demanded along the same demand curve."],"answer":0,"why":"External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0261","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes negative external costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A market economic system relies mainly on private ownership and price signals to allocate resources.","Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties.","Sellers supply products and respond to prices, costs and expected profit.","A market is an arrangement through which buyers and sellers exchange goods or services."],"answer":1,"why":"Negative external costs: Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0262","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Negative external costs","Surplus","Market","Determinants of PES"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates negative external costs: Negative external costs are harmful effects of production or consumption imposed on third parties."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0263","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 440 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 20%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["0.25","15","5","4"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (20%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 4."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0264","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to negative external costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","Resources that can be adjusted quickly make supply more elastic."],"answer":1,"why":"The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0265","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["River pollution may fall.","Farmers may face lower prices and profits.","Demand may change with the weather.","Extra harvesting can raise marginal production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"River pollution may fall."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0266","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Treatment raises operating costs for the factory.","Consumers may pay less for apples.","Managers may estimate how sales volume will change."],"answer":1,"why":"Treatment raises operating costs for the factory."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0267","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to require the paint factory to treat wastewater?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity.","Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage.","Savings depend on electricity use and machine reliability.","Fishing livelihoods and health may improve."],"answer":3,"why":"Fishing livelihoods and health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0268","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to require the paint factory to treat wastewater less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective.","The retailer may avoid spending on unwanted stock.","Production can be aligned more closely with expected market prices.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."],"answer":0,"why":"Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0269","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to require the paint factory to treat wastewater. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["River pollution may fall.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","Growers could reach more potential customers.","The firm may expand output when prices rise."],"answer":0,"why":"River pollution may fall."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0270","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A paint factory discharges waste into a river used by downstream fishers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Consumers can switch more easily when close substitutes are available.","The interaction of buyers and sellers conveys price and quantity information.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power."],"answer":0,"why":"The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0271","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A producer initially supplies 530 units and observes a 10% price rise followed by a 50% increase in quantity supplied. What is price elasticity of supply (PES)?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["40","5","0.2","6"],"answer":1,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (50%) / percentage change in price (10%) = 5."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0272","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sellers in a market","Demand","Merit goods","Mixed economic system"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates mixed economic system: A mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0273","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of mixed economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Cinema ticket sales rise when the cinema lowers its ticket price.","Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals.","A gym raises its fee by 10% and membership falls by 20%.","Demand for delivery meals and the number of delivery kitchens both increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Private shops sell food while the government operates local public hospitals. This illustrates a mixed economic system combines market allocation with government intervention and public provision."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0274","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to mixed economic system?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures.","Higher prices can encourage an extension in quantity supplied, other things equal.","External benefits and imperfect information can cause underconsumption.","When planned purchases equal planned sales there is no pressure from shortage or surplus."],"answer":0,"why":"The combination aims to retain market incentives while addressing some market failures."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0275","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine private retail markets with public health provision. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand may change with the weather.","Competition may improve retail efficiency.","Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers.","Farmers may face lower prices and profits."],"answer":1,"why":"Competition may improve retail efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0276","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine private retail markets with public health provision. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","More people may receive protection from disease.","River pollution may fall.","State services require sufficient tax revenue."],"answer":3,"why":"State services require sufficient tax revenue."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0277","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to combine private retail markets with public health provision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need.","Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households.","Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period.","The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic."],"answer":1,"why":"Public hospitals may improve access for low-income households."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0278","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to combine private retail markets with public health provision less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated.","Less unsold food may be wasted.","More seasonal jobs may be offered.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0279","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to combine private retail markets with public health provision. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may offer products people are willing to purchase.","All passing vessels may benefit from safer navigation.","Higher sales volumes may be served.","Competition may improve retail efficiency."],"answer":3,"why":"Competition may improve retail efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0280","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A firm sells 170 units at $10 each. What is its total sales revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$170","$1,700","$180","$1,710"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = price × quantity = $10 × 170 = $1,700."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0281","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes indirect tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","Price elasticity of demand measures responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price.","Demand is the quantity buyers are willing and able to purchase at different prices during a period.","An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."],"answer":3,"why":"Indirect tax: An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0282","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Buyers in a market","Movement along supply","Indirect tax","Market economic system"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates indirect tax: An indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0283","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of indirect tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging.","A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%.","A factory with idle machines can respond faster to new orders than one running at full capacity.","Demand for electric bicycles rises after more cycling lanes are built."],"answer":0,"why":"A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. This illustrates an indirect tax is a tax on spending on goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0284","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to indirect tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","Demand combines willingness to buy with purchasing power.","Short-run biological constraints make farm output unresponsive to price.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction."],"answer":0,"why":"The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0285","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to tax single-use plastic packaging. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows.","Some consumers may be unable to afford essential services.","Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher prices may discourage plastic use."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0286","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to tax single-use plastic packaging. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More customers may buy tickets.","Competition may encourage innovation and lower prices.","Competition may increase the quantity offered.","Consumers may face higher prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers may face higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0287","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to tax single-use plastic packaging?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tax revenue could fund waste management.","The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs.","Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers.","Businesses may promote harmful products despite consumer demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Tax revenue could fund waste management."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0288","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A market schedule gives these data (price, quantity demanded, quantity supplied): ($38, 195, 155); ($40, 185, 165); ($42, 175, 175); ($44, 165, 185). What is the equilibrium price?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$42","$40","$38","$44"],"answer":0,"why":"At $42, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 175 units."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0289","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to tax single-use plastic packaging. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may discourage plastic use.","Fare revenue may rise if the proportional fall in journeys is smaller.","More customers may buy tickets.","The bakery may reduce unsold stock."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher prices may discourage plastic use."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0290","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government adds a per-unit tax to single-use plastic packaging. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","The demand curve shifts right, creating upward price pressure at the original price.","The PED magnitude is 0.3; quantity responds proportionately less than price."],"answer":0,"why":"The tax raises producers' costs and tends to raise consumer prices while reducing sales."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0291","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes subsidy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A surplus occurs when quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded at a particular price.","A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices.","Merit goods generate benefits that consumers may undervalue, so they tend to be underconsumed.","A non-price determinant changes the whole demand schedule for a good."],"answer":1,"why":"Subsidy: A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0292","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Market economic system","Consumer sovereignty","Subsidy","Shift in supply"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates subsidy: A subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0293","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of subsidy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided.","A bus operator raises fares by 10% while passenger journeys fall by 3%.","Private housing is profitable in an affluent district while low-income residents face overcrowding.","At $20 a pair, a shop offers 180 shoes but customers buy only 110."],"answer":0,"why":"A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. This illustrates a subsidy is government financial assistance that lowers production costs or consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0294","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to subsidy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producers responding to sales signals can redirect resources towards preferred goods.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","Both shifts raise equilibrium quantity, but the price effect is uncertain.","Excess demand puts upward pressure on price in a flexible market."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0295","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise low-emission public transport. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Farmers may face lower prices and profits.","New ovens require investment.","Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise."],"answer":3,"why":"Fares may fall and ridership may rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0296","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A retailer selling initially 480 units raises its price by 5% and quantity demanded decreases by 15%. What is the magnitude of price elasticity of demand (PED)?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["10","4","0.333","3"],"answer":3,"why":"Elasticity = percentage change in quantity (15%) / percentage change in price (5%) = 3."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0297","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to subsidise low-emission public transport?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply may be fixed in the very short run.","Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion.","Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution.","It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity."],"answer":2,"why":"Reduced car use can lower congestion and pollution."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0298","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to subsidise low-emission public transport less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators.","Unsold inventory and storage costs may fall.","Flexibility can help meet sudden increases in demand.","Reduced car travel may lower local pollution."],"answer":0,"why":"Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0299","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to subsidise low-emission public transport. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher prices may encourage extra rooms to be offered.","More people may purchase bicycles.","Firms may avoid price rises that lose many customers.","Fares may fall and ridership may rise."],"answer":3,"why":"Fares may fall and ridership may rise."},{"id":"0455-C02-P1-MCQ-0300","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government pays bus operators for each low-emission journey provided. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved suitability of cycling shifts demand right at each price.","Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available.","The market price may not include damage suffered by others, causing overproduction.","Price signals alone may not achieve fair distribution or account for social welfare."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower costs can shift supply right and make the service cheaper or more available."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define market. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• An arrangement bringing buyers and sellers into contact (1); To exchange goods and services / undertake purchases and sales (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how buyers influence what is produced in a market. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Buyers express willingness and ability to purchase (1); Firms respond by producing goods for which demand exists (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• More purchases indicate stronger demand (1); Profit-seeking sellers may allocate more resources to those products (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how sellers respond to prices in a market. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A higher price may make sales more profitable (1); Sellers may increase quantity supplied (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A lower price may reduce the return from supplying a product (1); Sellers may reduce quantity supplied (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define demand. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Willingness to buy a product (1); Ability to pay for it / willingness and ability at a given price and time (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what causes a movement along a demand curve. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A change in the product’s own price (1); Other demand conditions remain unchanged / this changes quantity demanded, not demand (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A fall in price causes an extension of demand (1); Quantity demanded increases along the existing curve (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define supply. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Willingness to sell a product (1); Ability to supply it / willingness and ability at a given price and time (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what causes a movement along a supply curve. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A change in the product’s own price (1); Other supply conditions remain unchanged / this changes quantity supplied, not supply (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A rise in price causes an extension of supply (1); Quantity supplied increases along the existing curve (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what causes a shift of a supply curve. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A non-price determinant of supply changes (1); A different quantity is offered at each price (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Lower input costs reduce unit production costs (1); Firms are willing to supply more at each price (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Better production technology raises efficiency (1); Supply can increase at unchanged prices (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define market equilibrium. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied (1); At the prevailing price / no shortage or surplus exists (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define shortage. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied (1); At a particular price / there is excess demand (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how an increase in demand affects equilibrium price and quantity when supply is unchanged. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Demand shifts right while supply is unchanged (1); Both equilibrium price and quantity rise (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Excess demand arises at the original price (1); Price rises until a new equilibrium is reached (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how an increase in supply affects equilibrium price and quantity when demand is unchanged. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Supply shifts right while demand is unchanged (1); Equilibrium price falls and equilibrium quantity rises (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Excess supply arises at the original price (1); Price falls towards the new equilibrium (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain why the effect on equilibrium price of simultaneous increases in demand and supply is uncertain. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Increasing demand tends to raise price (1); Increasing supply tends to lower price (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Demand and supply put opposing pressures on price (1); The relative sizes of the shifts determine the overall price change (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define price elasticity of demand. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Responsiveness of quantity demanded (1); To a change in the product’s price (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Percentage change in quantity demanded (1); Divided by percentage change in price (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define inelastic demand. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The percentage change in quantity demanded (1); Is smaller than the percentage change in the product’s price (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define price elasticity of supply. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Responsiveness of quantity supplied (1); To a change in the product’s price (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Percentage change in quantity supplied (1); Divided by percentage change in price (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define inelastic supply. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The percentage change in quantity supplied (1); Is smaller than the percentage change in the product’s price (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two determinants of price elasticity of supply. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Spare production capacity (1)\n• Stock levels (1)\n• Production time (1)\n• Factor mobility / ease of switching inputs (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define market economic system. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Resources are mainly privately owned (1); Allocation is mainly through demand, supply and the price mechanism (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define consumer sovereignty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Consumers’ purchasing decisions influence firms (1); Those decisions determine what is produced / allocation responds to consumer preferences (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define public goods. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Non-rival: one person’s consumption does not reduce availability to others (1); Non-excludable: non-payers cannot readily be prevented from consuming (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define merit goods. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Consumers underestimate the good’s benefits (1); It is underconsumed / underproduced when left to market forces (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define external costs. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Costs imposed on third parties (1); Arising from production or consumption / not borne by the decision-maker (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define mixed economic system. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Private and public sectors both operate (1); Resource allocation uses both market forces and government intervention (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M02-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define indirect tax. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A tax on expenditure / purchases (1); Levied on goods and services rather than directly on income or wealth (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to create a digital trading platform for local growers. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Buyers and sellers find each other more easily (1) → Search costs decrease (1) → More mutually beneficial exchanges can occur (1).\n\n2. Growers reach more customers (1) → Demand for their produce may rise (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. Buyers can compare competing offers (1) → Price information becomes more widely available (1) → Sellers face greater competitive pressure (1) → Consumers may pay lower prices (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to use customer demand information to plan bread output might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Bread output matches customers’ preferences (1) → Fewer unwanted loaves remain unsold (1) → Resources are allocated more efficiently (1).\n\n2. Less bread is thrown away (1) → Ingredient waste decreases (1) → Cost per loaf sold may fall (1).\n\n3. Information identifies periods of strong demand (1) → The bakery prepares sufficient stock (1) → Fewer sales are lost through shortages (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More sellers increase market supply (1) → Equilibrium tomato prices may fall (1) → Consumers can buy more with their income (1).\n\n2. Existing sellers face more rivals (1) → Firms have incentives to improve efficiency (1) → Unit costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. Additional sellers may offer different varieties (1) → Consumer choice increases (1) → Buyers can find products closer to their preferences (1) → Consumer satisfaction may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers survey local demand before ordering bicycles. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A survey estimates the quantity customers want (1) → Orders better match demand (1) → Unsold bicycle stocks may decrease (1).\n\n2. A survey identifies preferred bicycle features (1) → Retailers stock more desirable products (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. More accurate orders reduce excess inventory (1) → Less capital is tied up in slow-selling stock (1) → Storage and financing costs may fall (1) → Profit may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Lower ticket prices cause an extension of demand (1) → More empty seats are filled (1) → Capacity is used more fully (1).\n\n2. If demand is elastic, attendance rises proportionately more than price falls (1) → Total ticket revenue rises (1) → Profit may rise if extra costs are low (1).\n\n3. Cheaper tickets improve affordability (1) → Lower-income customers can attend (1) → Access to leisure increases (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Safer routes make cycling more attractive (1) → Demand for bicycles may increase (1) → Bicycle producers may expand output (1).\n\n2. Some car journeys are replaced by cycling (1) → Emissions and congestion may decrease (1) → External costs of travel fall (1).\n\n3. Cycling provides physical exercise (1) → Health may improve (1) → Fewer working days may be lost to illness (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to plan output using a detailed supply schedule might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A supply schedule relates output to price (1) → Firms can plan feasible production levels (1) → Unrealistic output targets may be avoided (1).\n\n2. Production plans show required inputs (1) → Labour and materials can be ordered in advance (1) → Shortages and interruptions may decrease (1).\n\n3. Matching supply to expected sales reduces excess stock (1) → Storage requirements may fall (1) → Inventory costs may decrease (1) → Profit may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher prices encourage an extension of supply (1) → More coffee can be sold (1) → Total revenue may increase (1).\n\n2. Extra harvesting requires workers (1) → Seasonal demand for labour rises (1) → Farm workers gain income (1).\n\n3. Harvesting more coffee reduces unpicked output (1) → Existing crops are used more fully (1) → Output per hectare harvested increases (1) → Cost per unit sold may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Efficient ovens use less energy per loaf (1) → Variable cost per loaf falls (1) → Average production cost decreases (1).\n\n2. Lower costs allow lower bread prices (1) → Consumers’ purchasing power rises (1) → Bread becomes more affordable (1).\n\n3. Lower energy use reduces fuel consumption (1) → Emissions from energy generation may fall (1) → External environmental costs decrease (1) → Social welfare may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Ordering the equilibrium quantity reduces excess stock (1) → Fewer umbrellas require storage (1) → Inventory costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Adequate stocks meet customers’ demand (1) → Fewer customers are turned away (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. Better matching purchases to sales reduces tied-up funds (1) → More finance remains available for other activities (1) → Borrowing requirements may fall (1) → Interest costs may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher prices discourage some purchases (1) → Quantity demanded contracts (1) → The shortage may decrease (1).\n\n2. A higher price signals profit opportunities (1) → Producers extend supply (1) → More bread becomes available (1).\n\n3. Higher expected returns attract resources into baking (1) → Firms may invest in capacity (1) → Market supply may increase over time (1) → Bread shortages may become less persistent (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to discount shoes when inventories are excessive might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A lower shoe price extends demand (1) → More shoes are sold (1) → Excess supply is reduced (1).\n\n2. Selling old stock frees storage space (1) → Storage costs decrease (1) → Profit may improve (1).\n\n3. Discounting releases money tied up in inventory (1) → The retailer obtains cash (1) → Suppliers can be paid sooner (1) → Cash-flow difficulties may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher prices cause a contraction in demand (1) → Some bookings move to quieter periods (1) → Excess demand during the peak may decrease (1).\n\n2. Higher prices raise the return on available rooms (1) → Hotel revenue may increase (1) → Funds for maintenance may rise (1).\n\n3. Higher profits signal demand for accommodation (1) → Firms may invest in additional rooms (1) → Supply may increase over time (1) → Future peak-season shortages may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers support more efficient apple cultivation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Improved techniques raise apple yields (1) → More apples are produced per hectare (1) → Average costs may decrease (1).\n\n2. Lower costs shift supply to the right (1) → Equilibrium apple prices may fall (1) → Consumers can afford more fruit (1).\n\n3. Better harvesting and storage reduce spoilage (1) → A larger share of the crop can be sold (1) → Revenue from each harvest may increase (1) → Farm incomes may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Extra kitchen capacity increases possible output (1) → More orders can be accepted (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. More capacity reduces order delays (1) → Reliability and customer satisfaction may improve (1) → Repeat demand may increase (1).\n\n3. Expanding production requires additional staff (1) → Demand for kitchen labour rises (1) → Unemployed workers may obtain jobs (1) → Household incomes may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. PED predicts how quantity responds to a fee change (1) → The gym can estimate total revenue effects (1) → It can avoid a price change that reduces revenue (1).\n\n2. Elastic demand suggests a fee cut may raise revenue (1) → Membership can increase proportionately more than price falls (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n3. PED reveals customers’ price sensitivity (1) → The gym can choose a more suitable fee (1) → Empty capacity may be used more fully (1) → Average fixed cost per member may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Inelastic demand means passenger numbers fall proportionately less than fares rise (1) → Total fare revenue increases (1) → Operators can cover more of their costs (1).\n\n2. More revenue may finance better vehicles (1) → Reliability and service quality may improve (1) → Passengers may benefit from fewer cancellations (1).\n\n3. A cautious increase limits the extra cost per journey (1) → Low-income passengers face a smaller affordability loss (1) → More passengers can retain access to work (1) → Employment opportunities are less restricted (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to consider substitute availability when pricing products. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Many close substitutes make demand more elastic (1) → A price rise may cause substantial switching (1) → Avoiding a large price rise may protect revenue (1).\n\n2. Few close substitutes make demand less elastic (1) → A price rise may raise total revenue (1) → The firm can better cover costs (1).\n\n3. Knowledge of substitutes guides a price cut (1) → Price-sensitive customers may switch to the firm (1) → Sales volume may increase (1) → Spare capacity may be used more fully (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Flexible machinery can switch production quickly (1) → Output can respond faster to price increases (1) → Supply becomes more price elastic (1).\n\n2. Firms can expand output when demand rises (1) → Fewer sales are lost through shortages (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n3. Capacity can be moved away from weak-demand products (1) → Fewer unwanted goods are produced (1) → Waste of inputs decreases (1) → Resource allocation improves (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Cold storage prevents rapid deterioration (1) → More harvested crops remain saleable (1) → Waste decreases (1).\n\n2. Stored crops can be released when prices rise (1) → Supply responds more to market conditions (1) → Short-run price fluctuations may be reduced (1).\n\n3. Less produce must be sold immediately after harvest (1) → Farmers can avoid very low harvest-time prices (1) → Farm revenue may become more stable (1) → Farmers can plan investment more confidently (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Spare machinery permits a quick increase in output (1) → The firm can respond to higher prices (1) → Supply is more price elastic (1).\n\n2. Extra demand can be met without building a new plant (1) → Orders are fulfilled sooner (1) → Revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. Flexible spare capacity can replace broken equipment (1) → Production interruptions become shorter (1) → Delivery reliability improves (1) → Customer demand may be retained (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More restaurants increase competition (1) → Firms may lower prices to attract customers (1) → Consumers gain purchasing power (1).\n\n2. Rivalry encourages better service and food quality (1) → Restaurants must respond to customer preferences (1) → Consumer satisfaction may improve (1).\n\n3. Independent entrants offer different menus (1) → Consumer choice broadens (1) → More dietary preferences can be met (1) → Consumer welfare may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to let customer demand guide café menu decisions. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Menus reflect customers’ preferences (1) → More of the food produced is wanted (1) → Allocative efficiency may improve (1).\n\n2. Popular dishes attract more orders (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1) → Profit may increase (1).\n\n3. Less unpopular food is prepared (1) → Spoilage and ingredient waste decrease (1) → Variable costs may fall (1) → Resources are released for other uses (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers supplement market housing with targeted public housing. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Public provision adds housing supply (1) → More low-income households can obtain shelter (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Subsidised rents reduce housing costs (1) → Residents retain more disposable income (1) → Other basic needs become more affordable (1).\n\n3. Targeting can direct help to underserved households (1) → Fewer resources subsidise households able to pay market rents (1) → More needy households can be assisted from a given budget (1) → Redistribution becomes more effective (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to finance a public lighthouse through taxation could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Tax funding overcomes the free-rider problem (1) → A lighthouse can be provided despite non-excludability (1) → Ships receive navigation services (1).\n\n2. Better navigation reduces collision risks (1) → Fewer vessels and cargoes are lost (1) → Resource losses decrease (1).\n\n3. Safer routes make shipping more reliable (1) → Transport delays and risks may fall (1) → Trading costs may decrease (1) → International trade may expand (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to subsidise childhood vaccination services. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A subsidy lowers vaccination costs (1) → Vaccinations become cheaper to consumers (1) → Quantity demanded may increase (1).\n\n2. More children are protected against disease (1) → Illness and treatment needs may decrease (1) → Healthcare resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Vaccination reduces disease transmission to others (1) → Unvaccinated people receive external benefits (1) → Social benefits exceed private benefits (1) → Underconsumption may move towards a socially desirable level (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to require a paint factory that pollutes a river to treat its wastewater might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Treatment removes harmful waste before discharge (1) → River pollution falls (1) → External costs imposed on residents decrease (1).\n\n2. Cleaner water protects fish stocks (1) → Fishers can maintain catches (1) → Their incomes may improve (1).\n\n3. Cleaner water lowers exposure to pollutants (1) → Illness may decrease (1) → Medical spending and work absences may fall (1) → Social welfare may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to combine private retail markets with public health provision. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Private retailers respond to consumer demand (1) → Product choice and availability may improve (1) → Consumer satisfaction may increase (1).\n\n2. Public healthcare reaches people unable to pay (1) → Access to essential treatment improves (1) → Absolute poverty and ill health may fall (1).\n\n3. Public provision addresses underconsumption of healthcare (1) → Healthier people transmit fewer illnesses (1) → Third parties receive external benefits (1) → Social welfare may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers tax single-use plastic packaging. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Tax raises the price of plastic packaging (1) → Quantity demanded may fall (1) → Less packaging may be discarded (1).\n\n2. Tax encourages use of reusable substitutes (1) → Demand shifts to less harmful materials (1) → External pollution costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. The government receives tax revenue (1) → More funds can support waste collection (1) → Litter and cleanup problems may decrease (1) → Environmental quality may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M04-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to subsidise low-emission public transport could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Subsidies lower public transport operating costs (1) → Fares may fall (1) → Quantity demanded may increase (1).\n\n2. Cheaper services may replace private car journeys (1) → Road congestion and emissions decrease (1) → External costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Improved affordability helps low-income passengers travel (1) → More workplaces become accessible (1) → Geographical mobility of labour may increase (1) → Employment opportunities may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to create a digital trading platform for local growers. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Buyers and sellers find each other more easily (1) → Search costs decrease (1) → More mutually beneficial exchanges can occur (1).\n\n2. Growers reach more customers (1) → Demand for their produce may rise (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. Buyers can compare competing offers (1) → Price information becomes more widely available (1) → Sellers face greater competitive pressure (1) → Consumers may pay lower prices (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to use customer demand information to plan bread output may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Bread output matches customers’ preferences (1) → Fewer unwanted loaves remain unsold (1) → Resources are allocated more efficiently (1).\n\n2. Less bread is thrown away (1) → Ingredient waste decreases (1) → Cost per loaf sold may fall (1).\n\n3. Information identifies periods of strong demand (1) → The bakery prepares sufficient stock (1) → Fewer sales are lost through shortages (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More sellers increase market supply (1) → Equilibrium tomato prices may fall (1) → Consumers can buy more with their income (1).\n\n2. Existing sellers face more rivals (1) → Firms have incentives to improve efficiency (1) → Unit costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. Additional sellers may offer different varieties (1) → Consumer choice increases (1) → Buyers can find products closer to their preferences (1) → Consumer satisfaction may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to survey local demand before ordering bicycles. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A survey estimates the quantity customers want (1) → Orders better match demand (1) → Unsold bicycle stocks may decrease (1).\n\n2. A survey identifies preferred bicycle features (1) → Retailers stock more desirable products (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. More accurate orders reduce excess inventory (1) → Less capital is tied up in slow-selling stock (1) → Storage and financing costs may fall (1) → Profit may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Lower ticket prices cause an extension of demand (1) → More empty seats are filled (1) → Capacity is used more fully (1).\n\n2. If demand is elastic, attendance rises proportionately more than price falls (1) → Total ticket revenue rises (1) → Profit may rise if extra costs are low (1).\n\n3. Cheaper tickets improve affordability (1) → Lower-income customers can attend (1) → Access to leisure increases (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Safer routes make cycling more attractive (1) → Demand for bicycles may increase (1) → Bicycle producers may expand output (1).\n\n2. Some car journeys are replaced by cycling (1) → Emissions and congestion may decrease (1) → External costs of travel fall (1).\n\n3. Cycling provides physical exercise (1) → Health may improve (1) → Fewer working days may be lost to illness (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to plan output using a detailed supply schedule may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A supply schedule relates output to price (1) → Firms can plan feasible production levels (1) → Unrealistic output targets may be avoided (1).\n\n2. Production plans show required inputs (1) → Labour and materials can be ordered in advance (1) → Shortages and interruptions may decrease (1).\n\n3. Matching supply to expected sales reduces excess stock (1) → Storage requirements may fall (1) → Inventory costs may decrease (1) → Profit may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher prices encourage an extension of supply (1) → More coffee can be sold (1) → Total revenue may increase (1).\n\n2. Extra harvesting requires workers (1) → Seasonal demand for labour rises (1) → Farm workers gain income (1).\n\n3. Harvesting more coffee reduces unpicked output (1) → Existing crops are used more fully (1) → Output per hectare harvested increases (1) → Cost per unit sold may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Efficient ovens use less energy per loaf (1) → Variable cost per loaf falls (1) → Average production cost decreases (1).\n\n2. Lower costs allow lower bread prices (1) → Consumers’ purchasing power rises (1) → Bread becomes more affordable (1).\n\n3. Lower energy use reduces fuel consumption (1) → Emissions from energy generation may fall (1) → External environmental costs decrease (1) → Social welfare may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Ordering the equilibrium quantity reduces excess stock (1) → Fewer umbrellas require storage (1) → Inventory costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Adequate stocks meet customers’ demand (1) → Fewer customers are turned away (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. Better matching purchases to sales reduces tied-up funds (1) → More finance remains available for other activities (1) → Borrowing requirements may fall (1) → Interest costs may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher prices discourage some purchases (1) → Quantity demanded contracts (1) → The shortage may decrease (1).\n\n2. A higher price signals profit opportunities (1) → Producers extend supply (1) → More bread becomes available (1).\n\n3. Higher expected returns attract resources into baking (1) → Firms may invest in capacity (1) → Market supply may increase over time (1) → Bread shortages may become less persistent (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to discount shoes when inventories are excessive may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A lower shoe price extends demand (1) → More shoes are sold (1) → Excess supply is reduced (1).\n\n2. Selling old stock frees storage space (1) → Storage costs decrease (1) → Profit may improve (1).\n\n3. Discounting releases money tied up in inventory (1) → The retailer obtains cash (1) → Suppliers can be paid sooner (1) → Cash-flow difficulties may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher prices cause a contraction in demand (1) → Some bookings move to quieter periods (1) → Excess demand during the peak may decrease (1).\n\n2. Higher prices raise the return on available rooms (1) → Hotel revenue may increase (1) → Funds for maintenance may rise (1).\n\n3. Higher profits signal demand for accommodation (1) → Firms may invest in additional rooms (1) → Supply may increase over time (1) → Future peak-season shortages may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to support more efficient apple cultivation. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Improved techniques raise apple yields (1) → More apples are produced per hectare (1) → Average costs may decrease (1).\n\n2. Lower costs shift supply to the right (1) → Equilibrium apple prices may fall (1) → Consumers can afford more fruit (1).\n\n3. Better harvesting and storage reduce spoilage (1) → A larger share of the crop can be sold (1) → Revenue from each harvest may increase (1) → Farm incomes may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Extra kitchen capacity increases possible output (1) → More orders can be accepted (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. More capacity reduces order delays (1) → Reliability and customer satisfaction may improve (1) → Repeat demand may increase (1).\n\n3. Expanding production requires additional staff (1) → Demand for kitchen labour rises (1) → Unemployed workers may obtain jobs (1) → Household incomes may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. PED predicts how quantity responds to a fee change (1) → The gym can estimate total revenue effects (1) → It can avoid a price change that reduces revenue (1).\n\n2. Elastic demand suggests a fee cut may raise revenue (1) → Membership can increase proportionately more than price falls (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n3. PED reveals customers’ price sensitivity (1) → The gym can choose a more suitable fee (1) → Empty capacity may be used more fully (1) → Average fixed cost per member may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Inelastic demand means passenger numbers fall proportionately less than fares rise (1) → Total fare revenue increases (1) → Operators can cover more of their costs (1).\n\n2. More revenue may finance better vehicles (1) → Reliability and service quality may improve (1) → Passengers may benefit from fewer cancellations (1).\n\n3. A cautious increase limits the extra cost per journey (1) → Low-income passengers face a smaller affordability loss (1) → More passengers can retain access to work (1) → Employment opportunities are less restricted (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to consider substitute availability when pricing products could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Many close substitutes make demand more elastic (1) → A price rise may cause substantial switching (1) → Avoiding a large price rise may protect revenue (1).\n\n2. Few close substitutes make demand less elastic (1) → A price rise may raise total revenue (1) → The firm can better cover costs (1).\n\n3. Knowledge of substitutes guides a price cut (1) → Price-sensitive customers may switch to the firm (1) → Sales volume may increase (1) → Spare capacity may be used more fully (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Flexible machinery can switch production quickly (1) → Output can respond faster to price increases (1) → Supply becomes more price elastic (1).\n\n2. Firms can expand output when demand rises (1) → Fewer sales are lost through shortages (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n3. Capacity can be moved away from weak-demand products (1) → Fewer unwanted goods are produced (1) → Waste of inputs decreases (1) → Resource allocation improves (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Cold storage prevents rapid deterioration (1) → More harvested crops remain saleable (1) → Waste decreases (1).\n\n2. Stored crops can be released when prices rise (1) → Supply responds more to market conditions (1) → Short-run price fluctuations may be reduced (1).\n\n3. Less produce must be sold immediately after harvest (1) → Farmers can avoid very low harvest-time prices (1) → Farm revenue may become more stable (1) → Farmers can plan investment more confidently (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Spare machinery permits a quick increase in output (1) → The firm can respond to higher prices (1) → Supply is more price elastic (1).\n\n2. Extra demand can be met without building a new plant (1) → Orders are fulfilled sooner (1) → Revenue may increase (1).\n\n3. Flexible spare capacity can replace broken equipment (1) → Production interruptions become shorter (1) → Delivery reliability improves (1) → Customer demand may be retained (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More restaurants increase competition (1) → Firms may lower prices to attract customers (1) → Consumers gain purchasing power (1).\n\n2. Rivalry encourages better service and food quality (1) → Restaurants must respond to customer preferences (1) → Consumer satisfaction may improve (1).\n\n3. Independent entrants offer different menus (1) → Consumer choice broadens (1) → More dietary preferences can be met (1) → Consumer welfare may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to let customer demand guide café menu decisions could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Menus reflect customers’ preferences (1) → More of the food produced is wanted (1) → Allocative efficiency may improve (1).\n\n2. Popular dishes attract more orders (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1) → Profit may increase (1).\n\n3. Less unpopular food is prepared (1) → Spoilage and ingredient waste decrease (1) → Variable costs may fall (1) → Resources are released for other uses (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to supplement market housing with targeted public housing. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Public provision adds housing supply (1) → More low-income households can obtain shelter (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Subsidised rents reduce housing costs (1) → Residents retain more disposable income (1) → Other basic needs become more affordable (1).\n\n3. Targeting can direct help to underserved households (1) → Fewer resources subsidise households able to pay market rents (1) → More needy households can be assisted from a given budget (1) → Redistribution becomes more effective (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to finance a public lighthouse through taxation could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Tax funding overcomes the free-rider problem (1) → A lighthouse can be provided despite non-excludability (1) → Ships receive navigation services (1).\n\n2. Better navigation reduces collision risks (1) → Fewer vessels and cargoes are lost (1) → Resource losses decrease (1).\n\n3. Safer routes make shipping more reliable (1) → Transport delays and risks may fall (1) → Trading costs may decrease (1) → International trade may expand (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to subsidise childhood vaccination services. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A subsidy lowers vaccination costs (1) → Vaccinations become cheaper to consumers (1) → Quantity demanded may increase (1).\n\n2. More children are protected against disease (1) → Illness and treatment needs may decrease (1) → Healthcare resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Vaccination reduces disease transmission to others (1) → Unvaccinated people receive external benefits (1) → Social benefits exceed private benefits (1) → Underconsumption may move towards a socially desirable level (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to require a paint factory that pollutes a river to treat its wastewater may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Treatment removes harmful waste before discharge (1) → River pollution falls (1) → External costs imposed on residents decrease (1).\n\n2. Cleaner water protects fish stocks (1) → Fishers can maintain catches (1) → Their incomes may improve (1).\n\n3. Cleaner water lowers exposure to pollutants (1) → Illness may decrease (1) → Medical spending and work absences may fall (1) → Social welfare may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to combine private retail markets with public health provision could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Private retailers respond to consumer demand (1) → Product choice and availability may improve (1) → Consumer satisfaction may increase (1).\n\n2. Public healthcare reaches people unable to pay (1) → Access to essential treatment improves (1) → Absolute poverty and ill health may fall (1).\n\n3. Public provision addresses underconsumption of healthcare (1) → Healthier people transmit fewer illnesses (1) → Third parties receive external benefits (1) → Social welfare may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to tax single-use plastic packaging. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Tax raises the price of plastic packaging (1) → Quantity demanded may fall (1) → Less packaging may be discarded (1).\n\n2. Tax encourages use of reusable substitutes (1) → Demand shifts to less harmful materials (1) → External pollution costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. The government receives tax revenue (1) → More funds can support waste collection (1) → Litter and cleanup problems may decrease (1) → Environmental quality may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M06-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to subsidise low-emission public transport could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Subsidies lower public transport operating costs (1) → Fares may fall (1) → Quantity demanded may increase (1).\n\n2. Cheaper services may replace private car journeys (1) → Road congestion and emissions decrease (1) → External costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Improved affordability helps low-income passengers travel (1) → More workplaces become accessible (1) → Geographical mobility of labour may increase (1) → Employment opportunities may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0001","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to create a digital trading platform for local growers would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Buyers and sellers find each other more easily → Search costs decrease → More mutually beneficial exchanges can occur.\n\n2. Growers reach more customers → Demand for their produce may rise → Sales revenue may increase.\n\n3. Buyers can compare competing offers → Price information becomes more widely available → Sellers face greater competitive pressure → Consumers may pay lower prices.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some growers may not have reliable internet access → Excluded growers may lose sales to connected rivals.\n\n2. Platform fees might increase sellers' costs → Higher costs may reduce growers’ profit.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0002","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to use customer demand information to plan bread output. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Bread output matches customers’ preferences → Fewer unwanted loaves remain unsold → Resources are allocated more efficiently.\n\n2. Less bread is thrown away → Ingredient waste decreases → Cost per loaf sold may fall.\n\n3. Information identifies periods of strong demand → The bakery prepares sufficient stock → Fewer sales are lost through shortages → Sales revenue may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Demand could change before new production is ready → Bread may remain unsold despite planning.\n\n2. Past purchasing patterns may not predict future preferences → Resource allocation may remain inefficient.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0003","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.1","topicName":"The role of markets in allocating resources","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to encourage more sellers to enter a local tomato market. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More sellers increase market supply → Equilibrium tomato prices may fall → Consumers can buy more with their income.\n\n2. Existing sellers face more rivals → Firms have incentives to improve efficiency → Unit costs may decrease.\n\n3. Additional sellers may offer different varieties → Consumer choice increases → Buyers can find products closer to their preferences → Consumer satisfaction may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Farmers may face lower prices and profits → Lower farm incomes may discourage production.\n\n2. Entry may remain difficult if transport costs are high → Competition may not increase as expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0004","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to survey local demand before ordering bicycles is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A survey estimates the quantity customers want → Orders better match demand → Unsold bicycle stocks may decrease.\n\n2. A survey identifies preferred bicycle features → Retailers stock more desirable products → Sales revenue may increase.\n\n3. More accurate orders reduce excess inventory → Less capital is tied up in slow-selling stock → Storage and financing costs may fall → Profit may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Survey answers may differ from actual purchases → Retailers may order unwanted bicycles.\n\n2. Unexpected income changes could alter demand → Unsold stocks tie up finance.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0005","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reduce cinema ticket prices on quiet weekdays would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Lower ticket prices cause an extension of demand → More empty seats are filled → Capacity is used more fully.\n\n2. If demand is elastic, attendance rises proportionately more than price falls → Total ticket revenue rises → Profit may rise if extra costs are low.\n\n3. Cheaper tickets improve affordability → Lower-income customers can attend → Access to leisure increases → Non-material living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Revenue from each ticket will be lower → Additional attendance must offset lost revenue per ticket.\n\n2. The increase in ticket sales may be small if demand is inelastic → Total ticket revenue may fall.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0006","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.2","topicName":"Demand","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to build safer cycle routes to encourage bicycle use would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Safer routes make cycling more attractive → Demand for bicycles may increase → Bicycle producers may expand output.\n\n2. Some car journeys are replaced by cycling → Emissions and congestion may decrease → External costs of travel fall.\n\n3. Cycling provides physical exercise → Health may improve → Fewer working days may be lost to illness → Labour productivity may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Cycle routes use public funds and road space → Other public transport projects may be delayed.\n\n2. Weather or safety concerns may still limit cycling → The number of journeys switched from cars may be small.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0007","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to plan output using a detailed supply schedule would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A supply schedule relates output to price → Firms can plan feasible production levels → Unrealistic output targets may be avoided.\n\n2. Production plans show required inputs → Labour and materials can be ordered in advance → Shortages and interruptions may decrease.\n\n3. Matching supply to expected sales reduces excess stock → Storage requirements may fall → Inventory costs may decrease → Profit may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Unpredictable production conditions may make planned supply impossible → Harvested quantities may fall below the planned level.\n\n2. Input prices may change after plans are made → Planned production may no longer be profitable.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0008","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to expand coffee harvesting when the market price rises. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher prices encourage an extension of supply → More coffee can be sold → Total revenue may increase.\n\n2. Extra harvesting requires workers → Seasonal demand for labour rises → Farm workers gain income.\n\n3. Harvesting more coffee reduces unpicked output → Existing crops are used more fully → Output per hectare harvested increases → Cost per unit sold may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Extra harvesting may require costly overtime or less productive resources, raising average costs → Profit may not rise with revenue.\n\n2. Prices might fall before the extra crop reaches buyers → Producers may receive less than expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0009","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.3","topicName":"Supply","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to introduce energy-saving ovens in bakeries. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Efficient ovens use less energy per loaf → Variable cost per loaf falls → Average production cost decreases.\n\n2. Lower costs allow lower bread prices → Consumers’ purchasing power rises → Bread becomes more affordable.\n\n3. Lower energy use reduces fuel consumption → Emissions from energy generation may fall → External environmental costs decrease → Social welfare may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. New ovens require investment → Funds are diverted from other investments.\n\n2. Savings depend on electricity use and machine reliability → Cost reductions may be smaller than forecast.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0010","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to adjust umbrella orders towards the equilibrium quantity is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Ordering the equilibrium quantity reduces excess stock → Fewer umbrellas require storage → Inventory costs may fall.\n\n2. Adequate stocks meet customers’ demand → Fewer customers are turned away → Sales revenue may increase.\n\n3. Better matching purchases to sales reduces tied-up funds → More finance remains available for other activities → Borrowing requirements may fall → Interest costs may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Demand may change with the weather → Stocks may still be too high or too low.\n\n2. The equilibrium estimate may use inaccurate data → Orders may fail to match actual demand.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0011","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to permit prices to respond to a temporary bread shortage would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher prices discourage some purchases → Quantity demanded contracts → The shortage may decrease.\n\n2. A higher price signals profit opportunities → Producers extend supply → More bread becomes available.\n\n3. Higher expected returns attract resources into baking → Firms may invest in capacity → Market supply may increase over time → Bread shortages may become less persistent.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Low-income households may find bread less affordable → Some families may reduce essential food consumption.\n\n2. Production may not expand quickly enough to remove the shortage → High prices may persist despite the price signal.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0012","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.4","topicName":"Price determination","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to discount shoes when inventories are excessive would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A lower shoe price extends demand → More shoes are sold → Excess supply is reduced.\n\n2. Selling old stock frees storage space → Storage costs decrease → Profit may improve.\n\n3. Discounting releases money tied up in inventory → The retailer obtains cash → Suppliers can be paid sooner → Cash-flow difficulties may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Revenue per pair of shoes will decline → Total revenue may fall if sales rise too little.\n\n2. The discount might still fail to clear unpopular designs → Unsold inventories may continue to incur storage costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0013","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to allow temporary hotel prices to rise during peak demand would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher prices cause a contraction in demand → Some bookings move to quieter periods → Excess demand during the peak may decrease.\n\n2. Higher prices raise the return on available rooms → Hotel revenue may increase → Funds for maintenance may rise.\n\n3. Higher profits signal demand for accommodation → Firms may invest in additional rooms → Supply may increase over time → Future peak-season shortages may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Visitors may face higher accommodation costs → Some households may be priced out of travel.\n\n2. Supply may be fixed in the very short run → Price rises may not produce more rooms immediately.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0014","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to support more efficient apple cultivation. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Improved techniques raise apple yields → More apples are produced per hectare → Average costs may decrease.\n\n2. Lower costs shift supply to the right → Equilibrium apple prices may fall → Consumers can afford more fruit.\n\n3. Better harvesting and storage reduce spoilage → A larger share of the crop can be sold → Revenue from each harvest may increase → Farm incomes may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Farmers may receive a lower price per kilogram → Farm revenue may fall if price falls more than output rises.\n\n2. Harvest output depends on weather and storage capacity → Planned supply increases may not occur.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0015","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.5","topicName":"Price changes","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand delivery-kitchen capacity as demand grows. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Extra kitchen capacity increases possible output → More orders can be accepted → Sales revenue may rise.\n\n2. More capacity reduces order delays → Reliability and customer satisfaction may improve → Repeat demand may increase.\n\n3. Expanding production requires additional staff → Demand for kitchen labour rises → Unemployed workers may obtain jobs → Household incomes may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Investment could be wasted if demand growth slows → Unused kitchens may leave high average costs.\n\n2. The direction of price change is uncertain without shift magnitudes → Higher demand and supply need not raise prices.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0016","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to calculate PED before changing gym membership fees is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. PED predicts how quantity responds to a fee change → The gym can estimate total revenue effects → It can avoid a price change that reduces revenue.\n\n2. Elastic demand suggests a fee cut may raise revenue → Membership can increase proportionately more than price falls → Sales revenue may rise.\n\n3. PED reveals customers’ price sensitivity → The gym can choose a more suitable fee → Empty capacity may be used more fully → Average fixed cost per member may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Past responsiveness may not hold after competitors change prices → Quantity changes may differ from the forecast.\n\n2. PED estimates can be unreliable with limited sales data → The fee chosen may reduce revenue.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0017","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to raise fares cautiously when bus demand is inelastic would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Inelastic demand means passenger numbers fall proportionately less than fares rise → Total fare revenue increases → Operators can cover more of their costs.\n\n2. More revenue may finance better vehicles → Reliability and service quality may improve → Passengers may benefit from fewer cancellations.\n\n3. A cautious increase limits the extra cost per journey → Low-income passengers face a smaller affordability loss → More passengers can retain access to work → Employment opportunities are less restricted.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher fares impose a greater burden on regular passengers → Real income available for other purchases falls.\n\n2. Demand may become more elastic over a longer time period → Revenue gains may disappear as passengers find substitutes.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0018","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.6","topicName":"Price elasticity of demand (PED)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to consider substitute availability when pricing products would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Many close substitutes make demand more elastic → A price rise may cause substantial switching → Avoiding a large price rise may protect revenue.\n\n2. Few close substitutes make demand less elastic → A price rise may raise total revenue → The firm can better cover costs.\n\n3. Knowledge of substitutes guides a price cut → Price-sensitive customers may switch to the firm → Sales volume may increase → Spare capacity may be used more fully.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Competitors can introduce new substitutes unexpectedly → Demand may become more elastic than expected.\n\n2. Some buyers lack information about alternative products → Price decisions based on assumed switching may be wrong.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0019","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to invest in flexible production capacity to respond to prices would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Flexible machinery can switch production quickly → Output can respond faster to price increases → Supply becomes more price elastic.\n\n2. Firms can expand output when demand rises → Fewer sales are lost through shortages → Sales revenue may rise.\n\n3. Capacity can be moved away from weak-demand products → Fewer unwanted goods are produced → Waste of inputs decreases → Resource allocation improves.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Spare capacity is costly to maintain → Average costs may rise when capacity stays idle.\n\n2. Specialist inputs may still limit rapid expansion → Supply may remain inelastic despite investment.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0020","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to improve cold storage for a crop with inelastic short-run supply. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Cold storage prevents rapid deterioration → More harvested crops remain saleable → Waste decreases.\n\n2. Stored crops can be released when prices rise → Supply responds more to market conditions → Short-run price fluctuations may be reduced.\n\n3. Less produce must be sold immediately after harvest → Farmers can avoid very low harvest-time prices → Farm revenue may become more stable → Farmers can plan investment more confidently.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Cold storage has high energy and capital costs → Extra storage costs may exceed revenue gains.\n\n2. It does not immediately increase the harvested quantity → It cannot remove a physical crop shortage immediately.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0021","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.7","topicName":"Price elasticity of supply (PES)","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to maintain some flexible spare capacity at a factory. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Spare machinery permits a quick increase in output → The firm can respond to higher prices → Supply is more price elastic.\n\n2. Extra demand can be met without building a new plant → Orders are fulfilled sooner → Revenue may increase.\n\n3. Flexible spare capacity can replace broken equipment → Production interruptions become shorter → Delivery reliability improves → Customer demand may be retained.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Idle machines still require maintenance and space → Fixed costs remain even without production.\n\n2. Low demand may leave spare capacity unused for long periods → Average fixed costs may stay high.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0022","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to allow independent firms to compete in restaurant services is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More restaurants increase competition → Firms may lower prices to attract customers → Consumers gain purchasing power.\n\n2. Rivalry encourages better service and food quality → Restaurants must respond to customer preferences → Consumer satisfaction may improve.\n\n3. Independent entrants offer different menus → Consumer choice broadens → More dietary preferences can be met → Consumer welfare may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some consumers may remain unable to afford restaurant meals → Disadvantaged consumers may still lack access.\n\n2. Pollution and other external costs may be ignored → Social costs may exceed firms’ private costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0023","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to let customer demand guide café menu decisions would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Menus reflect customers’ preferences → More of the food produced is wanted → Allocative efficiency may improve.\n\n2. Popular dishes attract more orders → Sales revenue may rise → Profit may increase.\n\n3. Less unpopular food is prepared → Spoilage and ingredient waste decrease → Variable costs may fall → Resources are released for other uses.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Low-income consumers exert less influence because purchasing power differs → Output may favour richer buyers rather than greatest need.\n\n2. Businesses may promote unhealthy products when consumers underestimate their harm → Consumption may impose costs on consumers or third parties.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0024","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.8","topicName":"Market economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to supplement market housing with targeted public housing would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Public provision adds housing supply → More low-income households can obtain shelter → Absolute poverty may fall.\n\n2. Subsidised rents reduce housing costs → Residents retain more disposable income → Other basic needs become more affordable.\n\n3. Targeting can direct help to underserved households → Fewer resources subsidise households able to pay market rents → More needy households can be assisted from a given budget → Redistribution becomes more effective.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Public housing programmes require tax funding → Other public services may receive less funding.\n\n2. Weak targeting may benefit households that are not most in need → Redistribution may be less effective.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0025","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to finance a public lighthouse through taxation would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Tax funding overcomes the free-rider problem → A lighthouse can be provided despite non-excludability → Ships receive navigation services.\n\n2. Better navigation reduces collision risks → Fewer vessels and cargoes are lost → Resource losses decrease.\n\n3. Safer routes make shipping more reliable → Transport delays and risks may fall → Trading costs may decrease → International trade may expand.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Tax finance has an opportunity cost for other services → Other worthwhile services must be forgone.\n\n2. Operating and maintenance costs may be high → A larger continuing tax burden may be needed.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0026","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to subsidise childhood vaccination services. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A subsidy lowers vaccination costs → Vaccinations become cheaper to consumers → Quantity demanded may increase.\n\n2. More children are protected against disease → Illness and treatment needs may decrease → Healthcare resources are conserved.\n\n3. Vaccination reduces disease transmission to others → Unvaccinated people receive external benefits → Social benefits exceed private benefits → Underconsumption may move towards a socially desirable level.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Subsidies require public funding → Other government projects may be displaced.\n\n2. Poor access or misinformation may still reduce take-up → Consumption may remain below the socially desirable level.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0027","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.9","topicName":"Market failure","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to require a paint factory that pollutes a river to treat its wastewater. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Treatment removes harmful waste before discharge → River pollution falls → External costs imposed on residents decrease.\n\n2. Cleaner water protects fish stocks → Fishers can maintain catches → Their incomes may improve.\n\n3. Cleaner water lowers exposure to pollutants → Illness may decrease → Medical spending and work absences may fall → Social welfare may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Treatment raises operating costs for the factory → Prices may rise or profit may fall.\n\n2. Weak enforcement may make regulation ineffective → Pollution and external costs may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0028","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to combine private retail markets with public health provision is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Private retailers respond to consumer demand → Product choice and availability may improve → Consumer satisfaction may increase.\n\n2. Public healthcare reaches people unable to pay → Access to essential treatment improves → Absolute poverty and ill health may fall.\n\n3. Public provision addresses underconsumption of healthcare → Healthier people transmit fewer illnesses → Third parties receive external benefits → Social welfare may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. State services require sufficient tax revenue → Higher taxes or borrowing may be necessary.\n\n2. Public and private responsibilities may be poorly coordinated → Resources may be duplicated or gaps left unserved.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0029","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to tax single-use plastic packaging would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Tax raises the price of plastic packaging → Quantity demanded may fall → Less packaging may be discarded.\n\n2. Tax encourages use of reusable substitutes → Demand shifts to less harmful materials → External pollution costs may decrease.\n\n3. The government receives tax revenue → More funds can support waste collection → Litter and cleanup problems may decrease → Environmental quality may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Consumers may face higher prices → Households retain less income for other goods.\n\n2. Demand may be too inelastic for usage to fall significantly → Environmental improvements may be small.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C02-P2-M08-0030","c":1,"chapter":"The Allocation of Resources","topic":"2.10","topicName":"Mixed economic system","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to subsidise low-emission public transport would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Subsidies lower public transport operating costs → Fares may fall → Quantity demanded may increase.\n\n2. Cheaper services may replace private car journeys → Road congestion and emissions decrease → External costs may fall.\n\n3. Improved affordability helps low-income passengers travel → More workplaces become accessible → Geographical mobility of labour may increase → Employment opportunities may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The subsidy has a cost to taxpayers → Other public spending may be reduced.\n\n2. Poor oversight may reward inefficient operators → Cost reductions or fare savings may not reach passengers.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes money as a medium of exchange?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry.","Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."],"answer":1,"why":"Money as a medium of exchange: Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Average total cost","Money as a medium of exchange","Secondary-sector firm","Characteristics of money"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates money as a medium of exchange: Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of money as a medium of exchange?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table."],"answer":3,"why":"A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. This illustrates money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to money as a medium of exchange?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods."],"answer":1,"why":"A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $6 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$56","$306","$300","$50"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $6 × 50 = $300."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage reliable digital payment services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges."],"answer":0,"why":"Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage reliable digital payment services?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["World timber prices may make income unstable.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production."],"answer":2,"why":"Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage reliable digital payment services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Payment systems may fail during electricity or network outages.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements."],"answer":0,"why":"Payment systems may fail during electricity or network outages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage reliable digital payment services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","The restaurant may serve more meals.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."],"answer":0,"why":"More transactions may be completed quickly."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":0,"why":"A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes money as a store of value?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.","A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Money as a store of value: Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumer confidence","Money as a store of value","Economies of scale","Commercial bank"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates money as a store of value: Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 70 loaves per day with 7 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["70 loaves","7 loaves","10 loaves","17 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 70 / 7 = 10 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to money as a store of value?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it."],"answer":3,"why":"Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit.","Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply.","More transport can increase congestion and emissions."],"answer":0,"why":"Households may be better prepared for emergencies."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings.","Additional sales could increase total revenue.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level."],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to promote safe and accessible savings facilities?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.","Savings can finance larger future purchases."],"answer":3,"why":"Savings can finance larger future purchases."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to promote safe and accessible savings facilities less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Faster production can allow more sales.","Low-income families may have little income left to save.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","Happier staff may provide more consistent service."],"answer":1,"why":"Low-income families may have little income left to save."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Savers may earn more income from deposits.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":1,"why":"Households may be better prepared for emergencies."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."],"answer":0,"why":"Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 50 items with a total cost of $500. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$10","$500","$15","$60"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $500 / 50 = $10 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Competitive market","Division of labour","Central bank","Average total cost"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates central bank: A central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of central bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores.","A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times."],"answer":2,"why":"A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises. This illustrates a central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to central bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":1,"why":"Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","More competition may lower prices.","Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment.","The restaurant may serve more meals."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries."],"answer":0,"why":"Slower price increases can protect purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices.","Reduced workloads may improve service quality.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses."],"answer":3,"why":"Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Firms may deliver goods more reliably."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $9 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$89","$720","$729","$80"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = $9 × 80 = $720."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0031","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes commercial bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses.","Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Commercial bank: A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0032","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply of labour","Variable cost","Fixed cost","Commercial bank"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates commercial bank: A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0033","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of commercial bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue."],"answer":1,"why":"A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. This illustrates a commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0034","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to commercial bank?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."],"answer":2,"why":"Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0035","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand responsible lending to small businesses. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","Consumers may face less choice or higher prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Investment may support more production and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0036","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand responsible lending to small businesses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Borrowers may default and create bank losses."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowers may default and create bank losses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0037","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand responsible lending to small businesses?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings.","Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns."],"answer":1,"why":"Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0038","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 130 loaves per day with 10 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["23 loaves","130 loaves","13 loaves","10 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 130 / 10 = 13 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0039","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand responsible lending to small businesses. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","Output per employee may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Investment may support more production and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0040","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."],"answer":2,"why":"Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0041","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes characteristics of money?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted.","Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."],"answer":0,"why":"Characteristics of money: Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0042","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Household borrowing","Demand for labour","Characteristics of money","Household saving"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates characteristics of money: Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0043","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of characteristics of money?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise.","A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years."],"answer":0,"why":"Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. This illustrates effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0044","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to characteristics of money?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."],"answer":2,"why":"Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0045","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Expanding requires capital and management capacity.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Unit costs can be high if wages rise."],"answer":1,"why":"Traders may become more confident accepting payment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0046","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 80 items with a total cost of $1,040. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$18","$13","$1,040","$93"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $1,040 / 80 = $13 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0047","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.","Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries."],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0048","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Happier staff may provide more consistent service.","Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","Faster production can allow more sales."],"answer":0,"why":"Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0049","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Savers may earn more income from deposits.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Traders may become more confident accepting payment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0050","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."],"answer":1,"why":"Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0051","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes household saving?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.","Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption.","People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."],"answer":2,"why":"Household saving: Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0052","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Commercial bank","Fixed cost","Capital-intensive production","Household saving"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates household saving: Saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0053","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of household saving?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise."],"answer":3,"why":"A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. This illustrates saving is the part of disposable income not spent on current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0054","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to household saving?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0055","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $12 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$122","$1,320","$1,332","$110"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = $12 × 110 = $1,320."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0056","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The restaurant may serve more meals.","Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":1,"why":"Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0057","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage households to maintain emergency savings?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans."],"answer":3,"why":"Having reserves may reduce reliance on expensive loans."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0058","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage households to maintain emergency savings less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More consistent products may attract buyers.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much."],"answer":3,"why":"Families on low incomes may be unable to save much."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0059","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers."],"answer":2,"why":"Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0060","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0061","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes household borrowing?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing.","Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest.","A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."],"answer":1,"why":"Household borrowing: Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0062","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Monopoly market","Household borrowing","Competitive market","Labour-intensive production"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates household borrowing: Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0063","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 208 loaves per day with 13 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["208 loaves","13 loaves","16 loaves","29 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 208 / 13 = 16 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0064","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to household borrowing?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":2,"why":"Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0065","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","Borrowers may default and create bank losses.","Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit."],"answer":0,"why":"Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0066","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Debt repayments reduce future disposable income.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Firms may deliver goods more reliably.","The firm may meet orders faster."],"answer":0,"why":"Debt repayments reduce future disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0067","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The firm may face insufficient demand for the larger output.","Purchases can support demand for local suppliers.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Extra lamps may be difficult to sell."],"answer":1,"why":"Purchases can support demand for local suppliers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0068","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.","Borrowers may struggle if their income falls."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowers may struggle if their income falls."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0069","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":1,"why":"Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0070","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary."],"answer":1,"why":"Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0071","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 110 items with a total cost of $1,760. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$16","$21","$126","$1,760"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $1,760 / 110 = $16 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0072","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["National minimum wage","Primary-sector firm","Total revenue","Interest rates and households"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates interest rates and households: Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0073","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of interest rates and households?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car.","Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches."],"answer":0,"why":"Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car. This illustrates higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0074","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to interest rates and households?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending."],"answer":3,"why":"More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0075","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Savers may earn more income from deposits.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity."],"answer":0,"why":"Savers may earn more income from deposits."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0076","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Output per employee may rise.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0077","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households may build stronger financial buffers.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.","Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods."],"answer":0,"why":"Households may build stronger financial buffers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0078","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty."],"answer":1,"why":"Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0079","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","More competition may lower prices.","Savers may earn more income from deposits."],"answer":3,"why":"Savers may earn more income from deposits."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0080","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $15 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$765","$750","$65","$50"],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue = $15 × 50 = $750."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0081","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consumer confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A commercial bank accepts deposits, processes payments and provides loans to households and businesses.","Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost.","A central bank oversees monetary conditions and usually issues currency and supports banking-system stability.","Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumer confidence: Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0082","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Economies of scale","Characteristics of money","Money as a store of value","Consumer confidence"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer confidence: Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0083","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies.","Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. This illustrates consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0084","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":3,"why":"Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0085","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Unit costs can be high if wages rise.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."],"answer":3,"why":"Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0086","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."],"answer":0,"why":"Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0087","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line."],"answer":0,"why":"More stable demand can help firms plan production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0088","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 304 loaves per day with 16 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["304 loaves","35 loaves","16 loaves","19 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 304 / 16 = 19 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0089","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","More transactions may be completed quickly.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0090","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Consumers reduce discretionary purchases after news of possible redundancies. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":3,"why":"Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0091","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes non-wage job factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.","Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.","People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."],"answer":2,"why":"Non-wage job factors: People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0092","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Economies of scale","Variable cost","Labour-intensive production","Non-wage job factors"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates non-wage job factors: People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0093","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of non-wage job factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp."],"answer":0,"why":"A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. This illustrates people consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0094","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to non-wage job factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."],"answer":3,"why":"Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0095","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Borrowers may default and create bank losses."],"answer":1,"why":"Better conditions may improve staff retention."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0096","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 140 items with a total cost of $1,120. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$148","$8","$13","$1,120"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $1,120 / 140 = $8 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0097","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","The landlord may refuse to change the agreement.","Happier staff may provide more consistent service."],"answer":3,"why":"Happier staff may provide more consistent service."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0098","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Faster production can allow more sales.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.","Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Households may build stronger financial buffers."],"answer":2,"why":"Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0099","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Savers may earn more income from deposits.","Output per employee may rise.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":2,"why":"Better conditions may improve staff retention."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0100","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":1,"why":"Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0101","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes demand for labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for labour: Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0102","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for labour","Commercial bank","Average total cost","Interest rates and households"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates demand for labour: Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0103","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand for labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches.","A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables."],"answer":1,"why":"A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. This illustrates demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0104","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand for labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0105","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $6 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$80","$86","$480","$486"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $6 × 80 = $480."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0106","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Additional sales could increase total revenue."],"answer":0,"why":"Wage costs rise with the number of staff."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0107","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.","Reduced workloads may improve service quality.","Ingredient prices may rise despite lower waste.","Integration costs may prevent expected savings."],"answer":1,"why":"Reduced workloads may improve service quality."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0108","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Bookings may fall before recruitment costs are recovered.","Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings.","Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness."],"answer":1,"why":"Bookings may fall before recruitment costs are recovered."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0109","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Output per employee may rise.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","The restaurant may serve more meals."],"answer":3,"why":"The restaurant may serve more meals."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0110","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0111","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Supply of labour: The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0112","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consumer confidence","Supply of labour","Secondary-sector firm","Primary-sector firm"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates supply of labour: The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0113","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 63 loaves per day with 7 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["16 loaves","63 loaves","9 loaves","7 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 63 / 7 = 9 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0114","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."],"answer":1,"why":"Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0115","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand training places for nurses. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."],"answer":3,"why":"The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0116","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand training places for nurses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Output per employee may rise.","Training is costly and takes several years."],"answer":3,"why":"Training is costly and takes several years."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0117","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand training places for nurses?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs.","Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","Loans may be unaffordable when interest rates are high."],"answer":2,"why":"Staff shortages and waiting times may decline."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0118","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand training places for nurses less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.","The business may support local craft employment.","Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0119","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand training places for nurses. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","Output per employee may rise.","Investment may support more production and jobs."],"answer":1,"why":"The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0120","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."],"answer":0,"why":"Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0121","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 30 items with a total cost of $330. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$330","$11","$41","$16"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $330 / 30 = $11 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0122","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Central bank","National minimum wage","Supply of labour","Competitive market"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates national minimum wage: A national minimum wage is a legal lower limit on wages paid to covered workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0123","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of national minimum wage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years."],"answer":1,"why":"A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees. This illustrates a national minimum wage is a legal lower limit on wages paid to covered workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0124","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to national minimum wage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":0,"why":"A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0125","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overharvesting may damage forests and biodiversity.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","Producing secure notes may be expensive."],"answer":1,"why":"Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0126","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Savers may earn more income from deposits."],"answer":1,"why":"Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0127","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty.","Automation may reduce some production jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher pay could reduce in-work poverty."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0128","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit."],"answer":3,"why":"Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0129","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."],"answer":3,"why":"Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0130","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $9 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$990","$999","$119","$110"],"answer":0,"why":"Total revenue = $9 × 110 = $990."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0131","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes division of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."],"answer":2,"why":"Division of labour: Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0132","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Commercial bank","Division of labour","Household saving","Total revenue"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates division of labour: Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0133","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of division of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches.","In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue."],"answer":2,"why":"In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. This illustrates division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0134","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to division of labour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending."],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0135","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producing secure notes may be expensive.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Training takes time away from current output."],"answer":1,"why":"Practice may raise labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0136","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":1,"why":"Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0137","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment."],"answer":0,"why":"Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0138","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 120 loaves per day with 10 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["12 loaves","10 loaves","120 loaves","22 loaves"],"answer":0,"why":"Labour productivity = 120 / 10 = 12 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0139","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies."],"answer":1,"why":"Practice may raise labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0140","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0141","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes primary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."],"answer":1,"why":"Primary-sector firm: A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0142","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Diseconomies of scale","Primary-sector firm","Average total cost","Competitive market"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates primary-sector firm: A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0143","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of primary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A worker saves part of a monthly salary to buy furniture next year.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A local hospital struggles to recruit nurses because too few people have the required qualifications.","A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills."],"answer":3,"why":"A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. This illustrates a primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0144","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to primary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."],"answer":2,"why":"The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0145","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Producing secure notes may be expensive.","Unit costs can be high if wages rise.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Initial investment can be very expensive."],"answer":2,"why":"Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0146","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 60 items with a total cost of $840. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$840","$74","$19","$14"],"answer":3,"why":"Average total cost = $840 / 60 = $14 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0147","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Ingredient prices may rise despite lower waste.","Output may be slower than with suitable machinery.","Rural employment may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Rural employment may increase."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0148","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Entrepreneurs may obtain funds unavailable from their own savings."],"answer":1,"why":"World timber prices may make income unstable."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0149","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The restaurant may serve more meals.","Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":3,"why":"Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0150","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":1,"why":"The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0151","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes secondary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."],"answer":1,"why":"Secondary-sector firm: A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0152","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Division of labour","Household saving","Interest rates and households","Secondary-sector firm"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates secondary-sector firm: A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0153","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of secondary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country's central bank changes the policy interest rate after inflation rises.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts.","A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees."],"answer":2,"why":"A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. This illustrates a secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0154","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to secondary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange."],"answer":2,"why":"Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0155","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $12 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$50","$612","$600","$62"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $12 × 50 = $600."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0156","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Additional sales could increase total revenue.","The equipment requires capital expenditure."],"answer":3,"why":"The equipment requires capital expenditure."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0157","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.","Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit.","More consistent products may attract buyers.","Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later."],"answer":2,"why":"More consistent products may attract buyers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0158","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Staff shortages and waiting times may decline.","More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash."],"answer":2,"why":"Automation may reduce some production jobs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0159","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Better conditions may improve staff retention.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Output per employee may rise."],"answer":3,"why":"Output per employee may rise."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0160","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A workshop converts timber into chairs and shelving. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."],"answer":1,"why":"Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0161","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes tertiary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","A horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."],"answer":3,"why":"Tertiary-sector firm: A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0162","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tertiary-sector firm","Characteristics of money","Interest rates and households","Household borrowing"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates tertiary-sector firm: A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0163","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 195 loaves per day with 13 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["13 loaves","195 loaves","28 loaves","15 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 195 / 13 = 15 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0164","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to tertiary-sector firm?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers."],"answer":3,"why":"Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0165","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand local logistics services. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses.","Firms may deliver goods more reliably.","Training takes time away from current output.","Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may deliver goods more reliably."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0166","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand local logistics services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Practice may raise labour productivity.","More transport can increase congestion and emissions.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"More transport can increase congestion and emissions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0167","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand local logistics services?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Borrowers may struggle if their income falls.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Improved distribution can support sales."],"answer":3,"why":"Improved distribution can support sales."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0168","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand local logistics services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Happier staff may provide more consistent service.","Automated systems can improve uniformity.","Faster production can allow more sales.","Fuel-price increases may make services expensive."],"answer":3,"why":"Fuel-price increases may make services expensive."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0169","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand local logistics services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may deliver goods more reliably.","More competition may lower prices.","Additional sales could increase total revenue.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may deliver goods more reliably."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0170","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A logistics company transports manufactured goods to stores. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Non-financial benefits can compensate for some differences in wages."],"answer":1,"why":"Services such as transport and retail help goods reach consumers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0171","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 90 items with a total cost of $1,530. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$107","$17","$22","$1,530"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $1,530 / 90 = $17 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0172","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Derived demand for factors","Diseconomies of scale","Horizontal merger","Average total cost"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates horizontal merger: A horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0173","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of horizontal merger?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","A household increases monthly deposits after receiving a pay rise.","Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts."],"answer":2,"why":"Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations. This illustrates a horizontal merger joins businesses operating at the same stage in the same industry."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0174","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to horizontal merger?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry."],"answer":3,"why":"Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0175","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.","Training takes time away from current output.","Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0176","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","Consumers may face less choice or higher prices.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may face less choice or higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0177","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient."],"answer":3,"why":"Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0178","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fewer damaged notes could reduce replacement costs.","More customers might make later repeat purchases.","Savings can finance larger future purchases.","Integration costs may prevent expected savings."],"answer":3,"why":"Integration costs may prevent expected savings."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0179","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":1,"why":"Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0180","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $15 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$1,200","$95","$1,215","$80"],"answer":0,"why":"Total revenue = $15 × 80 = $1,200."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0181","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes derived demand for factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce.","A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","The supply of labour is the number of workers willing and able to work at different wage rates.","Higher interest rates generally increase rewards for saving and costs of borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Derived demand for factors: Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0182","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Characteristics of money","Division of labour","Derived demand for factors","Household saving"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates derived demand for factors: Demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0183","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of derived demand for factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches.","A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts."],"answer":3,"why":"A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. This illustrates demand for an input depends partly on demand for the final good it helps produce."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0184","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to derived demand for factors?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0185","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Regulation has administrative costs.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","The firm may meet orders faster."],"answer":3,"why":"The firm may meet orders faster."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0186","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0187","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.","Additional installations may raise revenue.","Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit."],"answer":2,"why":"Additional installations may raise revenue."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0188","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 288 loaves per day with 16 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["16 loaves","34 loaves","288 loaves","18 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 288 / 16 = 18 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0189","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Output per employee may rise.","Investment may support more production and jobs.","The firm may meet orders faster."],"answer":3,"why":"The firm may meet orders faster."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0190","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A solar-panel company orders more glass after receiving additional customer contracts. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0191","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes labour-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["People consider conditions, training, security, location and satisfaction as well as pay when choosing occupations.","Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products.","Demand for labour is derived from demand for the goods and services workers help produce."],"answer":1,"why":"Labour-intensive production: Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0192","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive production","Household saving","Primary-sector firm","Non-wage job factors"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates labour-intensive production: Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0193","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of labour-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","Two competing supermarket chains agree to combine their operations.","A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles."],"answer":0,"why":"A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. This illustrates labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0194","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to labour-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A common payment medium makes trade easier because a double coincidence of wants is unnecessary.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."],"answer":3,"why":"Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0195","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to retain skilled handmade production methods. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Borrowers may default and create bank losses.","Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation.","Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings.","Products can be customised for individual buyers."],"answer":3,"why":"Products can be customised for individual buyers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0196","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 120 items with a total cost of $1,080. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$14","$1,080","$129","$9"],"answer":3,"why":"Average total cost = $1,080 / 120 = $9 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0197","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to retain skilled handmade production methods?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.","The business may support local craft employment."],"answer":3,"why":"The business may support local craft employment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0198","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to retain skilled handmade production methods less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Output may be slower than with suitable machinery.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","Reduced workloads may improve service quality."],"answer":1,"why":"Output may be slower than with suitable machinery."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0199","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to retain skilled handmade production methods. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Traders may become more confident accepting payment."],"answer":2,"why":"Products can be customised for individual buyers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0200","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."],"answer":2,"why":"Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0201","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes capital-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","A tertiary-sector firm provides services rather than extracting or manufacturing products."],"answer":1,"why":"Capital-intensive production: Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0202","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Capital-intensive production","Labour productivity","Money as a store of value","National minimum wage"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates capital-intensive production: Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0203","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of capital-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["In a shoe factory, separate teams cut leather, stitch uppers and fit soles.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town."],"answer":2,"why":"A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. This illustrates capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0204","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to capital-intensive production?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Holding money allows purchasing power to be transferred into the future, although inflation may reduce it.","Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption."],"answer":1,"why":"Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0205","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $6 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$110","$116","$666","$660"],"answer":3,"why":"Total revenue = $6 × 110 = $660."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0206","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to automate a high-volume bottling plant. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may obtain useful equipment sooner.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Initial investment can be very expensive.","Output per employee may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Initial investment can be very expensive."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0207","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to automate a high-volume bottling plant?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Automated systems can improve uniformity.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses.","Integration costs may prevent expected savings."],"answer":0,"why":"Automated systems can improve uniformity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0208","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to automate a high-volume bottling plant less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.","Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Breakdowns can stop a large share of production.","More customers might make later repeat purchases."],"answer":2,"why":"Breakdowns can stop a large share of production."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0209","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to automate a high-volume bottling plant. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["High output may lower unit costs.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","More competition may lower prices.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level."],"answer":0,"why":"High output may lower unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0210","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bottling plant uses automated filling and packaging equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Uncertainty can raise precautionary saving and reduce current consumption.","Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."],"answer":3,"why":"Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0211","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes labour productivity?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.","A primary-sector firm extracts or harvests natural resources.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period."],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity: Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0212","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive production","Labour productivity","Central bank","Commercial bank"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates labour productivity: Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0213","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 56 loaves per day with 7 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["7 loaves","8 loaves","15 loaves","56 loaves"],"answer":1,"why":"Labour productivity = 56 / 7 = 8 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0214","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to labour productivity?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Manufacturing adds value by transforming inputs into finished goods.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."],"answer":3,"why":"More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0215","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to train bakers to improve output per hour. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones.","The equipment requires capital expenditure.","Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0216","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to train bakers to improve output per hour. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Training takes time away from current output."],"answer":3,"why":"Training takes time away from current output."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0217","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to train bakers to improve output per hour?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Low-income families may have little income left to save.","Faster production can allow more sales.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses."],"answer":2,"why":"Faster production can allow more sales."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0218","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to train bakers to improve output per hour less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","Slower price increases can protect purchasing power.","More consistent products may attract buyers.","Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0219","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to train bakers to improve output per hour. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Additional sales could increase total revenue."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher productivity may reduce costs per loaf."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0220","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."],"answer":3,"why":"More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0221","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 150 items with a total cost of $1,800. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$1,800","$162","$12","$17"],"answer":2,"why":"Average total cost = $1,800 / 150 = $12 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0222","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand for labour","Secondary-sector firm","Labour-intensive production","Fixed cost"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates fixed cost: A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0223","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of fixed cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A family uses a bank loan to buy a refrigerator rather than waiting two years.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","A forestry business grows and harvests timber for local sawmills.","A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables."],"answer":3,"why":"A workshop pays $2,000 monthly rent whether it produces 100 or 300 tables. This illustrates a fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0224","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to fixed cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised."],"answer":0,"why":"Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0225","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher labour costs increase operating expenses.","The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses.","Producing secure notes may be expensive."],"answer":1,"why":"The business may have lower total costs at every output level."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0226","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Households may be better prepared for emergencies."],"answer":2,"why":"A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0227","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand.","Automation may reduce some production jobs.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower fixed costs may help survival during weak demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0228","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may build stronger financial buffers.","Savings can finance larger future purchases.","Purchases can support demand for local suppliers.","The landlord may refuse to change the agreement."],"answer":3,"why":"The landlord may refuse to change the agreement."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0229","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The business may have lower total costs at every output level.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood."],"answer":0,"why":"The business may have lower total costs at every output level."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0230","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 50 units for $9 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$50","$59","$450","$459"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $9 × 50 = $450."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0231","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes variable cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes.","Money can retain purchasing power for use in later transactions.","Consumer confidence reflects expectations about future income and economic conditions.","A secondary-sector firm processes raw materials or manufactures products."],"answer":0,"why":"Variable cost: A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0232","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive production","Capital-intensive production","Fixed cost","Variable cost"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates variable cost: A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0233","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of variable cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches."],"answer":3,"why":"A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. This illustrates a variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0234","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to variable cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Usable money reduces transaction costs and increases confidence in exchange.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."],"answer":3,"why":"Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0235","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses.","Expanding requires capital and management capacity.","Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time."],"answer":0,"why":"Variable cost per sandwich may fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0236","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","Quality may suffer if portions become too small.","Lower borrowing may reduce excessive total demand.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Quality may suffer if portions become too small."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0237","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."],"answer":3,"why":"The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0238","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 110 loaves per day with 10 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["21 loaves","110 loaves","11 loaves","10 loaves"],"answer":2,"why":"Labour productivity = 110 / 10 = 11 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0239","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income."],"answer":1,"why":"Variable cost per sandwich may fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0240","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A sandwich shop uses more bread and filling when it makes more sandwiches. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry."],"answer":2,"why":"Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0241","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes average total cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A variable cost changes as the quantity of output changes.","Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced.","Money serves as a commonly accepted means of payment that reduces the need for barter.","Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted."],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost: Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0242","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Secondary-sector firm","Tertiary-sector firm","Average total cost","Central bank"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates average total cost: Average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0243","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of average total cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A carpenter pays for groceries with money instead of finding a grocer who wants a table.","A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles.","Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport."],"answer":1,"why":"A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. This illustrates average total cost equals total cost divided by units produced."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0244","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to average total cost?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."],"answer":2,"why":"Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0245","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More transport can increase congestion and emissions.","Producing secure notes may be expensive.","Fixed cost per unit could fall.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."],"answer":2,"why":"Fixed cost per unit could fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0246","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 40 items with a total cost of $600. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$15","$20","$55","$600"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $600 / 40 = $15 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0247","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses.","Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness.","Borrowers may struggle if their income falls."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower unit costs may improve price competitiveness."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0248","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More stable demand can help firms plan production.","Specialised machinery may increase efficiency.","Overlapping distribution networks may become more efficient.","Extra lamps may be difficult to sell."],"answer":3,"why":"Extra lamps may be difficult to sell."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0249","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Savings can help cover unexpected repairs or unemployment.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices.","High output may lower unit costs.","Fixed cost per unit could fall."],"answer":3,"why":"Fixed cost per unit could fall."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0250","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A firm spends $12,000 producing 600 lamps, giving an average cost of $20 per lamp. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","More expensive loans tend to discourage credit-financed spending.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":3,"why":"Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0251","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes total revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","Borrowing enables expenditure now in exchange for future repayments, often including interest."],"answer":1,"why":"Total revenue: Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0252","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Capital-intensive production","Competitive market","Horizontal merger","Total revenue"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates total revenue: Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0253","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of total revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town.","A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","A start-up asks a high-street bank for finance to purchase delivery vehicles."],"answer":0,"why":"A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. This illustrates total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0254","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to total revenue?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold.","Bank lending can move saved funds towards investment and production.","Higher final-product demand raises the need for inputs used in production."],"answer":1,"why":"Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0255","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 80 units for $12 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$972","$92","$960","$80"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $12 × 80 = $960."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0256","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Discounts lower revenue per cake.","High output may lower unit costs.","The restaurant may serve more meals.","Combined purchasing may achieve lower input prices."],"answer":0,"why":"Discounts lower revenue per cake."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0257","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt.","World timber prices may make income unstable.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line.","More customers might make later repeat purchases."],"answer":3,"why":"More customers might make later repeat purchases."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0258","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shorter task changes may reduce wasted time.","Rural employment may increase.","Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.","Automated systems can improve uniformity."],"answer":2,"why":"Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0259","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Additional sales could increase total revenue.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."],"answer":0,"why":"Additional sales could increase total revenue."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0260","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A bakery sells 500 cakes at $4 each, earning $2,000 in sales revenue. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production.","Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."],"answer":3,"why":"Revenue depends on the selling price and the quantity sold."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0261","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes competitive market?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Total revenue is the money received from sales, calculated as price times quantity sold.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice.","A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival.","Division of labour is the splitting of production into specialised tasks performed by different workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Competitive market: A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0262","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Fixed cost","Competitive market","Derived demand for factors","Demand for labour"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates competitive market: A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0263","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 182 loaves per day with 13 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["14 loaves","27 loaves","13 loaves","182 loaves"],"answer":0,"why":"Labour productivity = 182 / 13 = 14 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0264","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to competitive market?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Higher income can increase the ability to save, though spending preferences matter.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Labour-intensive output may suit small production runs and places with relatively low wages."],"answer":2,"why":"Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0265","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Wage costs rise with the number of staff.","More competition may lower prices.","A cheaper location may be less accessible to customers.","Improving facilities can be expensive."],"answer":1,"why":"More competition may lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0266","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Households may obtain useful equipment sooner."],"answer":0,"why":"Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0267","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills.","Fuel-price increases may make services expensive.","Consumers could gain greater variety.","Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Consumers could gain greater variety."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0268","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Improved distribution can support sales.","Some locations may not support many viable businesses.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Some locations may not support many viable businesses."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0269","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More transactions may be completed quickly.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","The firm may meet orders faster.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":3,"why":"More competition may lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0270","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Many independent cafés compete for customers along a busy street. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Monetary decisions influence borrowing costs, spending and price stability.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Larger scale may reduce unit costs but lessen rivalry.","The primary sector supplies raw materials for further production."],"answer":1,"why":"Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0271","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 70 items with a total cost of $490. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$490","$7","$12","$77"],"answer":1,"why":"Average total cost = $490 / 70 = $7 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0272","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Horizontal merger","Monopoly market","Consumer confidence","Household borrowing"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates monopoly market: A monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0273","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of monopoly market?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A graduate accepts a nearby teaching job instead of a higher-paying job with long travel times.","Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","A restaurant recruits additional chefs when bookings rise.","One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town."],"answer":3,"why":"One company is the only provider of piped water to a remote town. This illustrates a monopoly market has one supplier of a good or service with no close rival."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0274","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to monopoly market?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice.","Total variable costs rise when additional units require more materials.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."],"answer":0,"why":"Lack of direct competition may allow higher prices or less choice."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0275","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More transport can increase congestion and emissions.","Different branches may lose consistency.","Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Consumers may face less choice or higher prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0276","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Regulation has administrative costs.","Local sawmills may gain a stable supply of wood.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","Better information may reduce unnecessary panic spending cuts."],"answer":0,"why":"Regulation has administrative costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0277","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to regulate the sole piped-water supplier?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line."],"answer":2,"why":"Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0278","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to regulate the sole piped-water supplier less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Rural employment may increase.","Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment.","Small businesses may reach customers who do not carry cash.","The business may support local craft employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0279","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges.","Practice may raise labour productivity.","Variable cost per sandwich may fall.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow."],"answer":0,"why":"Oversight may protect customers from excessive charges."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0280","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A store sells 110 units for $15 each. Calculate total revenue.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$110","$1,665","$1,650","$125"],"answer":2,"why":"Total revenue = $15 × 110 = $1,650."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0281","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes economies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Labour-intensive methods use a relatively high proportion of labour compared with capital.","A fixed cost does not vary with output over the relevant short-run period.","Labour productivity measures output per worker or per worker-hour over a period.","Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."],"answer":3,"why":"Economies of scale: Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0282","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Economies of scale","Tertiary-sector firm","Diseconomies of scale","Money as a medium of exchange"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates economies of scale: Economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0283","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of economies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output.","A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","Traders prefer identical secure notes to fragile tokens that break during transport.","Following an interest-rate rise, a household postpones replacing its car."],"answer":0,"why":"A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. This illustrates economies of scale occur when a larger scale of production lowers average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0284","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to economies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit.","More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Machines can raise output per worker when production is large and standardised.","Dividing total costs by output identifies cost per unit and supports pricing decisions."],"answer":0,"why":"Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0285","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Debt repayments reduce future disposable income.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit.","Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs.","Higher labour costs increase operating expenses."],"answer":1,"why":"Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0286","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may be better prepared for emergencies.","Some workers may enjoy higher disposable income.","Expanding requires capital and management capacity.","More competition may lower prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Expanding requires capital and management capacity."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0287","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Families on low incomes may be unable to save much.","Specialised machinery may increase efficiency.","Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand.","Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses."],"answer":1,"why":"Specialised machinery may increase efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0288","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A bakery makes 272 loaves per day with 16 workers. What is daily output per worker?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["16 loaves","33 loaves","272 loaves","17 loaves"],"answer":3,"why":"Labour productivity = 272 / 16 = 17 loaves per worker."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0289","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The firm may meet orders faster.","High output may lower unit costs.","Products can be customised for individual buyers.","Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."],"answer":3,"why":"Bulk purchasing may reduce input cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0290","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A food processor negotiates bulk discounts after expanding its output. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers.","Credit brings purchases forward but creates a later repayment obligation.","A higher wage floor can raise some workers' pay but may increase labour costs.","Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."],"answer":3,"why":"Buying larger quantities or using specialised equipment can reduce cost per unit."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0291","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes diseconomies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Effective money is durable, portable, divisible, recognisable and generally accepted.","Capital-intensive production uses a relatively high proportion of machinery and equipment.","Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost.","A competitive market has numerous firms exerting pressure on price, quality and choice."],"answer":2,"why":"Diseconomies of scale: Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0292","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Diseconomies of scale","Consumer confidence","Primary-sector firm","Tertiary-sector firm"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates diseconomies of scale: Diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0293","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of diseconomies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A handmade textile producer hires artisans instead of buying automated weaving machines.","A government increases the legal hourly minimum for retail employees.","A bakery increases loaves per baker-hour after introducing training and better tools.","A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches."],"answer":3,"why":"A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. This illustrates diseconomies of scale occur when an excessively large firm experiences rising average total cost."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0294","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to diseconomies of scale?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Fixed costs must be covered even when output falls.","Repeated specialist tasks can raise speed but may reduce flexibility.","Higher product demand can make hiring more workers profitable.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0295","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","Improving facilities can be expensive.","Regulation has administrative costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Local managers may respond more quickly to customers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0296","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A workshop produces 100 items with a total cost of $1,000. Calculate average total cost per item.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$10","$1,000","$15","$110"],"answer":0,"why":"Average total cost = $1,000 / 100 = $10 per item."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0297","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination.","Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods.","Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns.","Low-income families may have little income left to save."],"answer":0,"why":"Shorter reporting chains could improve coordination."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0298","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers could gain greater variety.","Service standards may improve through enforceable requirements.","Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs.","The shop may improve profitability at unchanged prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0299","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More competition may lower prices.","Local managers may respond more quickly to customers.","The future pool of qualified applicants may grow.","Fixed cost per unit could fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Local managers may respond more quickly to customers."},{"id":"0455-C03-P1-MCQ-0300","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A growing retailer suffers coordination problems between many regional branches. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More output from the same labour input can lower average labour cost.","Limited suitably skilled labour can raise wages or leave vacancies unfilled.","Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands.","Customers can switch sellers, encouraging businesses to improve their offers."],"answer":2,"why":"Poor communication and management can increase cost per unit as size expands."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how money acts as a medium of exchange. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Money is generally accepted in payment (1); Buyers do not need a direct swap / double coincidence of wants (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Money separates buying from selling (1); A seller can spend receipts on a different transaction (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how money acts as a store of value. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Money can be held for later use (1); Purchasing power can be transferred into the future (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Saving money postpones consumption (1); It can be exchanged for goods and services later (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define central bank. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The authority responsible for a country’s monetary policy (1); Provides banking services to government / commercial banks or issues currency (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define commercial bank. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A financial institution accepting deposits (1); Lends to households / firms to earn profit (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Provides banking and payment services to customers (1); Aims to make profit from activities such as lending (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two characteristics of money. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Durability (1)\n• Portability (1)\n• Divisibility (1)\n• Recognisability (1)\n• General acceptability (1)\n• Relative scarcity (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what is meant by household borrowing. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Households obtain funds now that must be repaid (1); Repayment is in the future, usually with interest (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Borrowing brings spending forward (1); Future disposable income is committed to repayments (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how a rise in interest rates may affect household saving and borrowing. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Saving earns a higher return (1); Households may save more (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Borrowing becomes more costly (1); Households may borrow and spend less (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define consumer confidence. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Households’ expectations about future economic conditions (1); Especially expectations about income / employment security (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two non-wage factors that influence a worker’s choice of occupation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Working conditions (1)\n• Job security (1)\n• Training or promotion opportunities (1)\n• Location (1)\n• Job satisfaction (1)\n• Working hours (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain why demand for labour is a derived demand. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Workers are needed to produce goods and services (1); Demand for their labour depends on demand for that output (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Stronger demand for a firm’s product encourages output expansion (1); The firm may demand more workers (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define national minimum wage. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A legally set minimum wage rate (1); Employers cannot lawfully pay covered workers below it (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define division of labour. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Production is divided into separate tasks (1); Each worker concentrates on a particular task / specialises (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define primary-sector firm. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A firm extracting / harvesting natural resources (1); It operates at the first stage of production / obtains raw materials from nature (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define secondary-sector firm. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A firm in manufacturing or construction (1); It processes / transforms raw materials into other products (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define tertiary-sector firm. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A firm supplying services (1); Rather than extracting resources or manufacturing physical goods (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain why demand for a factor of production is a derived demand. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Demand for a factor depends on demand for the final product (1); Firms demand inputs because those inputs help produce saleable output (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Demand for houses increases (1); Builders may demand more construction workers / materials (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define labour-intensive production. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A method using a relatively high proportion of labour (1); Compared with capital / machinery (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define capital-intensive production. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A method using a relatively high proportion of capital (1); Compared with labour / workers (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define labour productivity. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Output produced (1); Per worker / per worker-hour over a period (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define fixed cost. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A cost unchanged when output changes (1); In the short run / over the relevant production range (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define average total cost. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Total cost (1); Divided by quantity of output (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Average fixed cost (1); Plus average variable cost (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define total revenue. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Total receipts from selling output (1); Calculated as price multiplied by quantity sold (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define competitive market. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A market supplied by many firms (1); No individual firm controls the market price / consumers can switch between rival suppliers (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define monopoly market. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A market supplied by a single seller (1); With no close substitutes for its product / protected against entry by potential competitors (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M02-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define economies of scale. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Lower average cost of production (1); As scale of production increases in the long run (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to encourage reliable digital payment services. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Payments can be completed more quickly (1) → Time spent handling cash decreases (1) → Business operating costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Remote payments become easier (1) → Firms can sell to customers farther away (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n3. Reliable transaction records reduce payment disputes (1) → Firms receive funds more predictably (1) → Cash-flow planning improves (1) → Production interruptions from unpaid bills may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to promote safe and accessible savings facilities might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Secure deposits reduce risk of losing savings (1) → More people are willing to use banks (1) → Deposits may rise (1).\n\n2. Nearby savings facilities reduce travel costs (1) → Saving becomes more convenient (1) → Financial access improves (1).\n\n3. Banks obtain more stable deposits (1) → More funds can support productive lending (1) → Firms can finance capital goods (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher interest raises borrowing costs (1) → Households and firms borrow and spend less (1) → Total demand may decrease (1).\n\n2. Higher returns encourage saving (1) → Households postpone consumption (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may fall (1).\n\n3. Higher interest may attract overseas deposits (1) → Demand for the currency increases (1) → Currency appreciation lowers import prices (1) → Imported cost-push inflation may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand responsible lending to small businesses. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Affordable credit finances machinery (1) → Small firms can expand output (1) → Employment may increase (1).\n\n2. Credit allows firms to buy materials before sales receipts arrive (1) → Production can continue through cash-flow gaps (1) → Orders can be completed reliably (1).\n\n3. Responsible assessment reduces unsustainable debt (1) → Fewer borrowers become unable to repay (1) → Bank losses may be lower (1) → Credit services may remain more stable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Secure notes are harder to counterfeit (1) → Fewer fake notes circulate (1) → Confidence in money increases (1).\n\n2. Durable notes need replacing less frequently (1) → Currency production costs decrease (1) → Public resources are saved (1).\n\n3. Greater trust increases general acceptability (1) → Buyers and sellers are more willing to use notes (1) → Exchange becomes easier (1) → Transaction costs may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Savings cover unexpected expenses (1) → Less emergency borrowing is required (1) → Interest costs may be avoided (1).\n\n2. A reserve supports consumption during unemployment (1) → Households can continue buying essentials (1) → Absolute poverty may be less likely (1).\n\n3. Deposits can earn interest (1) → Household wealth can accumulate (1) → Future purchasing power may increase (1) → Long-term material living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Loans spread an appliance’s cost over time (1) → Households can obtain essential equipment sooner (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).\n\n2. Regulated lending may limit exploitative terms (1) → Repayments may be more manageable (1) → Risk of severe household indebtedness may fall (1).\n\n3. Time-saving appliances reduce domestic work (1) → More time becomes available for paid employment (1) → Household earnings may increase (1) → Future ability to meet repayments improves (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher interest increases the reward for saving (1) → Households may deposit more funds (1) → Bank deposits may rise (1).\n\n2. Savers receive more interest income (1) → Their future spending power may increase (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).\n\n3. More deposits increase funds available for lending (1) → Viable firms can obtain investment finance (1) → Purchases of capital goods may rise (1) → Future productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better information reduces uncertainty about future income (1) → Households can plan spending more confidently (1) → Sudden consumption fluctuations may decrease (1).\n\n2. Credible information supports informed borrowing decisions (1) → Households may avoid unaffordable loans (1) → Debt-related financial difficulties may fall (1).\n\n3. Greater confidence can reduce precautionary saving (1) → Consumer expenditure may increase (1) → Firms may expand output to meet demand (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease if spare capacity exists (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better conditions make teaching more attractive (1) → More qualified workers apply (1) → Teacher vacancies may decrease (1).\n\n2. Improved conditions reduce stress and turnover (1) → Schools retain experienced staff (1) → Recruitment costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Better working conditions improve motivation (1) → Teaching quality may increase (1) → Pupils gain more useful skills (1) → Future labour productivity may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More chefs increase kitchen capacity (1) → The restaurant can serve more meals (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Additional staff reduce workloads per chef (1) → Fewer orders may be delayed or mistaken (1) → Customer satisfaction may improve (1).\n\n3. Hiring responds to higher demand for meals (1) → Derived demand for labour increases (1) → Unemployed chefs may obtain jobs (1) → Household wage income may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to expand training places for nurses might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More training places raise the supply of qualified nurses (1) → Healthcare vacancies become easier to fill (1) → Treatment capacity may increase (1).\n\n2. Training improves clinical skills (1) → More patients can be treated per nurse-hour (1) → Cost per successfully treated patient may fall (1).\n\n3. Training allows workers to enter a new occupation (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Skills mismatches may be reduced (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A higher wage floor raises pay for covered low-paid workers (1) → Household disposable income increases (1) → In-work poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Better pay can improve motivation and retention (1) → Absenteeism and staff turnover may decrease (1) → Recruitment and training costs may fall (1).\n\n3. A wider gap between wages and benefits encourages job seeking (1) → Labour-force participation may increase (1) → Vacant posts may be filled (1) → Output may rise if employment demand is maintained (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Repeated tasks make workers more skilled (1) → Output per hour may rise (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Specialisation reduces switching between tasks (1) → Less working time is wasted (1) → Production becomes faster (1).\n\n3. Specialist jobs can use task-specific equipment (1) → Machinery becomes easier to introduce (1) → More shoes can be produced per period (1) → Average costs may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Sustainable harvesting allows forests to regenerate (1) → Timber supplies remain available in future (1) → Long-run productive capacity is protected (1).\n\n2. Forestry firms employ local workers (1) → Rural wage income increases (1) → Rural poverty may decrease (1).\n\n3. Preserved forests support wildlife and absorb carbon (1) → External environmental benefits are maintained (1) → Environmental degradation is reduced (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Faster cutting and assembly equipment reduces task time (1) → More furniture can be produced per worker-hour (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. More precise equipment reduces material waste (1) → Timber cost per saleable item decreases (1) → Average variable costs may fall (1).\n\n3. New equipment can support better designs and finishes (1) → Product quality improves (1) → Demand may increase (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to expand local logistics services might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More logistics capacity reduces delivery delays (1) → Firms receive inputs when needed (1) → Production interruptions decrease (1).\n\n2. Competition between logistics firms may reduce transport charges (1) → Distribution costs fall (1) → Businesses may lower product prices (1).\n\n3. Better distribution links firms to more customers (1) → Market reach expands (1) → Firms may sell more output (1) → Employment may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A larger purchasing volume strengthens bargaining power (1) → Suppliers may offer discounts (1) → Average material costs decrease (1).\n\n2. Shared warehouses and management avoid duplication (1) → Fixed costs are spread across more sales (1) → Average costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Larger combined funds can finance better technology (1) → Distribution becomes more efficient (1) → Costs per item may decrease (1) → Consumers may benefit if savings lower prices (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Skilled installers allow more orders to be completed (1) → The firm can meet growing panel demand (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Skilled workers make fewer installation errors (1) → Rework and repair costs decrease (1) → Profit may increase (1).\n\n3. More installation jobs become available (1) → Demand for skilled labour rises (1) → Unemployed qualified workers gain earnings (1) → Household living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to retain skilled handmade production methods could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Craft skills create distinctive products (1) → The firm can appeal to specialist demand (1) → Higher selling prices may be possible (1).\n\n2. Retaining skilled manual jobs protects employment (1) → Workers keep their earnings (1) → Local consumer spending is supported (1).\n\n3. Handmade methods can adapt to individual orders (1) → Customisation becomes easier (1) → Customers’ specific preferences are better met (1) → Demand may be more secure (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to automate a high-volume bottling plant. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Machines replace repetitive manual filling (1) → Each worker can supervise more bottles per hour (1) → Unit labour costs fall (1).\n\n2. Machines fill bottles more consistently (1) → Fewer ingredients and bottles are wasted (1) → Material cost per saleable bottle falls (1).\n\n3. Large output spreads machinery costs over more units (1) → Average fixed cost decreases (1) → Average total cost may fall (1) → Price competitiveness may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to train bakers to improve output per hour might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training improves baking techniques (1) → More saleable bread is produced per worker-hour (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Better skills reduce mistakes and burnt products (1) → Fewer ingredients are wasted (1) → Unit variable costs fall (1).\n\n3. Multi-skilled bakers can switch between tasks (1) → Staff absences cause fewer bottlenecks (1) → Output becomes more reliable (1) → The bakery can retain customers (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Lower rent reduces total fixed cost (1) → Average fixed cost falls at each output (1) → Average total cost decreases (1).\n\n2. Lower costs increase profit at unchanged revenue (1) → More retained profit is available (1) → Investment may increase (1).\n\n3. Smaller fixed payments reduce required cash outflows (1) → Cash-flow pressure eases (1) → The workshop is less likely to close (1) → Existing employment may be protected (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Less waste means fewer ingredients per sandwich sold (1) → Variable cost per sandwich falls (1) → Profit per sandwich may rise at an unchanged price (1).\n\n2. Lower costs allow a lower selling price (1) → More price-sensitive customers can afford sandwiches (1) → Sales volume may increase (1).\n\n3. Reduced waste conserves food resources (1) → Disposal needs decrease (1) → Waste-management costs may fall (1) → External environmental costs may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More output shares existing rent and other fixed costs (1) → Average fixed cost falls (1) → Profit per unit may rise at unchanged prices (1).\n\n2. Better use of existing capacity raises output (1) → Unit production costs may decrease (1) → The firm may lower prices (1).\n\n3. Lower prices may increase quantity demanded (1) → More output can be sold (1) → Revenue may rise (1) → Profit may increase if extra revenue exceeds extra cost (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Promotion attracts additional customers (1) → Quantity of cakes sold rises (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n2. More sales reduce unsold perishable cakes (1) → Less output is discarded (1) → Cost per cake sold may fall (1).\n\n3. Increased sales use existing spare bakery capacity (1) → Fixed cost is spread over more units (1) → Average fixed cost decreases (1) → Profit may improve if promotion costs are covered (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Easier entry increases the number of cafés (1) → Competition may force prices down (1) → Consumers’ purchasing power rises (1).\n\n2. New cafés offer different products and services (1) → Consumer choice expands (1) → More preferences can be satisfied (1).\n\n3. New businesses require staff (1) → Demand for labour rises (1) → Local unemployment may fall (1) → Household incomes may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to regulate the sole piped-water supplier. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Price regulation can restrict monopoly prices (1) → Water becomes more affordable (1) → Consumers retain more disposable income (1).\n\n2. Service standards can require reliable supply (1) → Fewer interruptions occur (1) → Households and firms face lower disruption costs (1).\n\n3. Efficiency targets discourage avoidable costs (1) → The supplier has incentives to reduce waste (1) → Average costs may fall (1) → Lower costs may support lower prices (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Larger orders can attract bulk discounts (1) → Ingredient cost per unit falls (1) → Average production cost decreases (1).\n\n2. Specialist machinery becomes affordable at a larger scale (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n3. More output shares administrative and marketing overheads (1) → Average fixed overhead cost falls (1) → The firm may charge lower prices (1) → Sales competitiveness may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M04-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Local managers can respond without head-office delay (1) → Decisions are made more quickly (1) → Local sales opportunities are less likely to be lost (1).\n\n2. Smaller decision units shorten communication chains (1) → Messages are less likely to be distorted (1) → Coordination mistakes may decrease (1).\n\n3. Local staff gain greater responsibility (1) → Motivation may improve (1) → Productivity may rise (1) → Labour cost per unit may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to encourage reliable digital payment services. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Payments can be completed more quickly (1) → Time spent handling cash decreases (1) → Business operating costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Remote payments become easier (1) → Firms can sell to customers farther away (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n3. Reliable transaction records reduce payment disputes (1) → Firms receive funds more predictably (1) → Cash-flow planning improves (1) → Production interruptions from unpaid bills may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to promote safe and accessible savings facilities may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Secure deposits reduce risk of losing savings (1) → More people are willing to use banks (1) → Deposits may rise (1).\n\n2. Nearby savings facilities reduce travel costs (1) → Saving becomes more convenient (1) → Financial access improves (1).\n\n3. Banks obtain more stable deposits (1) → More funds can support productive lending (1) → Firms can finance capital goods (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher interest raises borrowing costs (1) → Households and firms borrow and spend less (1) → Total demand may decrease (1).\n\n2. Higher returns encourage saving (1) → Households postpone consumption (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may fall (1).\n\n3. Higher interest may attract overseas deposits (1) → Demand for the currency increases (1) → Currency appreciation lowers import prices (1) → Imported cost-push inflation may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand responsible lending to small businesses. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Affordable credit finances machinery (1) → Small firms can expand output (1) → Employment may increase (1).\n\n2. Credit allows firms to buy materials before sales receipts arrive (1) → Production can continue through cash-flow gaps (1) → Orders can be completed reliably (1).\n\n3. Responsible assessment reduces unsustainable debt (1) → Fewer borrowers become unable to repay (1) → Bank losses may be lower (1) → Credit services may remain more stable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Secure notes are harder to counterfeit (1) → Fewer fake notes circulate (1) → Confidence in money increases (1).\n\n2. Durable notes need replacing less frequently (1) → Currency production costs decrease (1) → Public resources are saved (1).\n\n3. Greater trust increases general acceptability (1) → Buyers and sellers are more willing to use notes (1) → Exchange becomes easier (1) → Transaction costs may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to encourage households to maintain emergency savings. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Savings cover unexpected expenses (1) → Less emergency borrowing is required (1) → Interest costs may be avoided (1).\n\n2. A reserve supports consumption during unemployment (1) → Households can continue buying essentials (1) → Absolute poverty may be less likely (1).\n\n3. Deposits can earn interest (1) → Household wealth can accumulate (1) → Future purchasing power may increase (1) → Long-term material living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Loans spread an appliance’s cost over time (1) → Households can obtain essential equipment sooner (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).\n\n2. Regulated lending may limit exploitative terms (1) → Repayments may be more manageable (1) → Risk of severe household indebtedness may fall (1).\n\n3. Time-saving appliances reduce domestic work (1) → More time becomes available for paid employment (1) → Household earnings may increase (1) → Future ability to meet repayments improves (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher interest increases the reward for saving (1) → Households may deposit more funds (1) → Bank deposits may rise (1).\n\n2. Savers receive more interest income (1) → Their future spending power may increase (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).\n\n3. More deposits increase funds available for lending (1) → Viable firms can obtain investment finance (1) → Purchases of capital goods may rise (1) → Future productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better information reduces uncertainty about future income (1) → Households can plan spending more confidently (1) → Sudden consumption fluctuations may decrease (1).\n\n2. Credible information supports informed borrowing decisions (1) → Households may avoid unaffordable loans (1) → Debt-related financial difficulties may fall (1).\n\n3. Greater confidence can reduce precautionary saving (1) → Consumer expenditure may increase (1) → Firms may expand output to meet demand (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease if spare capacity exists (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better conditions make teaching more attractive (1) → More qualified workers apply (1) → Teacher vacancies may decrease (1).\n\n2. Improved conditions reduce stress and turnover (1) → Schools retain experienced staff (1) → Recruitment costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Better working conditions improve motivation (1) → Teaching quality may increase (1) → Pupils gain more useful skills (1) → Future labour productivity may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More chefs increase kitchen capacity (1) → The restaurant can serve more meals (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Additional staff reduce workloads per chef (1) → Fewer orders may be delayed or mistaken (1) → Customer satisfaction may improve (1).\n\n3. Hiring responds to higher demand for meals (1) → Derived demand for labour increases (1) → Unemployed chefs may obtain jobs (1) → Household wage income may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to expand training places for nurses may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More training places raise the supply of qualified nurses (1) → Healthcare vacancies become easier to fill (1) → Treatment capacity may increase (1).\n\n2. Training improves clinical skills (1) → More patients can be treated per nurse-hour (1) → Cost per successfully treated patient may fall (1).\n\n3. Training allows workers to enter a new occupation (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Skills mismatches may be reduced (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A higher wage floor raises pay for covered low-paid workers (1) → Household disposable income increases (1) → In-work poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Better pay can improve motivation and retention (1) → Absenteeism and staff turnover may decrease (1) → Recruitment and training costs may fall (1).\n\n3. A wider gap between wages and benefits encourages job seeking (1) → Labour-force participation may increase (1) → Vacant posts may be filled (1) → Output may rise if employment demand is maintained (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Repeated tasks make workers more skilled (1) → Output per hour may rise (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Specialisation reduces switching between tasks (1) → Less working time is wasted (1) → Production becomes faster (1).\n\n3. Specialist jobs can use task-specific equipment (1) → Machinery becomes easier to introduce (1) → More shoes can be produced per period (1) → Average costs may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to support sustainable timber harvesting firms could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Sustainable harvesting allows forests to regenerate (1) → Timber supplies remain available in future (1) → Long-run productive capacity is protected (1).\n\n2. Forestry firms employ local workers (1) → Rural wage income increases (1) → Rural poverty may decrease (1).\n\n3. Preserved forests support wildlife and absorb carbon (1) → External environmental benefits are maintained (1) → Environmental degradation is reduced (1) → Non-material living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Faster cutting and assembly equipment reduces task time (1) → More furniture can be produced per worker-hour (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. More precise equipment reduces material waste (1) → Timber cost per saleable item decreases (1) → Average variable costs may fall (1).\n\n3. New equipment can support better designs and finishes (1) → Product quality improves (1) → Demand may increase (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to expand local logistics services may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More logistics capacity reduces delivery delays (1) → Firms receive inputs when needed (1) → Production interruptions decrease (1).\n\n2. Competition between logistics firms may reduce transport charges (1) → Distribution costs fall (1) → Businesses may lower product prices (1).\n\n3. Better distribution links firms to more customers (1) → Market reach expands (1) → Firms may sell more output (1) → Employment may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A larger purchasing volume strengthens bargaining power (1) → Suppliers may offer discounts (1) → Average material costs decrease (1).\n\n2. Shared warehouses and management avoid duplication (1) → Fixed costs are spread across more sales (1) → Average costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Larger combined funds can finance better technology (1) → Distribution becomes more efficient (1) → Costs per item may decrease (1) → Consumers may benefit if savings lower prices (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Skilled installers allow more orders to be completed (1) → The firm can meet growing panel demand (1) → Sales revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Skilled workers make fewer installation errors (1) → Rework and repair costs decrease (1) → Profit may increase (1).\n\n3. More installation jobs become available (1) → Demand for skilled labour rises (1) → Unemployed qualified workers gain earnings (1) → Household living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to retain skilled handmade production methods could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Craft skills create distinctive products (1) → The firm can appeal to specialist demand (1) → Higher selling prices may be possible (1).\n\n2. Retaining skilled manual jobs protects employment (1) → Workers keep their earnings (1) → Local consumer spending is supported (1).\n\n3. Handmade methods can adapt to individual orders (1) → Customisation becomes easier (1) → Customers’ specific preferences are better met (1) → Demand may be more secure (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to automate a high-volume bottling plant. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Machines replace repetitive manual filling (1) → Each worker can supervise more bottles per hour (1) → Unit labour costs fall (1).\n\n2. Machines fill bottles more consistently (1) → Fewer ingredients and bottles are wasted (1) → Material cost per saleable bottle falls (1).\n\n3. Large output spreads machinery costs over more units (1) → Average fixed cost decreases (1) → Average total cost may fall (1) → Price competitiveness may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to train bakers to improve output per hour may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training improves baking techniques (1) → More saleable bread is produced per worker-hour (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Better skills reduce mistakes and burnt products (1) → Fewer ingredients are wasted (1) → Unit variable costs fall (1).\n\n3. Multi-skilled bakers can switch between tasks (1) → Staff absences cause fewer bottlenecks (1) → Output becomes more reliable (1) → The bakery can retain customers (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Lower rent reduces total fixed cost (1) → Average fixed cost falls at each output (1) → Average total cost decreases (1).\n\n2. Lower costs increase profit at unchanged revenue (1) → More retained profit is available (1) → Investment may increase (1).\n\n3. Smaller fixed payments reduce required cash outflows (1) → Cash-flow pressure eases (1) → The workshop is less likely to close (1) → Existing employment may be protected (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Less waste means fewer ingredients per sandwich sold (1) → Variable cost per sandwich falls (1) → Profit per sandwich may rise at an unchanged price (1).\n\n2. Lower costs allow a lower selling price (1) → More price-sensitive customers can afford sandwiches (1) → Sales volume may increase (1).\n\n3. Reduced waste conserves food resources (1) → Disposal needs decrease (1) → Waste-management costs may fall (1) → External environmental costs may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More output shares existing rent and other fixed costs (1) → Average fixed cost falls (1) → Profit per unit may rise at unchanged prices (1).\n\n2. Better use of existing capacity raises output (1) → Unit production costs may decrease (1) → The firm may lower prices (1).\n\n3. Lower prices may increase quantity demanded (1) → More output can be sold (1) → Revenue may rise (1) → Profit may increase if extra revenue exceeds extra cost (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Promotion attracts additional customers (1) → Quantity of cakes sold rises (1) → Sales revenue may increase (1).\n\n2. More sales reduce unsold perishable cakes (1) → Less output is discarded (1) → Cost per cake sold may fall (1).\n\n3. Increased sales use existing spare bakery capacity (1) → Fixed cost is spread over more units (1) → Average fixed cost decreases (1) → Profit may improve if promotion costs are covered (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Easier entry increases the number of cafés (1) → Competition may force prices down (1) → Consumers’ purchasing power rises (1).\n\n2. New cafés offer different products and services (1) → Consumer choice expands (1) → More preferences can be satisfied (1).\n\n3. New businesses require staff (1) → Demand for labour rises (1) → Local unemployment may fall (1) → Household incomes may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to regulate the sole piped-water supplier could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Price regulation can restrict monopoly prices (1) → Water becomes more affordable (1) → Consumers retain more disposable income (1).\n\n2. Service standards can require reliable supply (1) → Fewer interruptions occur (1) → Households and firms face lower disruption costs (1).\n\n3. Efficiency targets discourage avoidable costs (1) → The supplier has incentives to reduce waste (1) → Average costs may fall (1) → Lower costs may support lower prices (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Larger orders can attract bulk discounts (1) → Ingredient cost per unit falls (1) → Average production cost decreases (1).\n\n2. Specialist machinery becomes affordable at a larger scale (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n3. More output shares administrative and marketing overheads (1) → Average fixed overhead cost falls (1) → The firm may charge lower prices (1) → Sales competitiveness may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M06-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Local managers can respond without head-office delay (1) → Decisions are made more quickly (1) → Local sales opportunities are less likely to be lost (1).\n\n2. Smaller decision units shorten communication chains (1) → Messages are less likely to be distorted (1) → Coordination mistakes may decrease (1).\n\n3. Local staff gain greater responsibility (1) → Motivation may improve (1) → Productivity may rise (1) → Labour cost per unit may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0001","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to encourage reliable digital payment services would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Payments can be completed more quickly → Time spent handling cash decreases → Business operating costs may fall.\n\n2. Remote payments become easier → Firms can sell to customers farther away → Sales revenue may rise.\n\n3. Reliable transaction records reduce payment disputes → Firms receive funds more predictably → Cash-flow planning improves → Production interruptions from unpaid bills may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Digital exclusion may affect people without smartphones → Some consumers cannot use the service.\n\n2. Payment systems may fail during electricity or network outages → Shops may lose sales when payments stop.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0002","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to promote safe and accessible savings facilities. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Secure deposits reduce risk of losing savings → More people are willing to use banks → Deposits may rise.\n\n2. Nearby savings facilities reduce travel costs → Saving becomes more convenient → Financial access improves.\n\n3. Banks obtain more stable deposits → More funds can support productive lending → Firms can finance capital goods → Productive capacity may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Inflation may reduce the real value of cash savings → Real purchasing power of deposits may fall.\n\n2. Low-income families may have little income left to save → Access alone may not increase saving.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0003","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to raise the policy interest rate to contain high inflation. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher interest raises borrowing costs → Households and firms borrow and spend less → Total demand may decrease.\n\n2. Higher returns encourage saving → Households postpone consumption → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may fall.\n\n3. Higher interest may attract overseas deposits → Demand for the currency increases → Currency appreciation lowers import prices → Imported cost-push inflation may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher borrowing costs can discourage investment → Firms buy fewer capital goods.\n\n2. Policy effects may take time and depend on borrower responses → Inflation may remain high in the short run.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0004","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to expand responsible lending to small businesses is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Affordable credit finances machinery → Small firms can expand output → Employment may increase.\n\n2. Credit allows firms to buy materials before sales receipts arrive → Production can continue through cash-flow gaps → Orders can be completed reliably.\n\n3. Responsible assessment reduces unsustainable debt → Fewer borrowers become unable to repay → Bank losses may be lower → Credit services may remain more stable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Borrowers may default and create bank losses → Losses reduce funds available for future lending.\n\n2. Loans may be unaffordable when interest rates are high → Debt service may force cuts in other business spending.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0005","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.1","topicName":"Money and banking","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to replace easily counterfeited notes with secure durable currency would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Secure notes are harder to counterfeit → Fewer fake notes circulate → Confidence in money increases.\n\n2. Durable notes need replacing less frequently → Currency production costs decrease → Public resources are saved.\n\n3. Greater trust increases general acceptability → Buyers and sellers are more willing to use notes → Exchange becomes easier → Transaction costs may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Producing secure notes may be expensive → Public funds have fewer alternative uses.\n\n2. Counterfeiters may eventually develop new methods → Further replacement costs may arise.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0006","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to encourage households to maintain emergency savings would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Savings cover unexpected expenses → Less emergency borrowing is required → Interest costs may be avoided.\n\n2. A reserve supports consumption during unemployment → Households can continue buying essentials → Absolute poverty may be less likely.\n\n3. Deposits can earn interest → Household wealth can accumulate → Future purchasing power may increase → Long-term material living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Less spending may reduce current demand for local businesses → Firms may reduce output and employment.\n\n2. Families on low incomes may be unable to save much → Emergency funds may remain inadequate.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0007","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to offer affordable regulated loans for essential appliances would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Loans spread an appliance’s cost over time → Households can obtain essential equipment sooner → Material living standards may improve.\n\n2. Regulated lending may limit exploitative terms → Repayments may be more manageable → Risk of severe household indebtedness may fall.\n\n3. Time-saving appliances reduce domestic work → More time becomes available for paid employment → Household earnings may increase → Future ability to meet repayments improves.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Debt repayments reduce future disposable income → Less income remains for later consumption.\n\n2. Borrowers may struggle if their income falls → Default may lead to financial distress.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0008","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to raise savings interest to encourage household deposits. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher interest increases the reward for saving → Households may deposit more funds → Bank deposits may rise.\n\n2. Savers receive more interest income → Their future spending power may increase → Material living standards may improve.\n\n3. More deposits increase funds available for lending → Viable firms can obtain investment finance → Purchases of capital goods may rise → Future productive capacity may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Borrowers may face higher repayments at the same time → Borrowers have less disposable income for other purchases.\n\n2. Households may prefer current spending despite higher returns → Bank deposits may rise less than expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0009","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.2","topicName":"Households","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to provide credible information to improve households' economic confidence. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better information reduces uncertainty about future income → Households can plan spending more confidently → Sudden consumption fluctuations may decrease.\n\n2. Credible information supports informed borrowing decisions → Households may avoid unaffordable loans → Debt-related financial difficulties may fall.\n\n3. Greater confidence can reduce precautionary saving → Consumer expenditure may increase → Firms may expand output to meet demand → Cyclical unemployment may decrease if spare capacity exists.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Optimism cannot prevent genuine job losses → Household income may still fall in a recession.\n\n2. Excessive confidence could encourage unsustainable debt → Repayments can become unaffordable.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0010","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to improve working conditions to attract skilled teachers is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better conditions make teaching more attractive → More qualified workers apply → Teacher vacancies may decrease.\n\n2. Improved conditions reduce stress and turnover → Schools retain experienced staff → Recruitment costs may fall.\n\n3. Better working conditions improve motivation → Teaching quality may increase → Pupils gain more useful skills → Future labour productivity may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Improving facilities can be expensive → Other school spending may be crowded out.\n\n2. Low pay may still push qualified teachers elsewhere → Staff shortages may continue.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0011","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to hire additional chefs during sustained demand growth would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More chefs increase kitchen capacity → The restaurant can serve more meals → Sales revenue may rise.\n\n2. Additional staff reduce workloads per chef → Fewer orders may be delayed or mistaken → Customer satisfaction may improve.\n\n3. Hiring responds to higher demand for meals → Derived demand for labour increases → Unemployed chefs may obtain jobs → Household wage income may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Wage costs rise with the number of staff → Profit falls unless extra sales cover the wages.\n\n2. Bookings may fall before recruitment costs are recovered → Excess staffing may raise average costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0012","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to expand training places for nurses would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More training places raise the supply of qualified nurses → Healthcare vacancies become easier to fill → Treatment capacity may increase.\n\n2. Training improves clinical skills → More patients can be treated per nurse-hour → Cost per successfully treated patient may fall.\n\n3. Training allows workers to enter a new occupation → Occupational mobility increases → Skills mismatches may be reduced → Structural unemployment may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training is costly and takes several years → Current services lose funding and benefits are delayed.\n\n2. Graduates may migrate to higher-paying countries → Domestic nursing shortages may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0013","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to increase the national minimum wage for low-paid workers would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A higher wage floor raises pay for covered low-paid workers → Household disposable income increases → In-work poverty may fall.\n\n2. Better pay can improve motivation and retention → Absenteeism and staff turnover may decrease → Recruitment and training costs may fall.\n\n3. A wider gap between wages and benefits encourages job seeking → Labour-force participation may increase → Vacant posts may be filled → Output may rise if employment demand is maintained.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Employers may reduce hiring if labour costs rise sharply → Unemployment may increase.\n\n2. Some workers already earning more than the minimum may not benefit → Poverty among excluded workers may remain unchanged.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0014","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.3","topicName":"Workers","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to increase task specialisation in a shoe factory. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Repeated tasks make workers more skilled → Output per hour may rise → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. Specialisation reduces switching between tasks → Less working time is wasted → Production becomes faster.\n\n3. Specialist jobs can use task-specific equipment → Machinery becomes easier to introduce → More shoes can be produced per period → Average costs may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Repetitive work may reduce staff motivation → Absenteeism or turnover may increase.\n\n2. A missing specialist can interrupt the whole production line → Other workers and machines may be left idle.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0015","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to support sustainable timber harvesting firms. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Sustainable harvesting allows forests to regenerate → Timber supplies remain available in future → Long-run productive capacity is protected.\n\n2. Forestry firms employ local workers → Rural wage income increases → Rural poverty may decrease.\n\n3. Preserved forests support wildlife and absorb carbon → External environmental benefits are maintained → Environmental degradation is reduced → Non-material living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Weakly enforced sustainability rules may still permit overharvesting → Future timber supplies may decrease.\n\n2. World timber prices may make income unstable → Firms and workers face unpredictable earnings.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0016","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to upgrade machinery at a furniture manufacturer is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Faster cutting and assembly equipment reduces task time → More furniture can be produced per worker-hour → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. More precise equipment reduces material waste → Timber cost per saleable item decreases → Average variable costs may fall.\n\n3. New equipment can support better designs and finishes → Product quality improves → Demand may increase → Sales revenue may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The equipment requires capital expenditure → Less money remains for other investments.\n\n2. Automation may reduce some production jobs → Displaced workers may lose their incomes.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0017","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand local logistics services would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More logistics capacity reduces delivery delays → Firms receive inputs when needed → Production interruptions decrease.\n\n2. Competition between logistics firms may reduce transport charges → Distribution costs fall → Businesses may lower product prices.\n\n3. Better distribution links firms to more customers → Market reach expands → Firms may sell more output → Employment may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. More transport can increase congestion and emissions → External costs can reduce social welfare.\n\n2. Fuel-price increases may make services expensive → Higher charges may reduce demand.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0018","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to permit two regional supermarket chains to merge would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A larger purchasing volume strengthens bargaining power → Suppliers may offer discounts → Average material costs decrease.\n\n2. Shared warehouses and management avoid duplication → Fixed costs are spread across more sales → Average costs may fall.\n\n3. Larger combined funds can finance better technology → Distribution becomes more efficient → Costs per item may decrease → Consumers may benefit if savings lower prices.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Consumers may face less choice or higher prices → Greater monopoly power may reduce consumer welfare.\n\n2. Integration costs may prevent expected savings → Merger expenditure may outweigh scale savings.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0019","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to hire more skilled installers when solar-panel orders rise would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Skilled installers allow more orders to be completed → The firm can meet growing panel demand → Sales revenue may rise.\n\n2. Skilled workers make fewer installation errors → Rework and repair costs decrease → Profit may increase.\n\n3. More installation jobs become available → Demand for skilled labour rises → Unemployed qualified workers gain earnings → Household living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher labour costs increase operating expenses → Profit may fall if extra revenue is insufficient.\n\n2. Orders may be temporary, leaving excess staff later → Wages must still be paid when demand falls.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0020","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to retain skilled handmade production methods. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Craft skills create distinctive products → The firm can appeal to specialist demand → Higher selling prices may be possible.\n\n2. Retaining skilled manual jobs protects employment → Workers keep their earnings → Local consumer spending is supported.\n\n3. Handmade methods can adapt to individual orders → Customisation becomes easier → Customers’ specific preferences are better met → Demand may be more secure.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Unit costs can be high if wages rise → High prices may reduce demand.\n\n2. Output may be slower than with suitable machinery → Firms may be unable to fulfil large orders.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0021","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to automate a high-volume bottling plant. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Machines replace repetitive manual filling → Each worker can supervise more bottles per hour → Unit labour costs fall.\n\n2. Machines fill bottles more consistently → Fewer ingredients and bottles are wasted → Material cost per saleable bottle falls.\n\n3. Large output spreads machinery costs over more units → Average fixed cost decreases → Average total cost may fall → Price competitiveness may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Initial investment can be very expensive → Finance may be diverted from other projects.\n\n2. Breakdowns can stop a large share of production → Output and sales revenue can fall sharply.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0022","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.5","topicName":"Firms and production","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to train bakers to improve output per hour is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training improves baking techniques → More saleable bread is produced per worker-hour → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. Better skills reduce mistakes and burnt products → Fewer ingredients are wasted → Unit variable costs fall.\n\n3. Multi-skilled bakers can switch between tasks → Staff absences cause fewer bottlenecks → Output becomes more reliable → The bakery can retain customers.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training takes time away from current output → Current sales or wages may be lost.\n\n2. Poor motivation may limit gains from new skills → Productivity may rise less than expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0023","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to negotiate a lower fixed rent for a workshop would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Lower rent reduces total fixed cost → Average fixed cost falls at each output → Average total cost decreases.\n\n2. Lower costs increase profit at unchanged revenue → More retained profit is available → Investment may increase.\n\n3. Smaller fixed payments reduce required cash outflows → Cash-flow pressure eases → The workshop is less likely to close → Existing employment may be protected.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The landlord may require a longer lease in return for lower rent → The firm may be locked into payments when demand weakens.\n\n2. The landlord may refuse to change the agreement → Fixed costs may remain unchanged.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0024","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to reduce ingredient waste per sandwich would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Less waste means fewer ingredients per sandwich sold → Variable cost per sandwich falls → Profit per sandwich may rise at an unchanged price.\n\n2. Lower costs allow a lower selling price → More price-sensitive customers can afford sandwiches → Sales volume may increase.\n\n3. Reduced waste conserves food resources → Disposal needs decrease → Waste-management costs may fall → External environmental costs may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Attempts to cut ingredients rather than genuine waste may reduce product quality → Consumers may switch to other sellers.\n\n2. Ingredient prices may rise despite lower waste → Unit costs may not fall overall.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0025","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to increase efficient output to spread fixed costs would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More output shares existing rent and other fixed costs → Average fixed cost falls → Profit per unit may rise at unchanged prices.\n\n2. Better use of existing capacity raises output → Unit production costs may decrease → The firm may lower prices.\n\n3. Lower prices may increase quantity demanded → More output can be sold → Revenue may rise → Profit may increase if extra revenue exceeds extra cost.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Output beyond efficient capacity may raise costs → Congestion or breakdowns may raise average costs.\n\n2. Additional output may be difficult to sell → Unsold output ties up funds and storage space.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0026","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.6","topicName":"Firms’ costs, revenue and objectives","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to offer a targeted promotion to increase cake sales. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Promotion attracts additional customers → Quantity of cakes sold rises → Sales revenue may increase.\n\n2. More sales reduce unsold perishable cakes → Less output is discarded → Cost per cake sold may fall.\n\n3. Increased sales use existing spare bakery capacity → Fixed cost is spread over more units → Average fixed cost decreases → Profit may improve if promotion costs are covered.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Discounts lower revenue per cake → Extra volume must offset the lower selling price.\n\n2. Production may be unable to meet a large increase in demand → Delays may damage customer satisfaction.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0027","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to reduce entry barriers for independent cafés. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Easier entry increases the number of cafés → Competition may force prices down → Consumers’ purchasing power rises.\n\n2. New cafés offer different products and services → Consumer choice expands → More preferences can be satisfied.\n\n3. New businesses require staff → Demand for labour rises → Local unemployment may fall → Household incomes may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Small firms may struggle to earn enough profit → Some firms may fail and workers lose jobs.\n\n2. Some locations may not support many viable businesses → Excess capacity can raise unit costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0028","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.7","topicName":"Types of markets","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to regulate the sole piped-water supplier is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Price regulation can restrict monopoly prices → Water becomes more affordable → Consumers retain more disposable income.\n\n2. Service standards can require reliable supply → Fewer interruptions occur → Households and firms face lower disruption costs.\n\n3. Efficiency targets discourage avoidable costs → The supplier has incentives to reduce waste → Average costs may fall → Lower costs may support lower prices.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Regulation has administrative costs → Taxpayers or customers must finance supervision.\n\n2. Price controls set too low may discourage maintenance investment → Service quality may deteriorate over time.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0029","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand a food-processing firm to gain scale economies would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Larger orders can attract bulk discounts → Ingredient cost per unit falls → Average production cost decreases.\n\n2. Specialist machinery becomes affordable at a larger scale → Output per worker rises → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n3. More output shares administrative and marketing overheads → Average fixed overhead cost falls → The firm may charge lower prices → Sales competitiveness may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Expanding requires capital and management capacity → Borrowing and coordination costs may rise.\n\n2. The firm may face insufficient demand for the larger output → Average costs may rise if equipment is underused.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C03-P2-M08-0030","c":2,"chapter":"Microeconomic Decision-makers","topic":"3.4","topicName":"Firms","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to decentralise decisions in a very large retailer would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Local managers can respond without head-office delay → Decisions are made more quickly → Local sales opportunities are less likely to be lost.\n\n2. Smaller decision units shorten communication chains → Messages are less likely to be distorted → Coordination mistakes may decrease.\n\n3. Local staff gain greater responsibility → Motivation may improve → Productivity may rise → Labour cost per unit may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Different branches may lose consistency → Service quality may differ between stores.\n\n2. Duplicate teams can increase administrative costs → Expected savings may be reduced.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes macroeconomic aims?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."],"answer":3,"why":"Macroeconomic aims: Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Deregulation","Macroeconomic aims","Cost-push inflation","Money supply"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates macroeconomic aims: Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of macroeconomic aims?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":2,"why":"A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. This illustrates macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to macroeconomic aims?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $53 billion and spending is $58 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$111 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus","$5 billion deficit","$58 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $58 − $53 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Investment demand and employment may rise.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.","Higher output may create jobs.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."],"answer":1,"why":"Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises."],"answer":3,"why":"Voters can assess whether economic outcomes match promises."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration.","Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Investment demand and employment may rise.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":1,"why":"Businesses may make plans with more confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another."],"answer":3,"why":"Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes growth versus environmental sustainability?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions.","The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services."],"answer":1,"why":"Growth versus environmental sustainability: Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Demand-pull inflation","Growth versus environmental sustainability","Education and training policy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates growth versus environmental sustainability: Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $300 billion to $312 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["6%","12%","104%","4%"],"answer":3,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($312 − $300) / $300 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to growth versus environmental sustainability?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."],"answer":3,"why":"More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Training may take years.","Maintaining matching services requires funding.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution."],"answer":2,"why":"Output can expand with less pollution per unit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":2,"why":"Renewable installations require upfront funding."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.","Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand.","Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities."],"answer":3,"why":"Cleaner technology may create new employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence.","Firms can fill open posts more quickly."],"answer":0,"why":"Intermittent power generation may require storage investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Firms may enter markets more easily.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit."],"answer":3,"why":"Output can expand with less pollution per unit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased."],"answer":0,"why":"More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 100 to 105.0. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["105.0%","5%","8%","105%"],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation rate = (105.0 − 100) / 100 × 100 = 5%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Inflation","Government budget surplus","Expansionary fiscal policy","Employment versus inflation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates employment versus inflation: Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of employment versus inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity."],"answer":3,"why":"A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity. This illustrates policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to employment versus inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."],"answer":1,"why":"Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Tax revenue may fall.","Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money.","Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":3,"why":"Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Construction jobs may be created.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."],"answer":1,"why":"Training takes time to improve employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Productive capacity could expand over time.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Productive capacity could expand over time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses.","Better roads can support future private investment.","Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","After-tax income inequality may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":0,"why":"Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 80,000 and 4,800 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["94%","0.06%","6%","8%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (4,800 / 80,000) × 100 = 6%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0031","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes redistribution of income?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."],"answer":0,"why":"Redistribution of income: Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0032","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Frictional unemployment","Structural unemployment","Infrastructure investment","Redistribution of income"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates redistribution of income: Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0033","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of redistribution of income?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens.","A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation."],"answer":3,"why":"A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. This illustrates redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0034","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to redistribution of income?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":0,"why":"Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0035","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More families may afford essential goods.","Inflation may rise if demand grows too quickly.","Lower government spending may weaken current demand.","Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest."],"answer":0,"why":"More families may afford essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0036","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output may create jobs.","Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners.","Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Health damage from emissions may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0037","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Greater equality may improve social outcomes.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."],"answer":2,"why":"Greater equality may improve social outcomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0038","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $65 billion and spending is $78 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$143 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus","$78 billion deficit","$13 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $78 − $65 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0039","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","More families may afford essential goods.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks."],"answer":1,"why":"More families may afford essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0040","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."],"answer":1,"why":"Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0041","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes government budget deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure.","Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time.","A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."],"answer":2,"why":"Government budget deficit: A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0042","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Consequences of growth","Government budget deficit","Cyclical unemployment","Deregulation"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates government budget deficit: A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0043","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of government budget deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":3,"why":"A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. This illustrates a budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0044","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to government budget deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited."],"answer":2,"why":"A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0045","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Very tight policy may lower employment.","Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets."],"answer":2,"why":"Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0046","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $700 billion to $756 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["10%","56%","8%","108%"],"answer":2,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($756 − $700) / $700 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0047","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.","Better roads can support future private investment."],"answer":3,"why":"Better roads can support future private investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0048","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher spending may increase production and employment.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0049","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."],"answer":3,"why":"Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0050","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":3,"why":"A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0051","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes government budget surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions."],"answer":0,"why":"Government budget surplus: A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0052","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Employment versus inflation","Government budget surplus","Recession"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates government budget surplus: A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0053","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of government budget surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion."],"answer":3,"why":"A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. This illustrates a budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0054","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to government budget surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."],"answer":3,"why":"A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0055","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 110 to 119.9. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["109%","119.9%","12%","9%"],"answer":3,"why":"Inflation rate = (119.9 − 110) / 110 × 100 = 9%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0056","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Lower government spending may weaken current demand.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower government spending may weaken current demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0057","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve.","Benefits may take years to appear.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."],"answer":0,"why":"Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0058","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms can fill open posts more quickly.","Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve."],"answer":1,"why":"Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0059","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Future interest payments may decline.","Businesses may retain more workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Future interest payments may decline."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0060","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt.","Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."],"answer":2,"why":"A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0061","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes progressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Progressive taxation: A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0062","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recession","Progressive taxation","Inflation","Cyclical unemployment"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates progressive taxation: A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0063","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 120,000 and 12,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["90%","0.1%","10%","12%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (12,000 / 120,000) × 100 = 10%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0064","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to progressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0065","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Exports may become more expensive abroad.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Projects need substantial funds up front."],"answer":1,"why":"Revenue may finance public health and education."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0066","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Inflationary pressure may ease."],"answer":0,"why":"Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0067","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","After-tax income inequality may fall.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand."],"answer":1,"why":"After-tax income inequality may fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0068","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Productive capacity could expand over time.","Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence.","Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue."],"answer":3,"why":"Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0069","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Revenue may finance public health and education."],"answer":3,"why":"Revenue may finance public health and education."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0070","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0071","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $77 billion and spending is $82 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$5 billion deficit","$159 billion deficit","$82 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $82 − $77 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0072","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure investment","Regressive taxation","Recession","Policies to promote growth"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates regressive taxation: A regressive tax takes a larger percentage of income from lower-income people than higher-income people."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0073","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of regressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year."],"answer":2,"why":"A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income. This illustrates a regressive tax takes a larger percentage of income from lower-income people than higher-income people."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0074","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to regressive taxation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":2,"why":"The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0075","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Government deficits can widen.","Renewable installations require upfront funding."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0076","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Tax revenue may fall.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Tax revenue may fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0077","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.","Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for basic foods may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0078","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.","Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0079","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher output may create jobs.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0080","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $1100 billion to $1144 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["4%","6%","44%","104%"],"answer":0,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($1144 − $1100) / $1100 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0081","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes expansionary fiscal policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Faster output growth can conflict with environmental protection if production increases emissions.","Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand.","Economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real GDP."],"answer":2,"why":"Expansionary fiscal policy: Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0082","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Macroeconomic aims","Infrastructure investment","Higher policy interest rate","Expansionary fiscal policy"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates expansionary fiscal policy: Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0083","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of expansionary fiscal policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs."],"answer":3,"why":"During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. This illustrates expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0084","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to expansionary fiscal policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0085","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Construction jobs may be created.","Exports may become more expensive abroad.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Very tight policy may lower employment."],"answer":0,"why":"Construction jobs may be created."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0086","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Budget deficits may increase.","Firms may access more productive workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Budget deficits may increase."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0087","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control.","Graduates may leave the local economy.","Improved transport can raise later productive capacity."],"answer":3,"why":"Improved transport can raise later productive capacity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0088","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 120 to 123.6. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["3%","6%","103%","123.6%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (123.6 − 120) / 120 × 100 = 3%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0089","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","More families may afford essential goods.","Construction jobs may be created.","Health damage from emissions may fall."],"answer":2,"why":"Construction jobs may be created."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0090","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0091","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.","Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."],"answer":2,"why":"Monetary policy: Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0092","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Expansionary fiscal policy","Monetary policy","Regressive taxation","Policies to promote growth"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates monetary policy: Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0093","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year."],"answer":0,"why":"A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. This illustrates monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0094","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."],"answer":0,"why":"Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0095","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training takes time to improve employment.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Support may increase government borrowing.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."],"answer":3,"why":"Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0096","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 70,000 and 2,100 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["0.03%","97%","3%","5%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (2,100 / 70,000) × 100 = 3%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0097","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","Rates alone do not show the quality of employment.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity.","Household spending financed by credit may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Household spending financed by credit may increase."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0098","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.","Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Job losses could be smaller during the downturn."],"answer":2,"why":"Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0099","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Revenue may finance public health and education.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.","Construction jobs may be created.","Health damage from emissions may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0100","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A central bank cuts its policy interest rate after consumer spending weakens. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":1,"why":"Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0101","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes higher policy interest rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time.","Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher policy interest rate: Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0102","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Government budget surplus","Higher policy interest rate","Demand-pull inflation","Progressive taxation"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates higher policy interest rate: Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0103","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of higher policy interest rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role.","A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households."],"answer":2,"why":"A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. This illustrates increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0104","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to higher policy interest rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0105","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $89 billion and spending is $102 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$13 billion deficit","$191 billion deficit","$102 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $102 − $89 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0106","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Investment and employment may weaken.","Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."],"answer":0,"why":"Investment and employment may weaken."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0107","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households."],"answer":3,"why":"Saving becomes more attractive for some households."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0108","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Productivity gains may support lasting growth.","Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand.","Better roads can support future private investment.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0109","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","More families may afford essential goods.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."],"answer":0,"why":"Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0110","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0111","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes money supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.","Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time."],"answer":3,"why":"Money supply: Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0112","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply-side policy","Structural unemployment","Money supply","Redistribution of income"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates money supply: Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0113","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $1500 billion to $1620 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["10%","8%","120%","108%"],"answer":1,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($1620 − $1500) / $1500 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0114","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to money supply?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0115","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to monitor excessive money and credit growth. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Training may take years.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Irrigation projects may be expensive.","Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits."],"answer":1,"why":"Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0116","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to monitor excessive money and credit growth. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","More families may afford essential goods.","Construction jobs may be created.","Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms."],"answer":3,"why":"Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0117","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to monitor excessive money and credit growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."],"answer":2,"why":"A stable financial environment can improve confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0118","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to monitor excessive money and credit growth less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.","Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.","Higher spending may increase production and employment.","Better roads can support future private investment."],"answer":1,"why":"Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0119","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to monitor excessive money and credit growth. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Future interest payments may decline.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":1,"why":"Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0120","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":1,"why":"Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0121","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 130 to 139.1. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["7%","107%","10%","139.1%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (139.1 − 130) / 130 × 100 = 7%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0122","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Exchange-rate channel of monetary policy","Frictional unemployment","Recession","Supply-side policy"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates exchange-rate channel of monetary policy: Interest-rate changes can affect international capital flows and the exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0123","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of exchange-rate channel of monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield. This illustrates interest-rate changes can affect international capital flows and the exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0124","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to exchange-rate channel of monetary policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":2,"why":"Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0125","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.","A typical basket may not reflect every household's purchases.","Maintaining matching services requires funding."],"answer":0,"why":"Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0126","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Exports may become more expensive abroad.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Revenue may finance public health and education."],"answer":1,"why":"Exports may become more expensive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0127","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak.","Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."],"answer":2,"why":"An appreciated currency can lower input costs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0128","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise.","Productive capacity could expand over time.","Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence.","Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends."],"answer":2,"why":"Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0129","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Future interest payments may decline.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."],"answer":3,"why":"Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0130","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 110,000 and 7,700 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["9%","93%","7%","0.07%"],"answer":2,"why":"Unemployment rate = (7,700 / 110,000) × 100 = 7%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0131","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply-side policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100.","Macroeconomic aims are broad objectives such as growth, employment, price stability and external balance.","Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.","Policies that raise total demand may reduce cyclical unemployment but also increase inflationary pressure."],"answer":2,"why":"Supply-side policy: Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0132","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Infrastructure investment","Money supply","Supply-side policy"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates supply-side policy: Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0133","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of supply-side policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year.","A flat tax on basic household purchases uses a greater share of a low earner's income.","A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs."],"answer":2,"why":"A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. This illustrates supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0134","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply-side policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0135","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may produce more at lower unit cost.","Tax revenue may fall.","Training may take years.","Irrigation projects may be expensive."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0136","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Projects need substantial funds up front.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."],"answer":0,"why":"Projects need substantial funds up front."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0137","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in skills and transport infrastructure?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue.","Weak monitoring can undermine the policy.","Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity."],"answer":2,"why":"Long-run growth and competitiveness may improve."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0138","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $101 billion and spending is $106 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$5 billion surplus","$5 billion deficit","$207 billion deficit","$106 billion deficit"],"answer":1,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $106 − $101 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0139","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may produce more at lower unit cost."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0140","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":0,"why":"Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0141","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education and training policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.","Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100.","Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Education and training policy: Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0142","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Education and training policy","Progressive taxation","Exchange-rate channel of monetary policy","Money supply"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates education and training policy: Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0143","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education and training policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers."],"answer":3,"why":"A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. This illustrates education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0144","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education and training policy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."],"answer":3,"why":"More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0145","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand targeted vocational education programmes. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Government deficits can widen.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may access more productive workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0146","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $1900 billion to $1976 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["104%","76%","4%","6%"],"answer":2,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($1976 − $1900) / $1900 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0147","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may qualify for better-paid employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0148","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.","An appreciated currency can lower input costs.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households."],"answer":0,"why":"Graduates may leave the local economy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0149","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand targeted vocational education programmes. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Inflationary pressure may ease."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may access more productive workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0150","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."],"answer":3,"why":"More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0151","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes deregulation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity.","Increasing the policy interest rate generally discourages borrowing and encourages saving.","A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises."],"answer":1,"why":"Deregulation: Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0152","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Deregulation","Infrastructure investment","Regressive taxation","Unemployment rate"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates deregulation: Deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0153","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of deregulation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training.","A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall.","A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy."],"answer":1,"why":"A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. This illustrates deregulation reduces some government rules affecting business activity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0154","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to deregulation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market.","The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0155","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 140 to 155.4. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["11%","111%","155.4%","14%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (155.4 − 140) / 140 × 100 = 11%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0156","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Construction jobs may be created.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links."],"answer":0,"why":"Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0157","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers."],"answer":0,"why":"Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0158","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Long-term unemployment could decline.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households.","Better roads can support future private investment.","Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."],"answer":3,"why":"Other barriers such as finance may still block entry."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0159","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Future interest payments may decline.","Investment demand and employment may rise.","Firms may enter markets more easily."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may enter markets more easily."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0160","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government removes redundant permits needed to open a small food stall. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0161","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes infrastructure investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers.","Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks."],"answer":3,"why":"Infrastructure investment: Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0162","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth versus environmental sustainability","Consequences of growth","Higher policy interest rate","Infrastructure investment"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates infrastructure investment: Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0163","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 60,000 and 6,600 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["11%","0.11%","89%","13%"],"answer":0,"why":"Unemployment rate = (6,600 / 60,000) × 100 = 11%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0164","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to infrastructure investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0165","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Government deficits can widen.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Investment and employment may weaken.","Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."],"answer":3,"why":"Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0166","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Construction is costly and may disrupt traffic."],"answer":3,"why":"Construction is costly and may disrupt traffic."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0167","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Firms may increase production because transport costs fall.","Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may increase production because transport costs fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0168","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["After-tax income inequality may fall.","Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.","A stable financial environment can improve confidence."],"answer":1,"why":"Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0169","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Repair spending may restore employment and transport links.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":0,"why":"Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0170","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."],"answer":2,"why":"Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0171","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $113 billion and spending is $126 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$126 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus","$13 billion deficit","$239 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $126 − $113 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0172","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Recession","Redistribution of income","Real GDP growth","Government budget surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates real gdp growth: Economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real GDP."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0173","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of real gdp growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers."],"answer":2,"why":"A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year. This illustrates economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real gdp."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0174","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to real gdp growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":2,"why":"The 5% real GDP rise signals more goods and services were produced."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0175","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support sustainable growth through productive investment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Higher output may create jobs.","Training may take years."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher output may create jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0176","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support sustainable growth through productive investment. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution."],"answer":3,"why":"Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0177","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support sustainable growth through productive investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.","Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."],"answer":1,"why":"Tax revenue may increase as incomes and profits rise."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0178","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support sustainable growth through productive investment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Household spending financed by credit may increase.","Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.","Long-term unemployment could decline.","Fiscal capacity for later emergencies may improve."],"answer":1,"why":"Benefits may be unevenly shared across households."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0179","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support sustainable growth through productive investment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Higher output may create jobs.","Future interest payments may decline.","Firms may enter markets more easily."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher output may create jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0180","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $2300 billion to $2484 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["8%","10%","184%","108%"],"answer":0,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($2484 − $2300) / $2300 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0181","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A budget deficit occurs when government expenditure exceeds government revenue in a period.","A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.","A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."],"answer":1,"why":"Recession: A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0182","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policies to promote growth","Deregulation","Recession","Inflation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates recession: A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0183","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods.","A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":2,"why":"A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. This illustrates a recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0184","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited."],"answer":0,"why":"Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0185","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use temporary targeted support during a recession. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Investment and employment may weaken.","Training may take years.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Tax revenue may fall."],"answer":2,"why":"Demand for essentials may be maintained."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0186","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use temporary targeted support during a recession. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Support may increase government borrowing.","Future interest payments may decline.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."],"answer":0,"why":"Support may increase government borrowing."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0187","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use temporary targeted support during a recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Job losses could be smaller during the downturn.","Money demand and velocity can change unpredictably.","Some jobs require qualifications applicants do not possess.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."],"answer":0,"why":"Job losses could be smaller during the downturn."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0188","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 150 to 157.5. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["8%","5%","157.5%","105%"],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation rate = (157.5 − 150) / 150 × 100 = 5%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0189","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use temporary targeted support during a recession. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Deliveries may become faster and more reliable."],"answer":2,"why":"Demand for essentials may be maintained."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0190","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."],"answer":3,"why":"Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0191","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes growth from better resources?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output.","A budget surplus occurs when government revenue exceeds government expenditure in a period.","Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."],"answer":3,"why":"Growth from better resources: Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0192","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Money supply","Real GDP growth","Growth from better resources","Cost-push inflation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates growth from better resources: Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0193","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of growth from better resources?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion."],"answer":2,"why":"A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. This illustrates improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0194","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to growth from better resources?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0195","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.","Training may take years.","Exports may become more expensive abroad."],"answer":0,"why":"Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0196","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 100,000 and 4,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["4%","0.04%","96%","6%"],"answer":0,"why":"Unemployment rate = (4,000 / 100,000) × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0197","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to finance efficient irrigation and technical training?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Better yields may support farmers' incomes.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers."],"answer":2,"why":"Better yields may support farmers' incomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0198","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to finance efficient irrigation and technical training less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","More stable prices can protect real incomes.","Water shortages could limit future expansion.","Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."],"answer":2,"why":"Water shortages could limit future expansion."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0199","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":1,"why":"Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0200","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0201","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consequences of growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output."],"answer":1,"why":"Consequences of growth: Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0202","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth from better resources","Recession","Structural unemployment","Consequences of growth"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates consequences of growth: Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0203","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consequences of growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training.","A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions.","During a recession, a government cuts household income tax and funds road repairs.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year."],"answer":1,"why":"A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. This illustrates growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0204","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consequences of growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Aims guide policy priorities but actions towards one aim can affect another.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0205","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $125 billion and spending is $130 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$5 billion deficit","$130 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus","$255 billion deficit"],"answer":0,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $130 − $125 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0206","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Firms may enter markets more easily.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence."],"answer":0,"why":"Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0207","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor targeting can increase programme costs.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."],"answer":3,"why":"Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0208","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Saving becomes more attractive for some households.","Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.","Weak monitoring can undermine the policy."],"answer":3,"why":"Weak monitoring can undermine the policy."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0209","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Health damage from emissions may fall.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable.","Future interest payments may decline."],"answer":0,"why":"Health damage from emissions may fall."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0210","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A rapidly growing industrial district gains factories and wages but also traffic emissions. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","More production may create jobs while increasing pollution and resource depletion."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0211","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes policies to promote growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels.","Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.","Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand.","Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers."],"answer":0,"why":"Policies to promote growth: Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0212","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Unemployment rate","Expansionary fiscal policy","Recession","Policies to promote growth"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates policies to promote growth: Fiscal, monetary and supply-side measures can stimulate growth through different channels."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0213","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $2700 billion to $2808 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["108%","4%","104%","6%"],"answer":1,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($2808 − $2700) / $2700 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0214","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to policies to promote growth?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."],"answer":1,"why":"Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0215","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Inflation may rise if demand grows too quickly.","Training is expensive and time-consuming.","Investment demand and employment may rise.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments."],"answer":2,"why":"Investment demand and employment may rise."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0216","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money.","Households may plan spending with more certainty.","Workers may gain access to new jobs."],"answer":1,"why":"Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0217","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Productivity gains may support lasting growth.","Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated."],"answer":0,"why":"Productivity gains may support lasting growth."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0218","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Productive capacity could expand over time.","Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0219","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Investment demand and employment may rise.","Businesses may retain more workers.","Output can expand with less pollution per unit.","Input costs may become more stable."],"answer":0,"why":"Investment demand and employment may rise."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0220","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy with high unemployment and weak investment considers tax relief and training. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time."],"answer":1,"why":"Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0221","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 160 to 174.4. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["12%","9%","109%","174.4%"],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation rate = (174.4 − 160) / 160 × 100 = 9%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0222","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Structural unemployment","Recession","Unemployment rate","Frictional unemployment"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates unemployment rate: The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0223","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher domestic interest rates attract some foreign investors seeking yield.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year.","A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million.","A government receives $76 billion in revenue but spends $70 billion."],"answer":2,"why":"A country has 400,000 unemployed people and a labour force of 8 million. This illustrates the unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0224","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output."],"answer":2,"why":"The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0225","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish reliable labour-force survey results. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Support may increase government borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0226","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish reliable labour-force survey results. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Input costs may become more stable.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0227","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish reliable labour-force survey results?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Weak monitoring can undermine the policy.","Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses."],"answer":2,"why":"Comparable statistics can guide training and job programmes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0228","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish reliable labour-force survey results less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.","Rates alone do not show the quality of employment.","Better yields may support farmers' incomes.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."],"answer":1,"why":"Rates alone do not show the quality of employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0229","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish reliable labour-force survey results. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0230","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 50,000 and 4,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["10%","0.08%","92%","8%"],"answer":3,"why":"Unemployment rate = (4,000 / 50,000) × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0231","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes frictional unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A recession is a period of declining economic activity and real output.","Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","The unemployment rate is the unemployed divided by the labour force, multiplied by 100."],"answer":1,"why":"Frictional unemployment: Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0232","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consequences of growth","Demand-pull inflation","Frictional unemployment","Expansionary fiscal policy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates frictional unemployment: Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0233","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of frictional unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country considers expanding coal-based electricity generation to boost industrial output.","A government proposes a large spending stimulus when factories are near full capacity.","A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role.","A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation."],"answer":2,"why":"A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. This illustrates frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0234","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to frictional unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Shorter travel times can reduce logistical costs and raise productivity."],"answer":0,"why":"Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0235","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve job-matching information services. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Very tight policy may lower employment.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0236","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve job-matching information services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Deliveries may become faster and more reliable.","Construction jobs may be created.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Maintaining matching services requires funding."],"answer":3,"why":"Maintaining matching services requires funding."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0237","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve job-matching information services?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.","External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve.","Intermittent power generation may require storage investment.","Firms can fill open posts more quickly."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms can fill open posts more quickly."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0238","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $137 billion and spending is $150 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$287 billion deficit","$150 billion deficit","$13 billion surplus","$13 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $150 − $137 = $13 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0239","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve job-matching information services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Workers may find suitable vacancies faster.","Slower spending may reduce upward pressure on prices.","Investment demand and employment may rise."],"answer":1,"why":"Workers may find suitable vacancies faster."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0240","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A shop assistant leaves a job and spends six weeks looking for another suitable role. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."],"answer":3,"why":"Job-search time creates temporary unemployment even in a healthy labour market."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0241","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes structural unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A progressive tax takes a larger proportion of income as income rises.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Redistribution policies seek to reduce income inequality through taxes and transfers.","Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Structural unemployment: Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0242","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Employment versus inflation","Exchange-rate channel of monetary policy","Structural unemployment","Progressive taxation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates structural unemployment: Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0243","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of structural unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year.","High-income households pay a higher marginal rate of income tax than low-income households.","A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians.","A farming region introduces more productive irrigation methods and trains equipment operators."],"answer":2,"why":"A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. This illustrates structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0244","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to structural unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources."],"answer":0,"why":"Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0245","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers may gain access to new jobs.","Reduced spending may slow growth.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers may gain access to new jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0246","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $3100 billion to $3348 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["8%","10%","248%","108%"],"answer":0,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($3348 − $3100) / $3100 × 100 = 8%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0247","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Long-term unemployment could decline.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs.","Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses.","Transitioning away from imported fuel takes time."],"answer":0,"why":"Long-term unemployment could decline."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0248","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["After-tax income inequality may fall.","Household spending financed by credit may increase.","Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies."],"answer":3,"why":"Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0249","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More families may afford essential goods.","Businesses may make plans with more confidence.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","Workers may gain access to new jobs."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may gain access to new jobs."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0250","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A traditional textile factory closes while new technology firms struggle to recruit technicians. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More relevant skills can raise output per worker and ease recruitment shortages.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable."],"answer":3,"why":"Changes in industry demand can leave existing skills unsuitable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0251","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes cyclical unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers.","Structural unemployment results from a long-term mismatch between workers' skills or locations and available jobs.","Education and training can improve human capital and worker productivity.","Expansionary fiscal policy uses higher government spending or lower taxes to raise total demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Cyclical unemployment: Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0252","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher policy interest rate","Cyclical unemployment","Education and training policy","Growth from better resources"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates cyclical unemployment: Cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0253","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of cyclical unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A finance ministry proposes higher benefits financed by progressive taxation.","A state collects $80 billion in revenue and spends $94 billion in a year.","A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers."],"answer":2,"why":"A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. This illustrates cyclical unemployment occurs when weak total demand reduces firms' need for workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0254","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to cyclical unemployment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Higher input productivity may shift the PPC outwards.","Higher disposable income and public purchases may support output and employment."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0255","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 170 to 175.1. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["3%","175.1%","6%","103%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (175.1 − 170) / 170 × 100 = 3%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0256","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to boost demand temporarily during a recession. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher output may create jobs.","Government deficits can widen.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","More families may afford essential goods."],"answer":1,"why":"Government deficits can widen."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0257","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to boost demand temporarily during a recession?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Graduates may leave the local economy.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated.","Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers.","Higher spending may increase production and employment."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher spending may increase production and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0258","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to boost demand temporarily during a recession less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Saving becomes more attractive for some households.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income.","Growth may become more environmentally sustainable."],"answer":2,"why":"Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0259","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to boost demand temporarily during a recession. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may enter markets more easily.","Demand for essentials may be maintained.","Businesses may retain more workers.","Agricultural output could rise without a proportional rise in resources."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may retain more workers."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0260","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower sales lead firms to cut production and employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0261","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply-side policy aims to increase productive capacity or efficiency in the economy.","Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services.","Money supply refers to the stock of money available in an economy at a given time.","Growth can raise incomes and job opportunities while increasing environmental pressures."],"answer":1,"why":"Inflation: Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0262","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Growth versus environmental sustainability","Supply-side policy","Money supply","Inflation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates inflation: Inflation is a sustained rise in the general price level of goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0263","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 90,000 and 10,800 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["0.12%","12%","14%","88%"],"answer":1,"why":"Unemployment rate = (10,800 / 90,000) × 100 = 12%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0264","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt.","Better infrastructure and skills can reduce production costs over time.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power."],"answer":0,"why":"A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0265","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep inflation low and predictable. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest.","Investment and employment may weaken.","Training may take years.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may plan spending with more certainty."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0266","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to keep inflation low and predictable. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand.","Revenue may finance public health and education.","Very tight policy may lower employment.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes."],"answer":2,"why":"Very tight policy may lower employment."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0267","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to keep inflation low and predictable?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some jobs require qualifications applicants do not possess.","Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence.","Benefits may take years to appear.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may make long-term contracts with greater confidence."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0268","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to keep inflation low and predictable less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may increase production because transport costs fall.","Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.","Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."],"answer":3,"why":"Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0269","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to keep inflation low and predictable. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Health damage from emissions may fall.","Lower-income households may retain more purchasing power.","Households may plan spending with more certainty."],"answer":3,"why":"Households may plan spending with more certainty."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0270","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A basket costing $100 last year now costs $106 on average. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Cheaper credit can increase consumption and investment, affecting total demand.","Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."],"answer":2,"why":"A 6% rise in the CPI implies the average price level has increased."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0271","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Government revenue is $149 billion and spending is $154 billion. Calculate the budget balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$5 billion surplus","$303 billion deficit","$5 billion deficit","$154 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Spending exceeds revenue by $154 − $149 = $5 billion, so this is a deficit."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0272","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Cyclical unemployment","Money supply","Regressive taxation","Demand-pull inflation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates demand-pull inflation: Demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0273","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand-pull inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity.","A country's real GDP falls as factories lose orders and reduce working hours.","A region funds vocational courses for technicians needed by manufacturers.","A government upgrades freight rail lines and funds technical training."],"answer":0,"why":"Strong credit growth leads shoppers to increase spending while factories are at full capacity. This illustrates demand-pull inflation occurs when total demand grows faster than available output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0274","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand-pull inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","Higher production creates economic opportunities alongside external costs.","Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level.","Stronger demand for domestic currency may cause appreciation and lower import prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Excess demand creates upward pressure on the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0275","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Training takes time to improve employment.","Inflationary pressure may ease.","Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force."],"answer":2,"why":"Inflationary pressure may ease."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0276","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Firms may access more productive workers.","Cheaper imports may reduce imported inflation.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Reduced spending may slow growth."],"answer":3,"why":"Reduced spending may slow growth."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0277","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More stable prices can protect real incomes.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Debt repayment can crowd out urgent public investment.","A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs."],"answer":0,"why":"More stable prices can protect real incomes."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0278","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Improved transport can raise later productive capacity.","Demand for basic foods may become more affordable.","Greater equality may improve social outcomes.","Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated."],"answer":3,"why":"Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0279","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Inflationary pressure may ease.","Future interest payments may decline.","Early warnings may help monetary authorities limit inflation risks.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Inflationary pressure may ease."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0280","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Real GDP rises from $3500 billion to $3640 billion in one year. What is the real GDP growth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["6%","4%","104%","140%"],"answer":1,"why":"Real GDP growth = ($3640 − $3500) / $3500 × 100 = 4%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0281","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes cost-push inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level.","Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output.","Frictional unemployment is short-term joblessness while workers move between jobs.","Monetary policy uses instruments such as interest rates and money supply to influence the economy."],"answer":0,"why":"Cost-push inflation: Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0282","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policies to promote growth","Recession","Cost-push inflation","Education and training policy"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates cost-push inflation: Cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0283","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of cost-push inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A hotel dismisses staff when tourism spending collapses during a recession.","An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy.","A monetary authority announces action to slow rapid growth in bank-created money.","A country's inflation-adjusted GDP rises from $200 billion to $210 billion in a year."],"answer":1,"why":"An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. This illustrates cost-push inflation results when rising production costs push up the overall price level."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0284","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to cost-push inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output.","The same payment consumes a larger proportion of smaller incomes.","Transfers can increase lower-income households' purchasing power.","A $6 billion surplus can reduce borrowing needs or debt."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0285","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Input costs may become more stable.","Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets.","Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners.","Support may increase government borrowing."],"answer":0,"why":"Input costs may become more stable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0286","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Future interest payments may decline.","Alternative energy projects require large initial investments.","Construction jobs may be created.","Borrowing for investment may become more affordable."],"answer":1,"why":"Alternative energy projects require large initial investments."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0287","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Benefits may be unevenly shared across households.","Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers.","Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation.","Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak."],"answer":2,"why":"Lower exposure to fuel shocks could limit future inflation."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0288","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The CPI rises from 180 to 192.6. What is the inflation rate for this period?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["7%","107%","10%","192.6%"],"answer":0,"why":"Inflation rate = (192.6 − 180) / 180 × 100 = 7%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0289","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may access more productive workers.","Firms may enter markets more easily.","Input costs may become more stable.","Skills shortages may ease without as much extra demand."],"answer":2,"why":"Input costs may become more stable."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0290","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An oil-price spike raises transport and manufacturing costs across the economy. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand support can use idle capacity while training can raise long-run output.","Lower compliance costs may encourage entry and investment.","Falling total demand can cause output, employment and incomes to contract.","Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may pass higher costs to consumers while reducing output."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0291","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consumer prices index?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure investment improves facilities such as transport, communication and power networks.","The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services.","Improvements in the quality or quantity of production inputs can raise potential output.","Economic growth is an increase in real output, commonly measured using real GDP."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumer Prices Index: The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0292","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumer Prices Index","Deregulation","Structural unemployment","Frictional unemployment"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer prices index: The Consumer Prices Index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0293","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer prices index?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A central bank raises rates after the CPI inflation rate accelerates.","A new government sets targets for inflation, joblessness and sustainable growth.","A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year.","A government repairs a congested bridge used by factories to move goods."],"answer":2,"why":"A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. This illustrates the consumer prices index tracks the price of a representative basket of consumer goods and services."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0294","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer prices index?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Changes in available liquidity can influence borrowing, spending and prices.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","A deficit of $14 billion must be financed through borrowing or other funding sources.","The unemployment rate is 5%, showing the share of the labour force without work and seeking it."],"answer":1,"why":"A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0295","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Budget deficits may increase.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information.","Renewable installations require upfront funding.","Government deficits can widen."],"answer":1,"why":"Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0296","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The labour force of an economy is 40,000 and 2,000 people are unemployed and seeking work. What is the unemployment rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["7%","5%","0.05%","95%"],"answer":1,"why":"Unemployment rate = (2,000 / 40,000) × 100 = 5%."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0297","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Other barriers such as finance may still block entry.","Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends.","Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies.","Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy-makers can monitor inflation trends."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0298","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may qualify for better-paid employment.","Productivity gains may support lasting growth.","Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons.","Entrepreneurs may spend less on administration."],"answer":2,"why":"Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0299","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Health damage from emissions may fall.","Firms may access more productive workers.","Policy-makers can track labour-market changes.","Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers and firms may negotiate with better price information."},{"id":"0455-C04-P1-MCQ-0300","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A statistics office compares the cost of a weighted basket with its cost in a base year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Stronger demand may support hiring but bid up prices when spare capacity is limited.","Higher financing costs tend to reduce credit-financed demand.","A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket.","Higher average tax burdens on the wealthy can help redistribute disposable income."],"answer":2,"why":"A rise in the index indicates inflation in the measured basket."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two government macroeconomic aims. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Economic growth (1)\n• Low unemployment (1)\n• Low and stable inflation (1)\n• Balance of payments stability (1)\n• Redistribution of income (1)\n• Environmental sustainability (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain why economic growth may conflict with environmental sustainability. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Expanding output can increase resource use and emissions (1); Pollution / depletion damages environmental sustainability (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• More industrial production may generate waste (1); External environmental costs may rise (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain why a policy to reduce unemployment may increase inflation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Expansionary policy raises total demand and employment (1); Near capacity, demand may grow faster than output and raise prices (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Lower interest rates stimulate spending and production (1); With limited spare capacity, inflationary pressure may increase (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define redistribution of income. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A change in how income is distributed (1); Using taxes / transfers to reduce differences between higher- and lower-income households (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define government budget deficit. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Total government expenditure (1); Exceeds total government revenue in a specified period (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define progressive taxation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The average tax rate / percentage of income paid in tax (1); Increases as income rises (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define regressive taxation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The average tax rate / percentage of income paid in tax (1); Falls as income rises / is higher for lower-income people (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define expansionary fiscal policy. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Higher government spending and/or lower taxation (1); Intended to raise total demand / economic activity (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define monetary policy. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Decisions affecting interest rates / money supply / exchange rate (1); Intended to influence economic activity / total demand and inflation (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how a rise in the policy interest rate affects borrowing and saving. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Higher interest makes borrowing more expensive (1); Households may borrow or spend less (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Higher interest increases returns to saving (1); Households may save more (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how a rise in interest rates may affect a floating exchange rate. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Higher domestic interest may attract overseas funds (1); Demand for the currency rises and it may appreciate (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Higher interest rewards holding domestic-currency assets (1); Buyers demand domestic currency to obtain those assets (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define supply-side policy. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Measures to improve productive capacity / efficiency (1); Intended to increase total supply / potential output (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how education and training may increase labour productivity. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Training increases workers’ skills (1); More output can be produced per worker-hour (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Education improves ability to use productive technology (1); Workers complete production tasks more effectively (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define deregulation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Removal or reduction of government rules (1); Affecting firms’ activities / entry and operation in markets (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain what is meant by investment in infrastructure. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Spending on facilities such as transport, power or communications (1); Creates or improves capital supporting economic activity (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Investment in roads or reliable electricity (1); Reduces transport delays or interruptions to production (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define recession. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A decrease in real GDP (1); For two consecutive quarters / six months or more (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how improvements in resources can increase an economy’s productive capacity. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Better skills or technology raise productivity (1); More output can be produced with existing resources (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• More capital or labour expands available inputs (1); The maximum possible output increases (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two possible consequences of economic growth. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Higher real incomes (1)\n• More employment (1)\n• Higher material living standards (1)\n• More pollution (1)\n• Depletion of natural resources (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two policies a government may use to promote economic growth. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Expansionary fiscal policy (1)\n• Expansionary monetary policy (1)\n• Education and training (1)\n• Infrastructure investment (1)\n• Incentives for productive investment (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define unemployment rate. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Number unemployed (1); Divided by labour force and multiplied by 100 (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define structural unemployment. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Unemployment caused by long-term changes in demand / production (1); Workers’ skills or locations no longer match available jobs (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define cyclical unemployment. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Unemployment caused by insufficient total demand (1); Firms reduce output and demand fewer workers (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define inflation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A sustained rise (1); In the general price level of goods and services (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• A continuing fall in money’s purchasing power (1); Across goods and services generally, not only one product (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define demand-pull inflation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Total demand rises faster than available output (1); The general price level increases (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M02-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define cost-push inflation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Higher production costs (1); Cause a rise in the general price level / firms raise prices to cover costs (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Measurable targets make policy success easier to assess (1) → Ineffective measures can be identified (1) → Resources can be redirected to more effective policies (1).\n\n2. Clear targets reduce uncertainty about government priorities (1) → Firms can plan investment more confidently (1) → Capital investment may rise (1).\n\n3. Published targets allow citizens to compare results (1) → Government decisions become more accountable (1) → Pressure to use public funds effectively increases (1) → Wasteful expenditure may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Cleaner generation reduces emissions per unit of electricity (1) → External pollution costs decrease (1) → Environmental quality may improve (1).\n\n2. Less air pollution reduces illness (1) → Workers lose fewer days to ill health (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1).\n\n3. Cleaner capacity supports production without equal pollution growth (1) → Environmental damage from expansion is limited (1) → Growth becomes more sustainable (1) → Future living standards are better protected (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training improves labour skills (1) → Productive capacity increases (1) → Extra output can meet demand with less inflationary pressure (1).\n\n2. Training targets workers with unsuitable skills (1) → Occupational mobility rises (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n3. Avoiding broad stimulus limits extra total demand (1) → Demand grows less rapidly near full capacity (1) → Pressure to increase prices is reduced (1) → Low inflation is easier to maintain (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Transfers raise low-income households’ disposable income (1) → More necessities become affordable (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Targeting directs assistance to the poorest households (1) → The income gap may narrow (1) → Relative poverty may decrease (1).\n\n3. Poorer households often spend a large share of extra income (1) → Demand for local goods and services rises (1) → Firms may increase output if capacity exists (1) → Cyclical unemployment may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Repairs remove damaged sections and road closures (1) → Delivery vehicles spend less time waiting or diverting (1) → Transport cost per delivery may fall (1).\n\n2. Repair spending creates demand for construction labour (1) → Unemployed workers may gain jobs (1) → Household income rises (1).\n\n3. Repaired roads improve access to workplaces (1) → Geographical mobility of labour rises (1) → More vacancies can be filled (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Lower outstanding debt reduces future interest payments (1) → Less tax revenue is committed to debt service (1) → More funds remain for public services (1).\n\n2. Debt repayment reduces future refinancing needs (1) → Government finances become less exposed to interest-rate rises (1) → Future spending becomes easier to plan (1).\n\n3. Smaller interest obligations ease pressure for future taxes (1) → Households may retain more disposable income (1) → Consumption or saving may rise (1) → Future living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher earners pay a larger share of income in tax (1) → Disposable income differences narrow (1) → Income inequality may fall (1).\n\n2. Extra tax revenue can finance benefits (1) → Low-income households receive more support (1) → Poverty may decrease (1).\n\n3. Revenue can fund education and healthcare (1) → Poorer households gain access to essential services (1) → Health and skills may improve (1) → Future earning opportunities may expand (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Removing sales tax lowers essential food prices if passed on (1) → Food becomes more affordable (1) → Low-income households can meet basic needs more easily (1).\n\n2. Poorer households spend a high income share on necessities (1) → Exemption reduces their tax burden proportionately more (1) → The tax system becomes less regressive (1).\n\n3. Lower food costs leave households with more income for other goods (1) → Effective demand for other products may rise (1) → Firms may expand output (1) → Employment may increase if spare capacity exists (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Government spending directly raises total demand (1) → Firms receive more orders (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n2. Infrastructure projects require workers (1) → Wage income increases (1) → Household consumption may rise (1).\n\n3. Better infrastructure lowers future business costs (1) → Firms become more productive (1) → Potential output increases (1) → Long-run economic growth may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Lower rates reduce borrowing costs (1) → Household spending on credit may rise (1) → Total demand increases (1).\n\n2. Lower loan interest makes investment more profitable (1) → Firms may buy more capital equipment (1) → Productive capacity may expand (1).\n\n3. Lower returns discourage saving (1) → Households may increase consumption (1) → Firms expand output if spare capacity exists (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher interest increases the cost of credit (1) → Borrowing-financed consumption falls (1) → Growth of total demand slows (1).\n\n2. Saving earns a greater return (1) → Households may postpone spending (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure decreases (1).\n\n3. Higher interest may attract overseas funds (1) → Demand for the currency may rise and cause appreciation (1) → More expensive exports and cheaper imports reduce net export demand (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to monitor excessive money and credit growth might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Monitoring identifies rapid lending growth (1) → Authorities can act before borrowing becomes excessive (1) → Growth of demand-pull inflation may be restrained (1).\n\n2. Better lending information reveals rising repayment risks (1) → Banks can tighten unsuitable lending (1) → Bad debts may be reduced (1).\n\n3. Information supports more suitable monetary-policy decisions (1) → Policy can respond to changing demand conditions (1) → Large economic fluctuations may be limited (1) → Price stability may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher rates may attract overseas funds (1) → Demand for domestic currency increases (1) → Appreciation may occur (1).\n\n2. Appreciation lowers domestic prices of imports (1) → Imported inputs cost less (1) → Cost-push inflation may decrease (1).\n\n3. Appreciation makes exports dearer to foreign buyers (1) → Export demand may fall (1) → Recognising this risk allows more balanced policy choices (1) → Avoidable losses of export employment may be limited (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest in skills and transport infrastructure. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training improves workers’ skills (1) → Labour productivity rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Better transport reduces delivery times (1) → Firms face lower distribution costs (1) → Price competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Better roads widen the area workers can travel (1) → Geographical mobility increases (1) → Skills can reach firms with vacancies (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Vocational courses teach skills demanded by employers (1) → More applicants meet job requirements (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).\n\n2. Better practical skills raise output per worker (1) → Employers can produce at lower unit cost (1) → Competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Qualifications allow workers to change occupation (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Workers can move towards growing industries (1) → Long-term earning prospects may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Simpler licensing reduces compliance time (1) → Firms can start operating sooner (1) → Entry costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Lower entry barriers attract new businesses (1) → Competition increases (1) → Consumers may benefit from lower prices (1).\n\n3. Fewer administrative costs leave firms with more funds (1) → More finance is available for equipment (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1) → Employment may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A reliable bridge reduces transport delays (1) → Inputs reach producers on time (1) → Production interruptions decrease (1).\n\n2. Faster journeys lower fuel and driver costs (1) → Distribution costs per unit fall (1) → Firms may lower selling prices (1).\n\n3. Reliable access makes local investment more attractive (1) → Firms may open or expand nearby (1) → Demand for labour may rise (1) → Local employment may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to support sustainable growth through productive investment. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Productive investment increases capital equipment (1) → More output can be produced (1) → Potential economic growth rises (1).\n\n2. More efficient equipment uses fewer inputs per unit (1) → Natural resources are conserved (1) → Growth becomes less environmentally damaging (1).\n\n3. Expanding sustainable productive capacity creates jobs (1) → Household incomes may rise (1) → Tax receipts may increase (1) → More resources become available for public services (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers use temporary targeted support during a recession. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Targeted support sustains incomes of affected households (1) → Spending on necessities falls less (1) → Recessionary falls in demand may be smaller (1).\n\n2. Assistance can keep viable firms operating temporarily (1) → Avoidable business closures decrease (1) → Existing jobs may be protected (1).\n\n3. Temporary measures can end when activity recovers (1) → Permanent expenditure commitments are avoided (1) → Future pressure on public finances is limited (1) → More room remains for later economic emergencies (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to finance efficient irrigation and technical training could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Efficient irrigation raises crop yields (1) → Output per hectare increases (1) → Farm incomes may rise (1).\n\n2. Technical training improves production methods (1) → Workers use inputs more effectively (1) → Average production costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Reliable irrigation reduces crop failure from dry conditions (1) → Agricultural output becomes less variable (1) → Food supply becomes more stable (1) → Food-price fluctuations may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Emission limits reduce harmful pollution (1) → External environmental costs fall (1) → Non-material living standards improve (1).\n\n2. Cleaner air reduces illness (1) → Workers are absent less often (1) → Labour productivity may improve (1).\n\n3. Pollution controls encourage cleaner technology (1) → Firms reduce waste and harmful emissions (1) → Environmental resources are better protected (1) → Future productive capacity is more sustainable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Investment incentives reduce the effective cost of equipment (1) → Firms may increase capital investment (1) → Productive capacity expands (1).\n\n2. Workforce training improves ability to use new machinery (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Training and investment can target expanding industries (1) → Workers gain skills for new equipment and jobs (1) → Skills mismatches may decrease (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to publish reliable labour-force survey results. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Surveys estimate the scale of unemployment (1) → Policymakers can judge its severity (1) → Support can be sized more appropriately (1).\n\n2. Data identify affected occupations and regions (1) → Training and job support can be targeted (1) → Public resources may be used more effectively (1).\n\n3. Repeated reliable surveys show trends over time (1) → Policies can be compared with employment outcomes (1) → Ineffective programmes can be changed (1) → Future employment policy may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers improve job-matching information services. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Vacancies are advertised to a wider range of workers (1) → More workers discover suitable jobs (1) → Frictional unemployment duration may decrease (1).\n\n2. Employers reach more suitable applicants (1) → Recruitment becomes faster (1) → Production bottlenecks from vacancies may fall (1).\n\n3. Wider information reveals jobs in other regions (1) → Workers may consider relocating (1) → Geographical mismatches can decrease (1) → Labour resources are used more fully (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Retraining supplies skills required in expanding industries (1) → More displaced workers qualify for vacancies (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).\n\n2. New skills increase output per worker (1) → Workers become more valuable to employers (1) → Their earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Employment replaces reliance on unemployment benefits (1) → Government benefit expenditure may decrease (1) → Income-tax receipts may rise (1) → More funds remain for other services (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to boost demand temporarily during a recession. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Higher spending increases demand for firms’ output (1) → Firms increase production (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n2. More employment raises household income (1) → Consumer expenditure may increase (1) → Recovery in demand may be reinforced (1).\n\n3. Higher sales improve firms’ expected returns (1) → Firms may resume investment (1) → Demand for capital goods may increase (1) → Future productive capacity may expand (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to keep inflation low and predictable might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Stable prices make future costs easier to predict (1) → Firms can plan investment more confidently (1) → Capital formation may increase (1).\n\n2. Low inflation protects the real value of fixed incomes (1) → Households retain purchasing power (1) → Material living standards are more secure (1).\n\n3. Fewer frequent price changes are necessary (1) → Firms incur lower menu costs (1) → Resources can be used for production instead (1) → Efficiency may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Tighter credit reduces borrowing-financed consumption (1) → Total demand grows more slowly (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may fall (1).\n\n2. Slower debt growth reduces future repayment burdens (1) → Households are less vulnerable to financial distress (1) → Consumption may become more stable (1).\n\n3. Weaker demand for imports may follow lower consumption (1) → Import spending may decrease (1) → Current-account payments may fall (1) → The current-account balance may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Switching away from expensive fuel lowers input costs (1) → Average production costs may fall (1) → Cost-push inflationary pressure decreases (1).\n\n2. Lower fuel imports reduce foreign-currency spending (1) → Import payments fall (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).\n\n3. More diverse energy sources reduce exposure to supply shocks (1) → Production becomes less vulnerable to fuel shortages (1) → Output and employment become more stable (1) → Economic welfare may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M04-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. CPI data reveal changes in consumer prices (1) → Real wages can be compared with nominal wages (1) → Households can assess purchasing power (1).\n\n2. Reliable inflation data guide monetary policy (1) → Authorities can identify rising inflationary pressure (1) → Policy responses can be better timed (1).\n\n3. Updated price information improves pay and benefit decisions (1) → Adjustments can reflect changes in living costs (1) → Severe losses of real income may be limited (1) → Poverty among vulnerable households may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Measurable targets make policy success easier to assess (1) → Ineffective measures can be identified (1) → Resources can be redirected to more effective policies (1).\n\n2. Clear targets reduce uncertainty about government priorities (1) → Firms can plan investment more confidently (1) → Capital investment may rise (1).\n\n3. Published targets allow citizens to compare results (1) → Government decisions become more accountable (1) → Pressure to use public funds effectively increases (1) → Wasteful expenditure may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Cleaner generation reduces emissions per unit of electricity (1) → External pollution costs decrease (1) → Environmental quality may improve (1).\n\n2. Less air pollution reduces illness (1) → Workers lose fewer days to ill health (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1).\n\n3. Cleaner capacity supports production without equal pollution growth (1) → Environmental damage from expansion is limited (1) → Growth becomes more sustainable (1) → Future living standards are better protected (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training improves labour skills (1) → Productive capacity increases (1) → Extra output can meet demand with less inflationary pressure (1).\n\n2. Training targets workers with unsuitable skills (1) → Occupational mobility rises (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n3. Avoiding broad stimulus limits extra total demand (1) → Demand grows less rapidly near full capacity (1) → Pressure to increase prices is reduced (1) → Low inflation is easier to maintain (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Transfers raise low-income households’ disposable income (1) → More necessities become affordable (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Targeting directs assistance to the poorest households (1) → The income gap may narrow (1) → Relative poverty may decrease (1).\n\n3. Poorer households often spend a large share of extra income (1) → Demand for local goods and services rises (1) → Firms may increase output if capacity exists (1) → Cyclical unemployment may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Repairs remove damaged sections and road closures (1) → Delivery vehicles spend less time waiting or diverting (1) → Transport cost per delivery may fall (1).\n\n2. Repair spending creates demand for construction labour (1) → Unemployed workers may gain jobs (1) → Household income rises (1).\n\n3. Repaired roads improve access to workplaces (1) → Geographical mobility of labour rises (1) → More vacancies can be filled (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Lower outstanding debt reduces future interest payments (1) → Less tax revenue is committed to debt service (1) → More funds remain for public services (1).\n\n2. Debt repayment reduces future refinancing needs (1) → Government finances become less exposed to interest-rate rises (1) → Future spending becomes easier to plan (1).\n\n3. Smaller interest obligations ease pressure for future taxes (1) → Households may retain more disposable income (1) → Consumption or saving may rise (1) → Future living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher earners pay a larger share of income in tax (1) → Disposable income differences narrow (1) → Income inequality may fall (1).\n\n2. Extra tax revenue can finance benefits (1) → Low-income households receive more support (1) → Poverty may decrease (1).\n\n3. Revenue can fund education and healthcare (1) → Poorer households gain access to essential services (1) → Health and skills may improve (1) → Future earning opportunities may expand (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Removing sales tax lowers essential food prices if passed on (1) → Food becomes more affordable (1) → Low-income households can meet basic needs more easily (1).\n\n2. Poorer households spend a high income share on necessities (1) → Exemption reduces their tax burden proportionately more (1) → The tax system becomes less regressive (1).\n\n3. Lower food costs leave households with more income for other goods (1) → Effective demand for other products may rise (1) → Firms may expand output (1) → Employment may increase if spare capacity exists (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Government spending directly raises total demand (1) → Firms receive more orders (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n2. Infrastructure projects require workers (1) → Wage income increases (1) → Household consumption may rise (1).\n\n3. Better infrastructure lowers future business costs (1) → Firms become more productive (1) → Potential output increases (1) → Long-run economic growth may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Lower rates reduce borrowing costs (1) → Household spending on credit may rise (1) → Total demand increases (1).\n\n2. Lower loan interest makes investment more profitable (1) → Firms may buy more capital equipment (1) → Productive capacity may expand (1).\n\n3. Lower returns discourage saving (1) → Households may increase consumption (1) → Firms expand output if spare capacity exists (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher interest increases the cost of credit (1) → Borrowing-financed consumption falls (1) → Growth of total demand slows (1).\n\n2. Saving earns a greater return (1) → Households may postpone spending (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure decreases (1).\n\n3. Higher interest may attract overseas funds (1) → Demand for the currency may rise and cause appreciation (1) → More expensive exports and cheaper imports reduce net export demand (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to monitor excessive money and credit growth may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Monitoring identifies rapid lending growth (1) → Authorities can act before borrowing becomes excessive (1) → Growth of demand-pull inflation may be restrained (1).\n\n2. Better lending information reveals rising repayment risks (1) → Banks can tighten unsuitable lending (1) → Bad debts may be reduced (1).\n\n3. Information supports more suitable monetary-policy decisions (1) → Policy can respond to changing demand conditions (1) → Large economic fluctuations may be limited (1) → Price stability may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher rates may attract overseas funds (1) → Demand for domestic currency increases (1) → Appreciation may occur (1).\n\n2. Appreciation lowers domestic prices of imports (1) → Imported inputs cost less (1) → Cost-push inflation may decrease (1).\n\n3. Appreciation makes exports dearer to foreign buyers (1) → Export demand may fall (1) → Recognising this risk allows more balanced policy choices (1) → Avoidable losses of export employment may be limited (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training improves workers’ skills (1) → Labour productivity rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Better transport reduces delivery times (1) → Firms face lower distribution costs (1) → Price competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Better roads widen the area workers can travel (1) → Geographical mobility increases (1) → Skills can reach firms with vacancies (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand targeted vocational education programmes could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Vocational courses teach skills demanded by employers (1) → More applicants meet job requirements (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).\n\n2. Better practical skills raise output per worker (1) → Employers can produce at lower unit cost (1) → Competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Qualifications allow workers to change occupation (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Workers can move towards growing industries (1) → Long-term earning prospects may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Simpler licensing reduces compliance time (1) → Firms can start operating sooner (1) → Entry costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Lower entry barriers attract new businesses (1) → Competition increases (1) → Consumers may benefit from lower prices (1).\n\n3. Fewer administrative costs leave firms with more funds (1) → More finance is available for equipment (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1) → Employment may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A reliable bridge reduces transport delays (1) → Inputs reach producers on time (1) → Production interruptions decrease (1).\n\n2. Faster journeys lower fuel and driver costs (1) → Distribution costs per unit fall (1) → Firms may lower selling prices (1).\n\n3. Reliable access makes local investment more attractive (1) → Firms may open or expand nearby (1) → Demand for labour may rise (1) → Local employment may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to support sustainable growth through productive investment could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Productive investment increases capital equipment (1) → More output can be produced (1) → Potential economic growth rises (1).\n\n2. More efficient equipment uses fewer inputs per unit (1) → Natural resources are conserved (1) → Growth becomes less environmentally damaging (1).\n\n3. Expanding sustainable productive capacity creates jobs (1) → Household incomes may rise (1) → Tax receipts may increase (1) → More resources become available for public services (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to use temporary targeted support during a recession. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Targeted support sustains incomes of affected households (1) → Spending on necessities falls less (1) → Recessionary falls in demand may be smaller (1).\n\n2. Assistance can keep viable firms operating temporarily (1) → Avoidable business closures decrease (1) → Existing jobs may be protected (1).\n\n3. Temporary measures can end when activity recovers (1) → Permanent expenditure commitments are avoided (1) → Future pressure on public finances is limited (1) → More room remains for later economic emergencies (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to finance efficient irrigation and technical training could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Efficient irrigation raises crop yields (1) → Output per hectare increases (1) → Farm incomes may rise (1).\n\n2. Technical training improves production methods (1) → Workers use inputs more effectively (1) → Average production costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Reliable irrigation reduces crop failure from dry conditions (1) → Agricultural output becomes less variable (1) → Food supply becomes more stable (1) → Food-price fluctuations may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Emission limits reduce harmful pollution (1) → External environmental costs fall (1) → Non-material living standards improve (1).\n\n2. Cleaner air reduces illness (1) → Workers are absent less often (1) → Labour productivity may improve (1).\n\n3. Pollution controls encourage cleaner technology (1) → Firms reduce waste and harmful emissions (1) → Environmental resources are better protected (1) → Future productive capacity is more sustainable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Investment incentives reduce the effective cost of equipment (1) → Firms may increase capital investment (1) → Productive capacity expands (1).\n\n2. Workforce training improves ability to use new machinery (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n3. Training and investment can target expanding industries (1) → Workers gain skills for new equipment and jobs (1) → Skills mismatches may decrease (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to publish reliable labour-force survey results could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Surveys estimate the scale of unemployment (1) → Policymakers can judge its severity (1) → Support can be sized more appropriately (1).\n\n2. Data identify affected occupations and regions (1) → Training and job support can be targeted (1) → Public resources may be used more effectively (1).\n\n3. Repeated reliable surveys show trends over time (1) → Policies can be compared with employment outcomes (1) → Ineffective programmes can be changed (1) → Future employment policy may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to improve job-matching information services. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Vacancies are advertised to a wider range of workers (1) → More workers discover suitable jobs (1) → Frictional unemployment duration may decrease (1).\n\n2. Employers reach more suitable applicants (1) → Recruitment becomes faster (1) → Production bottlenecks from vacancies may fall (1).\n\n3. Wider information reveals jobs in other regions (1) → Workers may consider relocating (1) → Geographical mismatches can decrease (1) → Labour resources are used more fully (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Retraining supplies skills required in expanding industries (1) → More displaced workers qualify for vacancies (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).\n\n2. New skills increase output per worker (1) → Workers become more valuable to employers (1) → Their earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Employment replaces reliance on unemployment benefits (1) → Government benefit expenditure may decrease (1) → Income-tax receipts may rise (1) → More funds remain for other services (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to boost demand temporarily during a recession. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Higher spending increases demand for firms’ output (1) → Firms increase production (1) → Cyclical unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n2. More employment raises household income (1) → Consumer expenditure may increase (1) → Recovery in demand may be reinforced (1).\n\n3. Higher sales improve firms’ expected returns (1) → Firms may resume investment (1) → Demand for capital goods may increase (1) → Future productive capacity may expand (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to keep inflation low and predictable may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Stable prices make future costs easier to predict (1) → Firms can plan investment more confidently (1) → Capital formation may increase (1).\n\n2. Low inflation protects the real value of fixed incomes (1) → Households retain purchasing power (1) → Material living standards are more secure (1).\n\n3. Fewer frequent price changes are necessary (1) → Firms incur lower menu costs (1) → Resources can be used for production instead (1) → Efficiency may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Tighter credit reduces borrowing-financed consumption (1) → Total demand grows more slowly (1) → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may fall (1).\n\n2. Slower debt growth reduces future repayment burdens (1) → Households are less vulnerable to financial distress (1) → Consumption may become more stable (1).\n\n3. Weaker demand for imports may follow lower consumption (1) → Import spending may decrease (1) → Current-account payments may fall (1) → The current-account balance may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Switching away from expensive fuel lowers input costs (1) → Average production costs may fall (1) → Cost-push inflationary pressure decreases (1).\n\n2. Lower fuel imports reduce foreign-currency spending (1) → Import payments fall (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).\n\n3. More diverse energy sources reduce exposure to supply shocks (1) → Production becomes less vulnerable to fuel shortages (1) → Output and employment become more stable (1) → Economic welfare may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M06-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. CPI data reveal changes in consumer prices (1) → Real wages can be compared with nominal wages (1) → Households can assess purchasing power (1).\n\n2. Reliable inflation data guide monetary policy (1) → Authorities can identify rising inflationary pressure (1) → Policy responses can be better timed (1).\n\n3. Updated price information improves pay and benefit decisions (1) → Adjustments can reflect changes in living costs (1) → Severe losses of real income may be limited (1) → Poverty among vulnerable households may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0001","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to publish clear and measurable economic policy targets would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Measurable targets make policy success easier to assess → Ineffective measures can be identified → Resources can be redirected to more effective policies.\n\n2. Clear targets reduce uncertainty about government priorities → Firms can plan investment more confidently → Capital investment may rise.\n\n3. Published targets allow citizens to compare results → Government decisions become more accountable → Pressure to use public funds effectively increases → Wasteful expenditure may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Governments may focus too narrowly on measured targets → Unmeasured welfare issues may be neglected.\n\n2. External shocks can make targets difficult to achieve → Rigid targets may encourage unsuitable policies.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0002","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to encourage cleaner electricity generation during industrial expansion. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Cleaner generation reduces emissions per unit of electricity → External pollution costs decrease → Environmental quality may improve.\n\n2. Less air pollution reduces illness → Workers lose fewer days to ill health → Labour productivity may rise.\n\n3. Cleaner capacity supports production without equal pollution growth → Environmental damage from expansion is limited → Growth becomes more sustainable → Future living standards are better protected.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Renewable installations require upfront funding → Other public or private investments may be delayed.\n\n2. Intermittent power generation may require storage investment → Reliable supply may be expensive to maintain.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0003","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to use targeted training instead of broad stimulus near full capacity. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training improves labour skills → Productive capacity increases → Extra output can meet demand with less inflationary pressure.\n\n2. Training targets workers with unsuitable skills → Occupational mobility rises → Structural unemployment may decrease.\n\n3. Avoiding broad stimulus limits extra total demand → Demand grows less rapidly near full capacity → Pressure to increase prices is reduced → Low inflation is easier to maintain.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training takes time to improve employment → Unemployment may stay high initially.\n\n2. Some unemployed workers may lack access to courses → The workers most in need may not benefit.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0004","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.1","topicName":"Government macroeconomic intervention","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to expand targeted support for the lowest-income households is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Transfers raise low-income households’ disposable income → More necessities become affordable → Absolute poverty may fall.\n\n2. Targeting directs assistance to the poorest households → The income gap may narrow → Relative poverty may decrease.\n\n3. Poorer households often spend a large share of extra income → Demand for local goods and services rises → Firms may increase output if capacity exists → Cyclical unemployment may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher taxes may affect incentives for some earners → Taxable income or productive activity may fall.\n\n2. Poor targeting can increase programme costs → Less support reaches the poorest per unit spent.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0005","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to temporarily run a deficit to repair damaged roads would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Repairs remove damaged sections and road closures → Delivery vehicles spend less time waiting or diverting → Transport cost per delivery may fall.\n\n2. Repair spending creates demand for construction labour → Unemployed workers may gain jobs → Household income rises.\n\n3. Repaired roads improve access to workplaces → Geographical mobility of labour rises → More vacancies can be filled → Productive capacity may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Public debt and interest obligations may increase → Future taxes or spending cuts may be needed.\n\n2. Spending may be inefficient if projects are poorly selected → Roads may deliver benefits below their construction costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0006","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to use a temporary surplus to reduce public debt would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Lower outstanding debt reduces future interest payments → Less tax revenue is committed to debt service → More funds remain for public services.\n\n2. Debt repayment reduces future refinancing needs → Government finances become less exposed to interest-rate rises → Future spending becomes easier to plan.\n\n3. Smaller interest obligations ease pressure for future taxes → Households may retain more disposable income → Consumption or saving may rise → Future living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Using funds for debt repayment rather than current government purchases may weaken demand → Firms may reduce output and employment.\n\n2. Debt repayment can displace urgent public investment → Future productivity gains from investment may be lost.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0007","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to increase progressive income-tax rates on very high incomes would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher earners pay a larger share of income in tax → Disposable income differences narrow → Income inequality may fall.\n\n2. Extra tax revenue can finance benefits → Low-income households receive more support → Poverty may decrease.\n\n3. Revenue can fund education and healthcare → Poorer households gain access to essential services → Health and skills may improve → Future earning opportunities may expand.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Very high rates may weaken incentives to work or invest → Labour supply or capital investment may fall.\n\n2. Tax avoidance can reduce the expected revenue → Public services may receive less funding than forecast.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0008","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to exempt essential foods from a broad sales tax. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Removing sales tax lowers essential food prices if passed on → Food becomes more affordable → Low-income households can meet basic needs more easily.\n\n2. Poorer households spend a high income share on necessities → Exemption reduces their tax burden proportionately more → The tax system becomes less regressive.\n\n3. Lower food costs leave households with more income for other goods → Effective demand for other products may rise → Firms may expand output → Employment may increase if spare capacity exists.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Tax revenue may fall → Less finance remains for public services.\n\n2. Businesses may not pass the full exemption to consumers → Food prices may not fall by the expected amount.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0009","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.2","topicName":"Fiscal policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to increase infrastructure spending during a demand-led recession. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Government spending directly raises total demand → Firms receive more orders → Cyclical unemployment may decrease.\n\n2. Infrastructure projects require workers → Wage income increases → Household consumption may rise.\n\n3. Better infrastructure lowers future business costs → Firms become more productive → Potential output increases → Long-run economic growth may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Budget deficits may increase → Future debt-service obligations may increase.\n\n2. A shortage of skilled builders could raise costs → Less infrastructure can be built from a given budget.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0010","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to lower interest rates during a demand-led slowdown is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Lower rates reduce borrowing costs → Household spending on credit may rise → Total demand increases.\n\n2. Lower loan interest makes investment more profitable → Firms may buy more capital equipment → Productive capacity may expand.\n\n3. Lower returns discourage saving → Households may increase consumption → Firms expand output if spare capacity exists → Cyclical unemployment may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Inflation may rise if demand grows too quickly → Real purchasing power may be reduced.\n\n2. Banks may not pass lower rates to borrowers → Borrowing and spending may not increase sufficiently.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0011","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to raise interest rates to contain demand-pull inflation would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher interest increases the cost of credit → Borrowing-financed consumption falls → Growth of total demand slows.\n\n2. Saving earns a greater return → Households may postpone spending → Demand-pull inflationary pressure decreases.\n\n3. Higher interest may attract overseas funds → Demand for the currency may rise and cause appreciation → More expensive exports and cheaper imports reduce net export demand → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Investment and employment may weaken → Cyclical unemployment may rise.\n\n2. Cost-push inflation may persist despite weaker demand → The policy may sacrifice output without removing the cost shock.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0012","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to monitor excessive money and credit growth would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Monitoring identifies rapid lending growth → Authorities can act before borrowing becomes excessive → Growth of demand-pull inflation may be restrained.\n\n2. Better lending information reveals rising repayment risks → Banks can tighten unsuitable lending → Bad debts may be reduced.\n\n3. Information supports more suitable monetary-policy decisions → Policy can respond to changing demand conditions → Large economic fluctuations may be limited → Price stability may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Restricting credit can hurt viable small firms → Production and employment may grow more slowly.\n\n2. The relationship between lending growth and spending may change unpredictably → Credit indicators may give misleading inflation signals.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0013","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.3","topicName":"Monetary policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to consider exchange-rate effects when raising interest rates would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher rates may attract overseas funds → Demand for domestic currency increases → Appreciation may occur.\n\n2. Appreciation lowers domestic prices of imports → Imported inputs cost less → Cost-push inflation may decrease.\n\n3. Appreciation makes exports dearer to foreign buyers → Export demand may fall → Recognising this risk allows more balanced policy choices → Avoidable losses of export employment may be limited.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Exports may become more expensive abroad → Overseas demand and export jobs may fall.\n\n2. Capital flows also depend on risk and confidence → Appreciation is not guaranteed by higher rates.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0014","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to invest in skills and transport infrastructure. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training improves workers’ skills → Labour productivity rises → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. Better transport reduces delivery times → Firms face lower distribution costs → Price competitiveness may improve.\n\n3. Better roads widen the area workers can travel → Geographical mobility increases → Skills can reach firms with vacancies → Structural unemployment may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Projects need substantial funds up front → Taxes or borrowing may need to rise.\n\n2. Benefits may take years to appear → Current unemployment and costs may remain high.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0015","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand targeted vocational education programmes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Vocational courses teach skills demanded by employers → More applicants meet job requirements → Structural unemployment may fall.\n\n2. Better practical skills raise output per worker → Employers can produce at lower unit cost → Competitiveness may improve.\n\n3. Qualifications allow workers to change occupation → Occupational mobility increases → Workers can move towards growing industries → Long-term earning prospects may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training is expensive and time-consuming → Current output or tax funds must be sacrificed.\n\n2. Graduates may leave the local economy → Local skills shortages may remain.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0016","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to simplify licensing rules for safe small businesses is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Simpler licensing reduces compliance time → Firms can start operating sooner → Entry costs may fall.\n\n2. Lower entry barriers attract new businesses → Competition increases → Consumers may benefit from lower prices.\n\n3. Fewer administrative costs leave firms with more funds → More finance is available for equipment → Productive capacity may increase → Employment may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Weak safeguards could reduce product quality or safety → Consumers may face harmful products.\n\n2. Other barriers such as finance may still block entry → New firms may still be unable to start.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0017","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.4","topicName":"Supply-side policy","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to upgrade an unreliable freight bridge would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A reliable bridge reduces transport delays → Inputs reach producers on time → Production interruptions decrease.\n\n2. Faster journeys lower fuel and driver costs → Distribution costs per unit fall → Firms may lower selling prices.\n\n3. Reliable access makes local investment more attractive → Firms may open or expand nearby → Demand for labour may rise → Local employment may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Construction is costly and may disrupt traffic → Current business costs may rise during the works.\n\n2. Benefits depend on whether enough firms use the bridge → Cost per user may be too high to justify the project.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0018","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to support sustainable growth through productive investment would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Productive investment increases capital equipment → More output can be produced → Potential economic growth rises.\n\n2. More efficient equipment uses fewer inputs per unit → Natural resources are conserved → Growth becomes less environmentally damaging.\n\n3. Expanding sustainable productive capacity creates jobs → Household incomes may rise → Tax receipts may increase → More resources become available for public services.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Growth may increase resource depletion and pollution → Non-material living standards may deteriorate.\n\n2. Benefits may be unevenly shared across households → Poverty may persist despite higher national output.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0019","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to use temporary targeted support during a recession would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Targeted support sustains incomes of affected households → Spending on necessities falls less → Recessionary falls in demand may be smaller.\n\n2. Assistance can keep viable firms operating temporarily → Avoidable business closures decrease → Existing jobs may be protected.\n\n3. Temporary measures can end when activity recovers → Permanent expenditure commitments are avoided → Future pressure on public finances is limited → More room remains for later economic emergencies.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Support may increase government borrowing → Interest costs and future tax burdens may rise.\n\n2. Poorly targeted support can prolong unproductive activity → Resources remain tied to low-productivity firms.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0020","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to finance efficient irrigation and technical training. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Efficient irrigation raises crop yields → Output per hectare increases → Farm incomes may rise.\n\n2. Technical training improves production methods → Workers use inputs more effectively → Average production costs may fall.\n\n3. Reliable irrigation reduces crop failure from dry conditions → Agricultural output becomes less variable → Food supply becomes more stable → Food-price fluctuations may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Irrigation projects may be expensive → Other productive projects may receive less finance.\n\n2. Water shortages could limit future expansion → Expected increases in crop output may not occur.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0021","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to introduce pollution controls during industrial growth. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Emission limits reduce harmful pollution → External environmental costs fall → Non-material living standards improve.\n\n2. Cleaner air reduces illness → Workers are absent less often → Labour productivity may improve.\n\n3. Pollution controls encourage cleaner technology → Firms reduce waste and harmful emissions → Environmental resources are better protected → Future productive capacity is more sustainable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Compliance costs may reduce firms' short-run profits → Firms may cut investment or raise prices.\n\n2. Weak monitoring can undermine the policy → Pollution may remain above the intended level.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0022","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.5","topicName":"Economic growth","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to combine temporary investment incentives with workforce training is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Investment incentives reduce the effective cost of equipment → Firms may increase capital investment → Productive capacity expands.\n\n2. Workforce training improves ability to use new machinery → Output per worker rises → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n3. Training and investment can target expanding industries → Workers gain skills for new equipment and jobs → Skills mismatches may decrease → Structural unemployment may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Tax relief reduces revenue and training costs money → Less revenue remains for other public services.\n\n2. Firms may not invest if expected demand remains weak → Capital formation may fail to increase.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0023","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to publish reliable labour-force survey results would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Surveys estimate the scale of unemployment → Policymakers can judge its severity → Support can be sized more appropriately.\n\n2. Data identify affected occupations and regions → Training and job support can be targeted → Public resources may be used more effectively.\n\n3. Repeated reliable surveys show trends over time → Policies can be compared with employment outcomes → Ineffective programmes can be changed → Future employment policy may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Surveys may miss discouraged workers outside the labour force → The wider shortage of employment may be understated.\n\n2. Rates alone do not show the quality of employment → Low-paid or insecure jobs may be overlooked.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0024","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to improve job-matching information services would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Vacancies are advertised to a wider range of workers → More workers discover suitable jobs → Frictional unemployment duration may decrease.\n\n2. Employers reach more suitable applicants → Recruitment becomes faster → Production bottlenecks from vacancies may fall.\n\n3. Wider information reveals jobs in other regions → Workers may consider relocating → Geographical mismatches can decrease → Labour resources are used more fully.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Maintaining matching services requires funding → Other public services face an opportunity cost.\n\n2. Some jobs require qualifications applicants do not possess → Structural unemployment may remain despite better information.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0025","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to retrain displaced textile workers for growing industries would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Retraining supplies skills required in expanding industries → More displaced workers qualify for vacancies → Structural unemployment may fall.\n\n2. New skills increase output per worker → Workers become more valuable to employers → Their earnings may rise.\n\n3. Employment replaces reliance on unemployment benefits → Government benefit expenditure may decrease → Income-tax receipts may rise → More funds remain for other services.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training may take years → Workers may remain without income while studying.\n\n2. Some workers cannot easily relocate to areas with vacancies → Job vacancies may remain geographically inaccessible.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0026","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.6","topicName":"Employment and unemployment","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to boost demand temporarily during a recession. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Higher spending increases demand for firms’ output → Firms increase production → Cyclical unemployment may decrease.\n\n2. More employment raises household income → Consumer expenditure may increase → Recovery in demand may be reinforced.\n\n3. Higher sales improve firms’ expected returns → Firms may resume investment → Demand for capital goods may increase → Future productive capacity may expand.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Government deficits can widen → Future debt interest may reduce fiscal room.\n\n2. Stimulus may be ineffective if households save extra income → Total demand may rise less than expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0027","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to keep inflation low and predictable. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Stable prices make future costs easier to predict → Firms can plan investment more confidently → Capital formation may increase.\n\n2. Low inflation protects the real value of fixed incomes → Households retain purchasing power → Material living standards are more secure.\n\n3. Fewer frequent price changes are necessary → Firms incur lower menu costs → Resources can be used for production instead → Efficiency may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Very tight policy may lower employment → Cyclical unemployment and poverty may rise.\n\n2. Global commodity-price shocks may be difficult to control → Inflation may remain above target despite domestic restraint.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0028","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to restrain excessive credit-financed consumer spending is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Tighter credit reduces borrowing-financed consumption → Total demand grows more slowly → Demand-pull inflationary pressure may fall.\n\n2. Slower debt growth reduces future repayment burdens → Households are less vulnerable to financial distress → Consumption may become more stable.\n\n3. Weaker demand for imports may follow lower consumption → Import spending may decrease → Current-account payments may fall → The current-account balance may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Reduced spending may slow growth → Cyclical unemployment may rise.\n\n2. Supply shortages may keep some prices elevated → Demand restraint may not remove cost-push inflation.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0029","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reduce dependence on expensive imported fuel would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Switching away from expensive fuel lowers input costs → Average production costs may fall → Cost-push inflationary pressure decreases.\n\n2. Lower fuel imports reduce foreign-currency spending → Import payments fall → The trade in goods balance may improve.\n\n3. More diverse energy sources reduce exposure to supply shocks → Production becomes less vulnerable to fuel shortages → Output and employment become more stable → Economic welfare may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Alternative energy projects require large initial investments → Other investment may have to be postponed.\n\n2. Transitioning away from imported fuel takes time → High imported fuel costs may persist initially.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C04-P2-M08-0030","c":3,"chapter":"Government and the Macroeconomy","topic":"4.7","topicName":"Inflation","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to publish transparent and regularly updated CPI data would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. CPI data reveal changes in consumer prices → Real wages can be compared with nominal wages → Households can assess purchasing power.\n\n2. Reliable inflation data guide monetary policy → Authorities can identify rising inflationary pressure → Policy responses can be better timed.\n\n3. Updated price information improves pay and benefit decisions → Adjustments can reflect changes in living costs → Severe losses of real income may be limited → Poverty among vulnerable households may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. A typical basket may not reflect every household's purchases → Some households’ cost of living may rise faster than measured inflation.\n\n2. Changes in product quality can complicate price comparisons → Real price changes may be misestimated.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes real gdp per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period.","The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.","Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."],"answer":3,"why":"Real GDP per head: Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Natural resource endowments","Real GDP per head","Death rate","Progressive tax and poverty"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates real gdp per head: Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of real gdp per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold.","A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave."],"answer":0,"why":"Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. This illustrates real gdp per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to real gdp per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty."],"answer":0,"why":"The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 18 births and 6 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["18","24","12","6"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 18 − 6 = 12 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Transport and electricity networks may improve.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","More children may access education."],"answer":1,"why":"The figure hides inequalities in income distribution."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn."],"answer":0,"why":"Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Social and workplace participation may improve.","New industries may become possible outside mining.","It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","New sectors may create skilled employment."],"answer":2,"why":"It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Firms may keep experienced employees.","More children may access education.","Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."],"answer":3,"why":"Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."],"answer":3,"why":"The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes human development index?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure.","Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty.","Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."],"answer":1,"why":"Human Development Index: The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and poverty","Human Development Index","Real GDP per head","Population age structure"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates human development index: The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 65 thousand people immigrate and 18 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["65 thousand","83 thousand","47 thousand","18 thousand"],"answer":2,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 65 − 18 = 47 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to human development index?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."],"answer":2,"why":"The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners.","Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Spending requirements can exceed fiscal capacity."],"answer":0,"why":"Health and education outcomes receive greater attention."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More learners may acquire employable skills.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Workers may use their skills more effectively."],"answer":1,"why":"A national average conceals regional inequalities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Remote households may still face travel barriers.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.","Governments may identify non-income development gaps.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"Governments may identify non-income development gaps."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.","Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Child poverty may fall in the short term.","The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes."],"answer":3,"why":"The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":0,"why":"Health and education outcomes receive greater attention."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."],"answer":1,"why":"The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $30 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$6,000","$1,200","$30,000","$30"],"answer":0,"why":"GDP per head = $30 billion / 5 million people = $6,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Life expectancy","Unemployment and poverty","Healthcare differences","Relative poverty"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates life expectancy: Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of life expectancy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months."],"answer":1,"why":"A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve. This illustrates life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to life expectancy?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential."],"answer":1,"why":"Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Public spending commitments may rise.","Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption."],"answer":1,"why":"Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Output per worker may increase.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.","Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."],"answer":3,"why":"Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["New sectors may create skilled employment.","Life expectancy and well-being may increase.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":2,"why":"Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 21 births and 9 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["30","9","21","12"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 21 − 9 = 12 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0031","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education indicator?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."],"answer":3,"why":"Education indicator: Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0032","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Non-material living standards","Low wages and poverty","Education indicator","Purchasing power"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates education indicator: Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0033","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education indicator?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water.","A country has a growing share of people aged over 65.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers."],"answer":3,"why":"A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. This illustrates years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0034","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education indicator?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."],"answer":2,"why":"Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0035","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide more affordable secondary school places. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New industries may need expensive infrastructure.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.","Forecasts about future population can be wrong.","More students may complete formal education."],"answer":3,"why":"More students may complete formal education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0036","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide more affordable secondary school places. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Preventable deaths may decline.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching."],"answer":1,"why":"Teacher shortages may reduce quality."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0037","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide more affordable secondary school places?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future workers may become more productive.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."],"answer":0,"why":"Future workers may become more productive."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0038","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 95 thousand people immigrate and 27 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["95 thousand","68 thousand","27 thousand","122 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 95 − 27 = 68 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0039","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide more affordable secondary school places. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","More students may complete formal education.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching."],"answer":2,"why":"More students may complete formal education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0040","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold."],"answer":2,"why":"Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0041","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."],"answer":3,"why":"Income inequality: Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0042","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Human Development Index","Income inequality","Education and poverty","Net migration"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates income inequality: Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0043","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure."],"answer":0,"why":"Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. This illustrates income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0044","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":2,"why":"Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0045","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Programmes require funding and trained staff.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0046","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $72 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$72,000","$1,125","$9,000","$72"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $72 billion / 8 million people = $9,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0047","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education.","The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0048","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.","Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0049","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Preventable deaths may decline.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Nutrition and health may improve.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0050","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."],"answer":0,"why":"Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0051","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes purchasing power?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes.","Income inequality describes how unevenly income is distributed between people or households.","Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued.","Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."],"answer":3,"why":"Purchasing power: Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0052","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Birth rate","Purchasing power","Productivity differences","Natural resource endowments"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates purchasing power: Purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0053","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of purchasing power?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%.","Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare."],"answer":1,"why":"A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. This illustrates purchasing power is the quantity of goods and services a given income can buy."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0054","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to purchasing power?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."],"answer":2,"why":"Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0055","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 24 births and 5 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["24","19","29","5"],"answer":1,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 24 − 5 = 19 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0056","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More funds may become available for business finance.","Support payments increase fiscal costs.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":1,"why":"Support payments increase fiscal costs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0057","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect low-income households during rapid inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue.","Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0058","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect low-income households during rapid inflation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Income inequality may decrease.","Workers may retain more productive working time.","Social and workplace participation may improve."],"answer":0,"why":"Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0059","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Families may maintain spending on essentials.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":0,"why":"Families may maintain spending on essentials."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0060","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."],"answer":0,"why":"Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0061","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes non-material living standards?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."],"answer":0,"why":"Non-material living standards: Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0062","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Population age structure","Population growth differences","Unemployment and poverty","Non-material living standards"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates non-material living standards: Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0063","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 125 thousand people immigrate and 36 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["89 thousand","36 thousand","125 thousand","161 thousand"],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 125 − 36 = 89 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0064","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to non-material living standards?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0065","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to include environmental quality when assessing development. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Healthcare programmes need investment.","Investment needs finance and technical skills."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0066","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to include environmental quality when assessing development. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."],"answer":0,"why":"Measuring non-material benefits is difficult."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0067","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to include environmental quality when assessing development?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."],"answer":3,"why":"Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0068","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to include environmental quality when assessing development less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Social and workplace participation may improve.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","Future workforce productivity may improve.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0069","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to include environmental quality when assessing development. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":2,"why":"Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0070","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues.","More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0071","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $10 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$2,500","$10","$5,000","$10,000"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $10 billion / 2 million people = $5,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0072","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Income inequality","Saving and investment","Progressive tax and poverty","International comparison limits"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates international comparison limits: Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0073","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of international comparison limits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector.","Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income."],"answer":0,"why":"Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access. This illustrates living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0074","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to international comparison limits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0075","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","Healthcare programmes need investment.","Pension and care expenditure may rise."],"answer":0,"why":"Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0076","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Data quality may differ between countries.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs."],"answer":1,"why":"Data quality may differ between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0077","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to compare several living-standard indicators across countries?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","Policy may target the most serious social gaps.","Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost."],"answer":2,"why":"Policy may target the most serious social gaps."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0078","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to compare several living-standard indicators across countries less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Income inequality may decrease.","A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise.","A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.","Workers may retain more productive working time."],"answer":1,"why":"A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0079","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Output per worker may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0080","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 17 births and 8 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["25","8","17","9"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 17 − 8 = 9 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0081","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes absolute poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions."],"answer":0,"why":"Absolute poverty: Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0082","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Absolute poverty","Natural resource endowments","Death rate","Net migration"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates absolute poverty: Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0083","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of absolute poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave.","Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water."],"answer":3,"why":"Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. This illustrates absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0084","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to absolute poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."],"answer":1,"why":"Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0085","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","Redistribution needs tax revenue.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Spending requirements can exceed fiscal capacity."],"answer":0,"why":"Nutrition and health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0086","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Providing services needs sustained funding.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."],"answer":2,"why":"Providing services needs sustained funding."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0087","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.","Children may be better able to attend school.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Children may be better able to attend school."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0088","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 155 thousand people immigrate and 45 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["110 thousand","45 thousand","155 thousand","200 thousand"],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 155 − 45 = 110 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0089","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Nutrition and health may improve.","Firms may keep experienced employees.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."],"answer":1,"why":"Nutrition and health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0090","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life."],"answer":0,"why":"Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0091","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes relative poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."],"answer":1,"why":"Relative poverty: Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0092","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sector composition","Non-material living standards","Natural resource endowments","Relative poverty"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates relative poverty: Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0093","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of relative poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. This illustrates relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0094","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to relative poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."],"answer":0,"why":"Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0095","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","More households may participate in education and community life.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","Housing and services may face extra demand."],"answer":1,"why":"More households may participate in education and community life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0096","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $40 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$8,000","$1,600","$40,000","$40"],"answer":0,"why":"GDP per head = $40 billion / 5 million people = $8,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0097","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide targeted assistance to low-income households?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Some families may still require children to work.","Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted."],"answer":0,"why":"Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0098","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to provide targeted assistance to low-income households less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Children may become healthier future workers.","The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise.","Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.","Policy may target the most serious social gaps."],"answer":1,"why":"The relative poverty threshold can rise as median incomes rise."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0099","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide targeted assistance to low-income households. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Output per worker may increase.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income."],"answer":2,"why":"More households may participate in education and community life."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0100","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."],"answer":3,"why":"Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0101","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes unemployment and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","Living standards also involve health, safety, leisure and environmental quality, not just output.","Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ."],"answer":0,"why":"Unemployment and poverty: Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0102","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Non-material living standards","Life expectancy","Unemployment and poverty","Relative poverty"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates unemployment and poverty: Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0103","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of unemployment and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%.","A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year.","A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income."],"answer":3,"why":"A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. This illustrates unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0104","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to unemployment and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure."],"answer":2,"why":"Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0105","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 20 births and 11 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["11","31","20","9"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 20 − 11 = 9 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0106","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","More funds may become available for business finance.","Government benefit expenditure may increase.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":2,"why":"Government benefit expenditure may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0107","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Better job matching may speed the return to work.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise."],"answer":1,"why":"Better job matching may speed the return to work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0108","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More people may remain able to work.","New sectors may create skilled employment.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."],"answer":2,"why":"New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0109","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching."],"answer":3,"why":"Families may maintain essential spending while searching."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0110","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income."],"answer":1,"why":"Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0111","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes low wages and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs.","Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","The Human Development Index combines indicators of health, education and income into a composite measure."],"answer":0,"why":"Low wages and poverty: Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0112","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Low wages and poverty","State benefits","Productivity differences","Real GDP per head"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates low wages and poverty: Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0113","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 185 thousand people immigrate and 54 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["239 thousand","131 thousand","185 thousand","54 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 185 − 54 = 131 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0114","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to low wages and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."],"answer":3,"why":"A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0115","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Programmes require funding and trained staff.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding."],"answer":0,"why":"Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0116","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":0,"why":"Some employers may reduce hours or hiring."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0117","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce a carefully set minimum wage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs.","Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0118","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce a carefully set minimum wage less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","New industries may become possible outside mining.","Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers outside formal employment may not benefit."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0119","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce a carefully set minimum wage. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."],"answer":3,"why":"Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0120","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Full-time farm workers struggle to pay for rent and childcare. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions."],"answer":2,"why":"A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0121","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $32 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$32,000","$500","$4,000","$32"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $32 billion / 8 million people = $4,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0122","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Progressive tax and poverty","Healthcare and poverty","Relative poverty","Purchasing power"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates healthcare and poverty: Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0123","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of healthcare and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months.","Two countries have similar GDP per head but different school attendance and healthcare access.","Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates."],"answer":0,"why":"An illness stops a self-employed driver working for three months. This illustrates poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0124","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to healthcare and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life."],"answer":2,"why":"Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0125","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Data quality may differ between countries.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","New industries may need expensive infrastructure.","Healthcare programmes need investment."],"answer":1,"why":"Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0126","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Public healthcare requires doctors and funding.","Output per worker may increase.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Public healthcare requires doctors and funding."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0127","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to make essential primary healthcare more accessible?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Workers may retain more productive working time.","Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Workers may retain more productive working time."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0128","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to make essential primary healthcare more accessible less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy may target the most serious social gaps.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days.","New sectors may create skilled employment.","Remote households may still face travel barriers."],"answer":3,"why":"Remote households may still face travel barriers."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0129","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","More children may access education.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Transport and electricity networks may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0130","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 23 births and 7 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["30","23","7","16"],"answer":3,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 23 − 7 = 16 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0131","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","Low wages may leave employed people unable to afford basic needs.","Living-standard comparisons can be misleading because national averages and measurement methods differ.","Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."],"answer":3,"why":"Education and poverty: Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0132","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Birth rate","Purchasing power","Education and poverty","Net migration"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates education and poverty: Limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0133","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment."],"answer":0,"why":"Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. This illustrates limited education can reduce access to well-paid employment and perpetuate poverty."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0134","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."],"answer":0,"why":"A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0135","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending."],"answer":2,"why":"More learners may acquire employable skills."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0136","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Preventable deaths may decline.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Work absences caused by illness may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Education benefits appear after a long delay."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0137","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.","Future wages and productivity may increase.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Future wages and productivity may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0138","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 215 thousand people immigrate and 63 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["63 thousand","152 thousand","215 thousand","278 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 215 − 63 = 152 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0139","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More learners may acquire employable skills.","Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","More funds may become available for business finance."],"answer":0,"why":"More learners may acquire employable skills."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0140","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."],"answer":3,"why":"A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0141","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes state benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.","State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."],"answer":3,"why":"State benefits: State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0142","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Education and poverty","State benefits","Low wages and poverty","Purchasing power"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates state benefits: State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0143","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of state benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","Country A produces $60 billion of real output for 6 million residents.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold."],"answer":3,"why":"A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. This illustrates state benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0144","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to state benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0145","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment needs finance and technical skills.","Pension and care expenditure may rise.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."],"answer":3,"why":"Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0146","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $14 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$14","$14,000","$7,000","$3,500"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $14 billion / 2 million people = $7,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0147","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","The index omits some environmental and freedom-related outcomes.","Child poverty may fall in the short term."],"answer":3,"why":"Child poverty may fall in the short term."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0148","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future workforce productivity may improve.","Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.","Incorrect eligibility decisions may exclude needy households.","Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents."],"answer":2,"why":"Incorrect eligibility decisions may exclude needy households."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0149","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","More students may complete formal education.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."],"answer":3,"why":"Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0150","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues.","Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."],"answer":2,"why":"Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0151","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes progressive tax and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there.","Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.","Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society."],"answer":2,"why":"Progressive tax and poverty: Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0152","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Progressive tax and poverty","Life expectancy","Emigration","Healthcare and poverty"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates progressive tax and poverty: Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0153","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of progressive tax and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers.","A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates."],"answer":2,"why":"A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. This illustrates progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0154","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to progressive tax and poverty?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0155","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 26 births and 10 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["10","36","16","26"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 26 − 10 = 16 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0156","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."],"answer":0,"why":"High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0157","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some families may still require children to work.","Income inequality may decrease.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue."],"answer":1,"why":"Income inequality may decrease."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0158","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Future workforce productivity may improve.","Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness.","Social and workplace participation may improve.","Children may become healthier future workers."],"answer":1,"why":"Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0159","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Families may maintain spending on essentials.","More children may access education.","Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs."],"answer":3,"why":"Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0160","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","Growth in averages need not improve the living standard of every group.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0161","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes birth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year.","Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued."],"answer":2,"why":"Birth rate: The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0162","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Absolute poverty","Natural resource endowments","Birth rate","Low wages and poverty"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates birth rate: The birth rate measures live births per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0163","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 245 thousand people immigrate and 72 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["173 thousand","317 thousand","72 thousand","245 thousand"],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 245 − 72 = 173 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0164","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to birth rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities.","More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","The $10,000 figure estimates average real output per resident, not each person's income.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential."],"answer":1,"why":"More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0165","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Support payments increase fiscal costs.","Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Infant and maternal health may improve.","Public healthcare requires doctors and funding."],"answer":2,"why":"Infant and maternal health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0166","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy choices may better reflect health and quality of life.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Healthcare programmes need investment.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output."],"answer":2,"why":"Healthcare programmes need investment."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0167","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on the quality of public services.","Children may become healthier future workers.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise."],"answer":1,"why":"Children may become healthier future workers."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0168","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks.","Income inequality may decrease.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth."],"answer":2,"why":"A growing child population also raises schooling costs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0169","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Analysts can identify strengths and weaknesses not shown by income.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Infant and maternal health may improve."],"answer":3,"why":"Infant and maternal health may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0170","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Insufficient basic consumption directly damages health and welfare.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."],"answer":0,"why":"More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0171","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $15 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$3,000","$15,000","$15","$600"],"answer":0,"why":"GDP per head = $15 billion / 5 million people = $3,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0172","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Education differences","Purchasing power","Income inequality","Death rate"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates death rate: The death rate measures deaths per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0173","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of death rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers.","A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff."],"answer":2,"why":"A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year. This illustrates the death rate measures deaths per thousand people in the population in a year."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0174","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to death rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."],"answer":0,"why":"Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0175","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment needs finance and technical skills.","Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.","Data quality may differ between countries.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Preventable deaths may decline."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0176","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Programmes require funding and trained staff.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output.","Output per worker may increase.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods."],"answer":0,"why":"Programmes require funding and trained staff."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0177","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","More people may remain able to work.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."],"answer":2,"why":"More people may remain able to work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0178","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Lower real-income losses may reduce poverty risks.","An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high.","Future workers may become more productive."],"answer":2,"why":"An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0179","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More students may complete formal education.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Preventable deaths may decline."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0180","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 19 births and 6 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["25","6","13","19"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 19 − 6 = 13 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0181","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period.","Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","The death rate measures deaths per thousand people in the population in a year."],"answer":0,"why":"Net migration: Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0182","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Birth rate","Relative poverty","Real GDP per head","Net migration"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates net migration: Net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0183","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common.","A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave.","A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year.","A factory closure leaves several households without their main source of income."],"answer":1,"why":"A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. This illustrates net migration is immigration minus emigration over a period."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0184","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."],"answer":3,"why":"A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0185","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Public spending commitments may rise.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."],"answer":3,"why":"Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0186","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Housing and services may face extra demand.","More funds may become available for business finance."],"answer":2,"why":"Housing and services may face extra demand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0187","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","Remote households may still face travel barriers."],"answer":2,"why":"Tax revenues may increase as migrants work."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0188","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 275 thousand people immigrate and 81 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["81 thousand","356 thousand","194 thousand","275 thousand"],"answer":2,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 275 − 81 = 194 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0189","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Work absences caused by illness may fall."],"answer":0,"why":"Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0190","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country receives 100,000 arrivals while 70,000 residents leave. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities."],"answer":0,"why":"A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0191","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes immigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.","Progressive taxation can finance redistribution by taking a larger income share from higher earners.","Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."],"answer":3,"why":"Immigration: Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0192","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Optimum population","Immigration","Education and poverty","Purchasing power"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates immigration: Immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0193","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of immigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","Average incomes rise in a city, but most extra income goes to its highest earners.","A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure."],"answer":0,"why":"A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. This illustrates immigration occurs when people enter a country to live there."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0194","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to immigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."],"answer":3,"why":"New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0195","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to provide integration and language support for new migrants. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Higher saving can reduce current consumption.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Providing services needs sustained funding."],"answer":2,"why":"Workers may use their skills more effectively."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0196","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $48 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$750","$6,000","$48","$48,000"],"answer":1,"why":"GDP per head = $48 billion / 8 million people = $6,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0197","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to provide integration and language support for new migrants?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Social and workplace participation may improve."],"answer":3,"why":"Social and workplace participation may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0198","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to provide integration and language support for new migrants less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may become more competitive abroad.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days.","Governments may identify non-income development gaps."],"answer":1,"why":"Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0199","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to provide integration and language support for new migrants. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","More children may access education.","Workers may use their skills more effectively."],"answer":3,"why":"Workers may use their skills more effectively."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0200","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A city attracts healthcare workers from abroad. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Tax-and-transfer arrangements can narrow differences in disposable income.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":2,"why":"New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0201","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes emigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development.","Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Absolute poverty means lacking the resources to meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water."],"answer":1,"why":"Emigration: Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0202","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Saving and investment","Absolute poverty","Emigration","Death rate"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates emigration: Emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0203","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of emigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers.","A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%."],"answer":1,"why":"Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. This illustrates emigration occurs when residents leave their country to live abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0204","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to emigration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."],"answer":1,"why":"Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0205","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 22 births and 9 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["13","9","31","22"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 22 − 9 = 13 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0206","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More students may complete formal education.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Higher public and private wage costs may follow.","Nutrition and health may improve."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher public and private wage costs may follow."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0207","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","The tax base may be more stable."],"answer":3,"why":"The tax base may be more stable."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0208","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Life expectancy and well-being may increase.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Workers may retain more productive working time.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."],"answer":1,"why":"Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0209","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","Firms may keep experienced employees.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","More learners may acquire employable skills."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may keep experienced employees."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0210","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0211","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes population age structure?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions.","Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups.","Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."],"answer":2,"why":"Population age structure: Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0212","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Death rate","Progressive tax and poverty","Non-material living standards","Population age structure"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates population age structure: Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0213","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 305 thousand people immigrate and 90 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["305 thousand","395 thousand","90 thousand","215 thousand"],"answer":3,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 305 − 90 = 215 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0214","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to population age structure?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."],"answer":3,"why":"Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0215","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to adapt public services for an ageing population. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Government benefit expenditure may increase.","Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending."],"answer":0,"why":"Older people may receive more suitable healthcare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0216","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to adapt public services for an ageing population. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Families may maintain spending on essentials.","Pension and care expenditure may rise.","Lower-income families may afford more essential goods.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees."],"answer":1,"why":"Pension and care expenditure may rise."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0217","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to adapt public services for an ageing population?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted.","Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs.","Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents."],"answer":3,"why":"Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0218","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to adapt public services for an ageing population less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher earnings could improve household nutrition and housing.","Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue.","Healthier workers may miss fewer working days."],"answer":2,"why":"A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0219","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to adapt public services for an ageing population. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.","More students may complete formal education.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare."],"answer":3,"why":"Older people may receive more suitable healthcare."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0220","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country has a growing share of people aged over 65. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Relative deprivation can restrict participation in ordinary social and economic activities.","Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":1,"why":"Ageing can raise demand for healthcare and pensions relative to some tax revenues."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0221","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $18 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$18","$4,500","$9,000","$18,000"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $18 billion / 2 million people = $9,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0222","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Productivity differences","International comparison limits","Optimum population","Healthcare differences"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates optimum population: An optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0223","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of optimum population?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates."],"answer":2,"why":"A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure. This illustrates an optimum population is a population size consistent with the highest potential output or income per person under given conditions."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0224","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to optimum population?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher money incomes can coexist with reduced well-being.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime."],"answer":2,"why":"Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0225","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan population and infrastructure together. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Data quality may differ between countries."],"answer":2,"why":"Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0226","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to plan population and infrastructure together. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More students may complete formal education.","Forecasts about future population can be wrong.","Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Work absences caused by illness may fall."],"answer":1,"why":"Forecasts about future population can be wrong."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0227","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to plan population and infrastructure together?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Benefits depend on the quality of public services.","Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors.","Automation may displace some existing jobs.","A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively."],"answer":3,"why":"A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0228","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to plan population and infrastructure together less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.","Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth.","Future workforce productivity may improve.","Children may be better able to attend school."],"answer":0,"why":"Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0229","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to plan population and infrastructure together. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":3,"why":"Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0230","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 25 births and 5 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["20","5","30","25"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 25 − 5 = 20 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0231","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes productivity differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels.","Real GDP per head is inflation-adjusted total domestic output divided by population.","Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."],"answer":0,"why":"Productivity differences: Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0232","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Immigration","Population growth differences","Productivity differences","Birth rate"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates productivity differences: Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0233","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of productivity differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","A household can buy basic food but cannot afford services considered normal in its community.","A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families."],"answer":0,"why":"Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. This illustrates differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0234","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to productivity differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal."],"answer":2,"why":"More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0235","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Government benefit expenditure may increase.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Output per worker may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Output per worker may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0236","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Preventable deaths may decline.","Investment needs finance and technical skills.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand."],"answer":1,"why":"Investment needs finance and technical skills."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0237","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may become more competitive abroad.","It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may become more competitive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0238","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 335 thousand people immigrate and 99 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["99 thousand","434 thousand","236 thousand","335 thousand"],"answer":2,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 335 − 99 = 236 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0239","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Analysts can compare average economic output across populations.","Output per worker may increase.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":1,"why":"Output per worker may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0240","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Country C's factories use modern machines while Country D's rely on outdated equipment. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head.","Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head."],"answer":2,"why":"More productive inputs can support higher wages and output per head."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0241","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes saving and investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor health can lower earning ability and impose medical expenses on families.","Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital.","Years of schooling are used within the education dimension of human development.","The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."],"answer":1,"why":"Saving and investment: Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0242","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Saving and investment","Real GDP per head","Education differences","Non-material living standards"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates saving and investment: Saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0243","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of saving and investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure.","Young engineers move overseas after receiving attractive job offers.","A sparsely populated resource-rich region lacks sufficient workers to use its infrastructure.","A country records 20 births per 1,000 inhabitants in a year."],"answer":0,"why":"A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. This illustrates saving provides funds that may finance investment in productive capital."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0244","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to saving and investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0245","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More funds may become available for business finance.","Measuring non-material benefits is difficult.","Housing and services may face extra demand.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution."],"answer":0,"why":"More funds may become available for business finance."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0246","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $25 billion and a population of 5 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$25,000","$5,000","$25","$1,000"],"answer":1,"why":"GDP per head = $25 billion / 5 million people = $5,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0247","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare.","A growing child population also raises schooling costs.","Capital formation may raise future output.","Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult."],"answer":2,"why":"Capital formation may raise future output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0248","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Communities may use existing skills and experience of older residents.","Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds.","Social and workplace participation may improve.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work."],"answer":1,"why":"Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0249","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","More households may participate in education and community life.","More funds may become available for business finance."],"answer":3,"why":"More funds may become available for business finance."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0250","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A low-income country has limited domestic saving and outdated infrastructure. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Increasing population can initially improve resource use, but overcrowding may later reduce output per head.","A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Insufficient investment can slow improvements in capital and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0251","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes education differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels.","Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity.","Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."],"answer":1,"why":"Education differences: Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0252","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Education differences","Absolute poverty","Education indicator","Birth rate"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates education differences: Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0253","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of education differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates.","Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","Residents earn more after a factory opens but face noisier streets and worse air quality.","A government provides child-support payments to households below an income threshold."],"answer":0,"why":"One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. This illustrates unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0254","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to education differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty."],"answer":2,"why":"Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0255","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 18 births and 8 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["10","18","26","8"],"answer":0,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 18 − 8 = 10 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0256","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand quality education in lower-income communities. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","More households may participate in education and community life.","More learners may acquire employable skills.","Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly."],"answer":3,"why":"Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0257","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand quality education in lower-income communities?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad.","Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness.","Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities.","Some families may still require children to work."],"answer":2,"why":"Young people may gain better-paid employment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0258","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand quality education in lower-income communities less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A suitable workforce can help resources be used productively.","The tax base may be more stable.","Future workers may become more productive.","Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs."],"answer":3,"why":"Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0259","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand quality education in lower-income communities. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Nutrition and health may improve.","Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","A larger skilled workforce may raise output."],"answer":3,"why":"A larger skilled workforce may raise output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0260","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: One country offers broad secondary education while another has high school dropout rates. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Changes in mortality affect total population and can reflect health conditions or age structure.","Departures can reduce domestic labour supply, though remittances may support families."],"answer":0,"why":"Differences in human capital affect productivity and earning potential."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0261","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes healthcare differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Relative poverty refers to having much lower resources than the typical standard in a society.","State benefits transfer resources to eligible people, often to support those on low incomes.","Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Healthcare differences: Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0262","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Healthcare differences","Healthcare and poverty","Death rate","Immigration"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates healthcare differences: Unequal healthcare access can contribute to differences in development and productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0263","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 365 thousand people immigrate and 108 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["365 thousand","473 thousand","108 thousand","257 thousand"],"answer":3,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 365 − 108 = 257 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0264","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to healthcare differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More births can increase the number of children relative to working-age residents.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","Education can raise knowledge, skills and future earning potential.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0265","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Providing services needs sustained funding.","A national average conceals regional inequalities.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","Healthcare programmes need investment."],"answer":2,"why":"Work absences caused by illness may fall."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0266","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","Healthcare projects need recurrent spending.","Analysts can compare average economic output across populations."],"answer":2,"why":"Healthcare projects need recurrent spending."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0267","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Life expectancy and well-being may increase.","Workers outside formal employment may not benefit.","Some families may still require children to work.","Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs."],"answer":0,"why":"Life expectancy and well-being may increase."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0268","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits.","Tax revenues may increase as migrants work.","Transfers can reduce hardship and support participation in education.","Policy may target the most serious social gaps."],"answer":0,"why":"Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0269","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Work absences caused by illness may fall.","More households may participate in education and community life.","Families may afford more nutritious food and school supplies."],"answer":1,"why":"Work absences caused by illness may fall."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0270","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Workers in one country miss many days because preventable diseases are common. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."],"answer":3,"why":"Better health can extend working lives and improve labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0271","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $64 billion and a population of 8 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$64,000","$64","$8,000","$1,000"],"answer":2,"why":"GDP per head = $64 billion / 8 million people = $8,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0272","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Saving and investment","Sector composition","Emigration","Productivity differences"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates sector composition: The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0273","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of sector composition?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Young adults without qualifications have difficulty entering skilled occupations.","Country B reports life expectancy, years of schooling and national income per head.","A finance ministry uses income-tax receipts to support low-income families.","One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector."],"answer":3,"why":"One economy relies heavily on commodity farming while another has a diverse services sector. This illustrates the balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0274","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to sector composition?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs.","Real purchasing power falls when prices grow faster than nominal income.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","The HDI gives a broader view of development than output alone."],"answer":0,"why":"Sectoral structure affects exposure to commodity shocks and opportunities for higher-value jobs."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0275","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Healthcare programmes need investment.","The figure hides inequalities in income distribution.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price.","Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding."],"answer":2,"why":"The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0276","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["New industries may need expensive infrastructure.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","Transport and housing capacity may keep pace with demand.","Firms may keep experienced employees."],"answer":0,"why":"New industries may need expensive infrastructure."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0277","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage.","Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost.","Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","New sectors may create skilled employment."],"answer":3,"why":"New sectors may create skilled employment."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0278","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Reduced financial exclusion can support opportunity.","Social and workplace participation may improve.","Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors.","Governments may identify non-income development gaps."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0279","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Illness may be treated earlier and at lower cost.","Families may maintain essential spending while searching.","Workers may use their skills more effectively.","The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."],"answer":3,"why":"The economy may be less exposed to one commodity price."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0280","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country records 21 births and 11 deaths per 1,000 population in a year. Ignoring migration, what is the natural population increase per 1,000?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["11","21","10","32"],"answer":2,"why":"Natural increase = birth rate − death rate = 21 − 11 = 10 per 1,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0281","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes natural resource endowments?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development.","Unemployment can cause poverty by removing earnings from work.","Unequal access to education can cause persistent gaps in workforce skills between countries.","Population age structure is the distribution of people across different age groups."],"answer":0,"why":"Natural resource endowments: The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0282","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Natural resource endowments","Healthcare differences","Unemployment and poverty","Population growth differences"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates natural resource endowments: The quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0283","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of natural resource endowments?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country has a growing share of people aged over 65.","A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff.","A worker's wage rises by 2% while consumer prices rise by 5%.","A country records eight deaths per 1,000 inhabitants in a year."],"answer":1,"why":"A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. This illustrates the quantity and quality of natural resources can influence productive capacity and development."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0284","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to natural resource endowments?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use.","A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","Economic indicators require interpretation alongside social conditions."],"answer":1,"why":"Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0285","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transfer payments may require higher taxation.","Education benefits appear after a long delay.","Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Public healthcare requires doctors and funding."],"answer":2,"why":"Transport and electricity networks may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0286","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Nutrition and health may improve.","World commodity prices are volatile.","Pay may rise for some low-wage employees.","Preventable deaths may decline."],"answer":1,"why":"World commodity prices are volatile."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0287","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","Weak distribution systems may prevent aid reaching people.","New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn.","New industries may become possible outside mining."],"answer":3,"why":"New industries may become possible outside mining."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0288","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"In one year, 395 thousand people immigrate and 117 thousand emigrate. What is net migration?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["512 thousand","278 thousand","395 thousand","117 thousand"],"answer":1,"why":"Net migration = immigration − emigration = 395 − 117 = 278 thousand."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0289","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Transport and electricity networks may improve.","Infant and maternal health may improve.","Extra resources may reach households with urgent needs.","Fewer preventable deaths may improve welfare."],"answer":0,"why":"Transport and electricity networks may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0290","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country has major copper reserves but limited roads and technical staff. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","A small wage relative to living costs can produce in-work poverty.","Lost income and treatment costs can deepen poverty.","Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."],"answer":3,"why":"Resources may support export revenue if infrastructure and institutions enable efficient use."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0291","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes population growth differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Differences in output per worker help explain why countries have different income levels.","Life expectancy indicates the average years a newborn would live if current mortality patterns continued.","Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services.","The balance between primary, secondary and tertiary production varies with development."],"answer":2,"why":"Population growth differences: Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0292","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Relative poverty","Population growth differences","Purchasing power","Life expectancy"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates population growth differences: Rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0293","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of population growth differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year.","A country's life expectancy rises after drinking-water systems and clinics improve.","Some families cannot afford enough nutritious meals or safe drinking water.","A country expands access to secondary schooling for rural teenagers."],"answer":0,"why":"A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. This illustrates rapid population growth can affect income per head and demands on public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0294","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to population growth differences?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A lack of marketable skills can restrict earnings over a lifetime.","Better health conditions can improve longevity and support quality of life.","Transfers can immediately increase disposable income and reduce hardship.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."],"answer":3,"why":"Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0295","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More children may access education.","Teacher shortages may reduce quality.","Some employers may reduce hours or hiring.","Government benefit expenditure may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"More children may access education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0296","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country has a real GDP of $8 billion and a population of 2 million. What is real GDP per head?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$8,000","$4,000","$2,000","$8"],"answer":1,"why":"GDP per head = $8 billion / 2 million people = $4,000."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0297","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply.","Future workforce productivity may improve.","Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation.","Remote households may still face travel barriers."],"answer":1,"why":"Future workforce productivity may improve."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0298","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Communities could avoid some hidden costs of growth.","Benefits depend on the quality of public services.","The tax base may be more stable.","Changes over time can help monitor average material prosperity."],"answer":1,"why":"Benefits depend on the quality of public services."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0299","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Health and education outcomes receive greater attention.","Older people may receive more suitable healthcare.","More children may access education.","Vacancies in shortage occupations may be filled."],"answer":2,"why":"More children may access education."},{"id":"0455-C05-P1-MCQ-0300","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country's output grows 3% while population grows 4% in the same year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A positive net migration of 30,000 increases population, other things equal.","Lost wages reduce disposable income and may push families below a poverty threshold.","Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output.","New arrivals can increase labour supply and demand for local goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Real GDP per head may fall if population grows faster than output."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define real GDP per head. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Inflation-adjusted GDP / GDP at constant prices (1); Divided by population / average real output per person (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define the Human Development Index (HDI). [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A composite measure of living standards / development (1); Combining income, education and life expectancy (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define life expectancy. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Average number of years a newborn is expected to live (1); Based on current mortality rates / patterns (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how years of schooling can be used as an indicator of living standards. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• More years of schooling indicate greater educational attainment (1); This may improve skills and future employment opportunities (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Schooling measures access to education beyond income alone (1); Countries with similar GDP per head may have different educational living standards (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define income inequality. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Uneven distribution of income (1); Between individuals / households or groups in a population (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Identify two aspects of non-material living standards. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Award 1 mark for each of two distinct answers from:\n• Health (1)\n• Safety (1)\n• Leisure time (1)\n• Environmental quality (1)","note":"Any two valid answers; do not count synonymous alternatives twice. Credit other accurate answers within the question’s scope."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain one reason why comparing living standards using national averages may be misleading. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• National averages hide the distribution of income (1); High average income may coexist with widespread poverty (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Average income omits non-material welfare (1); Equal incomes do not ensure equal health, leisure or environmental quality (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define absolute poverty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Insufficient income / resources (1); To meet basic needs such as food, shelter and clean water (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define relative poverty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Low income compared with others in the same society (1); Inability to afford its usual / average standard of living (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how unemployment may cause poverty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Losing a job removes wage income (1); A household may no longer afford basic necessities (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Unemployment benefits may be lower than previous earnings (1); Household income may fall below its society’s usual standard (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how poor health may cause poverty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Illness restricts the ability to work (1); Earnings may fall below the level needed for essentials (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Medical treatment is costly (1); Less household income remains for food and shelter (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how limited education may cause poverty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Limited education reduces skills and qualifications (1); Fewer well-paid jobs are accessible (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Poorly qualified workers face higher unemployment risk (1); Without earnings they may not meet basic needs (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define state benefits. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Government payments to eligible people (1); Not made in return for current production / work (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how progressive taxation may help reduce poverty. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Higher-income people pay a larger share of income (1); Disposable income inequality may decrease (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Tax receipts fund transfers or services for poor households (1); Their resources for basic needs increase (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define birth rate. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Number of live births in a year (1); Per 1,000 people in the population (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define net migration. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Immigration (1); Minus emigration over a period (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define immigration. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• People entering a country (1); To live there / settle from another country (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define emigration. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• People leaving a country (1); To live in another country (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define population age structure. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Distribution of population (1); Between different age groups (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define optimum population. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Population size (1); At which output / income per head is maximised under existing conditions (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how saving may finance investment. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Savings are deposited in financial institutions (1); Banks can lend these funds to firms buying capital goods (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Firms retain part of their income (1); Retained funds can be used to buy productive equipment (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how differences in access to education may cause differences in economic development between countries. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Better access to education improves workforce skills (1); Higher productivity can raise income per head (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Limited education restricts access to skilled jobs (1); Low earnings may limit saving and investment (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how differences in healthcare may cause differences in economic development between countries. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Better healthcare reduces illness (1); Workers can produce more through improved health and attendance (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Poor healthcare increases avoidable illness (1); Lower earning ability and higher treatment costs may restrict development (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how the relative importance of economic sectors may differ between countries at different levels of development. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Lower-income economies often rely more on primary activities (1); Higher-income economies tend to have larger service sectors (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Development supports manufacturing and service expansion (1); Employment can shift away from agriculture (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M02-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how natural resource endowments may affect economic development. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Natural resources provide inputs and possible export earnings (1); Receipts can finance capital investment and development (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Scarce or depleted resources constrain production (1); Growth may require imported inputs or alternative industries (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Real GDP removes the effect of inflation (1) → Output changes are separated from price changes (1) → Growth comparisons become more meaningful (1).\n\n2. Dividing by population measures average output per person (1) → Population growth is taken into account (1) → Changes in average material living standards are clearer (1).\n\n3. Other indicators reveal health and education outcomes (1) → Weaknesses hidden by average income can be identified (1) → Policies can target those weaknesses (1) → Economic welfare may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. HDI includes health as well as income (1) → Low life expectancy becomes visible (1) → Healthcare needs can be prioritised (1).\n\n2. HDI includes education outcomes (1) → Schooling weaknesses can be identified (1) → Education resources can be targeted (1).\n\n3. Comparing HDI with GDP per head reveals uneven development (1) → High income alone is not assumed to mean high welfare (1) → Spending can address missing social improvements (1) → Living standards may improve more broadly (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Prevention reduces incidence of disease (1) → Fewer people die prematurely (1) → Life expectancy may rise (1).\n\n2. Healthier workers lose fewer working days (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → National output may rise (1).\n\n3. Fewer serious illnesses require costly treatment (1) → Healthcare resources are released (1) → More patients can be treated from the same budget (1) → Access to care may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers provide more affordable secondary school places. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Lower school costs allow poorer pupils to enrol (1) → Education participation rises (1) → Inequality of opportunity may fall (1).\n\n2. More education improves workforce skills (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1) → Future earnings may increase (1).\n\n3. Qualifications widen the range of available occupations (1) → Occupational mobility improves (1) → Skills mismatches may decrease (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Transfers increase poorer households’ disposable income (1) → More basic necessities become affordable (1) → Absolute poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Lower-income households gain relatively more income (1) → Disposable income differences narrow (1) → Relative poverty may fall (1).\n\n3. Better access to food and healthcare improves health (1) → Adults can work more reliably (1) → Labour earnings may improve (1) → Dependence on transfers may decrease over time (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Support offsets rises in essential living costs (1) → Households retain access to food and shelter (1) → Absolute poverty may be limited (1).\n\n2. Targeting households on fixed or low incomes protects purchasing power (1) → Real income losses are reduced (1) → Material living standards are better maintained (1).\n\n3. Maintaining essential consumption supports local firms (1) → Sales fall less sharply (1) → Firms may retain workers (1) → Further poverty from job loss may be avoided (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to include environmental quality when assessing development might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Environmental indicators reveal pollution missed by GDP (1) → Non-material losses are recognised (1) → Development comparisons become more complete (1).\n\n2. Evidence of environmental harm guides regulation (1) → Harmful emissions may decrease (1) → Health and environmental quality may improve (1).\n\n3. Resource depletion can be identified early (1) → Policies can encourage sustainable use (1) → Natural productive resources last longer (1) → Future living standards are better protected (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. GDP per head indicates average material output (1) → HDI adds education and health information (1) → Comparisons cover more dimensions of welfare (1).\n\n2. Income-distribution measures reveal inequality (1) → Poverty hidden by high national averages becomes visible (1) → Assistance can be targeted more effectively (1).\n\n3. Environmental and leisure indicators capture non-material welfare (1) → Similar incomes are not assumed to imply equal wellbeing (1) → Policy priorities can reflect wider needs (1) → Economic development can be judged more accurately (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Food access improves nutrition (1) → Fewer people suffer hunger (1) → Absolute poverty decreases (1).\n\n2. Safe water reduces waterborne illness (1) → Households need less medical treatment (1) → Disposable resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Better nutrition and health improve ability to work (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1) → Household earnings may increase (1) → Families may be less trapped in poverty (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to provide targeted assistance to low-income households could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Assistance increases income available for essentials (1) → More households meet basic needs (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Means-tested support directs spending to those in need (1) → Less funding subsidises higher-income groups (1) → A given budget can assist more poor households (1).\n\n3. Support can finance travel or training for work (1) → Job-search barriers decrease (1) → More people may obtain employment (1) → Future wage income may reduce poverty (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Job-search support reveals suitable vacancies (1) → Workers find employment faster (1) → Frictional unemployment decreases (1).\n\n2. Temporary income assistance maintains essential consumption (1) → Families can meet basic needs while searching (1) → Absolute poverty may be avoided (1).\n\n3. Employment increases earnings and tax payments (1) → Reliance on benefits may fall (1) → Public funds become available for other uses (1) → Anti-poverty support may become more sustainable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to introduce a carefully set minimum wage might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A binding wage floor raises covered workers’ pay (1) → Disposable income increases (1) → In-work poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Better wages may improve motivation and retention (1) → Turnover and absenteeism may decline (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1).\n\n3. A carefully chosen rate limits pressure on employers’ costs (1) → Fewer jobs may be lost than with a very high floor (1) → More workers retain employment while gaining pay (1) → Household living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to make essential primary healthcare more accessible. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Accessible care treats illness earlier (1) → Serious health problems may be prevented (1) → Households face fewer major medical bills (1).\n\n2. Better health allows more regular employment (1) → Working days and earnings may increase (1) → Poverty may fall (1).\n\n3. Lower treatment charges leave more disposable income (1) → Families can afford food and shelter more easily (1) → Basic needs are better met (1) → Absolute poverty may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Subsidies reduce schooling costs (1) → More low-income learners can attend (1) → Education access becomes more equal (1).\n\n2. Vocational courses supply job-relevant skills (1) → More learners meet employers’ requirements (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n3. Better qualifications raise potential earnings (1) → Families can buy more necessities (1) → Saving may increase (1) → Poverty may be reduced across generations (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Child benefits raise eligible families’ disposable income (1) → More food and clothing become affordable (1) → Child poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Support helps pay school-related costs (1) → Children can attend education more consistently (1) → Future earning prospects may improve (1).\n\n3. Better nutrition supports children’s health (1) → Illness and absence from school may fall (1) → Skills development becomes more reliable (1) → Future productivity may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Progressive taxation places a larger income-share burden on higher earners (1) → Disposable income inequality narrows (1) → Relative poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Tax revenue funds payments to poor households (1) → Their disposable income increases (1) → Basic needs become more affordable (1).\n\n3. Revenue can finance free education and healthcare (1) → Poorer households gain skills and health (1) → Employability and productivity may rise (1) → Long-term poverty may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Maternal care reduces preventable deaths in childbirth (1) → More mothers survive (1) → Family welfare improves (1).\n\n2. Child healthcare reduces infant illness and mortality (1) → More children survive to adulthood (1) → Life expectancy may rise (1).\n\n3. Better family health reduces treatment costs and caring time (1) → Adults can participate more consistently in work (1) → Household earnings may increase (1) → Poverty may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Prevention reduces exposure to disease (1) → Premature deaths decrease (1) → Life expectancy may increase (1).\n\n2. Healthier adults work more regularly (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → National income may increase (1).\n\n3. Households spend less on avoidable illness (1) → More income remains for food and education (1) → Children’s development may improve (1) → Future productive potential may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Immigration supplies workers for unfilled skilled jobs (1) → Skills shortages decrease (1) → Firms can expand production (1).\n\n2. Needed migrants may pay taxes (1) → Government revenue increases (1) → More services can be financed (1).\n\n3. Migrants bring knowledge and experience (1) → Local workers can learn new techniques (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Potential output may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to provide integration and language support for new migrants could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Language support improves communication with employers (1) → Migrants can apply for a wider range of jobs (1) → Skills are less likely to be wasted (1).\n\n2. Integration support helps workers understand qualifications and job requirements (1) → Suitable vacancies become easier to access (1) → Unemployment among migrants may fall (1).\n\n3. Better employment raises migrants’ earned incomes (1) → Reliance on income support may decrease (1) → Tax receipts may increase (1) → Pressure on government finances may ease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better domestic pay and career prospects reduce incentives to emigrate (1) → More skilled workers remain (1) → Labour shortages may decrease (1).\n\n2. Retaining trained workers preserves human capital (1) → Firms maintain productive skills (1) → National output may be higher (1).\n\n3. Publicly funded training benefits the domestic economy for longer (1) → More trained workers pay domestic taxes (1) → Public revenue may increase (1) → Education and healthcare budgets become easier to sustain (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to adapt public services for an ageing population might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More age-appropriate healthcare meets older people’s needs (1) → Untreated illness may decrease (1) → Non-material living standards improve (1).\n\n2. Accessible transport and facilities support independence (1) → Older people can reach services more easily (1) → Social welfare may increase (1).\n\n3. Suitable support reduces unpaid caring demands on families (1) → Working-age carers may work more hours (1) → Labour-force participation may increase (1) → Household earnings and tax revenue may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to plan population and infrastructure together. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Forecasting population identifies future service demand (1) → Schools and hospitals can be sized appropriately (1) → Overcrowding may be avoided (1).\n\n2. Infrastructure can expand with labour supply (1) → Workers can reach jobs and use productive equipment (1) → Output per person may improve (1).\n\n3. Coordinated planning avoids unused facilities in shrinking areas (1) → Wasteful construction is reduced (1) → Scarce public funds are conserved (1) → More resources remain for urgent needs (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better machinery increases output per worker (1) → Labour productivity rises (1) → Potential earnings may increase (1).\n\n2. Higher productivity reduces unit production costs (1) → Local firms become more competitive (1) → Sales may rise (1).\n\n3. More productive capital expands feasible output (1) → Firms can supply more goods (1) → Real income per head may increase (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Safe savings facilities encourage deposits (1) → Funds available for bank lending may rise (1) → Firms can finance investment (1).\n\n2. Productive investment increases capital equipment (1) → Output per worker may increase (1) → National income may rise (1).\n\n3. Greater income permits more saving (1) → More investment can be financed (1) → Productive capacity expands further (1) → The low-income saving-and-investment cycle may be weakened (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to expand quality education in lower-income communities. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Wider education access improves workers’ skills (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Earnings may rise (1).\n\n2. Qualifications allow movement between occupations (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n3. More educated households can earn and save more (1) → Funds available for investment may rise (1) → Productive capital can expand (1) → Long-run development may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Vaccination reduces preventable disease (1) → Fewer people die prematurely (1) → Life expectancy may rise (1).\n\n2. Accessible primary care treats illness before it worsens (1) → Serious treatment costs may decrease (1) → Household resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Healthier workers lose fewer working days (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1) → Household income increases (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More export industries reduce reliance on one product (1) → A fall in one commodity price has a smaller effect on total earnings (1) → Export income becomes more stable (1).\n\n2. Processing and services can add value to primary output (1) → Export receipts per unit may increase (1) → National income may rise (1).\n\n3. A wider range of industries creates different skilled jobs (1) → Employment opportunities broaden (1) → Occupational options improve (1) → Structural vulnerability to one declining industry decreases (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Roads and power financed by export earnings reduce firms’ costs (1) → Private investment becomes more attractive (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).\n\n2. Infrastructure construction requires labour (1) → Employment increases during construction (1) → Household earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Investing temporary resource earnings creates longer-lived assets (1) → Income generation becomes less dependent on resource extraction (1) → Future output can be sustained after depletion (1) → Economic development becomes more durable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M04-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More school places prevent rising class overcrowding (1) → Education quality is better maintained (1) → Future workforce skills may improve (1).\n\n2. Expanded infrastructure supports a larger workforce (1) → Transport and utility shortages are reduced (1) → Output per worker may be protected (1).\n\n3. Better access to education widens future job options (1) → Occupational mobility improves (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1) → Income per head may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Real GDP removes the effect of inflation (1) → Output changes are separated from price changes (1) → Growth comparisons become more meaningful (1).\n\n2. Dividing by population measures average output per person (1) → Population growth is taken into account (1) → Changes in average material living standards are clearer (1).\n\n3. Other indicators reveal health and education outcomes (1) → Weaknesses hidden by average income can be identified (1) → Policies can target those weaknesses (1) → Economic welfare may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. HDI includes health as well as income (1) → Low life expectancy becomes visible (1) → Healthcare needs can be prioritised (1).\n\n2. HDI includes education outcomes (1) → Schooling weaknesses can be identified (1) → Education resources can be targeted (1).\n\n3. Comparing HDI with GDP per head reveals uneven development (1) → High income alone is not assumed to mean high welfare (1) → Spending can address missing social improvements (1) → Living standards may improve more broadly (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Prevention reduces incidence of disease (1) → Fewer people die prematurely (1) → Life expectancy may rise (1).\n\n2. Healthier workers lose fewer working days (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → National output may rise (1).\n\n3. Fewer serious illnesses require costly treatment (1) → Healthcare resources are released (1) → More patients can be treated from the same budget (1) → Access to care may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to provide more affordable secondary school places. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Lower school costs allow poorer pupils to enrol (1) → Education participation rises (1) → Inequality of opportunity may fall (1).\n\n2. More education improves workforce skills (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1) → Future earnings may increase (1).\n\n3. Qualifications widen the range of available occupations (1) → Occupational mobility improves (1) → Skills mismatches may decrease (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Transfers increase poorer households’ disposable income (1) → More basic necessities become affordable (1) → Absolute poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Lower-income households gain relatively more income (1) → Disposable income differences narrow (1) → Relative poverty may fall (1).\n\n3. Better access to food and healthcare improves health (1) → Adults can work more reliably (1) → Labour earnings may improve (1) → Dependence on transfers may decrease over time (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to protect low-income households during rapid inflation. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Support offsets rises in essential living costs (1) → Households retain access to food and shelter (1) → Absolute poverty may be limited (1).\n\n2. Targeting households on fixed or low incomes protects purchasing power (1) → Real income losses are reduced (1) → Material living standards are better maintained (1).\n\n3. Maintaining essential consumption supports local firms (1) → Sales fall less sharply (1) → Firms may retain workers (1) → Further poverty from job loss may be avoided (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to include environmental quality when assessing development may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Environmental indicators reveal pollution missed by GDP (1) → Non-material losses are recognised (1) → Development comparisons become more complete (1).\n\n2. Evidence of environmental harm guides regulation (1) → Harmful emissions may decrease (1) → Health and environmental quality may improve (1).\n\n3. Resource depletion can be identified early (1) → Policies can encourage sustainable use (1) → Natural productive resources last longer (1) → Future living standards are better protected (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to compare several living-standard indicators across countries could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. GDP per head indicates average material output (1) → HDI adds education and health information (1) → Comparisons cover more dimensions of welfare (1).\n\n2. Income-distribution measures reveal inequality (1) → Poverty hidden by high national averages becomes visible (1) → Assistance can be targeted more effectively (1).\n\n3. Environmental and leisure indicators capture non-material welfare (1) → Similar incomes are not assumed to imply equal wellbeing (1) → Policy priorities can reflect wider needs (1) → Economic development can be judged more accurately (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Food access improves nutrition (1) → Fewer people suffer hunger (1) → Absolute poverty decreases (1).\n\n2. Safe water reduces waterborne illness (1) → Households need less medical treatment (1) → Disposable resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Better nutrition and health improve ability to work (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1) → Household earnings may increase (1) → Families may be less trapped in poverty (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to provide targeted assistance to low-income households could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Assistance increases income available for essentials (1) → More households meet basic needs (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).\n\n2. Means-tested support directs spending to those in need (1) → Less funding subsidises higher-income groups (1) → A given budget can assist more poor households (1).\n\n3. Support can finance travel or training for work (1) → Job-search barriers decrease (1) → More people may obtain employment (1) → Future wage income may reduce poverty (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Job-search support reveals suitable vacancies (1) → Workers find employment faster (1) → Frictional unemployment decreases (1).\n\n2. Temporary income assistance maintains essential consumption (1) → Families can meet basic needs while searching (1) → Absolute poverty may be avoided (1).\n\n3. Employment increases earnings and tax payments (1) → Reliance on benefits may fall (1) → Public funds become available for other uses (1) → Anti-poverty support may become more sustainable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to introduce a carefully set minimum wage may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A binding wage floor raises covered workers’ pay (1) → Disposable income increases (1) → In-work poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Better wages may improve motivation and retention (1) → Turnover and absenteeism may decline (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1).\n\n3. A carefully chosen rate limits pressure on employers’ costs (1) → Fewer jobs may be lost than with a very high floor (1) → More workers retain employment while gaining pay (1) → Household living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to make essential primary healthcare more accessible could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Accessible care treats illness earlier (1) → Serious health problems may be prevented (1) → Households face fewer major medical bills (1).\n\n2. Better health allows more regular employment (1) → Working days and earnings may increase (1) → Poverty may fall (1).\n\n3. Lower treatment charges leave more disposable income (1) → Families can afford food and shelter more easily (1) → Basic needs are better met (1) → Absolute poverty may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Subsidies reduce schooling costs (1) → More low-income learners can attend (1) → Education access becomes more equal (1).\n\n2. Vocational courses supply job-relevant skills (1) → More learners meet employers’ requirements (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n3. Better qualifications raise potential earnings (1) → Families can buy more necessities (1) → Saving may increase (1) → Poverty may be reduced across generations (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand well-targeted child-support benefits could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Child benefits raise eligible families’ disposable income (1) → More food and clothing become affordable (1) → Child poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Support helps pay school-related costs (1) → Children can attend education more consistently (1) → Future earning prospects may improve (1).\n\n3. Better nutrition supports children’s health (1) → Illness and absence from school may fall (1) → Skills development becomes more reliable (1) → Future productivity may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Progressive taxation places a larger income-share burden on higher earners (1) → Disposable income inequality narrows (1) → Relative poverty may decrease (1).\n\n2. Tax revenue funds payments to poor households (1) → Their disposable income increases (1) → Basic needs become more affordable (1).\n\n3. Revenue can finance free education and healthcare (1) → Poorer households gain skills and health (1) → Employability and productivity may rise (1) → Long-term poverty may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Maternal care reduces preventable deaths in childbirth (1) → More mothers survive (1) → Family welfare improves (1).\n\n2. Child healthcare reduces infant illness and mortality (1) → More children survive to adulthood (1) → Life expectancy may rise (1).\n\n3. Better family health reduces treatment costs and caring time (1) → Adults can participate more consistently in work (1) → Household earnings may increase (1) → Poverty may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Prevention reduces exposure to disease (1) → Premature deaths decrease (1) → Life expectancy may increase (1).\n\n2. Healthier adults work more regularly (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → National income may increase (1).\n\n3. Households spend less on avoidable illness (1) → More income remains for food and education (1) → Children’s development may improve (1) → Future productive potential may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Immigration supplies workers for unfilled skilled jobs (1) → Skills shortages decrease (1) → Firms can expand production (1).\n\n2. Needed migrants may pay taxes (1) → Government revenue increases (1) → More services can be financed (1).\n\n3. Migrants bring knowledge and experience (1) → Local workers can learn new techniques (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Potential output may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to provide integration and language support for new migrants could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Language support improves communication with employers (1) → Migrants can apply for a wider range of jobs (1) → Skills are less likely to be wasted (1).\n\n2. Integration support helps workers understand qualifications and job requirements (1) → Suitable vacancies become easier to access (1) → Unemployment among migrants may fall (1).\n\n3. Better employment raises migrants’ earned incomes (1) → Reliance on income support may decrease (1) → Tax receipts may increase (1) → Pressure on government finances may ease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better domestic pay and career prospects reduce incentives to emigrate (1) → More skilled workers remain (1) → Labour shortages may decrease (1).\n\n2. Retaining trained workers preserves human capital (1) → Firms maintain productive skills (1) → National output may be higher (1).\n\n3. Publicly funded training benefits the domestic economy for longer (1) → More trained workers pay domestic taxes (1) → Public revenue may increase (1) → Education and healthcare budgets become easier to sustain (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to adapt public services for an ageing population may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More age-appropriate healthcare meets older people’s needs (1) → Untreated illness may decrease (1) → Non-material living standards improve (1).\n\n2. Accessible transport and facilities support independence (1) → Older people can reach services more easily (1) → Social welfare may increase (1).\n\n3. Suitable support reduces unpaid caring demands on families (1) → Working-age carers may work more hours (1) → Labour-force participation may increase (1) → Household earnings and tax revenue may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to plan population and infrastructure together could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Forecasting population identifies future service demand (1) → Schools and hospitals can be sized appropriately (1) → Overcrowding may be avoided (1).\n\n2. Infrastructure can expand with labour supply (1) → Workers can reach jobs and use productive equipment (1) → Output per person may improve (1).\n\n3. Coordinated planning avoids unused facilities in shrinking areas (1) → Wasteful construction is reduced (1) → Scarce public funds are conserved (1) → More resources remain for urgent needs (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better machinery increases output per worker (1) → Labour productivity rises (1) → Potential earnings may increase (1).\n\n2. Higher productivity reduces unit production costs (1) → Local firms become more competitive (1) → Sales may rise (1).\n\n3. More productive capital expands feasible output (1) → Firms can supply more goods (1) → Real income per head may increase (1) → Material living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Safe savings facilities encourage deposits (1) → Funds available for bank lending may rise (1) → Firms can finance investment (1).\n\n2. Productive investment increases capital equipment (1) → Output per worker may increase (1) → National income may rise (1).\n\n3. Greater income permits more saving (1) → More investment can be financed (1) → Productive capacity expands further (1) → The low-income saving-and-investment cycle may be weakened (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to expand quality education in lower-income communities. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Wider education access improves workers’ skills (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Earnings may rise (1).\n\n2. Qualifications allow movement between occupations (1) → Occupational mobility increases (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1).\n\n3. More educated households can earn and save more (1) → Funds available for investment may rise (1) → Productive capital can expand (1) → Long-run development may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Vaccination reduces preventable disease (1) → Fewer people die prematurely (1) → Life expectancy may rise (1).\n\n2. Accessible primary care treats illness before it worsens (1) → Serious treatment costs may decrease (1) → Household resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Healthier workers lose fewer working days (1) → Labour productivity may rise (1) → Household income increases (1) → Absolute poverty may fall (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More export industries reduce reliance on one product (1) → A fall in one commodity price has a smaller effect on total earnings (1) → Export income becomes more stable (1).\n\n2. Processing and services can add value to primary output (1) → Export receipts per unit may increase (1) → National income may rise (1).\n\n3. A wider range of industries creates different skilled jobs (1) → Employment opportunities broaden (1) → Occupational options improve (1) → Structural vulnerability to one declining industry decreases (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Roads and power financed by export earnings reduce firms’ costs (1) → Private investment becomes more attractive (1) → Productive capacity may increase (1).\n\n2. Infrastructure construction requires labour (1) → Employment increases during construction (1) → Household earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Investing temporary resource earnings creates longer-lived assets (1) → Income generation becomes less dependent on resource extraction (1) → Future output can be sustained after depletion (1) → Economic development becomes more durable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M06-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More school places prevent rising class overcrowding (1) → Education quality is better maintained (1) → Future workforce skills may improve (1).\n\n2. Expanded infrastructure supports a larger workforce (1) → Transport and utility shortages are reduced (1) → Output per worker may be protected (1).\n\n3. Better access to education widens future job options (1) → Occupational mobility improves (1) → Structural unemployment may decrease (1) → Income per head may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0001","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to use real GDP per head alongside other living-standard indicators would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Real GDP removes the effect of inflation → Output changes are separated from price changes → Growth comparisons become more meaningful.\n\n2. Dividing by population measures average output per person → Population growth is taken into account → Changes in average material living standards are clearer.\n\n3. Other indicators reveal health and education outcomes → Weaknesses hidden by average income can be identified → Policies can target those weaknesses → Economic welfare may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The figure hides inequalities in income distribution → High average income may coexist with widespread poverty.\n\n2. It excludes much unpaid work and environmental damage → Non-material welfare may be misrepresented.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0002","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to use HDI as well as GDP per head in development planning. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. HDI includes health as well as income → Low life expectancy becomes visible → Healthcare needs can be prioritised.\n\n2. HDI includes education outcomes → Schooling weaknesses can be identified → Education resources can be targeted.\n\n3. Comparing HDI with GDP per head reveals uneven development → High income alone is not assumed to mean high welfare → Spending can address missing social improvements → Living standards may improve more broadly.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. A national average conceals regional inequalities → Poorer regions may not receive enough targeted help.\n\n2. The index omits environmental quality and other non-material outcomes → Important aspects of living standards remain unmeasured.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0003","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to invest in preventative healthcare to raise life expectancy. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Prevention reduces incidence of disease → Fewer people die prematurely → Life expectancy may rise.\n\n2. Healthier workers lose fewer working days → Labour productivity may increase → National output may rise.\n\n3. Fewer serious illnesses require costly treatment → Healthcare resources are released → More patients can be treated from the same budget → Access to care may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Hospitals and programmes require reliable funding → Other public services may lose funds.\n\n2. Benefits may be limited by poor sanitation outside healthcare → Preventable disease can remain widespread.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0004","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to provide more affordable secondary school places is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Lower school costs allow poorer pupils to enrol → Education participation rises → Inequality of opportunity may fall.\n\n2. More education improves workforce skills → Labour productivity may rise → Future earnings may increase.\n\n3. Qualifications widen the range of available occupations → Occupational mobility improves → Skills mismatches may decrease → Structural unemployment may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Teacher shortages may reduce quality → Greater enrolment may not produce better learning.\n\n2. Some families may still require children to work → Attendance may stay low despite lower fees.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0005","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to introduce targeted transfers to reduce severe income inequality would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Transfers increase poorer households’ disposable income → More basic necessities become affordable → Absolute poverty may decrease.\n\n2. Lower-income households gain relatively more income → Disposable income differences narrow → Relative poverty may fall.\n\n3. Better access to food and healthcare improves health → Adults can work more reliably → Labour earnings may improve → Dependence on transfers may decrease over time.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Redistribution needs tax revenue → Higher taxes may affect incentives.\n\n2. Weak targeting may fail to reach the poorest households → Poverty may remain among excluded recipients.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0006","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to protect low-income households during rapid inflation would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Support offsets rises in essential living costs → Households retain access to food and shelter → Absolute poverty may be limited.\n\n2. Targeting households on fixed or low incomes protects purchasing power → Real income losses are reduced → Material living standards are better maintained.\n\n3. Maintaining essential consumption supports local firms → Sales fall less sharply → Firms may retain workers → Further poverty from job loss may be avoided.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Support payments increase fiscal costs → Taxes or government borrowing may rise.\n\n2. Payments may not keep pace with very rapid inflation → Real purchasing power may still fall.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0007","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to include environmental quality when assessing development would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Environmental indicators reveal pollution missed by GDP → Non-material losses are recognised → Development comparisons become more complete.\n\n2. Evidence of environmental harm guides regulation → Harmful emissions may decrease → Health and environmental quality may improve.\n\n3. Resource depletion can be identified early → Policies can encourage sustainable use → Natural productive resources last longer → Future living standards are better protected.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Measuring non-material benefits is difficult → Cross-country rankings may be unreliable.\n\n2. Environmental protection may raise short-run production costs → Some jobs or output may be lost initially.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0008","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.1","topicName":"Living standards","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to compare several living-standard indicators across countries. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. GDP per head indicates average material output → HDI adds education and health information → Comparisons cover more dimensions of welfare.\n\n2. Income-distribution measures reveal inequality → Poverty hidden by high national averages becomes visible → Assistance can be targeted more effectively.\n\n3. Environmental and leisure indicators capture non-material welfare → Similar incomes are not assumed to imply equal wellbeing → Policy priorities can reflect wider needs → Economic development can be judged more accurately.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Data quality may differ between countries → Apparent differences may reflect measurement rather than welfare.\n\n2. A large collection of indicators can be difficult to summarise → Policymakers may find comparisons less clear.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0009","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand access to essential food and water for very poor households. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Food access improves nutrition → Fewer people suffer hunger → Absolute poverty decreases.\n\n2. Safe water reduces waterborne illness → Households need less medical treatment → Disposable resources are conserved.\n\n3. Better nutrition and health improve ability to work → Labour productivity may rise → Household earnings may increase → Families may be less trapped in poverty.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Providing services needs sustained funding → Other public services have an opportunity cost.\n\n2. Weak distribution systems may prevent aid reaching people → Hunger and disease may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0010","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to provide targeted assistance to low-income households is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Assistance increases income available for essentials → More households meet basic needs → Absolute poverty may fall.\n\n2. Means-tested support directs spending to those in need → Less funding subsidises higher-income groups → A given budget can assist more poor households.\n\n3. Support can finance travel or training for work → Job-search barriers decrease → More people may obtain employment → Future wage income may reduce poverty.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Transfer payments may require higher taxation → Taxpayers’ disposable income may fall.\n\n2. Typical incomes may rise faster than transfer payments → Relative poverty may remain despite higher real income.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0011","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to provide job-search support and temporary income assistance would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Job-search support reveals suitable vacancies → Workers find employment faster → Frictional unemployment decreases.\n\n2. Temporary income assistance maintains essential consumption → Families can meet basic needs while searching → Absolute poverty may be avoided.\n\n3. Employment increases earnings and tax payments → Reliance on benefits may fall → Public funds become available for other uses → Anti-poverty support may become more sustainable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Government benefit expenditure may increase → Taxes or borrowing may need to increase.\n\n2. New jobs may be scarce in a local downturn → Better search information may not create vacancies.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0012","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to introduce a carefully set minimum wage would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A binding wage floor raises covered workers’ pay → Disposable income increases → In-work poverty may decrease.\n\n2. Better wages may improve motivation and retention → Turnover and absenteeism may decline → Labour productivity may rise.\n\n3. A carefully chosen rate limits pressure on employers’ costs → Fewer jobs may be lost than with a very high floor → More workers retain employment while gaining pay → Household living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some employers may reduce hours or hiring → Total earnings or employment may fall for affected workers.\n\n2. Workers outside formal employment may not benefit → Informal workers may remain in poverty.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0013","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to make essential primary healthcare more accessible would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Accessible care treats illness earlier → Serious health problems may be prevented → Households face fewer major medical bills.\n\n2. Better health allows more regular employment → Working days and earnings may increase → Poverty may fall.\n\n3. Lower treatment charges leave more disposable income → Families can afford food and shelter more easily → Basic needs are better met → Absolute poverty may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Public healthcare requires doctors and funding → Other public spending must be forgone.\n\n2. Remote households may still face travel barriers → Access and health inequalities may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0014","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to subsidise schooling and vocational courses in low-income areas. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Subsidies reduce schooling costs → More low-income learners can attend → Education access becomes more equal.\n\n2. Vocational courses supply job-relevant skills → More learners meet employers’ requirements → Structural unemployment may decrease.\n\n3. Better qualifications raise potential earnings → Families can buy more necessities → Saving may increase → Poverty may be reduced across generations.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Education benefits appear after a long delay → Poverty may not fall quickly.\n\n2. Courses may not match labour-market needs → Graduates may remain unable to find suitable work.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0015","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to expand well-targeted child-support benefits. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Child benefits raise eligible families’ disposable income → More food and clothing become affordable → Child poverty may decrease.\n\n2. Support helps pay school-related costs → Children can attend education more consistently → Future earning prospects may improve.\n\n3. Better nutrition supports children’s health → Illness and absence from school may fall → Skills development becomes more reliable → Future productivity may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Public spending commitments may rise → Future taxation or borrowing may have to rise.\n\n2. Incorrect eligibility decisions may exclude needy households → Child poverty may remain among excluded families.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0016","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.2","topicName":"Poverty","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to use progressive taxes to fund anti-poverty programmes is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Progressive taxation places a larger income-share burden on higher earners → Disposable income inequality narrows → Relative poverty may decrease.\n\n2. Tax revenue funds payments to poor households → Their disposable income increases → Basic needs become more affordable.\n\n3. Revenue can finance free education and healthcare → Poorer households gain skills and health → Employability and productivity may rise → Long-term poverty may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. High tax rates may reduce incentives for some earners → Labour supply or investment may be discouraged.\n\n2. Avoidance and administration costs can reduce effectiveness → Less revenue reaches anti-poverty programmes.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0017","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand maternal and child health services in a high-birth-rate region would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Maternal care reduces preventable deaths in childbirth → More mothers survive → Family welfare improves.\n\n2. Child healthcare reduces infant illness and mortality → More children survive to adulthood → Life expectancy may rise.\n\n3. Better family health reduces treatment costs and caring time → Adults can participate more consistently in work → Household earnings may increase → Poverty may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Healthcare programmes need investment → Other services may face budget constraints.\n\n2. A growing child population also raises schooling costs → Public spending pressures may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0018","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to improve disease prevention in communities with high mortality would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Prevention reduces exposure to disease → Premature deaths decrease → Life expectancy may increase.\n\n2. Healthier adults work more regularly → Output per worker rises → National income may increase.\n\n3. Households spend less on avoidable illness → More income remains for food and education → Children’s development may improve → Future productive potential may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Programmes require funding and trained staff → Taxes or other spending must cover programme costs.\n\n2. An ageing population can keep the crude death rate high → The death rate may not fall despite better care.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0019","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to make it easier for needed skilled workers to migrate legally would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Immigration supplies workers for unfilled skilled jobs → Skills shortages decrease → Firms can expand production.\n\n2. Needed migrants may pay taxes → Government revenue increases → More services can be financed.\n\n3. Migrants bring knowledge and experience → Local workers can learn new techniques → Labour productivity may increase → Potential output may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Housing and services may face extra demand → Rents and service congestion may increase.\n\n2. Overseas recruitment may harm source-country labour supply → Skilled-worker shortages abroad may worsen.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0020","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to provide integration and language support for new migrants. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Language support improves communication with employers → Migrants can apply for a wider range of jobs → Skills are less likely to be wasted.\n\n2. Integration support helps workers understand qualifications and job requirements → Suitable vacancies become easier to access → Unemployment among migrants may fall.\n\n3. Better employment raises migrants’ earned incomes → Reliance on income support may decrease → Tax receipts may increase → Pressure on government finances may ease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Public services may face short-run pressure → Existing users may experience congestion.\n\n2. Recognition of qualifications may remain difficult → Migrants may remain in jobs below their skill levels.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0021","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to improve domestic career opportunities to retain skilled workers. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better domestic pay and career prospects reduce incentives to emigrate → More skilled workers remain → Labour shortages may decrease.\n\n2. Retaining trained workers preserves human capital → Firms maintain productive skills → National output may be higher.\n\n3. Publicly funded training benefits the domestic economy for longer → More trained workers pay domestic taxes → Public revenue may increase → Education and healthcare budgets become easier to sustain.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher public and private wage costs may follow → Unit costs or public spending may rise.\n\n2. Some workers may still prefer opportunities abroad → Emigration may continue despite domestic improvements.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0022","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to adapt public services for an ageing population is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More age-appropriate healthcare meets older people’s needs → Untreated illness may decrease → Non-material living standards improve.\n\n2. Accessible transport and facilities support independence → Older people can reach services more easily → Social welfare may increase.\n\n3. Suitable support reduces unpaid caring demands on families → Working-age carers may work more hours → Labour-force participation may increase → Household earnings and tax revenue may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Pension and care expenditure may rise → Taxes or borrowing may need to rise.\n\n2. A smaller working-age share can constrain tax revenue → Funding per older person may be harder to maintain.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0023","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.3","topicName":"Population","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to plan population and infrastructure together would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Forecasting population identifies future service demand → Schools and hospitals can be sized appropriately → Overcrowding may be avoided.\n\n2. Infrastructure can expand with labour supply → Workers can reach jobs and use productive equipment → Output per person may improve.\n\n3. Coordinated planning avoids unused facilities in shrinking areas → Wasteful construction is reduced → Scarce public funds are conserved → More resources remain for urgent needs.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Forecasts about future population can be wrong → Too many or too few facilities may be built.\n\n2. Infrastructure expansion has a high upfront cost → Other public services may be postponed.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0024","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to invest in productivity-enhancing machinery in lower-income regions would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better machinery increases output per worker → Labour productivity rises → Potential earnings may increase.\n\n2. Higher productivity reduces unit production costs → Local firms become more competitive → Sales may rise.\n\n3. More productive capital expands feasible output → Firms can supply more goods → Real income per head may increase → Material living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Investment needs finance and technical skills → Other investments may be forgone.\n\n2. Automation may displace some existing jobs → Structural unemployment may rise initially.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0025","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to encourage accessible safe saving and productive investment would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Safe savings facilities encourage deposits → Funds available for bank lending may rise → Firms can finance investment.\n\n2. Productive investment increases capital equipment → Output per worker may increase → National income may rise.\n\n3. Greater income permits more saving → More investment can be financed → Productive capacity expands further → The low-income saving-and-investment cycle may be weakened.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Higher saving can reduce current consumption → Current demand and employment may weaken.\n\n2. Weak financial institutions may misallocate investment funds → Productive capacity may fail to improve.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0026","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to expand quality education in lower-income communities. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Wider education access improves workers’ skills → Labour productivity may increase → Earnings may rise.\n\n2. Qualifications allow movement between occupations → Occupational mobility increases → Structural unemployment may decrease.\n\n3. More educated households can earn and save more → Funds available for investment may rise → Productive capital can expand → Long-run development may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Teacher recruitment and school buildings are costly → Public budgets face an opportunity cost.\n\n2. Benefits depend on graduates finding appropriate jobs → Unemployment may persist despite higher qualifications.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0027","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to improve vaccination and primary care in underserved areas. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Vaccination reduces preventable disease → Fewer people die prematurely → Life expectancy may rise.\n\n2. Accessible primary care treats illness before it worsens → Serious treatment costs may decrease → Household resources are conserved.\n\n3. Healthier workers lose fewer working days → Labour productivity may rise → Household income increases → Absolute poverty may fall.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Healthcare projects need recurrent spending → Future taxes or borrowing may have to rise.\n\n2. Poor sanitation can undermine treatment benefits → Avoidable illness may remain common.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0028","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to encourage diversification beyond a single primary export is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More export industries reduce reliance on one product → A fall in one commodity price has a smaller effect on total earnings → Export income becomes more stable.\n\n2. Processing and services can add value to primary output → Export receipts per unit may increase → National income may rise.\n\n3. A wider range of industries creates different skilled jobs → Employment opportunities broaden → Occupational options improve → Structural vulnerability to one declining industry decreases.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. New industries may need expensive infrastructure → Scarce capital is diverted from other uses.\n\n2. Domestic firms may struggle against established foreign competitors → New firms may fail before becoming competitive.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0029","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to invest resource-export earnings in public infrastructure would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Roads and power financed by export earnings reduce firms’ costs → Private investment becomes more attractive → Productive capacity may increase.\n\n2. Infrastructure construction requires labour → Employment increases during construction → Household earnings may rise.\n\n3. Investing temporary resource earnings creates longer-lived assets → Income generation becomes less dependent on resource extraction → Future output can be sustained after depletion → Economic development becomes more durable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. World commodity prices are volatile → Infrastructure funding may be unstable.\n\n2. Poor governance may cause the revenues to be wasted → Productive assets may never be completed effectively.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C05-P2-M08-0030","c":4,"chapter":"Economic Development","topic":"5.4","topicName":"Differences in economic development between countries","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to expand schools and infrastructure as population grows would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More school places prevent rising class overcrowding → Education quality is better maintained → Future workforce skills may improve.\n\n2. Expanded infrastructure supports a larger workforce → Transport and utility shortages are reduced → Output per worker may be protected.\n\n3. Better access to education widens future job options → Occupational mobility improves → Structural unemployment may decrease → Income per head may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Spending requirements can exceed fiscal capacity → Higher taxes or public borrowing may be necessary.\n\n2. Benefits depend on the quality of public services → Extra facilities may deliver little learning or productivity gain.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes international specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.","Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system."],"answer":2,"why":"International specialisation: International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["International specialisation","Communication technology","Tariff","Consumer gains from trade"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates international specialisation: International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of international specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel.","A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services."],"answer":0,"why":"A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. This illustrates international specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to international specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":2,"why":"Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $55 billion of goods and imports $68 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$13 billion surplus","$68 billion deficit","$123 billion surplus","$13 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 55 − 68 = -13 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The country may become dependent on global seafood demand.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Export earnings may rise over time."],"answer":0,"why":"The country may become dependent on global seafood demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Export earnings could finance needed imports.","Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."],"answer":2,"why":"Export earnings could finance needed imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Government may collect customs revenue.","Imported inputs may become more affordable.","Firms may understand export and import trends.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources."],"answer":3,"why":"Overfishing could damage future productive resources."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Export demand may increase.","Local workers may gain employment and training.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes benefits of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity.","Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more."],"answer":2,"why":"Benefits of specialisation: Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Import quota","Exchange rate","Demand for currency","Benefits of specialisation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates benefits of specialisation: Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$5bn, primary income −$4bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$18 billion surplus","$26 billion surplus","$18 billion deficit","$19 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Current account = 14 + 5 − 4 + 3 = +$18 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to benefits of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."],"answer":2,"why":"Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency.","Specialist workers may become more productive.","Training costs money and takes time."],"answer":2,"why":"Specialist workers may become more productive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New firms may develop production experience.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Demand for bicycle parts could decline."],"answer":3,"why":"Demand for bicycle parts could decline."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Long-distance transport may increase emissions.","An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports."],"answer":1,"why":"Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Imported inputs may become more affordable.","Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities."],"answer":0,"why":"Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Investment equipment may cost less.","Specialist workers may become more productive.","Export demand may increase."],"answer":2,"why":"Specialist workers may become more productive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."],"answer":1,"why":"Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 83 foreign cents and now buys 108 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Its value has remained unchanged.","It has appreciated.","It has depreciated.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen."],"answer":1,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (108 rather than 83 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current-account surplus","Currency appreciation","Supply of currency","Risks of specialisation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates risks of specialisation: Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of risks of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts.","A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit."],"answer":2,"why":"A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts. This illustrates heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to risks of specialisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."],"answer":1,"why":"A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify export production away from one mineral. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Training and investment take time."],"answer":2,"why":"The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify export production away from one mineral. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Future output and living standards may increase.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Developing new industries costs money."],"answer":3,"why":"Developing new industries costs money."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to diversify export production away from one mineral?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some communities may remain digitally excluded.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home.","New activities may create additional jobs.","Foreign competition may limit prices charged."],"answer":2,"why":"New activities may create additional jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to diversify export production away from one mineral less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Supplier firms may win new contracts.","More funds may enter regulated financial channels.","Domestic capital formation may rise.","Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages."],"answer":3,"why":"Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to diversify export production away from one mineral. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Service export receipts may increase.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":0,"why":"The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 6 foreign units. A machine costs 420 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$420","$130","$70","$2520"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 420 foreign units / 6 = $70."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0031","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes free trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers.","People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets.","A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Free trade: Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0032","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower transport costs","Free trade","Supply of currency","Consumer gains from trade"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates free trade: Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0033","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of free trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods.","Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units.","A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients."],"answer":0,"why":"Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. This illustrates free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0034","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to free trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports."],"answer":3,"why":"Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0035","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to remove tariffs on common household appliances. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Port upgrades require significant public or private funds.","Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.","Quality upgrades can be expensive.","Consumers may pay lower prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Consumers may pay lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0036","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to remove tariffs on common household appliances. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Some local appliance makers may lose market share.","New firms may develop production experience.","Exporters may reach more customers."],"answer":1,"why":"Some local appliance makers may lose market share."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0037","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to remove tariffs on common household appliances?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.","Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.","Global demand may remain weak."],"answer":1,"why":"Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0038","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $75 billion of goods and imports $100 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$175 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus","$100 billion deficit","$25 billion deficit"],"answer":3,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 75 − 100 = -25 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0039","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to remove tariffs on common household appliances. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Consumers may pay lower prices.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Service export receipts may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Consumers may pay lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0040","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0041","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes consumer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumer gains from trade: International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0042","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exchange-rate speculation","Multinational company","Consumer gains from trade","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates consumer gains from trade: International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0043","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of consumer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments.","Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit.","Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month."],"answer":2,"why":"Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. This illustrates international trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0044","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to consumer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."],"answer":1,"why":"Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0045","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to allow more competition from imported food products. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Protection can keep prices artificially high.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Quality upgrades can be expensive.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may gain greater variety."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0046","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$3bn, primary income −$3bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$31 billion surplus","$28 billion surplus","$25 billion deficit","$25 billion surplus"],"answer":3,"why":"Current account = 22 + 3 − 3 + 3 = +$25 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0047","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to allow more competition from imported food products?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower prices may improve real purchasing power.","An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens.","Global demand may remain weak."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower prices may improve real purchasing power."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0048","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to allow more competition from imported food products less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Domestic capital formation may rise.","International coordination may become more efficient.","Transport-related emissions may increase.","Skilled employment could expand."],"answer":2,"why":"Transport-related emissions may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0049","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to allow more competition from imported food products. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Future output and living standards may increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may gain greater variety."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0050","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad.","Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare."],"answer":3,"why":"Imports enlarge product choice and may increase consumer welfare."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0051","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes producer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand.","A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."],"answer":0,"why":"Producer gains from trade: Export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0052","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Import dependence","Supply of currency","Globalisation","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates producer gains from trade: Export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0053","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of producer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services."],"answer":0,"why":"A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. This illustrates export markets allow firms to sell beyond the size of domestic demand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0054","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to producer gains from trade?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."],"answer":3,"why":"Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0055","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 87 foreign cents and now buys 107 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated.","It has appreciated."],"answer":3,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (107 rather than 87 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0056","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to help small manufacturers access export markets. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["New firms may develop production experience.","Firms may access specialised inputs.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings."],"answer":3,"why":"Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0057","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to help small manufacturers access export markets?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher volumes may allow more efficient production."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0058","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to help small manufacturers access export markets less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Meeting foreign standards may be costly.","Domestic capital formation may rise.","Skilled employment could expand.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency."],"answer":0,"why":"Meeting foreign standards may be costly."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0059","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to help small manufacturers access export markets. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Sales and revenue may increase.","Future output and living standards may increase.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Domestic tile makers may gain market share."],"answer":0,"why":"Sales and revenue may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0060","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."],"answer":3,"why":"Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0061","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes import dependence?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.","An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency.","A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."],"answer":0,"why":"Import dependence: Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0062","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Infant industry protection","Import dependence","Free trade","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates import dependence: Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0063","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 4 foreign units. A machine costs 440 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$440","$110","$1760","$150"],"answer":1,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 440 foreign units / 4 = $110."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0064","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to import dependence?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0065","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.","Training and investment take time.","Shortages may become less likely.","Port upgrades require significant public or private funds."],"answer":2,"why":"Shortages may become less likely."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0066","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Export demand may increase.","Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive.","Exporters may reach more customers."],"answer":2,"why":"Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0067","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere."],"answer":1,"why":"Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0068","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately."],"answer":0,"why":"Global shortages can affect all source countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0069","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Service export receipts may increase.","Shortages may become less likely.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":1,"why":"Shortages may become less likely."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0070","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":2,"why":"Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0071","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $95 billion of goods and imports $76 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$171 billion surplus","$19 billion deficit","$19 billion surplus","$76 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 95 − 76 = 19 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0072","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Trade and competition","Import dependence","Multinational company","Trade in goods balance"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates trade and competition: Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0073","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of trade and competition?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands. This illustrates open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0074","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to trade and competition?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":2,"why":"More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0075","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Developing local energy supplies is expensive.","A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.","Training costs money and takes time."],"answer":0,"why":"Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0076","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Local workers may gain employment and training.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":1,"why":"Some domestic workers may lose jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0077","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Global demand may remain weak.","Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Local firms may become more efficient to compete."],"answer":3,"why":"Local firms may become more efficient to compete."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0078","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","Foreign firms may dominate a very small local market.","Government may collect customs revenue.","International coordination may become more efficient."],"answer":1,"why":"Foreign firms may dominate a very small local market."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0079","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":2,"why":"Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0080","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$7bn, primary income −$2bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$21 billion surplus","$26 billion surplus","$22 billion surplus","$22 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 14 + 7 − 2 + 3 = +$22 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0081","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes globalisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts.","An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency."],"answer":0,"why":"Globalisation: Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0082","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Tariff","Currency depreciation","Risks of specialisation","Globalisation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates globalisation: Globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0083","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of globalisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others.","Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments.","An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units."],"answer":1,"why":"A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. This illustrates globalisation is the growing economic interconnection and interdependence of countries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0084","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to globalisation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality."],"answer":2,"why":"Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0085","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to participate in international production networks. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus.","Demand for bicycle parts could decline.","Training and investment take time.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may access specialised inputs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0086","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to participate in international production networks. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."],"answer":0,"why":"Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0087","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to participate in international production networks?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources."],"answer":2,"why":"Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0088","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 91 foreign cents and now buys 106 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It has appreciated.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated."],"answer":0,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (106 rather than 91 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0089","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to participate in international production networks. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export demand may increase.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":3,"why":"Firms may access specialised inputs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0090","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0091","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes lower transport costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.","Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower transport costs: Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0092","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumer gains from trade","Imported inflation","Lower transport costs","Supply of currency"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates lower transport costs: Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0093","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of lower transport costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit.","A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","A country relies on one mineral for most of its export receipts."],"answer":0,"why":"A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. This illustrates falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0094","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to lower transport costs?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."],"answer":1,"why":"Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0095","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Data can arrive with reporting delays.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may reach more customers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0096","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 2 foreign units. A machine costs 120 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$80","$120","$60","$240"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 120 foreign units / 2 = $60."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0097","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Imported production inputs may become cheaper.","Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported production inputs may become cheaper."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0098","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","Exporting firms may support additional jobs.","Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.","Domestic capital formation may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Expanded shipping may increase local pollution."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0099","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Sales and revenue may increase.","Future output and living standards may increase.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may reach more customers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0100","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit."],"answer":0,"why":"Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0101","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes communication technology?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.","Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."],"answer":2,"why":"Communication technology: Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0102","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producer gains from trade","Communication technology","Trade and competition","Exchange rate"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates communication technology: Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0103","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of communication technology?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units."],"answer":0,"why":"A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. This illustrates cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0104","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to communication technology?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0105","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $65 billion of goods and imports $108 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$43 billion deficit","$108 billion deficit","$173 billion surplus","$43 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 65 − 108 = -43 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0106","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."],"answer":3,"why":"Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0107","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Not all future political or economic events are predictable.","Global shortages can affect all source countries.","International coordination may become more efficient."],"answer":3,"why":"International coordination may become more efficient."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0108","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.","Some communities may remain digitally excluded.","Lower prices may improve real purchasing power."],"answer":2,"why":"Some communities may remain digitally excluded."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0109","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Export earnings may rise over time."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may sell services into new markets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0110","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."],"answer":3,"why":"Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0111","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes multinational company?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Falling shipping costs can make trading goods across borders more affordable.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.","A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Multinational company: A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0112","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Embargo","Trade in goods balance","Tariff","Multinational company"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates multinational company: A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0113","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$5bn, primary income −$6bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$36 billion surplus","$27 billion surplus","$24 billion surplus","$24 billion deficit"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 22 + 5 − 6 + 3 = +$24 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0114","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to multinational company?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."],"answer":2,"why":"Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0115","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.","Demand for bicycle parts could decline.","Payment services require compliance and monitoring.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":3,"why":"Local workers may gain employment and training."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0116","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country."],"answer":3,"why":"Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0117","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Supplier firms may win new contracts.","Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically."],"answer":2,"why":"Supplier firms may win new contracts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0118","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","New activities may create additional jobs."],"answer":2,"why":"The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0119","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":3,"why":"Local workers may gain employment and training."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0120","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An international electronics firm opens an assembly plant in another country. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0121","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 95 foreign cents and now buys 105 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["It has appreciated.","It has depreciated.","Its value has remained unchanged.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen."],"answer":0,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (105 rather than 95 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0122","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current account","Exchange-rate speculation","Import dependence","Tariff"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates tariff: A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0123","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of tariff?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier.","An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units."],"answer":0,"why":"A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles. This illustrates a tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0124","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to tariff?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0125","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Domestic tile makers may gain market share.","Developing new industries costs money.","Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.","Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive."],"answer":0,"why":"Domestic tile makers may gain market share."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0126","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Households and builders may face higher prices.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Future output and living standards may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Households and builders may face higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0127","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Government may collect customs revenue.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."],"answer":1,"why":"Government may collect customs revenue."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0128","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets.","Domestic manufacturing employment may expand.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours."],"answer":0,"why":"Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0129","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Domestic tile makers may gain market share.","Service export receipts may increase.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Export demand may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Domestic tile makers may gain market share."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0130","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 6 foreign units. A machine costs 600 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$100","$3600","$160","$600"],"answer":0,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 600 foreign units / 6 = $100."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0131","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes import quota?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."],"answer":0,"why":"Import quota: An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0132","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Trade in services balance","Import quota","Imported inflation","Multinational company"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates import quota: An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0133","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of import quota?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year.","A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units."],"answer":1,"why":"A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. This illustrates an import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0134","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to import quota?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."],"answer":3,"why":"Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0135","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations."],"answer":2,"why":"Some domestic shoe producers may sell more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0136","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Service export receipts may increase.","Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Consumers may face less choice and higher prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0137","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":1,"why":"Employment in protected firms may be preserved."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0138","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $85 billion of goods and imports $84 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$1 billion deficit","$1 billion surplus","$84 billion deficit","$169 billion surplus"],"answer":1,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 85 − 84 = 1 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0139","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Specialist workers may become more productive."],"answer":2,"why":"Some domestic shoe producers may sell more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0140","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government permits no more than 40,000 imported shoes each year. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":0,"why":"Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0141","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes embargo?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system.","International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."],"answer":3,"why":"Embargo: An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0142","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Benefits of specialisation","Tariff","Embargo"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates embargo: An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0143","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of embargo?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments."],"answer":0,"why":"A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. This illustrates an embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0144","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to embargo?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."],"answer":3,"why":"Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0145","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects.","Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency."],"answer":0,"why":"Domestic environmental risks may decrease."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0146","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$3bn, primary income −$5bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$15 billion deficit","$15 billion surplus","$17 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus"],"answer":1,"why":"Current account = 14 + 3 − 5 + 3 = +$15 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0147","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Transport-related emissions may increase.","Firms may develop safer alternative products.","Competitors abroad may lower their prices.","Transaction fees may reduce the amount received."],"answer":1,"why":"Firms may develop safer alternative products."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0148","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.","Enforcement and illegal trade may be difficult to control.","Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities.","Skilled employment could expand."],"answer":1,"why":"Enforcement and illegal trade may be difficult to control."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0149","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Investment equipment may cost less."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic environmental risks may decrease."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0150","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings."],"answer":1,"why":"Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0151","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes infant industry protection?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors.","A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers.","A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country.","An embargo is an official prohibition on trade with a particular country or in a particular product."],"answer":0,"why":"Infant industry protection: Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0152","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Infant industry protection","Consumer gains from trade","Exchange-rate speculation","Free trade"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates infant industry protection: Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0153","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of infant industry protection?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts."],"answer":1,"why":"A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. This illustrates infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0154","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to infant industry protection?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."],"answer":2,"why":"Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0155","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 99 foreign cents and now buys 129 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","It has appreciated.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated."],"answer":1,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (129 rather than 99 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0156","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Protection can keep prices artificially high.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs."],"answer":1,"why":"Protection can keep prices artificially high."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0157","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand.","Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0158","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","More funds may enter regulated financial channels.","Firms may become dependent on continuing protection.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production."],"answer":2,"why":"Firms may become dependent on continuing protection."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0159","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","New firms may develop production experience.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Consumers may pay lower prices."],"answer":1,"why":"New firms may develop production experience."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0160","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":2,"why":"Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0161","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes exchange rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Infant industry protection temporarily shields new domestic firms from established foreign competitors.","People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."],"answer":0,"why":"Exchange rate: An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0162","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Exchange rate","Import quota","Current account"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates exchange rate: An exchange rate is the price of one currency expressed in another currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0163","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 4 foreign units. A machine costs 200 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$800","$200","$90","$50"],"answer":3,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 200 foreign units / 4 = $50."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0164","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to exchange rate?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":1,"why":"The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0165","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Training and investment take time.","Some local appliance makers may lose market share.","Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings.","Travellers can compare currency conversion costs."],"answer":3,"why":"Travellers can compare currency conversion costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0166","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Firms may sell services into new markets.","Local workers may gain employment and training.","Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment.","Consumers may gain greater variety."],"answer":2,"why":"Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0167","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0168","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["International coordination may become more efficient.","Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved."],"answer":1,"why":"Transaction fees may reduce the amount received."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0169","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Consumers may pay lower prices."],"answer":0,"why":"Travellers can compare currency conversion costs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0170","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An exchange booth lists one domestic dollar as worth 0.80 foreign units. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."],"answer":0,"why":"The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0171","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $55 billion of goods and imports $60 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$60 billion deficit","$5 billion surplus","$5 billion deficit","$115 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 55 − 60 = -5 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0172","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Risks of specialisation","International specialisation","Current account","Currency appreciation"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates currency appreciation: Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0173","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of currency appreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries."],"answer":2,"why":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units. This illustrates appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0174","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to currency appreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."],"answer":3,"why":"The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0175","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Investment equipment may cost less.","Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business."],"answer":1,"why":"Investment equipment may cost less."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0176","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","New firms may develop production experience.","Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0177","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Imported inputs may become more affordable.","A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions.","Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported inputs may become more affordable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0178","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Government may collect customs revenue.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","International coordination may become more efficient."],"answer":0,"why":"Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0179","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export earnings may rise over time.","Export demand may increase.","Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Investment equipment may cost less."],"answer":3,"why":"Investment equipment may cost less."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0180","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$7bn, primary income −$4bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$28 billion deficit","$29 billion surplus","$28 billion surplus","$36 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 22 + 7 − 4 + 3 = +$28 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0181","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system.","Relying heavily on imported essentials can make supply vulnerable to overseas disruptions.","An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently."],"answer":0,"why":"Currency depreciation: Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0182","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Currency depreciation","Infant industry protection","Free trade","Tariff"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates currency depreciation: Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0183","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units.","Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","Two neighbouring countries remove import tariffs on most manufactured goods."],"answer":1,"why":"One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. This illustrates depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0184","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0185","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote exports after a currency depreciation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Demand for bicycle parts could decline.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":3,"why":"Exports may become more price competitive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0186","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to promote exports after a currency depreciation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More investment income may remain with local residents.","Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease."],"answer":1,"why":"Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0187","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to promote exports after a currency depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources."],"answer":0,"why":"Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0188","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 103 foreign cents and now buys 128 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Its value has remained unchanged.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","It has appreciated.","It has depreciated."],"answer":2,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (128 rather than 103 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0189","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to promote exports after a currency depreciation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Shortages may become less likely.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad."],"answer":3,"why":"Exports may become more price competitive abroad."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0190","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0191","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes demand for currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Demand for currency: Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0192","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Primary income balance","Demand for currency","Import quota","Consumer gains from trade"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates demand for currency: Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0193","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of demand for currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A small design company uses reliable video meetings to serve overseas clients.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product.","Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers."],"answer":2,"why":"Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. This illustrates foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0194","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to demand for currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0195","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","The country may become dependent on global seafood demand.","Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."],"answer":3,"why":"Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0196","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 2 foreign units. A machine costs 180 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$360","$180","$90","$110"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 180 foreign units / 2 = $90."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0197","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn.","Exporting firms may support additional jobs.","Import dependence may increase in strategic product categories."],"answer":2,"why":"Exporting firms may support additional jobs."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0198","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Competition between suppliers may reduce reliance on one firm.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Imported production inputs may become cheaper.","Competitors abroad may lower their prices."],"answer":3,"why":"Competitors abroad may lower their prices."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0199","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Consumers may gain greater variety.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Higher export sales may earn foreign currency.","Firms may access specialised inputs."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher export sales may earn foreign currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0200","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Overseas customers need a nation's currency to buy its tourism services. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."],"answer":2,"why":"Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0201","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes supply of currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets.","Open international markets can increase competitive pressure on domestic firms.","Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.","An import quota is a quantitative limit on the amount of a good that may be imported."],"answer":0,"why":"Supply of currency: People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0202","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported inflation","Trade and competition","Supply of currency","Risks of specialisation"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates supply of currency: People supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0203","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of supply of currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts."],"answer":1,"why":"Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. This illustrates people supply domestic currency on foreign-exchange markets when buying imports or overseas assets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0204","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to supply of currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."],"answer":2,"why":"More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0205","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $75 billion of goods and imports $92 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$92 billion deficit","$17 billion deficit","$17 billion surplus","$167 billion surplus"],"answer":1,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 75 − 92 = -17 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0206","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to make overseas investment rules more transparent. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may sell services into new markets.","Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency.","New firms may develop production experience.","Consumers may gain greater variety."],"answer":1,"why":"Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0207","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to make overseas investment rules more transparent?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Meeting foreign standards may be costly.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages.","Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities."],"answer":3,"why":"Greater capital mobility may increase diversification opportunities."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0208","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to make overseas investment rules more transparent less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Domestic firms may obtain cheaper imported components.","Supplier firms may win new contracts.","Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."],"answer":3,"why":"Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0209","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to make overseas investment rules more transparent. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["More investment income may remain with local residents.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Investors may make better informed decisions."],"answer":3,"why":"Investors may make better informed decisions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0210","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."],"answer":1,"why":"More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0211","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes exchange-rate speculation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions.","International specialisation occurs when a country concentrates resources on goods it can produce relatively efficiently.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."],"answer":0,"why":"Exchange-rate speculation: Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0212","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Producer gains from trade","Exchange-rate speculation","Current account","Tariff"],"answer":1,"why":"The situation illustrates exchange-rate speculation: Currency speculation is trading currencies based on expectations of future exchange-rate movements."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0213","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$5bn, primary income −$3bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$22 billion surplus","$19 billion deficit","$19 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Current account = 14 + 5 − 3 + 3 = +$19 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0214","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to exchange-rate speculation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","Digital communication lowers the cost of managing cross-border transactions.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."],"answer":2,"why":"Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0215","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Data can arrive with reporting delays.","Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may understand currency risks better."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0216","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Local workers may gain employment and training."],"answer":0,"why":"Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0217","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Import dependence may increase in strategic product categories.","Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions."],"answer":2,"why":"Reliable information may reduce some rumours."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0218","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Local firms may become more efficient to compete.","Suppliers may benefit from shared infrastructure.","Not all future political or economic events are predictable.","Domestic capital formation may rise."],"answer":2,"why":"Not all future political or economic events are predictable."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0219","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Service export receipts may increase.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer."],"answer":2,"why":"Businesses may understand currency risks better."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0220","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","Concentrating on suitable activities can raise output available through trade."],"answer":0,"why":"Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0221","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 107 foreign cents and now buys 127 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated.","It has appreciated."],"answer":3,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (127 rather than 107 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0222","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Currency depreciation","Lower transport costs","Imported inflation","Current account"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates imported inflation: Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0223","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of imported inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported sports shoes enter a market previously served by only two local brands.","Residents of a cold country buy affordable imported tropical fruit.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product."],"answer":2,"why":"A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel. This illustrates depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0224","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to imported inflation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0225","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Developing new industries costs money.","Profits may be remitted to the firm's home country."],"answer":1,"why":"Future import-cost shocks may be smaller."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0226","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Exporters may reach more customers.","Developing local energy supplies is expensive.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Sales and revenue may increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Developing local energy supplies is expensive."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0227","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global demand may remain weak.","Domestic energy industries may gain investment.","Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own."],"answer":1,"why":"Domestic energy industries may gain investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0228","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically.","Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","Firms may understand export and import trends."],"answer":0,"why":"Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0229","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Firms may access specialised inputs.","Export demand may increase."],"answer":1,"why":"Future import-cost shocks may be smaller."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0230","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 6 foreign units. A machine costs 240 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$40","$100","$1440","$240"],"answer":0,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 240 foreign units / 6 = $40."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0231","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes current account?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Appreciation is a rise in the value of a currency under a floating exchange-rate system.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Depreciation is a fall in a currency's value under a floating exchange-rate system.","The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."],"answer":3,"why":"Current account: The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0232","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Import dependence","Tariff","Current account","Producer gains from trade"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates current account: The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0233","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of current account?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad.","A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","A country imposes a 15% tariff on imported ceramic tiles.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel."],"answer":0,"why":"A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. This illustrates the current account records trade in goods and services, primary income and secondary income transactions."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0234","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to current account?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","Foreign investment can transfer capital, jobs and know-how across borders.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices."],"answer":2,"why":"Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0235","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to track the components of the current account separately. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Consumers may face less choice and higher prices.","Port upgrades require significant public or private funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0236","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to track the components of the current account separately. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Exporters may reach more customers.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller.","Data can arrive with reporting delays."],"answer":3,"why":"Data can arrive with reporting delays."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0237","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to track the components of the current account separately?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Firms may understand export and import trends.","Long-distance transport may increase emissions.","Some communities may remain digitally excluded.","Transaction fees may reduce the amount received."],"answer":0,"why":"Firms may understand export and import trends."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0238","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $95 billion of goods and imports $68 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["$163 billion surplus","$68 billion deficit","$27 billion deficit","$27 billion surplus"],"answer":3,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 95 − 68 = 27 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0239","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to track the components of the current account separately. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Exporters may reach more customers.","Domestic tile makers may gain market share."],"answer":0,"why":"Policy-makers may identify the sources of an imbalance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0240","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country earns export receipts but also pays for imports and income transfers abroad. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate.","Combining these flows produces the current-account balance.","Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Combining these flows produces the current-account balance."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0241","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes trade in goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.","Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.","The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports.","Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks."],"answer":2,"why":"Trade in goods balance: The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0242","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Embargo","Globalisation","Current-account surplus","Trade in goods balance"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates trade in goods balance: The goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0243","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of trade in goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Domestic investors exchange local money for foreign currency to purchase overseas shares.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.90 to buying 0.75 foreign units.","An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product."],"answer":2,"why":"An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. This illustrates the goods balance is the value of goods exports minus the value of goods imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0244","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to trade in goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","More rival suppliers can push domestic firms to improve price and quality.","More selling of the local currency can put downward pressure on its exchange rate."],"answer":1,"why":"The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0245","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Domestic farmers may face more competition.","Export demand may increase.","Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad.","Port upgrades require significant public or private funds."],"answer":1,"why":"Export demand may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0246","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$22bn, services +$3bn, primary income −$2bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$26 billion surplus","$30 billion surplus","$25 billion surplus","$26 billion deficit"],"answer":0,"why":"Current account = 22 + 3 − 2 + 3 = +$26 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0247","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.","Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed.","Domestic manufacturing employment may expand.","Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic manufacturing employment may expand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0248","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Export-oriented firms may increase output and jobs.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","Domestic manufacturing jobs may expand.","Global demand may remain weak."],"answer":3,"why":"Global demand may remain weak."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0249","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Export demand may increase.","Buyers may gain more choice and lower prices.","Shortages may become less likely."],"answer":1,"why":"Export demand may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0250","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: An economy exports $50 billion of merchandise and imports $65 billion. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices.","The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit.","A fall in that mineral's world price can sharply reduce foreign earnings.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."],"answer":1,"why":"The goods balance is a $15 billion deficit."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0251","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes trade in services balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.","The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports.","Specialisation can enable economies of scale and higher labour productivity.","Free trade means international exchange with few government-imposed trade barriers."],"answer":1,"why":"Trade in services balance: The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0252","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Trade in services balance","Producer gains from trade","Demand for currency","Globalisation"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates trade in services balance: The services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0253","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of trade in services balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services.","A small furniture producer begins selling to customers in neighbouring countries.","A country develops clusters of skilled bicycle-component manufacturers.","A government bans imports of a restricted hazardous product."],"answer":0,"why":"A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. This illustrates the services balance is the value of services exports minus services imports."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0254","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to trade in services balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Higher import costs can protect local suppliers while raising consumer prices.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies."],"answer":1,"why":"The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0255","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 111 foreign cents and now buys 126 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["It has depreciated.","It has appreciated.","Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged."],"answer":1,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (126 rather than 111 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0256","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to develop skills needed to export digital services. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Training costs money and takes time.","New firms may develop production experience.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Sales and revenue may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"Training costs money and takes time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0257","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to develop skills needed to export digital services?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Global shortages can affect all source countries.","Skilled employment could expand.","Not all future political or economic events are predictable.","Long-distance transport may increase emissions."],"answer":1,"why":"Skilled employment could expand."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0258","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to develop skills needed to export digital services less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Employment in protected firms may be preserved.","Government may collect customs revenue.","Foreign competition may limit prices charged.","Workers may gain employment in export-oriented industries."],"answer":2,"why":"Foreign competition may limit prices charged."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0259","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to develop skills needed to export digital services. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment equipment may cost less.","Exports may become more price competitive abroad.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Service export receipts may increase."],"answer":3,"why":"Service export receipts may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0260","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country earns $20 billion from tourism and software services but buys $12 billion in foreign services. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","The services balance is an $8 billion surplus.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale."],"answer":1,"why":"The services balance is an $8 billion surplus."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0261","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes primary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends.","Cheaper and faster communication can support international coordination of trade and investment.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","A tariff is a tax on imported goods that raises their cost to importers."],"answer":0,"why":"Primary income balance: Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0262","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Currency appreciation","Lower transport costs","Demand for currency","Primary income balance"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates primary income balance: Primary income includes cross-border earnings from work and investments, such as interest, profits and dividends."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0263","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 4 foreign units. A machine costs 320 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$1280","$320","$80","$120"],"answer":2,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 320 foreign units / 4 = $80."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0264","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to primary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Larger potential markets can support higher output and scale economies.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods.","Temporary protection may provide time to develop skills and scale.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition."],"answer":1,"why":"Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0265","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More investment income may remain with local residents.","Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations.","Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive."],"answer":0,"why":"More investment income may remain with local residents."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0266","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Exporters may reach more customers.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.","Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects.","Future import-cost shocks may be smaller."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0267","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Transaction fees may reduce the amount received.","Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Domestic capital formation may rise.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":2,"why":"Domestic capital formation may rise."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0268","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Local firms may become more efficient to compete.","Lower prices may improve real purchasing power.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks."],"answer":3,"why":"Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0269","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Firms may access specialised inputs.","More investment income may remain with local residents.","Consumers may gain greater variety.","Domestic environmental risks may decrease."],"answer":1,"why":"More investment income may remain with local residents."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0270","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Foreign-owned firms send dividends overseas while residents receive interest from foreign investments. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Lower transport costs improve the competitiveness of distant suppliers.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Repeated production can improve skills and reduce unit costs.","Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."],"answer":3,"why":"Net primary income affects the current account even without trade in goods."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0271","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country exports $65 billion of goods and imports $100 billion. What is its trade-in-goods balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["$100 billion deficit","$165 billion surplus","$35 billion deficit","$35 billion surplus"],"answer":2,"why":"Goods balance = exports − imports = 65 − 100 = -35 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0272","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Import quota","Import dependence","Secondary income balance","Lower transport costs"],"answer":2,"why":"The situation illustrates secondary income balance: Secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0273","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of secondary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Traders buy a currency after predicting that it will strengthen next month.","Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families.","One domestic dollar changes from buying 0.80 to buying 0.90 foreign units.","A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others."],"answer":1,"why":"Citizens working abroad regularly send money home to their families. This illustrates secondary income records one-way current transfers, such as remittances and some international aid."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0274","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to secondary income balance?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts.","Higher input costs may be passed to households as inflation.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates."],"answer":1,"why":"Worker remittances can increase net secondary income receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0275","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive.","Some domestic workers may lose jobs.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"Recipient households may receive more of each transfer."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0276","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.","Firms may gain experience and lower average costs.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Payment services require compliance and monitoring."],"answer":3,"why":"Payment services require compliance and monitoring."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0277","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["More funds may enter regulated financial channels.","Expanded shipping may increase local pollution.","The plant may relocate if costs rise elsewhere.","Transport-related emissions may increase."],"answer":0,"why":"More funds may enter regulated financial channels."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0278","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens.","Higher volumes may allow more efficient production.","Reliable information may reduce some rumours.","New activities may create additional jobs."],"answer":0,"why":"Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0279","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Some domestic shoe producers may sell more.","Recipient households may receive more of each transfer.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Investors may make better informed decisions."],"answer":1,"why":"Recipient households may receive more of each transfer."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0280","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A country's balances are: goods +$14bn, services +$7bn, primary income −$6bn and secondary income +$3bn. Calculate the current-account balance.","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["$18 billion surplus","$21 billion surplus","$18 billion deficit","$30 billion surplus"],"answer":0,"why":"Current account = 14 + 7 − 6 + 3 = +$18 billion."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0281","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes current-account deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.","Heavy concentration on one industry can expose an economy to price and demand shocks.","Foreign demand for a country's currency can arise from its exports and investment opportunities.","A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."],"answer":3,"why":"Current-account deficit: A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0282","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Currency depreciation","Primary income balance","Exchange-rate speculation"],"answer":0,"why":"The situation illustrates current-account deficit: A current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0283","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of current-account deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A phone designed in one country is assembled using components made in several others.","A coastal country exports fish products while importing machines it cannot make cheaply.","Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts.","A country imports almost all its fertiliser from a single foreign supplier."],"answer":2,"why":"Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. This illustrates a current-account deficit occurs when total current-account payments exceed total receipts."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0284","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to current-account deficit?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Lower barriers can increase competition and access to imports.","Banning trade can prevent specified imports but may disrupt supply.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness.","Supply interruptions or currency depreciation can raise input prices."],"answer":2,"why":"A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0285","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business.","Export earnings may rise over time.","Developing local energy supplies is expensive.","Households and builders may face higher prices."],"answer":1,"why":"Export earnings may rise over time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0286","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. Which of the following is a possible disadvantage?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Investment equipment may cost less.","Investors may make better informed decisions.","Sales and revenue may increase.","Training and investment take time."],"answer":3,"why":"Training and investment take time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0287","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets.","Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own.","Overfishing could damage future productive resources.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":0,"why":"Competitive firms may develop sustainable overseas markets."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0288","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"One home dollar previously bought 115 foreign cents and now buys 125 foreign cents. What has happened to the home currency?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Its purchasing power in foreign currency has fallen.","Its value has remained unchanged.","It has depreciated.","It has appreciated."],"answer":3,"why":"It now buys more foreign currency (125 rather than 115 foreign cents), so it has appreciated."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0289","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The economy may reduce exposure to a single price shock.","Firms may sell services into new markets.","Consumers may pay lower prices.","Export earnings may rise over time."],"answer":3,"why":"Export earnings may rise over time."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0290","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: Goods imports and overseas income payments exceed all export and transfer receipts. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Foreign buyers may find exports cheaper while local buyers pay more for imports.","A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."],"answer":3,"why":"A deficit may reflect high import demand or weak export competitiveness."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0291","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which statement best describes current-account surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["A multinational company owns or controls productive operations in more than one country.","Depreciation can raise domestic prices when imported inputs or consumer goods cost more.","A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments.","International trade can give households access to goods they could not buy domestically."],"answer":2,"why":"Current-account surplus: A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0292","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Consider this economic situation: A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Which concept is most directly illustrated?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Current-account deficit","Trade in services balance","Trade in goods balance","Current-account surplus"],"answer":3,"why":"The situation illustrates current-account surplus: A current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0293","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which situation is the clearest illustration of current-account surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers.","A new container terminal reduces the cost of exporting refrigerated fruit.","A young solar-equipment industry struggles to compete with large overseas manufacturers.","A weaker currency raises local-currency costs of imported wheat and diesel."],"answer":0,"why":"A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. This illustrates a current-account surplus occurs when current-account receipts exceed payments."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0294","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which economic explanation is most directly linked to current-account surplus?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Higher foreign demand can increase the currency's exchange value, other factors equal.","Restricting imported supply can support local firms but reduce competition.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment.","The stronger currency makes imports cheaper in domestic money but exports dearer abroad."],"answer":2,"why":"A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0295","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A decision-maker plans to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. Which of the following is a possible benefit of this decision?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings.","Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business.","Domestic farmers may face more competition."],"answer":0,"why":"Future output and living standards may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0296","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"The exchange rate is 1 home dollar = 2 foreign units. A machine costs 60 foreign units. How many home dollars are needed, ignoring fees?","scheme":"1 mark for option B.","note":"","options":["$60","$30","$120","$50"],"answer":1,"why":"Home-dollar cost = 60 foreign units / 2 = $30."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0297","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which of the following best supports a proposal to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity?","scheme":"1 mark for option C.","note":"","options":["Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions.","Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving.","Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency.","Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn."],"answer":2,"why":"Importing productive machinery may improve efficiency."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0298","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"Which factor might make the proposal to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity less successful?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["Exporting firms may support additional jobs.","Supplier firms may win new contracts.","Exporters may plan receipts and payments more accurately.","Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."],"answer":3,"why":"Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0299","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"An economist is considering whether to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity. Which outcome gives the most relevant economic justification?","scheme":"1 mark for option A.","note":"","options":["Future output and living standards may increase.","Travellers can compare currency conversion costs.","Businesses may understand currency risks better.","Firms may sell services into new markets."],"answer":0,"why":"Future output and living standards may increase."},{"id":"0455-C06-P1-MCQ-0300","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"1","type":"MCQ","marks":1,"case":"","q":"A case study states: A country earns more from exports and income flows than it spends on imports and transfers. Which explanation best accounts for the economic situation?","scheme":"1 mark for option D.","note":"","options":["The rate determines the domestic-currency cost of foreign transactions.","Expected appreciation can raise present demand and affect exchange rates.","Trade, investment and communication link producers and consumers across borders.","A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."],"answer":3,"why":"A surplus can reflect strong exports or restrained domestic spending and investment."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define international specialisation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A country concentrates production on particular goods or services (1); It trades to obtain other goods / focuses where production is relatively efficient (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain one benefit of international specialisation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Workers repeat specialist tasks and improve skills (1); Labour productivity rises (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Concentrated production increases scale (1); Economies of scale can lower average costs (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain one risk of international specialisation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Export earnings depend heavily on a narrow product range (1); A fall in its price or demand can sharply reduce national income (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Resources depend on one industry (1); Its decline can cause substantial structural unemployment (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define free trade. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Exchange of goods and services between countries (1); Without trade restrictions / government-imposed barriers (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain one benefit consumers may gain from international trade. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Imported supply increases consumer choice (1); Consumers can obtain products unavailable domestically (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Imported competition lowers prices (1); Consumers can buy more with a given income (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define import dependence. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A country relies heavily on imported goods (1); Overseas supply disruptions can interrupt access to essential products (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how international trade may increase competition. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Foreign producers enter or supply the domestic market (1); Domestic firms face more rivals (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Consumers can switch to imported substitutes (1); Domestic firms face pressure to lower prices or improve quality (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define globalisation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Growing economic interconnection / integration (1); Between countries through trade, investment and other links (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how lower transport costs may encourage international trade. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Cheaper transport lowers the cost of importing and exporting (1); More international transactions become profitable (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Lower shipping costs reduce delivered foreign prices (1); Overseas goods can compete more strongly with domestic products (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how improved communication technology may encourage globalisation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Faster communication makes overseas coordination easier (1); International trade and production networks can expand (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Cheaper communication reduces costs of dealing with foreign customers (1); Cross-border services become more viable (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define tariff. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A tax / duty (1); On imported goods (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define import quota. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A quantitative limit (1); On the amount of a product that may be imported (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define embargo. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A ban / prohibition (1); On trade in a product or trade with a particular country (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define infant industry protection. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Temporary protection for a new domestic industry (1); Allows time to develop efficiency before facing established foreign competition (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define exchange rate. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• The price / value of one currency (1); Expressed in terms of another currency (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define currency depreciation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• A fall in the value of a currency (1); Under a floating exchange-rate system / caused by market forces (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain one reason why demand for a country’s currency may increase. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Overseas buyers demand more exports (1); They need more of the exporter’s currency to pay for them (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Foreign investors buy domestic assets (1); They demand domestic currency to make the investment (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain one reason why the supply of a country’s currency on the foreign exchange market may increase. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Domestic buyers increase imports (1); They sell domestic currency to obtain foreign currency (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Residents buy more overseas assets (1); They supply domestic currency to acquire foreign currency (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define exchange-rate speculation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Buying or selling currencies (1); To gain from expected changes in exchange rates (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Explain how currency depreciation may cause inflation. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Depreciation raises domestic-currency import prices (1); More expensive imported consumer goods raise the price level (1).\nOR, an alternative route:\n• Imported raw materials become more expensive after depreciation (1); Higher production costs can cause cost-push inflation (1).","note":"Award 1 mark for the stated point and 1 for its linked explanation. Choose one route; do not combine repeated or contradictory points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define trade in goods balance. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Value of goods exports (1); Minus value of goods imports (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define trade in services balance. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Value of services exports (1); Minus value of services imports (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define primary income balance. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Primary income received from abroad (1); Minus primary income paid abroad / earnings from work and investment (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define secondary income balance. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Current transfers received from abroad (1); Minus current transfers paid abroad / transfers made without an exchange of goods or services (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M02-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"2-mark structured","marks":2,"case":"","q":"Define current-account deficit. [2]","scheme":"Maximum 2 marks. Credit the specific components below.\n• Total payments on goods, services, primary income and secondary income (1); Exceed total current-account receipts in a period (1).","note":"For this definition, credit each component once. Slash-separated wording gives alternatives for the same mark. Equivalent accurate wording is acceptable; no example or case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Specialisation allows workers to repeat processing tasks (1) → Skills and output per worker may increase (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Export production accesses a larger market (1) → Firms can operate on a larger scale (1) → Economies of scale may reduce average costs (1).\n\n3. Export sales earn foreign currency (1) → More essential imports can be financed (1) → Access to machinery and technology may improve (1) → Productive capacity may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Nearby component firms share specialist suppliers (1) → Transport and purchasing costs may fall (1) → Components can be sold at more competitive prices (1).\n\n2. A cluster attracts workers with relevant skills (1) → Firms recruit suitable labour more easily (1) → Productivity may increase (1).\n\n3. Firms exchange production knowledge (1) → Better methods spread through the industry (1) → Costs may decrease (1) → Export competitiveness may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to diversify export production away from one mineral. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A wider export mix creates alternative sources of earnings (1) → A mineral-price fall has a smaller effect on total receipts (1) → Government export-related revenue becomes more predictable (1).\n\n2. New industries provide alternative employment (1) → Workers can move away from declining extraction (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).\n\n3. Manufacturing or services can add value (1) → Higher-value exports may earn more income (1) → More funds become available for investment (1) → Future productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers remove tariffs on common household appliances. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Removing tariffs lowers import prices (1) → Appliances become more affordable (1) → Consumers’ purchasing power rises (1).\n\n2. Imported competition pressures domestic producers (1) → Firms may improve efficiency or quality (1) → Consumer welfare may improve (1).\n\n3. Cheaper appliances include productive household equipment (1) → Time spent on domestic tasks may fall (1) → More time can be available for employment (1) → Household earnings may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to allow more competition from imported food products could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Imported food increases market supply (1) → Equilibrium food prices may fall (1) → Basic food becomes more affordable (1).\n\n2. Domestic suppliers face stronger competition (1) → They have incentives to reduce waste and costs (1) → Efficiency may improve (1).\n\n3. More products and varieties become available (1) → Consumer choice increases (1) → Households can match purchases to preferences (1) → Consumer satisfaction may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to help small manufacturers access export markets. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Exporting expands the potential customer base (1) → Firms may sell more output (1) → Total revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. A larger market allows higher production scale (1) → Average fixed costs may fall (1) → Price competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Export sales earn foreign currency (1) → The country can finance additional imports (1) → Access to productive machinery may improve (1) → Future output may increase (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Multiple foreign suppliers reduce dependence on one source (1) → Disruption in one country need not stop all imports (1) → Agricultural input supplies become more reliable (1).\n\n2. More suppliers increase buyer choice (1) → Importers can compare prices (1) → Fertiliser purchase costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. Reliable fertiliser delivery supports crop production (1) → Farmers face fewer input shortages (1) → Crop yields and food supply may be maintained (1) → Food-price instability may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More footwear brands increase consumer choice (1) → Different styles and prices become available (1) → More preferences can be satisfied (1).\n\n2. Foreign competition limits domestic firms’ pricing power (1) → Prices may fall (1) → Consumers can buy more from a given income (1).\n\n3. Domestic firms face pressure to improve quality (1) → Product standards and durability may rise (1) → Consumers replace shoes less frequently (1) → Value for money may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers participate in international production networks. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Firms specialise in production stages they perform efficiently (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Unit costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Overseas networks provide access to larger markets (1) → Demand for local intermediate goods may rise (1) → Employment may increase (1).\n\n3. International links transfer technology and methods (1) → Domestic workers gain useful skills (1) → Production efficiency may improve (1) → Export competitiveness may strengthen (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Faster cargo handling reduces shipping delays (1) → Goods reach customers sooner (1) → Exporters may retain customers needing timely delivery (1).\n\n2. Shorter delays reduce storage and waiting costs (1) → Transport cost per unit falls (1) → Trade becomes more profitable (1).\n\n3. Better ports increase capacity to handle shipments (1) → More firms can reach overseas buyers (1) → Export sales may expand (1) → Employment and national income may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Reliable broadband allows services to be delivered online (1) → Firms reach foreign customers without physical shipment (1) → Export revenue may increase (1).\n\n2. Lower communication costs make coordination cheaper (1) → Service firms become more price-competitive (1) → Overseas demand may rise (1).\n\n3. Access to overseas clients supports business expansion (1) → Demand for skilled service workers rises (1) → Employment and earnings may increase (1) → The services balance may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A multinational builds production facilities (1) → Capital investment increases (1) → Productive capacity expands (1).\n\n2. New factories demand local labour (1) → Employment and wage income rise (1) → Household consumption may increase (1).\n\n3. Multinationals may train workers and share technology (1) → Domestic skills and productivity improve (1) → Local suppliers may become more efficient (1) → Future output may rise (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Tariffs raise prices of imported tiles (1) → Domestic tiles become relatively cheaper (1) → Demand for domestic output may increase (1).\n\n2. Domestic producers may expand production (1) → Demand for labour rises (1) → Jobs may be protected or created (1).\n\n3. Import duties generate tax revenue (1) → Government funds become available (1) → Spending on public services may increase (1) → Social welfare may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. A quota limits imported footwear supply (1) → Domestic firms face less foreign competition (1) → Demand for domestic footwear may rise (1).\n\n2. Domestic firms may expand to replace imports (1) → Employment may increase (1) → Household earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Fewer footwear imports require payment abroad (1) → Import expenditure may decrease (1) → Foreign-currency outflows may fall (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. An import ban reduces availability of a harmful product (1) → Consumption of that product may decrease (1) → Environmental damage may fall (1).\n\n2. Less exposure to harmful production or consumption reduces health risks (1) → Treatment needs may decrease (1) → Healthcare resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Demand can shift to less harmful domestic substitutes (1) → Cleaner industries may expand (1) → Employment in those industries may rise (1) → More sustainable production may develop (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Temporary protection gives new firms time to grow (1) → Output can expand towards a more efficient scale (1) → Average costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Reduced initial foreign competition allows firms to survive (1) → Workers gain production experience (1) → Productivity may increase (1).\n\n3. A developing domestic industry creates jobs (1) → Wage income and skills rise (1) → Future firms may compete without protection (1) → Export opportunities may eventually expand (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Transparent quotations reveal currency conversion costs (1) → Buyers can compare providers (1) → Unfavourable exchange deals may be avoided (1).\n\n2. Better information reduces uncertainty about transaction values (1) → Firms can price international contracts more accurately (1) → Trading decisions may improve (1).\n\n3. More visible competing rates increase pressure on currency dealers (1) → Excessive conversion margins may narrow (1) → Transaction costs may fall (1) → International exchange becomes cheaper (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Appreciation lowers domestic-currency machinery prices (1) → Firms can afford more capital equipment (1) → Investment may increase (1).\n\n2. Cheaper machinery reduces capital cost per unit of output (1) → Average production cost may decrease (1) → Firms become more competitive (1).\n\n3. New imported equipment may be more productive (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → Productive capacity increases (1) → Long-run growth may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers promote exports after a currency depreciation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Depreciation can lower foreign-currency export prices (1) → Foreign demand for exports may increase (1) → Export revenue may rise if demand responds sufficiently (1).\n\n2. Rising export orders encourage firms to expand output (1) → Derived demand for labour increases (1) → Employment may rise (1).\n\n3. Increased export receipts add to current-account credits (1) → Net export earnings may increase (1) → The current-account deficit may narrow (1) → Foreign-currency needs may be easier to finance (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better quality makes domestic exports more attractive (1) → Overseas demand may increase (1) → Export revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Higher quality can justify higher selling prices (1) → Revenue per unit may increase (1) → Profit may rise (1).\n\n3. Reliable quality helps retain overseas customers (1) → Export orders become more stable (1) → Firms can plan production more confidently (1) → Employment may become more secure (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to make overseas investment rules more transparent. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Clear investment rules reduce uncertainty for overseas investors (1) → More foreign direct investment may occur (1) → Domestic capital stock may increase (1).\n\n2. Transparent procedures reduce administrative delays (1) → Investment projects can start sooner (1) → Employment may rise (1).\n\n3. Investors can better assess legal costs and obligations (1) → Fewer unexpected compliance costs arise (1) → Investment returns become easier to predict (1) → The country may attract longer-term productive projects (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better information makes currency prices easier to compare (1) → Firms avoid unnecessarily expensive conversions (1) → Trading costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Reliable exchange-rate information improves budgeting (1) → Importers can estimate future payment needs (1) → Cash-flow planning may improve (1).\n\n3. Less uncertainty helps exporters set suitable prices (1) → Fewer contracts may become unexpectedly unprofitable (1) → Export activity may be more stable (1) → Jobs in trading firms may be better protected (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Domestic energy replaces dearer imported energy (1) → Firms’ exposure to exchange-rate-driven fuel costs falls (1) → Cost-push inflation may decrease (1).\n\n2. Lower energy imports reduce foreign-currency spending (1) → Import payments may fall (1) → The current-account balance may improve (1).\n\n3. More diverse domestic energy supplies reduce external disruption risks (1) → Production becomes more reliable (1) → Firms lose fewer working days (1) → Output and employment may be more stable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers track the components of the current account separately. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Separate goods and services data identify where a deficit arises (1) → Policies can target the relevant sector (1) → Policy effectiveness may improve (1).\n\n2. Income-flow data distinguish trade problems from investment-income outflows (1) → Authorities avoid treating every deficit as weak exports (1) → Better-matched responses may conserve resources (1).\n\n3. Tracking components reveals changes over time (1) → Policymakers can identify whether measures are working (1) → Ineffective measures can be revised (1) → External-balance policy may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Better export quality increases overseas willingness to buy (1) → Export demand may rise (1) → Export receipts may increase (1).\n\n2. Quality improvements reduce returns and complaints (1) → Firms avoid replacement and repair costs (1) → Profit may rise (1).\n\n3. Stronger product reputation can retain customers (1) → Export demand becomes more secure (1) → Firms may invest with greater confidence (1) → Future productive capacity may expand (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two possible economic benefits of a decision to develop skills needed to export digital services. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Training develops skills required for digital services (1) → More workers can meet overseas customers’ needs (1) → Service exports may increase (1).\n\n2. Better skills raise output per worker (1) → Unit service costs may fall (1) → International competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Digital exports add an income source independent of goods exports (1) → A fall in one goods-export price has a smaller effect on total receipts (1) → Employment may become more stable (1) → Households can plan spending more confidently (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways in which a decision to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings might improve outcomes. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Local saving supplies funds for investment (1) → Firms can buy more capital equipment (1) → Productive capacity increases (1).\n\n2. Domestic funding reduces reliance on foreign borrowing (1) → Future foreign interest payments may be lower (1) → Primary income outflows may decrease (1).\n\n3. More productive equipment raises output per worker (1) → National income may rise (1) → Further saving becomes possible (1) → Investment-led growth may become more sustainable (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two reasons why decision-makers might choose to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Lower transfer fees leave recipients with more money (1) → Disposable income from remittances rises (1) → Households can afford more essentials (1).\n\n2. Cheaper formal transfers encourage use of regulated channels (1) → The risk of loss in informal channels may decrease (1) → Transfers become more reliable (1).\n\n3. More remittance income can finance education or small businesses (1) → Skills or productive capital may increase (1) → Future domestic earnings may rise (1) → Poverty may decrease (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two advantages that might arise if decision-makers improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. More competitive exports attract foreign customers (1) → Export receipts may increase (1) → The current-account balance may improve (1).\n\n2. Avoiding broad import bans preserves access to imported inputs (1) → Firms can obtain suitable machinery and materials (1) → Production costs may remain lower (1).\n\n3. Better competitiveness can sustain overseas sales without permanent restrictions (1) → Firms gain continuing export markets (1) → Output and employment may become more stable (1) → Long-run living standards may improve (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M04-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"4-mark structured","marks":4,"case":"","q":"Explain two ways a proposal to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity could affect economic welfare positively. [4]","scheme":"Maximum 4 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown across three possible explanations. Credit two distinct explanations, up to 2 marks each: one for a relevant benefit or reason and one for its linked economic explanation. Additional steps show alternative development, not extra marks for that explanation.\n1. Reinvested earnings finance productive equipment (1) → Capital stock increases (1) → Potential output rises (1).\n\n2. New equipment can raise labour productivity (1) → Unit costs may fall (1) → Competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Domestic investment creates demand for workers and suppliers (1) → Employment and household income may increase (1) → Tax revenue may rise (1) → More public services can be financed (1).","note":"Each route gives a specific benefit or reason and its economic explanation. Credit equivalent valid routes. Do not reward the same underlying point twice or add more than 4 marks. No case study is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Specialisation allows workers to repeat processing tasks (1) → Skills and output per worker may increase (1) → Unit labour costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Export production accesses a larger market (1) → Firms can operate on a larger scale (1) → Economies of scale may reduce average costs (1).\n\n3. Export sales earn foreign currency (1) → More essential imports can be financed (1) → Access to machinery and technology may improve (1) → Productive capacity may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Nearby component firms share specialist suppliers (1) → Transport and purchasing costs may fall (1) → Components can be sold at more competitive prices (1).\n\n2. A cluster attracts workers with relevant skills (1) → Firms recruit suitable labour more easily (1) → Productivity may increase (1).\n\n3. Firms exchange production knowledge (1) → Better methods spread through the industry (1) → Costs may decrease (1) → Export competitiveness may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to diversify export production away from one mineral could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A wider export mix creates alternative sources of earnings (1) → A mineral-price fall has a smaller effect on total receipts (1) → Government export-related revenue becomes more predictable (1).\n\n2. New industries provide alternative employment (1) → Workers can move away from declining extraction (1) → Structural unemployment may fall (1).\n\n3. Manufacturing or services can add value (1) → Higher-value exports may earn more income (1) → More funds become available for investment (1) → Future productive capacity may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to remove tariffs on common household appliances. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Removing tariffs lowers import prices (1) → Appliances become more affordable (1) → Consumers’ purchasing power rises (1).\n\n2. Imported competition pressures domestic producers (1) → Firms may improve efficiency or quality (1) → Consumer welfare may improve (1).\n\n3. Cheaper appliances include productive household equipment (1) → Time spent on domestic tasks may fall (1) → More time can be available for employment (1) → Household earnings may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to allow more competition from imported food products could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Imported food increases market supply (1) → Equilibrium food prices may fall (1) → Basic food becomes more affordable (1).\n\n2. Domestic suppliers face stronger competition (1) → They have incentives to reduce waste and costs (1) → Efficiency may improve (1).\n\n3. More products and varieties become available (1) → Consumer choice increases (1) → Households can match purchases to preferences (1) → Consumer satisfaction may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to help small manufacturers access export markets. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Exporting expands the potential customer base (1) → Firms may sell more output (1) → Total revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. A larger market allows higher production scale (1) → Average fixed costs may fall (1) → Price competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Export sales earn foreign currency (1) → The country can finance additional imports (1) → Access to productive machinery may improve (1) → Future output may increase (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Multiple foreign suppliers reduce dependence on one source (1) → Disruption in one country need not stop all imports (1) → Agricultural input supplies become more reliable (1).\n\n2. More suppliers increase buyer choice (1) → Importers can compare prices (1) → Fertiliser purchase costs may decrease (1).\n\n3. Reliable fertiliser delivery supports crop production (1) → Farmers face fewer input shortages (1) → Crop yields and food supply may be maintained (1) → Food-price instability may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More footwear brands increase consumer choice (1) → Different styles and prices become available (1) → More preferences can be satisfied (1).\n\n2. Foreign competition limits domestic firms’ pricing power (1) → Prices may fall (1) → Consumers can buy more from a given income (1).\n\n3. Domestic firms face pressure to improve quality (1) → Product standards and durability may rise (1) → Consumers replace shoes less frequently (1) → Value for money may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to participate in international production networks. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Firms specialise in production stages they perform efficiently (1) → Labour productivity may increase (1) → Unit costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Overseas networks provide access to larger markets (1) → Demand for local intermediate goods may rise (1) → Employment may increase (1).\n\n3. International links transfer technology and methods (1) → Domestic workers gain useful skills (1) → Production efficiency may improve (1) → Export competitiveness may strengthen (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Faster cargo handling reduces shipping delays (1) → Goods reach customers sooner (1) → Exporters may retain customers needing timely delivery (1).\n\n2. Shorter delays reduce storage and waiting costs (1) → Transport cost per unit falls (1) → Trade becomes more profitable (1).\n\n3. Better ports increase capacity to handle shipments (1) → More firms can reach overseas buyers (1) → Export sales may expand (1) → Employment and national income may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Reliable broadband allows services to be delivered online (1) → Firms reach foreign customers without physical shipment (1) → Export revenue may increase (1).\n\n2. Lower communication costs make coordination cheaper (1) → Service firms become more price-competitive (1) → Overseas demand may rise (1).\n\n3. Access to overseas clients supports business expansion (1) → Demand for skilled service workers rises (1) → Employment and earnings may increase (1) → The services balance may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A multinational builds production facilities (1) → Capital investment increases (1) → Productive capacity expands (1).\n\n2. New factories demand local labour (1) → Employment and wage income rise (1) → Household consumption may increase (1).\n\n3. Multinationals may train workers and share technology (1) → Domestic skills and productivity improve (1) → Local suppliers may become more efficient (1) → Future output may rise (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Tariffs raise prices of imported tiles (1) → Domestic tiles become relatively cheaper (1) → Demand for domestic output may increase (1).\n\n2. Domestic producers may expand production (1) → Demand for labour rises (1) → Jobs may be protected or created (1).\n\n3. Import duties generate tax revenue (1) → Government funds become available (1) → Spending on public services may increase (1) → Social welfare may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. A quota limits imported footwear supply (1) → Domestic firms face less foreign competition (1) → Demand for domestic footwear may rise (1).\n\n2. Domestic firms may expand to replace imports (1) → Employment may increase (1) → Household earnings may rise (1).\n\n3. Fewer footwear imports require payment abroad (1) → Import expenditure may decrease (1) → Foreign-currency outflows may fall (1) → The trade in goods balance may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. An import ban reduces availability of a harmful product (1) → Consumption of that product may decrease (1) → Environmental damage may fall (1).\n\n2. Less exposure to harmful production or consumption reduces health risks (1) → Treatment needs may decrease (1) → Healthcare resources are conserved (1).\n\n3. Demand can shift to less harmful domestic substitutes (1) → Cleaner industries may expand (1) → Employment in those industries may rise (1) → More sustainable production may develop (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Temporary protection gives new firms time to grow (1) → Output can expand towards a more efficient scale (1) → Average costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Reduced initial foreign competition allows firms to survive (1) → Workers gain production experience (1) → Productivity may increase (1).\n\n3. A developing domestic industry creates jobs (1) → Wage income and skills rise (1) → Future firms may compete without protection (1) → Export opportunities may eventually expand (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Transparent quotations reveal currency conversion costs (1) → Buyers can compare providers (1) → Unfavourable exchange deals may be avoided (1).\n\n2. Better information reduces uncertainty about transaction values (1) → Firms can price international contracts more accurately (1) → Trading decisions may improve (1).\n\n3. More visible competing rates increase pressure on currency dealers (1) → Excessive conversion margins may narrow (1) → Transaction costs may fall (1) → International exchange becomes cheaper (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Appreciation lowers domestic-currency machinery prices (1) → Firms can afford more capital equipment (1) → Investment may increase (1).\n\n2. Cheaper machinery reduces capital cost per unit of output (1) → Average production cost may decrease (1) → Firms become more competitive (1).\n\n3. New imported equipment may be more productive (1) → Output per worker rises (1) → Productive capacity increases (1) → Long-run growth may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to promote exports after a currency depreciation. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Depreciation can lower foreign-currency export prices (1) → Foreign demand for exports may increase (1) → Export revenue may rise if demand responds sufficiently (1).\n\n2. Rising export orders encourage firms to expand output (1) → Derived demand for labour increases (1) → Employment may rise (1).\n\n3. Increased export receipts add to current-account credits (1) → Net export earnings may increase (1) → The current-account deficit may narrow (1) → Foreign-currency needs may be easier to finance (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better quality makes domestic exports more attractive (1) → Overseas demand may increase (1) → Export revenue may rise (1).\n\n2. Higher quality can justify higher selling prices (1) → Revenue per unit may increase (1) → Profit may rise (1).\n\n3. Reliable quality helps retain overseas customers (1) → Export orders become more stable (1) → Firms can plan production more confidently (1) → Employment may become more secure (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to make overseas investment rules more transparent. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Clear investment rules reduce uncertainty for overseas investors (1) → More foreign direct investment may occur (1) → Domestic capital stock may increase (1).\n\n2. Transparent procedures reduce administrative delays (1) → Investment projects can start sooner (1) → Employment may rise (1).\n\n3. Investors can better assess legal costs and obligations (1) → Fewer unexpected compliance costs arise (1) → Investment returns become easier to predict (1) → The country may attract longer-term productive projects (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better information makes currency prices easier to compare (1) → Firms avoid unnecessarily expensive conversions (1) → Trading costs may fall (1).\n\n2. Reliable exchange-rate information improves budgeting (1) → Importers can estimate future payment needs (1) → Cash-flow planning may improve (1).\n\n3. Less uncertainty helps exporters set suitable prices (1) → Fewer contracts may become unexpectedly unprofitable (1) → Export activity may be more stable (1) → Jobs in trading firms may be better protected (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Domestic energy replaces dearer imported energy (1) → Firms’ exposure to exchange-rate-driven fuel costs falls (1) → Cost-push inflation may decrease (1).\n\n2. Lower energy imports reduce foreign-currency spending (1) → Import payments may fall (1) → The current-account balance may improve (1).\n\n3. More diverse domestic energy supplies reduce external disruption risks (1) → Production becomes more reliable (1) → Firms lose fewer working days (1) → Output and employment may be more stable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to track the components of the current account separately. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Separate goods and services data identify where a deficit arises (1) → Policies can target the relevant sector (1) → Policy effectiveness may improve (1).\n\n2. Income-flow data distinguish trade problems from investment-income outflows (1) → Authorities avoid treating every deficit as weak exports (1) → Better-matched responses may conserve resources (1).\n\n3. Tracking components reveals changes over time (1) → Policymakers can identify whether measures are working (1) → Ineffective measures can be revised (1) → External-balance policy may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Better export quality increases overseas willingness to buy (1) → Export demand may rise (1) → Export receipts may increase (1).\n\n2. Quality improvements reduce returns and complaints (1) → Firms avoid replacement and repair costs (1) → Profit may rise (1).\n\n3. Stronger product reputation can retain customers (1) → Export demand becomes more secure (1) → Firms may invest with greater confidence (1) → Future productive capacity may expand (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two economic reasons why decision-makers may choose to develop skills needed to export digital services. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Training develops skills required for digital services (1) → More workers can meet overseas customers’ needs (1) → Service exports may increase (1).\n\n2. Better skills raise output per worker (1) → Unit service costs may fall (1) → International competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Digital exports add an income source independent of goods exports (1) → A fall in one goods-export price has a smaller effect on total receipts (1) → Employment may become more stable (1) → Households can plan spending more confidently (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a decision to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings may improve economic outcomes. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Local saving supplies funds for investment (1) → Firms can buy more capital equipment (1) → Productive capacity increases (1).\n\n2. Domestic funding reduces reliance on foreign borrowing (1) → Future foreign interest payments may be lower (1) → Primary income outflows may decrease (1).\n\n3. More productive equipment raises output per worker (1) → National income may rise (1) → Further saving becomes possible (1) → Investment-led growth may become more sustainable (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse two ways in which a plan to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances could benefit the groups involved. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Lower transfer fees leave recipients with more money (1) → Disposable income from remittances rises (1) → Households can afford more essentials (1).\n\n2. Cheaper formal transfers encourage use of regulated channels (1) → The risk of loss in informal channels may decrease (1) → Transfers become more reliable (1).\n\n3. More remittance income can finance education or small businesses (1) → Skills or productive capital may increase (1) → Future domestic earnings may rise (1) → Poverty may decrease (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse why it may be beneficial to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. More competitive exports attract foreign customers (1) → Export receipts may increase (1) → The current-account balance may improve (1).\n\n2. Avoiding broad import bans preserves access to imported inputs (1) → Firms can obtain suitable machinery and materials (1) → Production costs may remain lower (1).\n\n3. Better competitiveness can sustain overseas sales without permanent restrictions (1) → Firms gain continuing export markets (1) → Output and employment may become more stable (1) → Long-run living standards may improve (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M06-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"6-mark structured","marks":6,"case":"","q":"Analyse how a policy to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity could create economic gains. [6]","scheme":"Maximum 6 marks. Ten alternative credit points are shown below. Credit two distinct analytical routes, up to 3 marks per route. Parenthesised marks identify specific creditworthy points; the fourth step in route 3 is an alternative development, not a fourth mark for that route.\n1. Reinvested earnings finance productive equipment (1) → Capital stock increases (1) → Potential output rises (1).\n\n2. New equipment can raise labour productivity (1) → Unit costs may fall (1) → Competitiveness may improve (1).\n\n3. Domestic investment creates demand for workers and suppliers (1) → Employment and household income may increase (1) → Tax revenue may rise (1) → More public services can be financed (1).","note":"A linked analytical step must follow logically from the point being developed. Credit other correct economic links; do not reward repetition within or across routes. The combined score cannot exceed 6. No case-study application is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0001","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to specialise in sustainable seafood processing for export would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Specialisation allows workers to repeat processing tasks → Skills and output per worker may increase → Unit labour costs may fall.\n\n2. Export production accesses a larger market → Firms can operate on a larger scale → Economies of scale may reduce average costs.\n\n3. Export sales earn foreign currency → More essential imports can be financed → Access to machinery and technology may improve → Productive capacity may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. The country may become dependent on global seafood demand → Export incomes become vulnerable to overseas recessions.\n\n2. Overfishing could damage future productive resources → Future seafood output may fall.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0002","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to support a regional bicycle-component production cluster. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Nearby component firms share specialist suppliers → Transport and purchasing costs may fall → Components can be sold at more competitive prices.\n\n2. A cluster attracts workers with relevant skills → Firms recruit suitable labour more easily → Productivity may increase.\n\n3. Firms exchange production knowledge → Better methods spread through the industry → Costs may decrease → Export competitiveness may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Demand for bicycle parts could decline → Cluster firms may face excess capacity.\n\n2. Regions may become vulnerable to one industry's downturn → Regional structural unemployment may rise.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0003","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to diversify export production away from one mineral. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A wider export mix creates alternative sources of earnings → A mineral-price fall has a smaller effect on total receipts → Government export-related revenue becomes more predictable.\n\n2. New industries provide alternative employment → Workers can move away from declining extraction → Structural unemployment may fall.\n\n3. Manufacturing or services can add value → Higher-value exports may earn more income → More funds become available for investment → Future productive capacity may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Developing new industries costs money → Other public or private projects may be delayed.\n\n2. Diversification may reduce some existing scale advantages → Average costs in established industries may increase.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0004","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to remove tariffs on common household appliances is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Removing tariffs lowers import prices → Appliances become more affordable → Consumers’ purchasing power rises.\n\n2. Imported competition pressures domestic producers → Firms may improve efficiency or quality → Consumer welfare may improve.\n\n3. Cheaper appliances include productive household equipment → Time spent on domestic tasks may fall → More time can be available for employment → Household earnings may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some local appliance makers may lose market share → Domestic output and employment may fall.\n\n2. Import dependence may increase in strategic product categories → Overseas disruptions may threaten supplies.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0005","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to allow more competition from imported food products would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Imported food increases market supply → Equilibrium food prices may fall → Basic food becomes more affordable.\n\n2. Domestic suppliers face stronger competition → They have incentives to reduce waste and costs → Efficiency may improve.\n\n3. More products and varieties become available → Consumer choice increases → Households can match purchases to preferences → Consumer satisfaction may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Domestic farmers may face more competition → Agricultural employment and incomes may fall.\n\n2. Transport-related emissions may increase → External environmental costs may increase.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0006","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to help small manufacturers access export markets would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Exporting expands the potential customer base → Firms may sell more output → Total revenue may rise.\n\n2. A larger market allows higher production scale → Average fixed costs may fall → Price competitiveness may improve.\n\n3. Export sales earn foreign currency → The country can finance additional imports → Access to productive machinery may improve → Future output may increase.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Exchange-rate changes can reduce export earnings → Domestic-currency revenue may become less predictable.\n\n2. Meeting foreign standards may be costly → Small firms may lack funds to enter markets.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0007","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to diversify sources of essential fertiliser imports would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Multiple foreign suppliers reduce dependence on one source → Disruption in one country need not stop all imports → Agricultural input supplies become more reliable.\n\n2. More suppliers increase buyer choice → Importers can compare prices → Fertiliser purchase costs may decrease.\n\n3. Reliable fertiliser delivery supports crop production → Farmers face fewer input shortages → Crop yields and food supply may be maintained → Food-price instability may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Multiple supplier contracts may be more expensive → Average input costs may increase.\n\n2. Global shortages can affect all source countries → Diversification may not prevent crop-input shortages.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0008","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.1","topicName":"Specialisation and free trade","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to permit additional overseas footwear brands to enter. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More footwear brands increase consumer choice → Different styles and prices become available → More preferences can be satisfied.\n\n2. Foreign competition limits domestic firms’ pricing power → Prices may fall → Consumers can buy more from a given income.\n\n3. Domestic firms face pressure to improve quality → Product standards and durability may rise → Consumers replace shoes less frequently → Value for money may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Some domestic workers may lose jobs → Domestic wage income may fall.\n\n2. Foreign firms may dominate a very small local market → Later market power may permit higher prices.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0009","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to participate in international production networks. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Firms specialise in production stages they perform efficiently → Labour productivity may increase → Unit costs may fall.\n\n2. Overseas networks provide access to larger markets → Demand for local intermediate goods may rise → Employment may increase.\n\n3. International links transfer technology and methods → Domestic workers gain useful skills → Production efficiency may improve → Export competitiveness may strengthen.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Global disruptions can interrupt supply chains → Missing imported components can stop domestic production.\n\n2. Long-distance transport may increase emissions → External environmental costs may rise.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0010","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to modernise ports to reduce international shipping delays is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Faster cargo handling reduces shipping delays → Goods reach customers sooner → Exporters may retain customers needing timely delivery.\n\n2. Shorter delays reduce storage and waiting costs → Transport cost per unit falls → Trade becomes more profitable.\n\n3. Better ports increase capacity to handle shipments → More firms can reach overseas buyers → Export sales may expand → Employment and national income may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Port upgrades require significant public or private funds → Other investment opportunities may be sacrificed.\n\n2. Expanded shipping may increase local pollution → Nearby residents may bear external costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0011","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to expand reliable broadband for exporting service firms would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Reliable broadband allows services to be delivered online → Firms reach foreign customers without physical shipment → Export revenue may increase.\n\n2. Lower communication costs make coordination cheaper → Service firms become more price-competitive → Overseas demand may rise.\n\n3. Access to overseas clients supports business expansion → Demand for skilled service workers rises → Employment and earnings may increase → The services balance may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Poor cybersecurity could disrupt business → Lost information or outages may reduce service exports.\n\n2. Some communities may remain digitally excluded → Regional income gaps may persist.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0012","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to attract a multinational electronics manufacturer would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A multinational builds production facilities → Capital investment increases → Productive capacity expands.\n\n2. New factories demand local labour → Employment and wage income rise → Household consumption may increase.\n\n3. Multinationals may train workers and share technology → Domestic skills and productivity improve → Local suppliers may become more efficient → Future output may rise.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Profits may be remitted to the firm’s home country → Primary income payments abroad increase.\n\n2. The plant may relocate if domestic costs rise relative to other countries → Jobs and productive capacity may be lost.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0013","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to introduce a temporary tariff on imported ceramic tiles would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Tariffs raise prices of imported tiles → Domestic tiles become relatively cheaper → Demand for domestic output may increase.\n\n2. Domestic producers may expand production → Demand for labour rises → Jobs may be protected or created.\n\n3. Import duties generate tax revenue → Government funds become available → Spending on public services may increase → Social welfare may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Households and builders may face higher prices → Building costs and household spending burdens rise.\n\n2. Trading partners may retaliate with tariffs of their own → Domestic export sales may fall.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0014","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to cap annual imports of low-cost footwear. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. A quota limits imported footwear supply → Domestic firms face less foreign competition → Demand for domestic footwear may rise.\n\n2. Domestic firms may expand to replace imports → Employment may increase → Household earnings may rise.\n\n3. Fewer footwear imports require payment abroad → Import expenditure may decrease → Foreign-currency outflows may fall → The trade in goods balance may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Consumers may face less choice and higher prices → Consumer purchasing power may decrease.\n\n2. Foreign suppliers may respond with trade restrictions → Exporting industries may lose jobs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0015","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to ban imports of a product associated with severe environmental harm. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. An import ban reduces availability of a harmful product → Consumption of that product may decrease → Environmental damage may fall.\n\n2. Less exposure to harmful production or consumption reduces health risks → Treatment needs may decrease → Healthcare resources are conserved.\n\n3. Demand can shift to less harmful domestic substitutes → Cleaner industries may expand → Employment in those industries may rise → More sustainable production may develop.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Consumers could face higher prices for substitutes → Real incomes may be reduced.\n\n2. Enforcement and illegal trade may be difficult to control → Public funds may be spent without stopping harmful imports.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0016","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.2","topicName":"Globalisation and trade restrictions","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to protect a new solar-equipment industry for a limited period is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Temporary protection gives new firms time to grow → Output can expand towards a more efficient scale → Average costs may fall.\n\n2. Reduced initial foreign competition allows firms to survive → Workers gain production experience → Productivity may increase.\n\n3. A developing domestic industry creates jobs → Wage income and skills rise → Future firms may compete without protection → Export opportunities may eventually expand.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Protection can keep prices artificially high → Consumers have less income for other goods.\n\n2. Firms may become dependent on continuing protection → Firms may lack incentives to reduce costs.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0017","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to publish transparent foreign-exchange quotations would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Transparent quotations reveal currency conversion costs → Buyers can compare providers → Unfavourable exchange deals may be avoided.\n\n2. Better information reduces uncertainty about transaction values → Firms can price international contracts more accurately → Trading decisions may improve.\n\n3. More visible competing rates increase pressure on currency dealers → Excessive conversion margins may narrow → Transaction costs may fall → International exchange becomes cheaper.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Exchange rates can fluctuate between quotation and payment → Actual transaction costs may differ from the quoted amount.\n\n2. Transaction fees may reduce the amount received → Transparency alone may not make exchange inexpensive.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0018","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to use cheaper imported machinery after currency appreciation would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Appreciation lowers domestic-currency machinery prices → Firms can afford more capital equipment → Investment may increase.\n\n2. Cheaper machinery reduces capital cost per unit of output → Average production cost may decrease → Firms become more competitive.\n\n3. New imported equipment may be more productive → Output per worker rises → Productive capacity increases → Long-run growth may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Exporters may lose price competitiveness abroad → Export demand and domestic employment may fall.\n\n2. Future depreciation could reverse the cost saving → Machinery or repayment costs could rise again.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0019","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to promote exports after a currency depreciation would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Depreciation can lower foreign-currency export prices → Foreign demand for exports may increase → Export revenue may rise if demand responds sufficiently.\n\n2. Rising export orders encourage firms to expand output → Derived demand for labour increases → Employment may rise.\n\n3. Increased export receipts add to current-account credits → Net export earnings may increase → The current-account deficit may narrow → Foreign-currency needs may be easier to finance.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Imported fuel and machinery may become more expensive → Production costs may rise and inflation may increase.\n\n2. Export demand may respond slowly if contracts are fixed → The trade balance may fail to improve quickly.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0020","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to improve export quality to attract overseas buyers. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better quality makes domestic exports more attractive → Overseas demand may increase → Export revenue may rise.\n\n2. Higher quality can justify higher selling prices → Revenue per unit may increase → Profit may rise.\n\n3. Reliable quality helps retain overseas customers → Export orders become more stable → Firms can plan production more confidently → Employment may become more secure.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. A stronger currency may later reduce export competitiveness → Foreign demand may weaken despite quality gains.\n\n2. Competitors abroad may lower their prices → Market share may not rise as expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0021","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to make overseas investment rules more transparent. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Clear investment rules reduce uncertainty for overseas investors → More foreign direct investment may occur → Domestic capital stock may increase.\n\n2. Transparent procedures reduce administrative delays → Investment projects can start sooner → Employment may rise.\n\n3. Investors can better assess legal costs and obligations → Fewer unexpected compliance costs arise → Investment returns become easier to predict → The country may attract longer-term productive projects.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Large outflows could weaken the domestic currency → Import costs and inflation may rise.\n\n2. Funds invested abroad may reduce investment at home → Domestic capital formation may be reduced.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0022","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to improve transparency of foreign-exchange market information is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better information makes currency prices easier to compare → Firms avoid unnecessarily expensive conversions → Trading costs may fall.\n\n2. Reliable exchange-rate information improves budgeting → Importers can estimate future payment needs → Cash-flow planning may improve.\n\n3. Less uncertainty helps exporters set suitable prices → Fewer contracts may become unexpectedly unprofitable → Export activity may be more stable → Jobs in trading firms may be better protected.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Speculative flows can still cause sharp fluctuations → Firms still face exchange-rate risk.\n\n2. Not all future political or economic events are predictable → Reliable information cannot remove all uncertainty.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0023","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.3","topicName":"Foreign exchange rates","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to reduce reliance on imported energy after depreciation would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Domestic energy replaces dearer imported energy → Firms’ exposure to exchange-rate-driven fuel costs falls → Cost-push inflation may decrease.\n\n2. Lower energy imports reduce foreign-currency spending → Import payments may fall → The current-account balance may improve.\n\n3. More diverse domestic energy supplies reduce external disruption risks → Production becomes more reliable → Firms lose fewer working days → Output and employment may be more stable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Developing local energy supplies is expensive → Funds are diverted from other productive uses.\n\n2. Some energy resources cannot be produced domestically → Imports may remain necessary despite investment.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0024","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to track the components of the current account separately would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Separate goods and services data identify where a deficit arises → Policies can target the relevant sector → Policy effectiveness may improve.\n\n2. Income-flow data distinguish trade problems from investment-income outflows → Authorities avoid treating every deficit as weak exports → Better-matched responses may conserve resources.\n\n3. Tracking components reveals changes over time → Policymakers can identify whether measures are working → Ineffective measures can be revised → External-balance policy may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Data can arrive with reporting delays → Policy may react too late to recent changes.\n\n2. A headline balance does not capture all economic conditions → Appropriate policy still requires wider information.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0025","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether the benefits of a decision to improve the quality of domestically produced export goods would outweigh the disadvantages. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Better export quality increases overseas willingness to buy → Export demand may rise → Export receipts may increase.\n\n2. Quality improvements reduce returns and complaints → Firms avoid replacement and repair costs → Profit may rise.\n\n3. Stronger product reputation can retain customers → Export demand becomes more secure → Firms may invest with greater confidence → Future productive capacity may expand.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Quality upgrades can be expensive → Less finance remains for other improvements.\n\n2. Global demand may remain weak → Higher quality may not bring enough new orders.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0026","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far it would be economically beneficial to develop skills needed to export digital services. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Training develops skills required for digital services → More workers can meet overseas customers’ needs → Service exports may increase.\n\n2. Better skills raise output per worker → Unit service costs may fall → International competitiveness may improve.\n\n3. Digital exports add an income source independent of goods exports → A fall in one goods-export price has a smaller effect on total receipts → Employment may become more stable → Households can plan spending more confidently.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training costs money and takes time → Current public or private spending is sacrificed.\n\n2. Foreign competition may limit prices charged → Export earnings may be less than expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0027","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether decision-makers should prioritise a proposal to encourage productive domestic investment financed by local savings. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Local saving supplies funds for investment → Firms can buy more capital equipment → Productive capacity increases.\n\n2. Domestic funding reduces reliance on foreign borrowing → Future foreign interest payments may be lower → Primary income outflows may decrease.\n\n3. More productive equipment raises output per worker → National income may rise → Further saving becomes possible → Investment-led growth may become more sustainable.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Domestic savings may be insufficient for large projects → Some productive projects may remain unfunded.\n\n2. Foreign direct investment can bring expertise that local finance lacks → Technological gains from overseas investors may be lost.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0028","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a plan to lower fees for formal cross-border remittances is likely to improve economic outcomes. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Lower transfer fees leave recipients with more money → Disposable income from remittances rises → Households can afford more essentials.\n\n2. Cheaper formal transfers encourage use of regulated channels → The risk of loss in informal channels may decrease → Transfers become more reliable.\n\n3. More remittance income can finance education or small businesses → Skills or productive capital may increase → Future domestic earnings may rise → Poverty may decrease.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Payment services require compliance and monitoring → Costs may limit how far fees can fall.\n\n2. Remittance income may fall if overseas employment weakens → Recipient households may still face unstable incomes.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0029","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss how far a decision to improve export competitiveness rather than imposing broad import bans would be successful in the long run. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. More competitive exports attract foreign customers → Export receipts may increase → The current-account balance may improve.\n\n2. Avoiding broad import bans preserves access to imported inputs → Firms can obtain suitable machinery and materials → Production costs may remain lower.\n\n3. Better competitiveness can sustain overseas sales without permanent restrictions → Firms gain continuing export markets → Output and employment may become more stable → Long-run living standards may improve.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Training and investment take time → Current-account improvements may be delayed.\n\n2. An overseas slowdown may still reduce exports → Export earnings may not rise despite better productivity.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."},{"id":"0455-C06-P2-M08-0030","c":5,"chapter":"International Trade and Globalisation","topic":"6.4","topicName":"Current account of the balance of payments","paper":"2","type":"8-mark structured","marks":8,"case":"","q":"Discuss whether a policy or business decision to invest some surplus earnings in productive domestic capacity would be justified. [8]","scheme":"Maximum 8 marks, using the discussion rubric. The following 14 economic points provide alternative material for developed arguments; they are not 14 additive marks.\n\nArguments supporting the proposal:\n1. Reinvested earnings finance productive equipment → Capital stock increases → Potential output rises.\n\n2. New equipment can raise labour productivity → Unit costs may fall → Competitiveness may improve.\n\n3. Domestic investment creates demand for workers and suppliers → Employment and household income may increase → Tax revenue may rise → More public services can be financed.\n\nArguments against / reasons for limited success:\n1. Extra domestic demand can reduce the surplus → Higher import purchases may reduce net export earnings.\n\n2. Poorly selected projects can waste the earnings → Potential output may rise less than expected.","note":"Assess the whole discussion with the 8-mark rubric below. Credit clear causal reasoning drawn from these points or other valid alternatives. Strong responses develop both sides and assess which is more convincing under relevant conditions. One-sided analysis can reach 3–5 marks; unsupported statements normally remain at 1–2. Do not mechanically total the listed points. No case-study reference is required."}];
